window.I18N_SP["en"] = {"ant":{"name":"Worker ant (pavement ant)","tagline":"In an ant colony, most members are sterile workers","survivalNote":"Worker lifespan data come mainly from laboratory colonies (months to a year). The steep drop during foraging is a rough estimate based on predation, heat and infection pressure in the field.","epitaph":"You are a worker ant in one tunnel beneath the grass. You spend your life going back and forth between the nest and the grass, and the nest will outlive you.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Insecta › Hymenoptera › Formicidae","size":"Worker body length about 3 mm","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Nest size","birth.rolls.2.label":"Season of birth","birth.siblingsLabel":"Workers that emerged in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are a sterile worker ant ({sex}). You are currently an egg, lying in {r0}. You will pass through the larval and pupal stages, growing up in {r1}. About {siblings} workers emerged in the same batch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a brood chamber under a meadow's topsoil","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a shallow nest beneath a stone","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a compacted patch of soil beside a path","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a small colony of a few hundred ants","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a large colony of more than a thousand ants","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A spring hatch, after the first warm rain","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A late-summer brood, in a new nest the queen had just dug","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A dry summer, with low humidity in the nest","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"The first warm afternoon after winter","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva","stages.2.name":"Pupa","stages.3.name":"Indoor work (nursing)","stages.4.name":"Outdoor work (foraging)","stages.5.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Caught by a meadow spider","causes.0.text":"A meadow spider drops from a grass blade. Its fangs sink in from behind, and its venom stops your struggling within moments.","causes.1.short":"Pecked up by a bird","causes.1.text":"A sparrow bursts out of the grass. Its beak snaps shut, and you are gone.","causes.2.short":"Killed in a raid by another colony","causes.2.text":"A small skirmish on the border. The other colony bites your leg and drags you into its tunnels.","causes.3.short":"Infected by Beauveria fungus","causes.3.text":"Spores of a fungus stick to your body wall and bore through it. Filaments grow throughout your body.","causes.4.short":"Nest flooded by heavy rain","causes.4.text":"Rain fills the underground tunnels. The eggs and larvae in the brood chamber are swept apart first, and you are wrapped in water and soon go still.","causes.5.short":"Dried out on the hot surface","causes.5.text":"The ground is hot enough to scorch your feet. When you stop in the shade, very little water is left in your body.","causes.6.short":"Starved","causes.6.text":"Food from the nest never reaches you in time. Your body grows lighter little by little, until it stops moving.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your legs slowly come to rest on the ground. You no longer climb, and you no longer return to the nest.","events.0.text":"The egg lies among its companions in the brood chamber, and the workers nudge each one along with their antennae.","events.1.text":"Rain seeping into the soil makes a sound in the tunnels outside, and the humidity in the brood chamber rises a little.","events.2.text":"A worker spits food from its mouth and offers it to your mouthparts. You are the one being fed.","events.3.text":"You lie still in the brood chamber while your body grows longer day by day. During the larval stage you can only be fed by others.","events.4.text":"A larger worker carries a bit of debris into the brood chamber, and the air fills with the sharp smell of formic acid.","events.5.text":"You stay still while your body slowly separates into parts, growing legs, antennae and mouthparts.","events.6.text":"You have emerged as an adult. Your body is still soft, and the workers keep touching you with their antennae, again and again, to confirm you are one of them.","events.7.text":"You clear away a pile of abandoned eggs and carry them to a spot with a better temperature.","events.8.text":"You lick the surface of a larva to clean off leftover mould spores.","events.9.text":"A tremor runs through the far end of the tunnel: something is digging through the soil. The workers in the nest all stop moving.","events.10.text":"A worker regurgitates liquid from its crop for you. It is the food the colony shares.","events.11.text":"For the first time you follow a pheromone trail to the nest entrance. Your antennae are still learning to tell it apart.","events.12.text":"You walk out of the nest. The ground's light is blinding, and your compound eyes can see nothing for a while. You follow the trail your companions left and keep going.","events.13.text":"You find a dead beetle. You and several companions drag it back to the nest.","events.14.text":"A hot wind blows down from the grass blades, and the ground quickly heats up. You hide in a patch of shade and wait a long time.","events.15.text":"A spider's web sways in the grass stems above your head. You go around it without looking back.","events.16.text":"You stop beside a group of aphids. The honeydew they secrete is sweet, and you drink deeply.","events.17.text":"A worker from another colony confronts you on the border. Your antennae touch, and then you both back away.","events.18.text":"A heavy rain floods a low tunnel. You and your companions cling together and climb to higher ground.","events.19.text":"About 2×10^16 ants are alive at any one time across the world. You are a very small part of that.","events.19.fact":"An estimated 2×10^16 ants are alive worldwide at any one time (Schultheiss et al. 2022, PNAS)","events.20.text":"Your walking grows slower, and each trip back to the nest needs a longer rest.","events.21.text":"Your legs are caked with dry mud, and several segments have worn bare.","events.22.text":"In the shade at the base of a grass blade you find a small piece of sugar. You eat it slowly.","events.23.text":"At the base of the grass you find a small heap of dropped seeds, and you carry them back to the nest one by one.","events.24.text":"You touch a drop of honeydew with your antennae, lick it up, and then go looking for the next plant with aphids.","events.25.text":"You retreat deep into the nest, where humidity stays steady. The hot wind cannot reach you here, and you stay quiet.","events.26.text":"You hide in the shade of a stone and go no further. The grass outside has been bleached white by the sun.","events.27.text":"You pause in a corner of the brood chamber. Your companions crowd together, and your antennae slowly droop.","events.28.text":"You stay a long time in the shade of a grass stem. Your body goes cold, and your legs no longer tingle.","events.29.text":"You leave familiar trails and walk a long way along a new root path. The smell is unfamiliar, and you do not dare stop.","events.30.text":"You cross a patch of bare sand and find a beetle shell that a bird has pecked open. You carry the fragments back to the nest.","events.31.text":"Over the week the nest warmed slowly, and the larvae fed fast. After you molted, your body suddenly plumped up.","events.32.text":"The first colony members of spring emerged. Together you carried grass seeds, moving quickly and in step.","events.33.text":"This nest had only one queen to look after you. Her food was scarce, and the larvae stayed small.","events.34.text":"You were one of the first workers out of this nest. You were small for the job, yet you had to go out and find food for the queen.","events.35.text":"The nest was dry. The larvae huddled into a heap, each covering the others with its body.","events.36.text":"You found a damp tunnel beneath the grass roots. The seeds there were a little mouldy, but at least they were wet.","events.37.text":"It grew a little warmer, and the ants woke and carried the larvae to a warmer spot.","events.38.text":"You went out to forage for the first time. Frozen dew still clung to the grass blades outside.","events.39.text":"You left your familiar foraging trail and climbed to the top of a grass stem to look over the unfamiliar meadow. The wind was strong, and you nearly got blown off.","events.40.text":"A bird landed in the grass. You all pulled back into the hole and stayed there until it flew away.","events.41.text":"You were smaller and thinner than the workers your age. The best larvae went first to the big workers, so you were left to tend the eggs in the corners.","events.42.text":"Your legs are strong enough to drag back a beetle twice your size in one go. Your nestmates step aside to make way.","decisions.0.title":"An invading colony","decisions.0.body":"Workers from another colony are pushing in through a tunnel on the far side of the nest. They are biting your companions' legs and trying to drag the eggs out of the brood chamber.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Join the defence and bite the invaders","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Defend the nest · May be fatal","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You bite the antenna of one invader and it lets go. One of your legs is bitten off in return.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Retreat into the deeper tunnels","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You retreat into a deeper tunnel. The invaders pause at the nest entrance for a while, then withdraw.","decisions.1.title":"Leaving the nest","decisions.1.body":"Your body has hardened, and the scent on your antennae has changed. The larvae in the brood chamber still need you, but out on the grass there is food, and a trail leads all the way to the nest entrance.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Walk out of the nest to look for food","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Outdoor death risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You queue behind a line of workers and walk out of the nest. On the first day, the heat of the ground numbs your feet.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay in the nest and keep tending the larvae","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stay on a while longer, clearing the egg piles and feeding the larvae. The food outside is still there, and you do not go to it.","decisions.2.title":"A bright trail","decisions.2.body":"Your antennae brush against a fresh pheromone trail, strong with scent, like a spot of sugar water or a cluster of aphids. A longer, winding path leads to a patch of grass with no trail at all.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Follow the trail","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You follow the scent until you reach a small heap of crushed fruit. At the edge of the grass, a web is waiting for you.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Take the long way, into the grass with no trail","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You find a few seeds in the grass and return to the nest before dark.","decisions.3.title":"A companion with white threads","decisions.3.body":"You see the body of a companion on the ground, covered in fine white threads. It can still be dragged back to the nest, and when your antennae touch it, you smell a sweet, rotten odour.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Drag the body back to the nest","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You haul it back to the nest. A few days later, a ring of fine white threads has grown in a corner of the brood chamber.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Go around it and keep foraging","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You go around the body. Its smell fades slowly behind you.","decisions.4.title":"A sparrow in the grass","decisions.4.body":"A sparrow hops through the grass, pecking up seeds from the ground. It is only a blade of grass away from you.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Slip into a gap among the grass roots","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You slip into a gap among the grass roots. The sparrow pecks a few times, then flies off.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Keep following the trail of seeds","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being pecked↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You pick up a seed and carry on. The sparrow's shadow passes over your head.","decisions.5.title":"A crushed piece of fruit","decisions.5.body":"A crushed piece of fruit lies on the grass, its juice spilled onto the ground and very rich in sugar. Workers from another colony are heading towards it through the grass.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Call companions to carry it back","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Conflict risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You carry the fruit back to the nest together. Twice on the way, workers from another colony rush at you.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Take one small sip, then leave at once","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You lick up a few drops of sugar water, then go around the crowd and back to the nest.","decisions.6.title":"Slowing down","decisions.6.body":"Your legs are caked with dry mud, and several segments have worn bare. There is food on the far side of the meadow, but the journey grows longer each day.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Go to a grass patch farther away for the last food","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stamina↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You find a small piece of sugar in the grass. You eat it slowly, and by the time you reach the nest entrance, it is already dark.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the nest and rely on the food in the brood chamber","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You return to the nest, and your companions feed you a few mouthfuls.","decisions.7.title":"Only one queen in the nest","decisions.7.body":"The nest is newly built, and the queen has only a few workers, yet food is scarce. The larvae are still waiting to be fed, and your own stomach is empty.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Give your food to the larvae","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Stamina↓ Offspring↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You spit the grass seed from your mouth to a larva. Everyone in the nest is surviving on those few scraps of food.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Go out and look for food yourself","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You leave the nest, cross a damp patch of grass, and find a small drop of aphid honeydew.","decisions.8.title":"Moisture in the soil","decisions.8.body":"The nest is getting drier. You can sense moisture somewhere deep in the ground, but it will take a long time to dig down to it.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Dig a deeper tunnel to find damp soil","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You dig down for a long time and finally reach a layer of damp soil.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay at the entrance and wait for dew","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You wait at the mouth of the nest for the morning dew. There is not much, but enough to keep you from thirst.","opening.title":"Which layer the queen lays the egg in","opening.body":"You are still a tiny white egg. The queen has just laid you, and there are two directions in which the nest could place you. One leads to a brood chamber deep underground, where temperature and humidity are steady and rain cannot seep in. The other leads to a shallow nest beneath a stone, which is warm and quickly reached by workers, but the rain and the sun-heated surface are close by.","opening.options.0.t":"Stay in the deep brood chamber underground","opening.options.0.hint":"Steady · Workers arrive slowly","opening.options.0.result":"Workers carry you into the deep brood chamber. The temperature and humidity here never change, but food reaches you only slowly.","opening.options.1.t":"Be placed in the shallow nest under the stone","opening.options.1.hint":"Warm · Storm and heat risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"You are placed in a small patch of soil under the stone, where it is warm. After a heavy storm, the tunnel nearly floods.","actions.forage.name":"Forage outside","actions.forage.desc":"Follow pheromone trails to the surface to find insect carcasses, sugar and aphid honeydew.","actions.hide.name":"Hide deep in the nest","actions.hide.desc":"Retreat into the depths of the tunnels, away from surface heat and invaders.","actions.rest.name":"Rest in the brood chamber","actions.rest.desc":"Stop, huddle among companions, and let your antennae and legs rest.","actions.explore.name":"Walk out to the grass edge","actions.explore.desc":"Follow an unmarked route to a grass patch farther away to look for food.","idle.0":"The brood chamber is warm, and eggs and larvae are crowded together.","idle.1":"Surface sounds barely reach this deep, and only a faint tremor passes through the soil.","idle.2":"The tunnel is damp, with a thin film of moisture on its walls.","idle.3":"A small pebble lands on the nest entrance, and the workers quickly carry it away.","idle.4":"Roots of distant grass stretch through the soil, pushing it apart bit by bit.","idle.5":"The nest is quiet, except for the faint sound of workers walking back and forth through the soil.","idle.6":"A worker stops beside you, touches you with its antennae, and walks away.","idle.7":"The air has a faint smell of formic acid, which soon fades.","actions.forage.text.0":"You follow the trail left by your companions towards the roots of the grass blades.","actions.forage.text.1":"A hot wind blows over the surface, and your feet heat up with each step.","actions.forage.text.2":"You touch a small drop of honeydew with your antennae, lick it, and keep searching.","actions.hide.text.0":"You retreat into the deep tunnels, where humidity stays steady and the hot wind cannot reach you.","actions.hide.text.1":"The soil at the nest entrance trembles now and then, but you do not go out to look.","actions.hide.text.2":"A scent of larvae drifts in from the direction of the brood chamber.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stop in a corner of the brood chamber, companions pressed close around you.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your antennae slowly droop.","actions.rest.text.2":"A worker steps over you without stopping.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk out of the nest, and the brightness of the ground is blinding.","actions.explore.text.1":"The smells between the grass stems shift layer by layer.","actions.explore.text.2":"You pause at the border and touch an unfamiliar member of your kind with your antennae."},"aphid":{"name":"Pea aphid","tagline":"For most of their lives, aphids are females that give birth without mating","survivalNote":"Survival of wild aphids varies widely with season and natural enemies. This curve is a rough estimate based on the lifespan of laboratory pea aphids (adults about 2–4 weeks) and pressure from ladybirds and parasitoid wasps. It is a typical type III curve.","epitaph":"You never met your father. Your body holds the next generation, and that generation's bodies hold the one after it.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Insecta › Hemiptera › Aphididae","size":"Body length about 2–4 mm","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Mother","birth.rolls.2.label":"Host plant","birth.siblingsLabel":"Nymphs born to the same mother","birth.intro":"You are a newborn nymph, about 1 mm long, pale green, with no wings yet. Your mother was {r1}. You were born {r0}. Inside your body you can already see the embryo of your own offspring, and inside that embryo is the next generation. About {siblings} nymphs were born to your mother in this batch.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"on the tender stem of a pea plant","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"on the underside of an alfalfa leaf","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"near the flower cluster of a clover plant","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"on the underside of a bean leaf where ants come and go","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a wingless female aphid","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a winged female aphid that had flown in from crowded old leaves","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A dense pea seedling, its leaves crowded with others of your kind","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A pea plant just in flower, its phloem rich with sap","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A plant tended by ants","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"An old, nutrient-poor plant with yellowing leaves","stages.0.name":"Nymph (instars one to three)","stages.1.name":"Fourth-instar nymph","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Eaten by a ladybird larva","causes.0.text":"A ladybird larva clamps its jaws onto you. It does not let go, and it eats you bite by bite.","causes.1.short":"Seized by a lacewing larva","causes.1.text":"A lacewing larva's sickle-shaped jaws close around you. It injects digestive fluid into your body and sucks you dry.","causes.2.short":"Parasitised and turned into a mummy","causes.2.text":"Parasitoid wasp eggs hatch inside you. Your body slowly swells and hardens, turning a shiny bronze-brown. Eventually the wasp chews a round hole through you and flies away.","causes.3.short":"Plant withered, sap cut off","causes.3.text":"The leaves dry and yellow, and the sap in the phloem grows thinner. Your stylet finds nothing to draw, and you stay hungry until you can no longer move.","causes.4.short":"Frost","causes.4.text":"Frost settles on the leaf surface overnight. Your body freezes, and your stylet cannot be pulled free again.","causes.5.short":"Old age","causes.5.text":"Your stylet moves slower and slower until it stops. An aphid's life usually lasts only a few weeks.","events.0.text":"You molt, and your pale green body grows a little larger.","events.1.text":"You push your stylet into a sieve tube of the phloem, and sap is pushed into your body.","events.2.text":"You are full, and a drop of clear honeydew comes out from your abdomen. It contains sugar, and it sticks to the leaf below.","events.3.text":"You begin giving birth. On the first day you produce {n} nymphs.","events.4.text":"You give birth to {n} more nymphs. Like you, they are all female, and none of them has a father.","events.5.text":"Your wings are clear in the sunlight. You are still giving birth, but today there are only {n}.","events.6.text":"A ladybird larva creeps along a vein towards you. You stop feeding, press your body against the leaf, and keep perfectly still.","events.7.text":"A lacewing larva approaches. Its jaws open, and you shift to the underside of the leaf. Those jaws snap shut where you had been.","events.8.text":"A hoverfly larva wriggles across the leaf surface, and the hooks at its front end pierce a nearby aphid. You move farther away from it.","events.9.text":"A companion beside you is bitten and releases an alarm scent. You immediately pull your stylet free and drop to the leaf below.","events.9.fact":"The aphid alarm pheromone (E)-β-farnesene was discovered by Bowers et al. and reported in Science in 1972 (Bowers et al. 1972)","events.10.text":"An ant climbs up your leg and taps your abdomen with its antennae. You release a drop of honeydew, and it licks it up.","events.10.fact":"Ants tap aphids' abdomens with their antennae to induce them to release honeydew (Verheggen et al. 2012)","events.11.text":"The leaves begin to turn yellow, and the sap in the phloem grows thinner.","events.12.text":"For several days no rain falls, and a layer of the leaf surface dries out. You cannot draw up enough sap.","events.13.text":"In the distance an ant walks past you along the leaf, without stopping.","events.14.text":"A dead companion hangs from the underside of a leaf, its body swollen and shiny, with a round hole in it. It is the exit left by a parasitoid wasp that has emerged.","events.15.text":"A parasitoid wasp settles on the leaf beside you and probes your body with its antennae.","events.16.text":"Your body already holds the embryo of the next generation, and inside those embryos are the embryos of the generation after.","events.17.text":"The overwintering eggs you laid are tucked into a crack in a leaf bud. They will not hatch into new nymphs until next spring.","events.18.text":"Your stylet moves slower and slower, and each sip takes a long pause.","events.19.text":"You are still giving birth, but fewer and fewer, one or two at a time.","events.20.text":"You are on the underside of a young leaf, your stylet driven hard into the phloem, and your abdomen suddenly swells.","events.21.text":"The phloem section you were drawing from is empty. You pull your stylet out, shuffle a few steps across the leaf, and insert it into another vein.","events.22.text":"You move to the underside of the vein, your body pressed against the fuzz. Raindrops fall on the leaf above, but none hits you.","events.23.text":"You crawl into a fold on the underside of the leaf. Companions' bodies surround you, layered one on another.","events.24.text":"You pull your stylet out and rest quietly on the underside of the leaf, while your body slowly absorbs the sap you have just drawn.","events.25.text":"You rest on the shady underside of the leaf all afternoon and eat nothing.","events.26.text":"You climb out of your original leaf and walk along the petiole towards another leaf. There is no cover on the way, and the sunlight is bright.","events.27.text":"You reach the leaf tip. Its sap has not yet been drained by companions, so you draw for a short while, then hurry back.","events.28.text":"You give birth to another nymph. It is pale green, and as soon as it touches the leaf surface it begins to feed.","events.29.text":"Your body holds the embryo of the next generation, and inside that generation are the embryos of the one after it. You have given birth to {n} nymphs.","events.30.text":"The old leaves are tough, and your stylet struggles to pierce them. You have to crowd to the leaf margin to find phloem you can drink from.","events.31.text":"The leaves are covered with your kin. Before your young are grown, they have already pushed past one another and are crawling toward the next leaf.","events.32.text":"The peas flower, and the phloem sap is rich and sweet. You lie on the flower stalk and drink until your body swells.","events.33.text":"After the flowers fall, the pods begin to grow. The plants around you slowly dry and harden, and you have to find fresh sap.","events.34.text":"An ant pauses beside you and strokes your back with its antennae. You release a drop of honeydew for it.","events.35.text":"The ants keep you and your kin on the underside of the leaf. A ladybird crawls over, and an ant swings its mandibles to drive it off.","events.36.text":"The leaves are turning yellow, and the sap is thin and weak. Every mouthful leaves you feeling a little lighter.","events.37.text":"The leaf edges begin to wither. You move to the other side of the stem, where a little sap is still left.","events.38.text":"You leave the familiar peas and climb onto a strange wild grass. The sap there is thin, but there are no ladybirds.","events.39.text":"A shadow passes over the leaf. You let go of your stylet at once and shrink under a vein on the underside of the leaf, motionless.","events.40.text":"You are a little smaller than the other nymphs your age. They have already claimed the midrib of the young leaf, so you are left crowding the leaf margin.","events.41.text":"Your stylet is strong, and with one light prick you find phloem. The companions around you are still searching everywhere.","decisions.0.title":"Too crowded","decisions.0.body":"The plant is already packed with others of your kind. Sap is thinning on the old leaves, and wing buds are beginning to grow on your body.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Grow wings and fly to another plant","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Dispersal · Risk↑ · Offspring↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"Your wings unfold, and a gust of wind carries you away from the plant.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · Still crowded","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You keep feeding on the old leaf, and more companions gather around you.","decisions.1.title":"An ant","decisions.1.body":"An ant climbs up beside you and strokes your back with its antennae. It wants your honeydew.","decisions.1.fact":"Ants and honeydew-producing homopterans, including aphids, form a mutualism: ants feed on honeydew and protect the aphids from some natural enemies (Way 1963, Annual Review of Entomology)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Let it look after you","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Natural enemies↓ · Honeydew taken","decisions.1.options.0.result":"It strokes you several times a day and licks up your honeydew. It patrols around you and drives off approaching ladybirds.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Shake it off","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Keep your honeydew · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You shake several times, and the ant falls to the ground and climbs back up to find another aphid.","decisions.2.title":"The leaves are turning yellow","decisions.2.body":"The leaves of the plant you are feeding on are turning yellow, the sap is thinning, and your abdomen is always empty.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Move to another plant","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Exposed while walking · Better sap","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You climb a slender stem and head for a neighbouring plant. On the way you pass a cluster of lacewing eggs, and you walk around them.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay and keep feeding","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Health↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You push your stylet deep into the phloem. The sap is thin and pale, and you draw for a long time without ever feeling full.","decisions.3.title":"A parasitoid wasp","decisions.3.body":"A small black wasp lands on your back, its abdomen curled up and aimed at your body. It means to lay its eggs inside you.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Shake violently and drop","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Escape · Risk of falling","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You tumble onto the leaf below, and the wasp does not follow.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep feeding","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Risk of being egg-laid on↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You lower your head and go on feeding. An egg slides in through a gap at your side.","decisions.4.title":"The days are getting shorter","decisions.4.body":"The days shorten and the nights grow cooler. The embryos in your body begin to differentiate; some will become males, and some will become egg-laying females.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Produce males and egg-laying females","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Overwintering eggs · Sexual reproduction","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You give birth to males and egg-laying females. They mate on the leaves, and the females then hide overwintering eggs in cracks in the leaf buds.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Keep producing females like yourself","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Offspring↑ · Winter survival depends on weather","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You continue giving birth to females like yourself. The weather keeps getting colder, and whether they survive the winter depends on whether this plant is still there.","decisions.5.title":"A ladybird larva","decisions.5.body":"A black-spotted ladybird larva creeps slowly along the vein, its jaws opening and closing. You can release your stylet and drop to the underside of the leaf, or you can keep feeding.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Release your stylet and drop to the underside","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You roll into the fuzz on the underside of the leaf. The ladybird pauses above for a while, then walks away.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Keep feeding","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being bitten↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You draw in one more mouthful of sap. The larva crawls on along the vein.","decisions.6.title":"The top of the leaf","decisions.6.body":"The leaves below have been drained by companions, and the sap is thin. The top of the leaf is still tender, but reaching it means crossing a stretch of open leaf surface.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Climb to the tender tip of the leaf","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Plenty of sap · Exposed","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You cross the leaf surface and draw a mouthful of very sweet sap, while the light around you is blinding.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay on the underside and drink less","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You draw only a few mouthfuls from the vein, then stop.","decisions.7.title":"Frost at night","decisions.7.body":"During the night a thin layer of frost forms on the leaf. Your body begins to stiffen, and your stylet is hard to pull out of the cold sap.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Burrow under the fallen leaves beneath the leaf","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You slip under the fallen leaves. It is slightly warmer there than on the leaf surface, and you slowly recover.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay on the leaf and keep feeding","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of freezing to death↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You feed for a long time, and your body slowly warms. When the sun comes up, half the frost has melted.","decisions.8.title":"No space on the old leaves","decisions.8.body":"The underside of the leaves is packed with others of your kind, and your stylet cannot get in. At the top of the plant, a young shoot looks to hold more sap, but it is closer to predators.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Climb to the young shoot at the top of the plant","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"The shoot is soft, and you crowd at its very edge to drink. Your neighbours soon come crawling over too.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Wait on the old leaf","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You wait a while. The others around you slowly move on, and a small patch of the leaf clears.","opening.title":"The first step after birth","opening.body":"You have just been born from your mother, a pale green nymph who cannot yet fly. The old leaf where your mother lives is crowded with companions, and the sap is thinning. Beside it, a new bud has pushed out from a young stem where the sap is plentiful, but you must first cross a stretch of bare leaf surface to reach it.","opening.options.0.t":"Stay on the old leaf with your mother","opening.options.0.hint":"Safe · Less food","opening.options.0.result":"You press against your mother's body and find a vein beside her. Companions are crowded around you, and there is not much sap.","opening.options.1.t":"Climb towards the new bud on the young stem","opening.options.1.hint":"Plenty of sap · Exposed","opening.options.1.result":"You climb up along the petiole. For part of the way the leaf surface has no cover, and you stop there for a long time. When you reach the young stem, the sap is very sweet.","actions.forage.name":"Feed on phloem sap","actions.forage.desc":"Insert your stylet into the phloem of a leaf vein and draw up sap.","actions.hide.name":"Hide under a leaf vein on the underside","actions.hide.desc":"Move to the underside of the leaf, close to the fuzz and veins, to avoid ladybirds and parasitoid wasps.","actions.rest.name":"Stay still on the underside of the leaf","actions.rest.desc":"Withdraw your stylet, pause to digest, and wait while the sap is slowly absorbed by your body.","actions.explore.name":"Crawl to another leaf","actions.explore.desc":"Follow the petiole to a neighbouring leaf.","actions.mate.name":"Give birth to live young","actions.mate.desc":"In summer, female aphids need no male to produce female nymphs identical to themselves.","actions.mate.done":"You give birth to {n} more nymphs.","idle.0":"Your stylet sits in the sap channels of the phloem, and sap seeps slowly into you.","idle.1":"The underside of the leaf is cool, and you cling close to the vein.","idle.2":"Your body is translucent, and you can see sap flowing through your gut.","idle.3":"You take a small step, then stop, and the companions around you do the same.","idle.4":"Your abdomen trembles faintly, and honeydew drips one drop at a time onto the leaf below.","idle.5":"A breeze passes, and the whole plant sways.","idle.6":"Something crawls on a leaf in the distance. You only twitch your antennae.","idle.7":"You spend most of your time on this plant and rarely leave it.","actions.forage.text.0":"Your stylet is in a leaf vein, and sap slowly flows into your body.","actions.forage.text.1":"Your abdomen quivers rhythmically, and honeydew drops onto the leaf below.","actions.forage.text.2":"You are full, so you move to another section of vein and keep feeding.","actions.hide.text.0":"You move to the underside of the leaf, your body pressed against the vein and fuzz.","actions.hide.text.1":"Something passes overhead, and you do not move.","actions.hide.text.2":"The underside of the leaf is dim, and companions' bodies are layered beside you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You pull your stylet out and stay quietly on the underside of the leaf.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your body slowly digests the sap you just drew.","actions.rest.text.2":"The leaf sways gently in the wind, and you sway with it.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the petiole towards another leaf.","actions.explore.text.1":"There is no cover on the way, and the sunlight is bright.","actions.explore.text.2":"The sap of the new leaf is still thick, so you stop and take a sip."},"bat":{"name":"Bat","tagline":"Roughly one in every five mammal species is a bat","survivalNote":"Juvenile bats usually survive their first year at only 40–50%; once adult, they survive about 60–80% per year. The upper lifespan limit draws on Podlutsky et al. (2005), a 41-year record for a wild Myotis brandtii. The actual lifespan of the East Asian pipistrelle (Pipistrellus abramus) is usually much shorter, and this curve is a rough estimate.","epitaph":"You flew through the night for many years. Few people knew you had been there, but your echoes lingered.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Chiroptera","size":"Body length about 4 cm, weight about 5 g","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Mother's condition","birth.rolls.2.label":"Winter roost","birth.siblingsLabel":"Pups born the same year","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}, your mother's only pup that year. Your mother, {r1}, gave birth to you, and you are {sex}. You cannot fly yet, and your eyes are still closed.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the attic of an old house","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a crack under a bridge","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a hollow in an old tree","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"an abandoned mine in the mountains","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an experienced female bat","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a young bat having her first litter","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A stone cave with steady winter temperatures and occasional drips from the ceiling","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A cavity inside a building, warmed by winter heating","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"The arch of an old bridge, with winter wind blowing through from both ends","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"A crack in the bark of an old tree, with no shelter in winter","stages.0.name":"Pup (not yet flying)","stages.1.name":"Young bat (learning to fly)","stages.2.name":"Adult bat","stages.3.name":"Old bat","causes.0.short":"Taken by an owl","causes.0.text":"A tawny owl drops from the canopy, and its claws close on your wing. You manage one squeal before its call drowns out your echoes.","causes.1.short":"Caught by a cat","causes.1.text":"A cat's paw reaches down through a gap in the roof tiles. You lunge, but your wing membrane catches on its claw.","causes.2.short":"Barotrauma from a wind turbine","causes.2.text":"As a blade passes, the air pressure around you suddenly drops. You are not struck, but tiny blood vessels in your lungs burst, and you lose your bearings in midair.","causes.2.fact":"Many bats die at wind farms from barotrauma, and many are not struck directly by the blades (Baerwald et al., Current Biology, 2008)","causes.3.short":"White-nose syndrome (fungal infection)","causes.3.text":"While you hibernated, a fungus grew over your nose and wing membranes. When you wake, there are ulcerated holes in your wings, and your fat is seeping away.","causes.3.fact":"White-nose syndrome is caused by a cold-loving fungus, first recorded in caves in New York State in 2006 (Blehert et al., Science, 2009)","causes.4.short":"Starved (cold nights, few insects)","causes.4.text":"For several cold nights you catch no insects at all. Your fat reserves run out, your heartbeat slows more and more, and you never wake.","causes.5.short":"Frozen during hibernation","causes.5.text":"The temperature inside your hibernation roost suddenly drops below freezing. Your body fails to warm back up, and you stay stiff against the wall.","causes.6.short":"Roost sealed off","causes.6.text":"Construction workers seal the opening. You cry out all night inside, and no one hears.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your wings grow slower and slower, and you finally stop on a beam in the maternity roost.","events.0.text":"Your mother has gone out to hunt insects. The maternity roost is packed with hundreds of pups, and you find her position on the wall by scent and call.","events.1.text":"Your mother returns. You crowd towards her to nurse, and her body carries the warmth of night flight.","events.2.text":"A pup next door falls to the ground, its cries growing fainter. There is nothing you can do.","events.3.text":"Fine blood vessel lines are beginning to show on your wing membranes, and your fingers slowly grow longer.","events.4.text":"A cat walks along the tiles outside, its claws scraping on the wood. Every pup in the roost starts calling.","events.5.text":"You catch your first moth outside. It flutters against your mouth, and you swallow it.","events.6.text":"You emit a string of ultrasonic calls, and the echo bounces back from a power line ahead. You dodge it.","events.6.fact":"Bats use echolocation, a phenomenon first confirmed by Griffin and Galambos in 1941 (Griffin & Galambos, 1941)","events.7.text":"You fly beyond your familiar streets for the first time and cross the river. Under the streetlamps on the far bank, the air is full of insects.","events.8.text":"A tawny owl bursts from the woods. You dive and hide beneath the eaves.","events.9.text":"You squeeze into a gap under the eaves, hang upside down, and wait quietly for daylight. Footsteps pass outside three times.","events.10.text":"During the day you hang upside down from a beam. Your body temperature slowly drops with the surroundings, and your heartbeat slows.","events.11.text":"On a summer night, mosquitoes swarm over the lake. You fly low over the water and catch many of them in a row.","events.12.text":"You fly south along the river valley, more than ten kilometres farther than usual, and return as dawn is nearly breaking.","events.13.text":"A large beetle's hard shell is too tough to bite through, so you eat only its soft belly and spit out the wing cases.","events.14.text":"A strong gust at night blows you off course. By the time you find your bearings again, half the night has gone.","events.15.text":"A wing membrane is torn on wire mesh. It takes days to heal, leaving a thin scar.","events.16.text":"On an autumn night, a male bat flaps around you and calls in a pattern unlike the one he uses when hunting.","events.17.text":"In June, you give birth to a pup. It is light, and you can hold it in one hand.","events.17.fact":"Most small temperate bats bear only one pup a year (Kunz & Fenton, Bat Ecology, 2003)","events.18.text":"The wind turbines spin endlessly, and their blades stir up gusts as they pass. You steer around them.","events.18.fact":"Wind farms in the United States and Canada kill large numbers of bats every year (Arnett et al., 2008)","events.19.text":"Someone with a torch comes into the hibernation cave to count the bats. You have a small patch of white mould on your nose.","events.20.text":"Your teeth are worn down, and some beetle shells are now too tough for you to bite.","events.21.text":"Distant lights grow fainter in your vision, but the echoes are still very clear.","events.22.text":"You rest in a gap in an old maternity roost. It still smells of decades past.","events.23.text":"A Myotis bat of the same genus, marked in the wild, lived 41 years, the longest known lifespan for a bat.","events.23.fact":"The longest recorded wild lifespan for Myotis brandtii is 41 years (Podlutsky et al., Journal of Gerontology, 2005)","events.24.text":"The stone cave is quiet in winter, and the temperature stays cool. Your heart slows, and you rarely wake.","events.25.text":"At night you fly out of the entrance, where the calls of many of your kind fill the dark. When you return, the cave has grown a little colder.","events.26.text":"In winter, warmth from the building's heating reaches your cavity. You wake earlier than other bats, and now and then you crawl out to move to a new spot.","events.27.text":"Someone has installed a light in the cavity. When it shines in, you stay in a corner for several days before you dare to come out.","events.28.text":"The wind under the bridge is strong. You grip the stone crack tightly, your body almost unable to move.","events.29.text":"Headlights sweep across the entrance from the bridge. You are jolted awake and crawl deeper into the crevice.","events.30.text":"You hang in a crack in the bark while the winter wind blows from both sides. You wake several times, each time feeling cold.","events.31.text":"A grey owl calls from the treetops, its voice carrying through the wood. You press against the bark and do not move.","events.32.text":"You are a little smaller than the other young bats, and when learning to fly you are always the last to land.","events.33.text":"Your wings are stronger than those of other young bats. On the nights you practise flying, you fly farther than the rest.","events.34.text":"You are always the first to fly out of the entrance, chasing a swarm of moths around a streetlight.","events.35.text":"You linger at the entrance for a long time, waiting until the wind outside dies down before you fly out.","events.36.text":"You peer out from under the eaves and see a moth resting on the wall, its wings shining under the light.","decisions.0.title":"First flight","decisions.0.body":"Your wings can now open fully. Tonight your mother and companions will go out, and she calls to you from the roost entrance. The night beyond the entrance is wide, and you have never flown.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Follow your mother outside","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Learn to hunt · More owls","decisions.0.options.0.result":"In the darkness you dive at your first moth. Behind you is your mother's call, and ahead of you a whole sheet of lights.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Wait in the roost","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You wait another night. The flying insects outside are fewer and fewer, and you begin to feel hungry.","decisions.1.title":"Insects are running out in autumn","decisions.1.body":"The nights are very cold now, and flying insects have almost disappeared. You have little fat, and your body asks: should you hibernate, or keep looking for insects?","decisions.1.fact":"Temperate bats enter deep hibernation in winter, with body temperature dropping towards the surroundings and heart rate falling to a small fraction of its waking rate (Kunz & Fenton, 2003)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Enter hibernation","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Metabolism drops sharply · Risk during hibernation","decisions.1.options.0.result":"Your body temperature falls to match the roost wall. Your heartbeat slows until it is almost impossible to count, and you hand yourself over to winter.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Keep hunting into winter","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Hunger comes faster","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You catch a few more insects, but your fat grows thinner and thinner.","decisions.2.title":"A row of wind turbines","decisions.2.body":"A row of tall turbines has appeared along your migration route, their blades turning slowly. Going around means flying several extra hours. Going straight through saves effort, but the air pressure in the wind farm changes sharply.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Fly low along the treetops","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Longer route · Safer","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You fly around the forest edge for a long time and reach a new roost only at dawn the next day.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Fly straight through the wind farm","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Energy saved · Risk of barotrauma","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You pass between the blades. When you look back, the turbines have turned far behind you.","decisions.3.title":"The attic is being renovated","decisions.3.body":"Workers are hammering on the roof, and boards have been pulled away. Your maternity roost now has a new slit of light. Should you move?","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Move to an old tree hollow nearby","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Takes time to find · Risk","decisions.3.options.0.result":"It takes you three nights to find a tree hollow. It is cold, but no one will come to knock on it.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Familiar · Entrance may be sealed","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay in the old gap until the workers seal it too.","decisions.4.title":"White mould in the hibernation cave","decisions.4.body":"On your way to the hibernation cave, you see white fur covering the walls. Several companions have ulcerated holes in their wings, and they have not flown out.","decisions.4.fact":"White-nose syndrome caused major declines within a few years in the little brown bat, a common small bat of eastern North America (Frick et al., Science, 2010)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Hibernate in a different cave","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Risk on the journey","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You fly for three nights and find another cave, colder and free of white mould.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Hibernate where you are","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"The fungus grows in winter","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You hang your body on the familiar cave wall. The white fur is only a finger's width from your nose.","decisions.5.title":"A male's call","decisions.5.body":"Under a bridge in autumn, a male bat calls repeatedly nearby. His voice is strong, but you do not know him.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Answer him","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Chance of offspring · Slightly higher risk","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You mate with him on an autumn night. His sperm will stay in your body, and fertilisation will not happen until spring.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Fly to another bridge","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Save your energy","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You hide in a crack under another bridge. The male is still calling, but you do not answer.","decisions.6.title":"Flying more slowly","decisions.6.body":"Your wing membranes have aged, and you can no longer catch moths at night. You can stay near your familiar maternity roost, or fly to a river valley far away, where there are new insect swarms but the journey is long.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay near the maternity roost","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You no longer fly far. Every night you circle among the familiar beams.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Fly to a new roost in the river valley","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"More food · Long flight","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You spend two nights flying over the ridge. In the new roost, the insects are so plentiful you cannot eat them all at once.","decisions.7.title":"Cold wind through the bark","decisions.7.body":"On the first winter night, the temperature in the bark crack drops close to freezing. You could fly to the lee side of a taller tree, but the way is far.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Fly to the lee side of another tree","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Stamina↓ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You fly for a long time before you find a tree sheltered from the wind. Several times on the way you nearly hit power lines.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the bark and save your strength","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Energy saved · Freezing risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay where you are, your body slowly growing cold, and you never warm up again before dawn.","opening.title":"Mother looks for a maternity roost","opening.body":"You have not been born yet. Your mother mated in autumn, and she will not give birth to you until spring of the following year. Now she must choose between two places. The attic ceiling is warm, but it has cats and people. The hollow of an old tree is cooler and quieter, and has fewer insects.","opening.options.0.t":"The attic ceiling","opening.options.0.hint":"Warm · Cats and people","opening.options.0.result":"You are born in the warmth under the roof tiles. As a cat walks across the tiles, your mother keeps you tucked under her body.","opening.options.1.t":"The hollow of an old tree","opening.options.1.hint":"Cool · Quiet · Fewer insects","opening.options.1.result":"You are born in the wood shavings of the tree hollow. The wind at the entrance is cold, and your mother presses her body against you.","actions.instinct.name":"Follow instinct","actions.instinct.desc":"Follow echoes and scent, and do what a bat normally does.","actions.forage.name":"Hunt insects at night","actions.forage.desc":"Search for flying insects over water and under streetlamps, using echoes to pinpoint them.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a crevice","actions.hide.desc":"Squeeze into a crack in the eaves or a tree hollow, hang upside down, and wait for daylight.","actions.rest.name":"Rest hanging upside down","actions.rest.desc":"Hang from beams during the day, lower your body temperature, and save a little energy.","actions.explore.name":"Long-distance night flight","actions.explore.desc":"Fly farther along river valleys and forest edges to find new insect swarms.","actions.mate.name":"Breed","actions.mate.done":"On one warm night you give birth to {n} pups. One of them latches onto your nipple and will not let go.","idle.0":"You hang upside down with your wings wrapped around your body.","idle.1":"Outside the roost it is noisy, and you can hear only your own echoes.","idle.2":"A leaf turns over in the wind, and your ears swivel towards it at once.","idle.3":"A distant streetlamp is a blur of light in your eyes.","idle.4":"You rub yourself against a beam and slowly smooth your wings.","idle.5":"The night is still long, and there is room in your stomach for a few more mosquitoes.","idle.6":"A moth circles above your head, and you do not move.","idle.7":"The maternity roost holds only crowded breathing and a faint smell.","actions.forage.text.0":"You fly low over the water, and the echoes fill your ears with the buzz of insect wings.","actions.forage.text.1":"A moth flutters in your mouth, and you swallow it.","actions.hide.text.0":"You press your body into a gap in the boards, and footsteps pass outside.","actions.hide.text.1":"A cat pauses under the eaves for a moment, then walks away.","actions.rest.text.0":"Your wings wrap around your body, leaving only the tip of your nose showing.","actions.explore.text.0":"You leave your familiar streets and fly towards the river.","actions.explore.text.1":"You pause in the crown of an unfamiliar tree for a while, then fly on."},"blue_whale":{"name":"Blue whale","tagline":"The largest animal known on Earth, which spends most of its life eating krill","survivalNote":"Early mortality in blue whales is relatively low, giving a curve close to type I (though calves are not free of death). Lifespan is often estimated at 80–90 years; the adult curve is a rough estimate.","epitaph":"You are the largest animal known on Earth. You spent most of your life eating krill, and mass whaling in the twentieth century drove blue whale numbers down to very low levels.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Cetartiodactyla › Balaenopteridae","size":"Adult body length about 24–30 m","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth waters","birth.rolls.1.label":"Population","birth.rolls.2.label":"That year's krill","birth.siblingsLabel":"Calves in the same birth (only you)","birth.intro":"You have just been born, about 7 m long and weighing about 2.5 tonnes. You were born in {r0}, and your mother quickly pushed you up to the surface to breathe. You are {sex}. A blue whale usually bears a single calf at a time, so your birth produced just {siblings} calf, which is you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"warm tropical or subtropical waters, one of the blue whale breeding grounds","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the waters near a chain of islands at low latitude","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"warm coastal waters of the Southern Hemisphere","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Cold Antarctic waters, where the Antarctic subspecies feeds on krill in summer","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"Warm Indian Ocean waters, home to the smaller pygmy blue whale","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"The northeastern Pacific coast, near the North American continental shelf","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A year of plentiful krill, so dense the water changed colour","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"An ordinary year, with krill in their usual places","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A warmer-than-usual year, with krill scattered widely","stages.0.name":"Calf (nursing)","stages.1.name":"Weaned calf","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (foraging on its own)","stages.3.name":"Adult (migrating)","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Struck by a large ship","causes.0.text":"The hull of a large ship strikes your back. The ship does not stop, and you slowly sink through the water.","causes.1.short":"Tangled in a fishing net","causes.1.text":"A fishing net wraps around your tail fluke. You struggle for days, the wound slowly becomes infected, and in the end you cannot swim back up to breathe.","causes.2.short":"Milk too little, starved","causes.2.text":"Your mother cannot find enough krill, and her milk grows scarcer each day. You grow weaker and finally stop nursing.","causes.3.short":"Attacked by killer whales","causes.3.text":"A pod of killer whales closes in. Your mother shields you with her body, but they take turns charging, and you cannot escape.","causes.4.short":"Krill decline, starved","causes.4.text":"Krill swarms have thinned out in this area. You cannot get enough to eat, your blubber layer grows thinner and thinner, and you can no longer swim.","causes.5.short":"Old age","causes.5.text":"The intervals between your breaths grow longer and longer. After your last dive you do not rise again, and the creatures of the deep sea keep you company in your final hours.","events.0.text":"You take your first breath, blowing a column of water, and your lungs fill with air for the first time.","events.1.text":"Your mother nudges you to the surface. You take a breath, then sink back down to nurse.","events.2.text":"You drink hundreds of litres of milk every day, and your body grows astonishingly fast.","events.2.fact":"A blue whale calf gains about 90 kg a day while nursing (Aquarium of the Pacific blue whale fact sheet)","events.3.text":"Your mother releases thick milk underwater, the colour of cream. You open your mouth and catch all of it.","events.4.text":"A pod of killer whales appears in the distance. Your mother turns on her side and circles, keeping you behind her.","events.5.text":"Your mother's milk has grown a little scarcer, and you feed more slowly. You can only keep following her closely.","events.6.text":"You stop nursing and start feeding on your own. Your food is mainly krill.","events.7.text":"You open your mouth, and seawater and krill rush in together. You close it, and the water flows out between the baleen plates, leaving the krill behind in your mouth.","events.8.text":"A swarm of krill churns around you. You swim above it and take a huge mouthful.","events.9.text":"A fragment of fishing net is tangled around your tail fluke. You struggle and dive.","events.10.text":"A pod of dolphins follows you for a while, leaping and falling back beside you.","events.11.text":"The krill near the surface scatter as summer ends. You must go to waters farther away to find them.","events.12.text":"You swim through many seas. Once you enter a patch of cold water where krill are abundant.","events.13.text":"The sound of a large ship grows from the distance, nearer and nearer, and you change direction.","events.14.text":"A group of whales swims together. Their calls echo back and forth through the water and carry far.","events.15.text":"For the first time you leap out of the water, landing heavily back in the sea with a high spray.","events.16.text":"You open your mouth wide and take in several tonnes of seawater at once, then filter out the krill.","events.17.text":"A ship passes over the surface above your back, and its shadow sweeps over you.","events.18.text":"In the breeding waters you hear another whale's call, travelling through a great distance of seawater.","events.19.text":"You give birth to your first calf. It swims beside you, having only just learned to breathe.","events.20.text":"You give birth to another calf. It depends on your milk to grow, and you must find more krill in farther waters.","events.21.text":"A swarm of krill is swallowed below you, and the water turns cloudy.","events.22.text":"The sea warms, and the krill move farther away. You eat a little less.","events.23.text":"Your swimming slows. The intervals between surfacing to breathe grow longer.","events.24.text":"Barnacles and small crustaceans have attached to your back.","events.25.text":"You have not given birth for a long time. Your body is slowly cutting back on energy use.","events.26.text":"You follow the adults to feed on krill for the first time. Your mouth is still small, and you take only a little, but you learn to open wide.","events.27.text":"You charge into the krill swarm with your mouth wide open, and seawater and krill surge in together.","events.28.text":"A swarm of krill gathers in the cold water into a red cloud. You open your mouth and take in tonnes of seawater, and the krill you filter out are enough to feed you for a while.","events.29.text":"You find a large swarm of krill in cold water and eat for several days in a row.","events.30.text":"You press close to your mother's flank. A ship's noise comes from far away, and your mother shields you behind her.","events.31.text":"You dive several hundred metres into cold water. The ship's sound rumbles dully from above.","events.32.text":"You float in the water, and it takes a long time between breaths. The sea is steady, and your body slowly recovers.","events.33.text":"Your mother drifts beside you, and you fall asleep pressed against her body.","events.34.text":"You swim into a sea area you have never visited. The water turns a deep colour. Somewhere far away, other whales are calling.","events.35.text":"You drift along the current for hundreds of kilometres, and there is no krill along the way.","events.36.text":"In the breeding waters you meet a male whale, and his call carries far through the water. You come close, and you rub against each other for a long time.","events.37.text":"You see an iceberg for the first time. Its shadow drifts slowly beneath the water, bigger than any whale you have seen.","events.38.text":"In the cold Antarctic water you open your mouth and take in a great swarm of krill. Seawater pours out through the gaps in your baleen plates.","events.39.text":"Pack ice cracks overhead. You surface through a gap to breathe, the ice around you as thick as a wall.","events.40.text":"You are much smaller than your Antarctic relatives. Your mother swims slowly with you through the warmer water, and you can barely lift your head above the surface to breathe.","events.41.text":"The krill here are smaller than those in Antarctica, so you have to eat more to feel full. You stay at the surface for a long time, feeding mouthful by mouthful.","events.42.text":"In a bay in the Indian Ocean you meet another whale. Its call is low, and you listen to each other underwater for a long time.","events.43.text":"The water near shore is shallow, and sunlight from the seabed reflects onto your body. A small boat sits in the distance, and someone on board raises a camera.","events.44.text":"You swim north along the continental shelf. The water is cold, and the krill gather underwater in red streaks.","events.45.text":"A viewing platform on the coast has people standing on it every weekend, their telescopes always pointed in your direction.","events.46.text":"Your mother has not left you for a long time, and her milk is rich. For the first time you feel the fullness in your belly.","events.47.text":"That year the krill pile up in layers just below the surface. You eat all day, and your belly grows a round bulge.","events.48.text":"Your blubber has grown much thicker. As you dive, the oil under your skin glints in the water.","events.49.text":"That year the krill are still in their old places. Every day you go to the same stretch of sea, eating neither too much nor too little.","events.50.text":"The sea surface is calm in summer. As you come up to breathe, the seabirds around you all take flight.","events.51.text":"You roam the same stretch of ocean for many years, finding krill in the same spots every summer.","events.52.text":"The krill have scattered widely, and you search for several days before you find a small group. The ribs along your side are becoming more visible.","events.53.text":"You swim into a distant cold-water area, where the krill are smaller, and you eat for a long time before you are full.","events.54.text":"Your companions swim south together. You follow behind, and with every breath you feel your belly is empty.","events.55.text":"You are the first to swim toward the boat, leaping once at the surface. Your mother gives a low call, and you sink back to her side.","events.56.text":"You press close to your mother, her back rising and falling against your side. Splashing sounds in the distance, and your whole body goes stiff.","events.57.text":"You swim into an undersea canyon you have never visited. On the canyon wall is a patch of glowing seaweed, and you circle it several times.","events.58.text":"You were born smaller than most blue whale calves. You nurse slowly, and often shiver with hunger at your mother’s side.","events.59.text":"Your body is sturdier than your peers'. When you compete with a companion for a swarm of krill, you push it to one side with your body.","decisions.0.title":"Weaning","decisions.0.body":"Your mother's milk is growing scarcer, and she is urging you to feed on your own. You can go out to forage alone, or stay by your mother's side for a few more months.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave your mother and forage alone","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You leave your mother and swim toward waters thick with krill. Behind you, the water quickly empties.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay by your mother for a few more months","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You keep following your mother. Her milk is less now, but you are safe beside her.","decisions.1.title":"Migration","decisions.1.body":"The seasons are changing. Your body tells you which way to go: the high-latitude waters have plenty of krill in summer, while the low-latitude waters suit breeding in winter.","decisions.1.fact":"Blue whales make seasonal migrations between summer high-latitude feeding grounds and winter low-latitude breeding grounds (Mackintosh 1965, The Stocks of Whales)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go to high-latitude waters to feed","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Stable risk","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You swim toward cold water. There is plenty of krill there, and you eat until you are full.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Go to low-latitude waters to breed","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Breeding chances↑ Hungry for a while","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You swim toward warm waters. The water is clear there, and you start noticing the females around you.","decisions.2.title":"A krill cloud","decisions.2.body":"Ahead, a great mass of krill colours the water like a red mist. You can speed in at full charge, or swim slowly and wait for them to scatter on their own.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Charge in with your mouth open","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Stamina↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You plunge into the krill cloud. The water pressure stretches your mouth wide, and krill and seawater surge in together.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Swim slowly and wait for them to scatter","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · A bit hungry","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You swim slowly. The krill scatter around you, and you manage to eat only a small part of them.","decisions.3.title":"A large ship","decisions.3.body":"A large ship approaches from directly above, its engine noise heavy. You can dive into the depths, or keep feeding.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Dive to the depths","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You dive deep. The ship passes overhead, and the sound of its propellers slowly fades in the water.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep feeding and ignore it","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Danger↑↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep eating krill. The ship's shadow presses down, and you do not have time to get out of the way.","decisions.4.title":"The edge of the net","decisions.4.body":"A row of gillnet floats lies across the water ahead. There are many krill there, but the net lines hang unseen beneath the surface, and you cannot see them clearly.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Steer around the net lines to another sea area","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety · A bit hungry","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You skirt around that patch of sea. The floats slowly sink from sight behind you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Pass through a gap in the net lines to feed on the krill there","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Danger↑↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You slip through a gap in the net lines and eat a great many krill. Afterwards, a thin line wraps around your tail fluke.","decisions.5.title":"Killer whales arrive","decisions.5.body":"A pod of killer whales in the distance churns the surface into spray. Your mother turns her side and keeps you between her body and the threat. But she is on the outside, and if the killer whales charge, she will be the first they reach.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Press close to your mother and hide behind her","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You press close to your mother and shelter in her shadow. The killer whales circle several times, then turn away.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Leave your mother and dive into the deep water","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Danger↑↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You dive. The killer whales turn, and you slip out through a gap in their formation, with several tooth marks left on your body.","decisions.6.title":"Krill retreating south","decisions.6.body":"Sea temperatures have risen over the years, and krill are shrinking their range, retreating into colder waters farther south. The sea where you used to feed holds fewer and fewer krill. You can head farther south for colder water, or stay in the old feeding ground and get by on the few krill left.","decisions.6.fact":"Krill populations in the Southern Ocean declined markedly in the second half of the twentieth century (Atkinson et al. 2004, Nature)","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Head south to colder water to find krill","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You swim south. Along the way you cross several shipping lanes, with ship noise rising and falling, but you find a new swarm of krill.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the old waters and eat less","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Safe","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You remain in your old feeding ground, where krill grow steadily scarcer. Your blubber layer grows thinner, bit by bit.","decisions.7.title":"The last deep dive","decisions.7.body":"Your breaths are coming further and further apart. Ahead lies deep cold water, where krill may be waiting. You can make one more deep dive, or stay at the surface and let your body grow lighter.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Make one last deep dive for krill","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stamina↓↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You dive into the deep water, find a swarm of krill and eat your fill. The climb back up is long, but you finally reach the surface and gasp for a few breaths.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and breathe slowly","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe · No more feeding","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay at the surface, breathing again and again. Seabirds settle on the water around you, and you do not move.","decisions.8.title":"A busy shipping lane","decisions.8.body":"Your waters are cut in two by an oil-tanker route. At night the lights of cargo ships line up in rows, and their engine noise carries a long way underwater. You have to decide when to cross.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Cross the lane at night","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food unchanged · Risk of ship strike","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You cross the lane at night. In the distance a tanker's propellers churn the water loudly, but they never come near you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Take the long way to the northern bay","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less krill","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You take a long detour. The northern bay is quiet, but there are far fewer krill than you remember.","decisions.9.title":"A year short of krill","decisions.9.body":"That summer the krill swarms are closer to the surface than usual, far from your waters. You could follow them, or stay in your old waters and live off the fat in your body.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Follow the krill to distant waters","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Costly journey","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You follow the krill swarms for two months. The journey is long, but you finally have a decent meal.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay in old waters and live off your blubber","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ · Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay in your old waters, eating only a little krill each day. The fat under your skin slowly grows thinner.","opening.title":"First breath","opening.body":"You have just been born and are still in the water. Your mother pushes you towards the surface with her nose, and your lungs have not yet fully opened. You must now decide how to follow her.","opening.options.0.t":"Press close to your mother's flank and breathe with her","opening.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Less milk","opening.options.0.result":"You rise alongside your mother's flank and take your first breath of air. Her body blocks the current, and you quickly learn to keep up with her rhythm.","opening.options.1.t":"Nurse from your mother's belly first, then rise","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Breathing a bit later","opening.options.1.result":"You drink a few mouthfuls of milk from your mother's belly. You rise a few seconds late, and the current carries you slightly off course.","actions.forage.name":"Lunge-feed on krill","actions.forage.desc":"Charge into a krill swarm with your mouth wide open, and use your baleen plates to filter out the seawater and keep the krill.","actions.hide.name":"Stay close to your mother or dive into deep water","actions.hide.desc":"Calves press against their mother's flank, and adults dive to cold water hundreds of metres down. Both leave the dangerous surface behind.","actions.rest.name":"Float and rest","actions.rest.desc":"Drift slowly in the water, cutting down on breathing and movement, and wait for your body to recover.","actions.explore.name":"Head for distant waters","actions.explore.desc":"Leave familiar waters, follow the currents into the distance, and seek out other krill swarms.","actions.mate.name":"Find a male in the breeding waters","actions.mate.desc":"In warm breeding waters, find a male by his calls and conceive a calf afterward.","actions.mate.done":"You meet a male whale, and over the following year you conceive {n} calves.","idle.0":"You swim slowly through the sea, your tail fluke rising and falling.","idle.1":"In the distance, another whale breathes, sending a column of water into the air.","idle.2":"The sea is deep, and the light below you turns blue.","idle.3":"You stop in the deep water and hear a ship far off, shaking the sea.","idle.4":"Small scratches mark your skin, left by rubbing against things in the sea.","idle.5":"When you dive, your heartbeat slows right down.","idle.6":"A flock of seabirds circles over the water in the distance, then quickly scatters.","idle.7":"The current around you is steady, and you drift slowly along with it.","actions.forage.text.0":"You open your mouth wide, and seawater and krill rush in together.","actions.forage.text.1":"You close your mouth, and the water flows out between the baleen plates, leaving the krill behind.","actions.forage.text.2":"A red cloud of krill churns around you.","actions.hide.text.0":"You press close to your mother's flank, and her body blocks the current.","actions.hide.text.1":"You dive into deep water, and the ship's sound rumbles dully from above.","actions.hide.text.2":"You stop in the deep water. It is dark and cold all around you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You float in the water, and it takes a long time between breaths.","actions.rest.text.1":"The water temperature is steady, and you hardly need to move.","actions.rest.text.2":"Your mother drifts beside you, and you fall asleep pressed against her body.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim into a sea area you have never visited, and the water turns a deeper colour.","actions.explore.text.1":"Other whales are calling in the distance, and you are not sure whether they are your kin.","actions.explore.text.2":"You drift along the current for hundreds of kilometres, and there is no krill along the way.","actions.mate.text.0":"You swim toward the warm breeding waters, where the water grows clearer.","actions.mate.text.1":"You hear a male whale calling in the sea, his voice carrying far.","actions.mate.text.2":"You come close, and you rub against each other for a long time."},"broiler":{"name":"Broiler chicken","tagline":"About 70 billion chickens are slaughtered each year, and you are one of them","survivalNote":"Fast-growing breeds are usually slaughtered at around 42 days, a cliff on the curve. Average mortality on farms is a few percent. Slow-growing breeds live longer; this is only a rough approximation.","epitaph":"You lived about six weeks. Your body weight grew dozens of times over, but you never once stood on grass.","taxon":"Chordata › Aves › Galliformes › Phasianidae","size":"About 2.5 kg at market weight","birth.rolls.0.label":"Rearing system","birth.rolls.1.label":"Country","birth.rolls.2.label":"Growth rate of the breed","birth.siblingsLabel":"Chicks hatched in the same batch","birth.intro":"You broke out of your shell in a hatchery incubator, still wet and weighing under 45 g. You are {sex}. The place you will live next is {r0}. About {siblings} chicks hatched in the same batch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a large indoor shed holding tens of thousands of birds at high density","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a small or medium indoor house with a moderate-growth breed","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a free-range farm with outdoor grass, raising a slow-growing breed","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A large farm in southern Brazil, with long sheds and drinker lines running end to end","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A farm in the US Midwest, with large sheds and automatically controlled ventilation","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"A farm in northern China, where the sheds need heating in winter","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"A farm in an EU country, where each bird has slightly more space","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A fast-growing commercial breed, reaching market weight in just over forty days","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A medium-growth breed, going to market at about sixty days","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A local slow-growing yellow-feathered breed, taking over three months to reach market weight","stages.0.name":"Chick","stages.1.name":"Brooding period","stages.2.name":"Growing period","stages.3.name":"Shortly before slaughter","causes.0.short":"Slaughter","causes.0.text":"A truck takes you and the birds from your batch to the slaughterhouse. You are hung on a conveyor, and within minutes it is all over.","causes.1.short":"Leg disease, unable to stand","causes.1.text":"Your legs cannot bear your ever-heavier body. After a few steps you collapse, and the feed tray is only a metre away. You cannot crawl there.","causes.2.short":"Heart failure (ascites)","causes.2.text":"Your heart and lungs cannot pump so much blood. Fluid builds up in your abdomen, and your breathing grows shallower.","causes.3.short":"E. coli infection","causes.3.text":"Bacteria infect your air sacs. You begin to cough, and then you stop eating.","causes.4.short":"Crushed in a corner","causes.4.text":"The flock is startled and presses together into one corner. You are pushed to the very bottom.","causes.5.short":"Heat stress","causes.5.text":"The weather is muggy, ventilation has failed, and the temperature in the shed rises several degrees one afternoon. You pant with your beak open until you can pant no more.","causes.6.short":"Died during catching or transport","causes.6.text":"Your wing was crushed while you were being loaded into a crate, and you did not survive the journey to the slaughterhouse.","events.0.text":"You are lifted out of the hatching tray with bits of eggshell still on you. The chicks from your batch huddle together in a cardboard box.","events.1.text":"A needle pierces the back of your neck. The hatchery vaccinated you against Marek's disease within a day of hatching.","events.2.text":"You and dozens of chicks are packed into a box and driven to the shed by truck. The road is very bumpy.","events.3.text":"You peck a few grains of feed from the tray and take a sip of water. From now on, all your energy has to come from these.","events.4.text":"The litter is damp, and a stinging ammonia smell rises from the floor. Your eyes begin to redden, and coughing rises from all over the shed.","events.4.fact":"The European Food Safety Authority (EFSA, 2023) scientific opinion on broiler welfare notes that litter quality and ammonia concentration are important factors affecting the eye and respiratory health of broilers","events.5.text":"The shed holds tens of thousands of birds. Every step means going around other bodies, and there is almost nowhere to be alone.","events.5.fact":"Council Directive 2007/43/EC sets a standard maximum stocking density for broilers of 33 kg per square metre, which may be relaxed under certain conditions (Council Directive 2007/43/EC)","events.6.text":"In a week your body has grown considerably heavier. Bone and muscle cannot keep up with the weight, and you sway from side to side as you walk.","events.6.fact":"A 1957 strain weighed about 0.9 kg at 56 days, a 1978 strain about 1.8 kg, and modern fast-growing breeds from 2005 about 4.2 kg (Zuidhof et al., 2014, Poultry Science)","events.7.text":"One leg suddenly gives way and you sit down. In the days that follow, you can only lie on the litter, while the feed tray grows farther and farther away.","events.7.fact":"Kestin et al. (1992, Veterinary Record): fast-growing broiler breeds have a markedly higher incidence of leg weakness","events.8.text":"Fans roar, and the shed is stuffy and hot. You pant with your beak open, your wings lifted slightly from your body.","events.9.text":"A neighbour pecks at your back. Several of its feathers are bare. You dodge away into a more crowded spot.","events.10.text":"The feed tray is topped up twice a day. You eat fast, and your crop bulges.","events.11.text":"The stockmen begin walking the rows, shining torches on every row of birds.","events.12.text":"The catching crew arrives. Each worker takes a bird in one hand and carries it upside down into the transport crate.","events.13.text":"A chicken beside you topples over and does not move. No one comes to look.","events.14.text":"Under the warm lamp it is hot, and you are crowded among other birds, unable to tell which is which.","events.15.text":"The lights are dimmed at night. The shed is quiet for several hours, and you and your companions are asleep.","events.16.text":"You walk out onto the grass, pecking at grass roots and insects. The sun warms your back, and you stay warm for a long time.","events.17.text":"You are packed into a plastic transport crate with a dozen other chickens, and you jolt on the truck all night.","events.18.text":"You are hung on the conveyor and carried into the slaughter line.","events.19.text":"You push towards the feed trough and peck up one grain, then another. The birds around you are pecking too, and none of them stops.","events.20.text":"The area around the trough is trampled. You lower your head, and another chicken's foot steps on your back.","events.21.text":"Pellets in the feed tray are overturned by the other birds and scattered across the floor. You pick them up one by one.","events.22.text":"You shrink into a corner. There is a damp smell there, and a sticky layer of litter on the floor.","events.23.text":"You hide behind a heap of chickens. No one notices you, and you do not get to eat.","events.24.text":"You crouch under the warm lamp, and the birds around you grow quiet one by one.","events.25.text":"You lie on the litter and slowly warm up. You close your eyes, and your breathing rises and falls.","events.26.text":"You walk along the shed wall. The litter is damp and hard, and every step requires lifting your feet high.","events.27.text":"You reach the far end of the shed, where the ventilation blows straight at you. Your feathers are ruffled, and your body feels chilled.","events.28.text":"You pass through the small door of the shed and step onto the grass. The grass roots smell fresh, and the sun warms your back.","events.29.text":"The shed is long, and the drinking line runs from one end to the other. You drink a lot of water, and your down soon gives way to feathers.","events.30.text":"The shed temperature rises day by day, and the fans keep humming. You pant with your beak open, and you eat less feed than the day before.","events.31.text":"The ventilation adjusts itself at night, and the air in the shed is clean. You sleep soundly.","events.32.text":"A worker weighs a few birds on an electronic scale. Your row is weighed, and then you are put back down.","events.33.text":"In winter the shed needs heating. The heater swings between high and low, so you huddle in a corner and wake several times in the night.","events.34.text":"Cold wind howls outside, and draughts creep in through the door gaps. You huddle together with the birds beside you to keep warm.","events.35.text":"The shed is a little roomier, so you can take a few steps across the floor before returning to the drinking line.","events.36.text":"There is some straw and a wooden platform in the shed. You jump up and stand there for a while.","events.37.text":"Your weight climbs faster each day. Your breast grows thick, and you lean forward as you walk.","events.38.text":"You do little besides eating and sleeping. Around the feed tray, the birds of your batch crowd in.","events.39.text":"You grow a little more slowly than the other birds in your batch, but your legs are stronger than theirs.","events.40.text":"You have not yet reached market weight, so the workers have not picked you out for now.","events.41.text":"Your down is yellowish, and you run quickly and erratically in the brooder box.","events.42.text":"You can run some distance across the shed. Your feathers are also denser than those of other breeds.","events.43.text":"You are a little smaller than the chicks in your batch. The drinker is always crowded with other birds, and you have trouble getting a drink.","events.44.text":"You are sturdier than your batch-mates. You jump onto a step beside the feed tray, which the other birds cannot reach.","events.45.text":"You are the first to rush to the feed tray. The crush knocks you off balance, but you still get the first peck.","events.46.text":"Someone opens the door, and you shrink to the very back of the flock. Long after the door closes, you stay there.","events.47.text":"You walk back and forth through the shed and find a small hole in the wall, with some fallen grain inside.","decisions.0.title":"A place under the warm lamp","decisions.0.body":"Under the warm lamp is the warmest spot in the brooding area, and also the most crowded. The feed tray is at the far end, more than ten steps from you. These choices cannot change how your life ends, but they will change how the next few days feel.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Squeeze in under the warm lamp","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓ Crushing risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You push into the crowd of companions, warm and secure. The chicks at the very bottom of the pile are gasping, and you do not know who they are.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Go to the feed tray","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ A bit cold","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You walk over. The way is cold, but you manage to eat a few mouthfuls of feed.","decisions.1.title":"A leg that won't cooperate","decisions.1.body":"Your leg hurts more and more. Standing lets you reach the feed tray, and lying down lets you rest, but you will eat far less.","decisions.1.fact":"Kestin et al. (1992): leg disease (leg weakness) is very common in fast-growing broiler breeds","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Stand up and go to the feed tray","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Leg injury↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You limp to the feed tray and eat a full meal. For the days that follow, your leg hurts even more.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Lie down and rest","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety Food↓","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You lie on the litter. The feed tray moves farther away, and you eat very little, but your leg does not get better.","decisions.2.title":"A sick neighbour","decisions.2.body":"The chicken beside you has half-closed eyes, coughs, and its feathers are ruffled. Other birds peck at it from time to time. You can leave, or stay beside it.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Stay away from it","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Infection risk↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You move to the far end of the shed. The sick bird's coughing still carries across to you.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay beside it","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Infection risk↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You lie down against it. A few days later, your throat starts to feel dry.","decisions.3.title":"The door opens","decisions.3.body":"The door swings open, and a man in boots walks in, the light falling across you. You can hide in a corner, or walk towards him and the feed tray. Neither choice will change the date of slaughter.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Hide in a corner","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You crouch in a corner, and no one looks your way.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Walk to the feed tray","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Stepped on↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You edge up to the tray and eat a few grains of feed. A passing man steps on your foot.","decisions.4.title":"Wet litter","decisions.4.body":"A patch of litter near the drinking line is soaked through. It is sticky underfoot, and the ammonia smell there is sharper. The dry litter is in the middle of the shed, where the chickens are packed. These choices will not change your slaughter date; they only change how comfortable you are over the next few days.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stand on the wet patch close to the water line","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Drinking↑ Ammonia↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You get a drink of water, but your eyes soon begin to sting.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Squeeze into the dry litter in the middle","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Dry↑ Crowding↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You settle into the dry patch, surrounded by the warmth of other chickens.","decisions.5.title":"The crowd in the aisle","decisions.5.body":"Between the feed tray and the drinking line there is only a narrow aisle, and the chickens push back and forth along it. You can force your way through the crowd, or wait for it to thin out.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Push through the aisle to eat","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stepped on↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You push across and eat a few mouthfuls of feed. One chicken steps on your leg.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Wait for the crowd to disperse","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You wait a good while before the aisle clears.","decisions.6.title":"A stuffy afternoon","decisions.6.body":"The fans grow louder, and the shed is stuffy and hot. All of you are panting with your beaks open. At one end of the shed a vent lets in cool air, but it is already packed with chickens.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Push towards the vent","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Cooler↑ Crushing↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You stand by the vent, and the cool air blows across your feathers. The chickens next to you keep pushing in this direction.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay where you are and pant","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Heat stress↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You pant with your beak open for a long time, until the air in the shed slowly cools.","decisions.7.title":"Heating cut out","decisions.7.body":"The heating shut off for twenty minutes overnight. The shed temperature is dropping, and the chicks are huddled into a single mass.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle of the chick pile","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Crushing risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You wedge into the middle of the chick pile. Your body warms slowly, but you are pressed hard, and it hurts.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay by the edge near the heater","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"A bit warmer · Unstable temperature","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You lie beside the heater, whose temperature rises and falls. Only when it starts working again do you slowly warm up.","decisions.8.title":"The small door is ajar","decisions.8.body":"The wind has pushed the shed's small door open a crack, and outside is a patch of ground with grass. You have to decide whether to step out or stay in.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Go out onto the grass","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Activity↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You walk onto the grass, which smells fresh at the roots. A bird's shadow passes across the sky.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay in the shed near the water","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You stay in the shed and take a sip of water. Outside, the wind slowly dies down.","opening.title":"Warm lamp or water line","opening.body":"You have just been taken out of the hatcher, still wet and weighing under 45 g. In the middle of the brooding area is a warm lamp, and a few steps away a row of drinking lines glints on the floor. You cannot yet stand steadily, but this step will decide which you tend to first in the coming days.","opening.options.0.t":"Huddle under the warm lamp","opening.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Crushing risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"You huddle among a pile of chicks under the warm lamp, and you quickly dry off. This choice does not change your slaughter date; it only changes your body temperature in the first few days.","opening.options.1.t":"Head for the drinking line","opening.options.1.hint":"Drink water · Cold on the way","opening.options.1.result":"You stagger to the drinking line and take your first sip of water. The floor is very cold, and you walk slowly.","actions.forage.name":"Crowd to the feeder","actions.forage.desc":"Squeeze along the feeder and peck up pellets. The tray is topped up twice a day, but many birds compete for the feed.","actions.hide.name":"Shrink into a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the busy places, shrink into a corner, or tuck in behind other birds.","actions.rest.name":"Crouch without moving","actions.rest.desc":"Lie on the litter, move less, and let your body rest.","actions.explore.name":"Walk around the shed","actions.explore.desc":"Walk along the shed to see other areas. The farther you go, the more birds and things you encounter.","idle.0":"You scrape a small hollow in the ground, and another chicken flattens it again.","idle.1":"For this stretch of time, you cannot tell day from night.","idle.2":"You lower your head and peck at the ground, mostly finding things other than food.","idle.3":"Your body is a little heavier than it was a few days ago.","idle.4":"The companions beside you make soft cooing sounds, then quickly fall quiet again.","idle.5":"You stretch your legs, then tuck them back under your body.","idle.6":"A vehicle sounds in the distance, then fades quickly.","idle.7":"A breeze passes, and the feathers on your body stir gently.","actions.forage.text.0":"You crowd to the edge of the feeder and peck at the pellets with your head down.","actions.forage.text.1":"The chickens around you push at you, and you do not let go.","actions.forage.text.2":"You eat quickly, swallowing again and again.","actions.hide.text.0":"You shrink into a corner, with the cold wall behind you.","actions.hide.text.1":"A chicken walks past, and you lower your head.","actions.hide.text.2":"The sounds in the shed grow a little quieter.","actions.rest.text.0":"You crouch on the litter, your feathers slightly ruffled.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your legs ache a little, so you tuck them under your body.","actions.rest.text.2":"The litter is a bit damp, so you shift to another spot.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the shed wall, your claws stepping on damp litter.","actions.explore.text.1":"You push through a group of chickens and reach the far end.","actions.explore.text.2":"The lamp above flickers on and off, and you stop to watch it for a while."},"cat":{"name":"Cat","tagline":"It has lived alongside people for tens of thousands of years, some on sofas, some in alleys","survivalNote":"Indoor cats usually live for well over a decade, while outdoor and stray cats face much higher mortality. This curve is a rough estimate across a mixed population.","epitaph":"When cats are awake at night, there are many others in the city like you.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Carnivora › Felidae","size":"About 4 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Origin","birth.rolls.1.label":"Breed and build","birth.rolls.2.label":"Where you live","birth.siblingsLabel":"Kittens from the same litter","birth.intro":"You are a newborn kitten, eyes still shut, with only a sparse coat of fur. You are {sex}. {r0} Your litter has {siblings} kittens in all.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"You were born behind the bins in an old neighbourhood, to a stray queen no one looked after.","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"You were born on a rooftop in an urban village, to a queen living among ownerless cats.","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"You were born on a family's balcony, and your owner gave you a name.","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Chinese rural cat: short coat, mixed colours, medium build","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"British Shorthair: round-bodied, with a coat as thick as an overcoat","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"Persian: short nose, large eyes, prone to tear staining","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"Maine Coon: may weigh over 7 kg as an adult","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"An old harbour street in Indonesia, with many strays that locals feed","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"An old hutong courtyard in Beijing, with a few cats on the wall tops year-round","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A farm in Australia, with mice and raptors in the barn","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"A high-rise flat in a northern city, confined to the balcony and the window","stages.0.name":"Kitten","stages.1.name":"Adolescent cat","stages.2.name":"Adult cat","stages.3.name":"Senior cat","causes.0.short":"Hit by a car","causes.0.text":"A car turns into the alley from the entrance. Before you can reach the roadside, its headlights are on you.","causes.1.short":"Wound infected from a fight","causes.1.text":"The other cat on the wall yowls, then leaps down on you. Your front paw is bitten through, and the wound quickly festers.","causes.2.short":"Immune system collapse","causes.2.text":"After a bite, your body slowly loses its defences. One minor infection follows another, and you cannot pull through.","causes.3.short":"Feline distemper","causes.3.text":"You stop eating for several days, and your stools turn watery. The virus spreads through the litter quickly.","causes.4.short":"Mother never returned, starved","causes.4.text":"The queen went out to find food and did not come back for three days. You are too hungry to cry, and you can only shiver in small jerks.","causes.5.short":"Poisoned","causes.5.text":"You lick a bit of rat poison from the ground, or chew on a lily flower. Then you start vomiting, and your kidneys gradually stop working.","causes.6.short":"Old-age illness, euthanasia","causes.6.text":"You have been losing weight for a long time. The vet says your kidneys can no longer work. You lie on a steel table and close your eyes.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"You sleep more and more, and even when awake you hardly want to move. On one quiet night, you do not wake again.","events.0.text":"Your mother licks you clean, and your body presses against her belly until you warm up.","events.1.text":"Your eyes open, and the world is a blue fog.","events.2.text":"Your mother picks you up in her mouth and moves you to another spot. You spend two days in a cardboard box before you are returned to where you were.","events.3.text":"A big dog charges from the mouth of the alley. Your mother carries you into a drain opening.","events.4.text":"Your owner teases you with a feather. You pounce on it and chew it into a mess.","events.5.text":"You eat your first can of cat food. It is softer than you imagined.","events.6.text":"You try to catch your own tail, spin three times, and tumble onto the ground.","events.7.text":"You chase a butterfly, roll across the ground, and then pretend nothing happened.","events.8.text":"You catch your first sparrow. It quickly wriggles free, leaving you with only a few feathers in your mouth.","events.8.fact":"Free-roaming cats in the United States are estimated to kill between 1.4 and 3.7 billion birds a year (Loss et al. 2013, Nature Communications)","events.9.text":"You learn to lie motionless in the grass and wait for prey to come closer.","events.10.text":"A strange tomcat invades your territory. You yowl at each other for a long time, and neither of you leaves first.","events.11.text":"This alley is your territory. The scent in the corner by the wall is one you left.","events.12.text":"One of your teeth has been broken off in a fight.","events.13.text":"You lie on the windowsill watching the birds outside, your tail flicking back and forth. Your owner shuts the window.","events.14.text":"Heavy rain falls all night, and the food by the bins is soaked through.","events.15.text":"A rat pokes its head out of a crack in the wall. You stay still and wait for it to come a little closer.","events.16.text":"You give birth to your first litter, {n} kittens in all.","events.17.text":"You give birth to another litter, {n} kittens in all.","events.18.text":"You sleep a lot at night, and sometimes during the day you forget where you were going.","events.19.text":"You start sleeping in a corner of the kitchen. The vet says you have lost a little more weight.","events.20.text":"On a winter night you find an old cardboard box and curl up inside to spend the night.","events.21.text":"Behind a pile of old boards, a cockroach slowly crawls towards you, and you pin it down with one paw.","events.22.text":"You crouch at the back-door steps of a noodle shop, waiting until someone tosses out a fish head from the bottom of a bowl.","events.23.text":"In the grass by a ditch, you pounce on a frog. It hops a few times and wriggles free.","events.24.text":"A car drives in from the alley entrance. You hide under it with your ears flat, and you only crawl out after it has driven away.","events.25.text":"A big dog comes running from the far end of the alley, barking. You squeeze into a cardboard box. Its barking fades into the distance, and you still dare not move.","events.26.text":"You sleep the whole afternoon in the sunlight on the windowsill.","events.27.text":"Your owner lays a soft blanket on the sofa, and you sink right into it and stay perfectly still.","events.28.text":"You climb over a low wall. In the next alley a strange cat stands with its tail raised, facing you.","events.29.text":"You walk the length of the rooftop and find a forgotten can in an empty flowerpot, tasting a faint saltiness.","events.30.text":"A strange tomcat yowls on a wall, and you follow the sound through two courtyards.","events.31.text":"You spend three days in a cardboard box in the corner of the wall, going out only once at night to look for food.","events.32.text":"Your mother carries you to a cardboard box at the foot of a wall. The box bears traces of rain, but you huddle together and stay warm.","events.33.text":"You climb onto the wall and look down a strange lane. Another tabby cries out in the distance, and you do not answer.","events.34.text":"At a street corner you recognise an old cat. It sniffs at you, then steps aside to make room.","events.35.text":"Your body is round, and you walk slowly. When you tumble, your owner laughs and picks you up.","events.36.text":"You grow heavier every year. Your owner feeds you a measured portion each day, yet you always feel it is not enough.","events.37.text":"You sprawl across the sofa, motionless, your thick coat pressed into the cushion.","events.38.text":"The skin under your eyes is always damp. Every day your owner wipes away the tears and eye discharge with a cotton ball.","events.39.text":"Your nose is short, and you wheeze when you breathe. On hot days you cannot run very fast.","events.40.text":"The vet says there are many small cysts in your kidneys. It is an inherited disease common in your breed.","events.41.text":"You are bigger than the others in your litter. When you climb the bookcase, it wobbles.","events.42.text":"You can hook the dried fish on the kitchen counter with one outstretched paw.","events.43.text":"Your hind legs are growing clumsy. Before you jump onto the windowsill, you test it first and only then dare to leap.","events.44.text":"When the fishing boats come in, small fish fall onto the dock. You wait behind a wooden crate with a crowd of cats.","events.45.text":"Every day the back door of a small restaurant throws out a bucket of fish heads. Your group of cats fights fast for them.","events.46.text":"In hot weather the street cats gather in the shade of a basement. The air smells of fish and damp.","events.47.text":"Someone is airing bedding in the yard, and an old cat is hiding under the quilt. You slip in and sleep all afternoon.","events.48.text":"Voices from the lane come and go beyond the wall. You lie on the windowsill, squinting your eyes into slits.","events.49.text":"On a winter morning, ice coats the eaves. You step onto it, your paws slip, and you nearly fall off the wall.","events.50.text":"Your mother gave birth to you in a hay pile in the barn. An owl lands on the roof, and you all freeze.","events.51.text":"In a corner of the barn you catch a mouse and bite through its neck in one go.","events.52.text":"The sound of a tractor comes from across the field bank. You jump out of the hay and hide in another barn.","events.53.text":"Your world is a balcony and a window. Through the glass you watch the pigeons below, hopping back and forth along the power lines.","events.54.text":"Afternoon sunlight comes through the window. You lie on the floor and warm your whole body in it.","events.55.text":"Someone is renovating downstairs, and the drill keeps whining. Your ears twitch constantly.","events.56.text":"You are the first to slip out through the door gap. The outside world is big, and you run three streets without stopping.","events.57.text":"You always crouch in the doorway for a long time, checking for dogs, before you dare to take a step out.","events.58.text":"You drag a ball of yarn under the bed and pull out one thread after another, playing all night.","events.59.text":"You are the lightest of your litter. Your mother gives her teat to other kittens, and you have to fight for a single sip.","events.60.text":"An old tomcat tries to take over your territory. You pounce straight at it and pin it against the wall.","decisions.0.title":"A road","decisions.0.body":"On the other side of the road lies a discarded chicken leg. The headlights of a car have appeared in the distance, not yet at the junction.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Wait for the car to pass before crossing","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You crouch at the roadside until the headlights slide past you, then slowly cross.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Dash across while the lights are still far off","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being hit by a car↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You grab the chicken leg, and behind you a squeal of brakes rings out. You do not look back.","decisions.1.title":"A bird on the windowsill","decisions.1.body":"A sparrow lands on the windowsill, about 30 cm from you. The window is shut, and it has not noticed you.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Pounce","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You lunge, but the glass stops you. The sparrow flies off, and you lie on the windowsill for a while.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Keep watching","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stare at it for a long time, until it flies away, and the tip of your tail keeps twitching.","decisions.2.title":"Territory","decisions.2.body":"A strange tomcat yowls on top of a wall. This alley is your territory, but its voice is louder than yours.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Charge down and fight","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Win the territory · Risk of injury↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You roll together at the foot of the wall. In the end it runs away, but your ear has been torn open by a bite.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Retreat to the corner","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · Lose the territory","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You back into the corner. The tomcat's yowling slowly fades, and this alley is no longer yours.","decisions.3.title":"The owner's decision","decisions.3.body":"The vet suggests neutering you, so you will stop always wanting to roam and will fight less.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Agree","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Surgery carries a small risk","decisions.3.options.0.result":"The surgery goes smoothly. You wear a round collar around your neck, and for a long time after waking you feel unwell.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Refuse","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Freedom · Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay as you are, still eager to roam and still fighting with other cats.","decisions.4.title":"A check-up in old age","decisions.4.body":"You have lost weight twice. The vet says you could have more tests, or you could stay quietly at home.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Have tests and start medication","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Expensive","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You take a pill each day, and your weight slowly comes back a little.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay quietly at home","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Comfort · Risk↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You bask on the balcony, and your owner sits with you every day.","decisions.5.title":"A mother cat under the wall","decisions.5.body":"In a cardboard box at the foot of the wall, a queen is with two kittens. She watches you warily, and her ears are already flattened.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Approach slowly and sniff the kittens","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Curiosity · Possible scratches","decisions.5.options.0.result":"The queen arches her back and lunges at you, and your nose is scratched. You back away a few steps, and the kittens are still crying.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Walk away and forage elsewhere","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safe · One meal missed","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You circle to the other side of the street. The crying from the box fades away behind you.","decisions.6.title":"A feverish night","decisions.6.body":"For two days you have had no appetite. Your body is hot, and your nose is dry. Your owner wants to take you to the vet, but you hate the carrier.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Go to the vet","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Health↑ · Expensive","decisions.6.options.0.result":"The vet gives you an injection. In the days that follow, your belly slowly softens.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Tough it out alone","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Risk↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You hide under the bed, not eating or moving, until the morning of the third day, when your body is no longer so hot.","decisions.7.title":"A winter night in the underground car park","decisions.7.body":"The temperature in the alley has dropped below zero, and the food by the bins has frozen. The residential car park's door is open, and inside it is warm, but there is a strong smell of motor oil and rat poison.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Slip into the car park and find a warm corner","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Warm · Risk of poisoning","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You curl up behind a pile of cardboard. In the middle of the night you lick your paw, and there is a bitter taste in your mouth.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the alley and wait it out","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Cold · Hungry","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You huddle in a corner for three nights, until the wind finally stops.","decisions.8.title":"A territory slipping away","decisions.8.body":"On top of the wall a young tomcat is calling. You jump, but fail to reach the top, and your claws scrape a line down the wall. This alley may no longer be yours.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Go to another alley","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You walk along a row of drying poles to another alley. There, someone has left a bowl of rice beside the bins.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Keep watch over your old corner","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You stay by the base of that wall. The young tomcat yowls a few times and does not climb up again.","decisions.9.title":"Hot weather","decisions.9.body":"It has been over thirty degrees for three days in a row. Your nose is working hard to breathe, and your owner stands by the air conditioner with a wet towel.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Stay in the cool room","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Activity↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You sprawl across the floor, and your breathing gradually settles.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Jump onto the balcony to bask in the sun","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Health↓ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You jump onto the balcony, and before long your mouth is open and you are panting. Your owner picks you up and carries you back inside.","decisions.10.title":"Dried fish on the kitchen counter","decisions.10.body":"A bag of dried fish sits on the counter, and your owner has just left the kitchen. You are only a step away from the fish.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Jump up and grab it","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Fall risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You leap down with the fish in your mouth, and its smell makes you forget what is happening behind you.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Wait for your owner to return","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.10.options.1.result":"Your owner comes back, sees you crouching at the edge of the counter, and puts the fish away.","decisions.11.title":"Rat poison in the barn corner","decisions.11.body":"A bag of rat poison sits in a corner of the barn, and beside it lies a freshly dead rat.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Step around it and don't touch","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Safe","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You circle around to the other side and jump onto the hay pile.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Lick it once","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You lick a little of the powder, and your mouth turns bitter. That night your stomach aches badly.","decisions.12.title":"Window not fully closed","decisions.12.body":"The window is open a crack, and the wind blows in through the gap. The pigeons on the power lines outside are calling.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Squeeze out through the gap","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"The outside world · Risk↑↑","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You squeeze out through the gap, and your paws land on the metal casing of an air conditioner. The wind is strong, and you can barely hold on.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Stay curled up indoors","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You crouch by the window and watch the pigeons slowly fly away.","opening.title":"First crawl","opening.body":"You have just been born. Your eyes are still sealed, and your ears are pressed flat against your head. Among the kittens in the nest, the middle is warmest, and there are only a few teats, so only those who get there first can feed.","opening.options.0.t":"Crawl towards the warmest spot in the middle of the nest","opening.options.0.hint":"Warmth · Teats taken first","opening.options.0.result":"You wriggle into the very centre, surrounded by the bodies of other kittens. You do not get any milk, but you are warm.","opening.options.1.t":"Go straight for the queen's teat","opening.options.1.hint":"First milk · Colder by the nest edge","opening.options.1.result":"You find a teat and suckle hard. The colostrum is thick and carries your mother's scent.","actions.forage.name":"Hunt","actions.forage.desc":"Chase rats, lizards and flying insects through alleys and grass, and now and then go through the rubbish.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Crouch under a car, in a cardboard box or against a wall, and stay quiet. You eat less, but you are least likely to be noticed.","actions.rest.name":"Find a warm place to sleep","actions.rest.desc":"Curl up to sleep in the sun or beside a heater. A cat spends most of its day asleep.","actions.explore.name":"Roam around","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your territory for other rooftops, alleys or courtyards. You will see more things, but you are more likely to clash with other cats.","actions.mate.name":"Look for a mate","actions.mate.desc":"Follow the scent of a cat in heat to find another cat. A neutered cat cannot do this.","actions.mate.done":"A litter of kittens is born, {n} in all.","idle.0":"The light slowly moves from the windowsill to the floor, and the room grows quiet.","idle.1":"Wind slips in through the gap under the door, and your ears twitch.","idle.2":"Far away, someone calls a cat's name. It is not yours.","idle.3":"A bird calls outside, and the tip of your tail trembles.","idle.4":"You rub against something soft, leaving a little of your own scent.","idle.5":"The sky darkens, and the room holds only a little light.","idle.6":"A car sounds in the distance and soon fades away.","idle.7":"Someone is cooking in a corner somewhere, and the smell drifts over.","actions.forage.text.0":"You crouch behind a vegetable crate, perfectly still, waiting for prey to come closer.","actions.forage.text.1":"There are faint scratching sounds in a rat hole by the wall, and you press your ear to the ground to listen.","actions.forage.text.2":"A lizard pokes its head out of a gap between the bricks, then ducks back in.","actions.hide.text.0":"You crouch under a car, your claws gripping the ground tightly.","actions.hide.text.1":"A big dog runs past from the far end of the alley. You hold your breath until it is gone.","actions.hide.text.2":"The cardboard box is dark, and you press your tail beneath your body.","actions.rest.text.0":"The sun shines on you, and you stretch and roll your belly over.","actions.rest.text.1":"You sleep deeply, your ears twitching now and then.","actions.rest.text.2":"A bird calls outside, and the tip of your tail twitches in response.","actions.explore.text.0":"You climb over a low wall and walk a long way along an unfamiliar alley.","actions.explore.text.1":"Roof tiles creak softly under your paws, and lamps come on one by one in the distance.","actions.explore.text.2":"A courtyard gate stands open. You pause at the entrance, then do not go in.","actions.mate.text.0":"You catch an unfamiliar scent beyond the wall, and you follow it for a long time.","actions.mate.text.1":"A tomcat yowls on a wall, and you call back to him across the barrier.","actions.mate.text.2":"You stop on an unfamiliar rooftop, your tail held high."},"cattle":{"name":"Cattle","tagline":"There are roughly 1.5 billion cattle in the world, and you are one of them, born in a place that people chose for you","survivalNote":"This is a rough weighted-average curve. Beef cattle are usually slaughtered at 18 to 24 months (the cliff near days 600 to 660 on the curve); dairy cows are often culled after 4 to 6 years; Indian and nomadic herds live longer, which the risk multipliers capture","epitaph":"You lived about two years, or about six. Other people decided much of your life for you, more than once.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Cetartiodactyla › Bovidae","size":"Adults weigh about 400 to 800 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Herd type","birth.rolls.1.label":"Pasture the year you were born","birth.rolls.2.label":"How you were born","birth.siblingsLabel":"Calves born the same year","birth.intro":"You were born at {r0}. You weigh about 30 to 40 kilograms and are {sex}. About {siblings} other calves were born the same year.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a dairy farm where the mothers are Holstein-type milk cows","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a ranch in the American Midwest, where calves stay with their mothers before being sent to a feedlot","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a cattle ranch in Brazil, where herds graze freely on large pastures","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a village household in rural India, where local cattle wander between the homes and the fields","birth.rolls.0.options.4.0":"a nomadic herd on the African savanna, moving with the seasons and the water","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A year of normal rainfall, with good grass growth","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A drought year: dry pasture, with hay bought in","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"A foot-and-mouth outbreak year, with all nearby cattle quarantined","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Born beside your mother, with enough colostrum","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Taken from your mother soon after birth and hand-fed from a bucket","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A difficult birth, pulled out with a rope by farm workers","stages.0.name":"Calf","stages.1.name":"Young animal","stages.2.name":"Fattening or growing period","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Slaughter","causes.0.text":"A truck carries you to the slaughterhouse. The conveyor, the pen, a few pairs of hands: it is over in a matter of minutes.","causes.1.short":"Bloat","causes.1.text":"The feed you eat produces more gas than your rumen can release. Your left side swells, and each breath becomes harder.","causes.2.short":"Rumen acidosis","causes.2.text":"Concentrate feed makes your rumen acidic. You develop diarrhea and stagger when you walk.","causes.3.short":"Bovine respiratory disease","causes.3.text":"Cattle in the herd infect one another. You cough for weeks, and finally you stop getting up.","causes.4.short":"Mastitis spreads","causes.4.text":"An infection in your udder goes untreated for two weeks, and your temperature stays high. The farm does not save you.","causes.5.short":"Drought, no water or grass","causes.5.text":"The grass dries up for months. You follow the herd a long way, but you still cannot find water.","causes.6.short":"Calf diarrhea and dehydration","causes.6.text":"Calf scours dehydrate you quickly. Your eyes sink, and you do not survive the first week.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth wear down, and you graze more and more slowly. You lie down in the grass and do not get up again.","events.0.text":"You try to stand as soon as you are born, your four legs trembling. Your mother licks you at your side and dries the wet fur on your body.","events.1.text":"You drink colostrum for the first time. It is thick and yellow and rich in nutrients, and you feed hungrily.","events.2.text":"A few hours later, a person leads you away from your mother and puts you in a separate pen. She calls for you for a long time.","events.3.text":"You stay with your mother. You can go back to her to nurse whenever you like.","events.4.text":"The farm staff fasten an ear tag to your ear and record your number.","events.5.text":"A worker burns off the buds of your horns. It hurts badly, and you cry out for a long time.","events.6.text":"The pasture is wide. You run with your companions, your tail held high.","events.7.text":"Flies circle around your eyes. You shake your head and swish your tail, but you cannot drive them off.","events.8.text":"A tick attaches to your neck and stays there for several days.","events.9.text":"You follow the herd to another pasture. The mud under your feet is deep.","events.10.text":"The feedlot troughs are filled with corn, soybean meal, and pellets. You eat fast, and your body grows heavier each day.","events.11.text":"The feedlot ground is a mix of mud and manure. Every step sinks your hooves in.","events.12.text":"One cow coughs all night. The next morning, the cows beside it start coughing too.","events.13.text":"You are driven into a narrow chute with iron railings on both sides. Cattle push from the front and from behind.","events.14.text":"Your body grows stronger month by month.","events.15.text":"You are led into the milking parlor for the first time. Metal milkers are fitted to your teats, twice a day.","events.16.text":"You are pregnant again. The breeder stands behind you for a moment, then walks away.","events.17.text":"The calf you just gave birth to is taken away. You call from the pen for several days.","events.18.text":"An old woman sets a bundle of grass in front of you. In this village, a cow is usually not sent to slaughter.","events.18.fact":"Most Indian states restrict or ban the slaughter of female cattle, with rules that vary by state (Business Standard, 2015, citing a reply in India's Parliament)","events.19.text":"At night the herd stands uneasily. In the distance a lion roars, and herders walk back and forth with torches.","events.20.text":"In a drought year, you and the herd walk hundreds of kilometers to find water.","events.21.text":"You lower your head to graze, and dew on the grass tips wets your nose.","events.22.text":"The trough holds corn, soybean meal, and pellets. You eat fast, and your rumen swells a little more each day.","events.23.text":"You walk into the shade and lie down on dry hay. There are fewer flies here.","events.24.text":"You push into the corner by the pen, away from the water trough. It smells of mud and manure.","events.25.text":"You lie in the grass and chew your cud slowly. Your body warms, and you close your eyes.","events.26.text":"You lie in the mud, and it slowly dries into a hard crust on your body.","events.27.text":"You follow the herd toward a distant pasture. You pass a small stream, and the water is cold.","events.28.text":"You walk out of the barn, circle the pasture, and come back to the gate.","events.29.text":"The breeder leads you into a narrow pen. You stand still, and over the next few days your body begins to change.","events.30.text":"You follow an older herd across a dry hillside. At the foot of the slope you find a pool of water.","events.31.text":"The grass in the pasture is dry and hard. Your mother lowers her head, trying to chew out a little juice.","events.32.text":"The water in the pond drops a little more each day, and the muddy bottom cracks. You walk in a line to a faraway place to drink.","events.33.text":"The farm buys a truckload of hay and hands it out by head. Your share is a handful smaller than the other cattle's.","events.34.text":"The grass on the pasture grows evenly, and the ground is soft underfoot. You eat until you are full.","events.35.text":"It rains a lot this year, and the grass grows back flush after flush. The herd walks slowly across the meadow.","events.36.text":"The grass grows especially tall this year. You stand in the grass and can barely see your own legs.","events.37.text":"Foot-and-mouth disease is found in the county, and all the cattle are shut in sheds. You stay in the shed for several weeks.","events.38.text":"The inspectors come and check each of you, looking in your mouths and giving you injections. They write every number down in a notebook.","events.39.text":"The trucks that carry cattle stop for a month. Feed grows scarcer by the day, and you live on what grass is left near the pasture edge.","events.40.text":"Your mother has a familiar smell. You lick at her teat with your tongue until the milk comes.","events.41.text":"It is your first taste of colostrum, thick and yellow. You gulp it down, and your belly swells quickly.","events.42.text":"You follow your mother across the pasture. Every so often she stops and lets you have a drink of milk.","events.43.text":"The keeper feeds you from a milk bucket. The milk is warm, but you have to suck hard, or the bucket gets pushed away.","events.44.text":"Your pen is small, and you can only stand or lie down. A calf in the next pen calls out, but no one answers you.","events.45.text":"You are more wary of people than the other calves in your group. When someone catches you, it takes a long time before you relax.","events.46.text":"Your legs are weak, and after standing for a while you sink to your knees. The workers dry you off and lay you on the hay.","events.47.text":"Your breathing is slow, with a little mucus in your nose. After a night you slowly catch your breath.","events.48.text":"You run more slowly than the other calves in your group. They have run far ahead, and you are still shuffling along behind.","events.49.text":"You are the first to run past the pasture fence. You stop on the far side and look back to see your companions still dawdling.","events.50.text":"You always stand on the outer edge of the herd, watching what people do. When someone comes close, you back off two steps first.","events.51.text":"You push your nose through a gap in the wire mesh and sniff about. On the other side is a meadow you have never visited.","events.52.text":"You are lighter than the other calves in your group, and when you stand you always wobble. When your mother licks you, you are nearly knocked over.","events.53.text":"Your shoulders are broad, and you walk steadily. The old cattle in the herd make way, and you reach the front of the trough.","decisions.0.title":"Your mother's call","decisions.0.body":"You are placed in a separate pen, with only a wall between you and your mother. She calls from the other side, and you call back. You can keep calling, or go quiet and lean your body against the wall.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Keep calling","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Stress↑ Stamina↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You call until your throat is hoarse. After a few days, her calls slowly stop.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Lean quietly against the wall","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Calm","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You lean against the wall and slowly fall asleep.","decisions.1.title":"A place at the trough","decisions.1.body":"The trough is narrow and crowded, with cattle packed in head to tail. You can push in and wait there, or stand at the edge and wait for the others to finish drinking.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Push in to the trough","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Crowding↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You push in and drink. A neighboring cow jabs your shoulder with its horn.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait at the edge","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Water↓ Safety↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You wait a long time before the cattle at the trough finally move off, and you get one mouthful.","decisions.2.title":"The grass is running out","decisions.2.body":"The pasture has been grazed bare. Far off on the hillside there is a patch of green, and the herd has started moving toward it. You can follow them or stay where you are.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Follow the herd","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk on the road↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You walk for two weeks, and the soles of your feet bleed. When you reach the hillside, there is even less grass than you expected.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You chew slowly on dry hay, and you grow thinner each day.","decisions.3.title":"The cow next door","decisions.3.body":"The cow next to you coughs hard, and green mucus runs from its nose. When it lies down beside you, you can smell its breath.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Move away","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Infection risk↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You move to the other side of the pen. The coughing still carries across to you.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep lying next to it","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Infection risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You sleep pressed together all night. The next day, you start coughing too.","decisions.4.title":"The trough is crowded","decisions.4.body":"Cattle crowd the trough, each one shoving forward. You can push to the front, or wait until the crowd thins out. Neither choice will change the day you leave for slaughter.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Push to the front","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Crowding↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You push to the front and get a mouthful of corn. A neighboring cow butts your shoulder with its head.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Wait for the crowd to thin out","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You wait a long time before your turn comes. The feed has already gone a little cold.","decisions.5.title":"Tender alfalfa","decisions.5.body":"Ahead is a patch of tender alfalfa, shining green. You want to gulp it all down, but eating too much of this young forage traps gas in the rumen. You can bury your face and eat greedily, or eat slowly and stop to chew your cud.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Bury your face and eat greedily","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Bloat risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You eat until you are stuffed. Before long, your left side starts to swell.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Eat slowly and stop to chew your cud","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Steady","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You eat slowly, stop after a while, and lie down to chew your cud.","decisions.6.title":"The milking machine","decisions.6.body":"The milker fits the machine to your teats twice a day, and it is loud. You can stand still and let them milk you, or kick the machine away. Neither choice will change the date of your culling.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stand and let them milk you","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Steady · Health↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"The machine turns on and off. You stand still, staring at the wall ahead.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Kick the machine away","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↓ Health↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You kick the milking machine, and it tips over on the floor. The milker shoves you and drags you back into the pen.","decisions.7.title":"Shade or the water's edge","decisions.7.body":"It is hot. There is a patch of dry ground in the shade, and tender grass grows at the water's edge, some distance away. Your legs are not as strong as they used to be.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Lie down in the shade","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You lie in the shade and chew your cud slowly.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Walk to the water far away","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Stamina cost↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You walk a long way, drink at the water, and find some tender grass. On the way back, you walk very slowly.","decisions.8.title":"Unable to stand","decisions.8.body":"You lie on the hay, your legs still weak. Your mother gives a low moo beside you. You can struggle to your feet and look for milk, or rest for a while first.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Struggle up to look for milk","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Colostrum↑ Stamina↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You stagger to your feet and follow your mother's scent to her udder.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Rest for a while first","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Colostrum late","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You lie on the hay, growing colder all the time. By the time you want to stand again, it is too late.","decisions.9.title":"Sharing the hay","decisions.9.body":"The pasture hands out hay by head. You are standing near the back, and the cattle in front have already scooped a hollow in the pile.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Push forward to grab some","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Crowding↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You shove your way in and grab a mouthful of hay. A cow butts your shoulder with its horn.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Wait at the back and eat what is left","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"All you get are the crumbs at the bottom of the pile.","decisions.10.title":"The quarantine pen","decisions.10.body":"The inspectors are herding you into a narrow pen. It is crowded, and your body is smeared with other cattle's saliva.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Stand in the middle and wait for inspection","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Health↓ Safety↓","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You stand in the middle and wait all afternoon. When your turn comes, the needle goes in deep.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Push to the edge of the pen","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You make it to the edge. The crowd moves about in the middle, and you are never called out.","decisions.11.title":"Calf beside the milk pail","decisions.11.body":"The keeper sets the milk pail on the ground. The calf from the next pen pushes its head through the bars and tries to steal your milk.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Stretch your head over and grab some","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Crowding↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You gulp a few mouthfuls, and the calf butts you with its head.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Wait for it to finish","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Food↓","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You wait a long time, and by then the pail is empty.","decisions.12.title":"Wire fence beyond the pasture","decisions.12.body":"The other side of the wire fence holds a large stretch of grass. The farm workers have not locked the gate, only hung a single wire across it.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Squeeze through","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Injury risk↑","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You squeeze under the wire and graze on that meadow for a long time. When you return, there is a scratch across your body.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Stay in the pen","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You stand in the pen, looking at the grass until dark.","opening.title":"Stand up and find the udder","opening.body":"You have just landed on wet grass, and your legs tremble. Your mother looks down at you, and her scent is all around. Standing, finding the udder, and getting colostrum takes real effort. Lying still saves energy, but the colostrum will come late.","opening.options.0.t":"Stand up at once and look for the udder","opening.options.0.hint":"Colostrum↑ Stamina↓","opening.options.0.result":"You wobble to your feet and follow your mother's scent to the udder. You get colostrum, and warmth slowly returns to your body.","opening.options.1.t":"Lie still and rest first","opening.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Colostrum late","opening.options.1.result":"You lie on the wet grass for a long time and stay cold. By the time you stand again, your mother has wandered off to graze elsewhere.","actions.forage.name":"Graze or eat feed","actions.forage.desc":"Graze on the pasture, or eat feed at the feedlot or the milking parlor.","actions.hide.name":"Find shade or the edge of the barn","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the herd and go to the shade of a tree, the side of a shed, or a corner of the pen.","actions.rest.name":"Lie down and chew your cud","actions.rest.desc":"Lie down, chew your cud slowly, and let your body relax.","actions.explore.name":"Head for another pasture","actions.explore.desc":"Leave where you are and walk to a farther pasture or to water.","actions.mate.name":"Breeding","actions.mate.desc":"Be led into the breeding pen and wait for the breeder to finish.","actions.mate.done":"You become pregnant with a calf.","idle.0":"A breeze crosses the grass, carrying the smell of soil and green plants.","idle.1":"You swish your tail to swat the flies.","idle.2":"A cow beside you chews its cud, its jaws moving slowly.","idle.3":"You lower your head to eat, then lift it again to look into the distance.","idle.4":"The night is quiet, with only the sound of cattle pressing against one another.","idle.5":"A bird lands on your back and flies off again.","idle.6":"Somewhere far off, someone shouts, and the sound slowly fades.","idle.7":"You have stood in one place for a long time, and your legs ache a little.","actions.forage.text.0":"You lower your head to graze, and dew on the grass tips wets your nose.","actions.forage.text.1":"You eat one mouthful after another, and the sound of chewing rises from your stomach.","actions.forage.text.2":"The cattle around you are eating too, now and then glancing out at the distance.","actions.hide.text.0":"You stand in the shade of a tree, and there are fewer flies.","actions.hide.text.1":"You walk to the side of the shed and lean your back against the wood.","actions.hide.text.2":"The herd is still moving in the distance, but you do not follow it.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie on the ground and chew your cud slowly.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your body warms a little, and you close your eyes.","actions.rest.text.2":"Your legs ache, so you switch to lying on the other side.","actions.explore.text.0":"You follow the herd toward a distant pasture, the mud deep under your feet.","actions.explore.text.1":"You pass a small stream, and the water is cold.","actions.explore.text.2":"You walk around the edge of the pasture and return to the herd."},"chimpanzee":{"name":"Chimpanzee","tagline":"Human and chimpanzee genomes are about 98.8% identical across the sequences that can be directly compared","survivalNote":"Data come from long-term individual tracking at Gombe in Tanzania and Taï in Côte d'Ivoire. Infant mortality in the first year is high; after adulthood, the annual death rate is lower, and lifespan tops out at about 40 to 50 years. The curve is a rough estimate.","epitaph":"You lived for decades in the forest. Your name was never written down, but your community remembers you.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Primates › Hominidae","size":"Body length about 0.7 to 0.9 m (standing upright); males weigh about 40 to 60 kg, females about 30 to 45 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Mother's situation","birth.rolls.2.label":"Size of the group","birth.rolls.3.label":"Nearby human activity","birth.siblingsLabel":"Offspring of the same mother","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}, to {r1}. You are {sex}. Chimpanzees usually have one infant at a time, so you have no siblings, but your mother will spend years caring for you alone.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the rainforest north of the Congo River","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the dense forest of Taï National Park in Côte d'Ivoire","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the riverside forest at Gombe in Tanzania","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"secondary forest at the edge of farmland","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an experienced mother with several sisters nearby","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a young mother having her first baby","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A large group of nearly a hundred, splitting into subgroups to feed","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A group of about fifty, divided into several small parties","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A small group of a dozen, with peripheral males often pushed aside","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"A protected national park, where rangers walk the forest every month","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"A logging concession, where new roads have cut the forest into pieces","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"A rescue centre enclosure, where staff put out fruit each day","stages.0.name":"Infant (clinging to its mother)","stages.1.name":"Juvenile (following companions)","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Taken by a leopard","causes.0.text":"A leopard drops from the tree. Your mother holds you tight against her chest, but its claws catch your leg.","causes.1.short":"Shot by a poacher","causes.1.text":"A gunshot rings out from the edge of the forest. Before you can turn, your left shoulder is pierced as if by a red-hot iron rod.","causes.2.short":"Wire snare, infected wound","causes.2.text":"A wire wraps around your wrist. You chew it free, but over several days the wound swells into a hard lump, and there is no medicine to treat it.","causes.3.short":"Attacked by males from a neighboring community","causes.3.text":"A group of males from a neighboring community charges out of the deep woods and pins you against a large tree. Their teeth and fists land on you.","causes.4.short":"Bitten to death by an intruding male","causes.4.text":"A strange male breaks into the nest while your mother is out searching for fruit. He bites you, and by the time she returns, it is too late.","causes.5.short":"Respiratory infection","causes.5.text":"Influenza spreads through the community. You cough harder and harder, your lungs filling as if with water, and after a few days you never wake again.","causes.6.short":"Ebola hemorrhagic fever","causes.6.text":"You start to run a fever, and then you begin to bleed. Your companions avoid you, but some of them have already begun to fall ill.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth loosen, and you can no longer crack hard fruit. You lie in the nest for an entire afternoon and do not get up again.","events.0.text":"You cling to your mother's belly. Her fur is thick and smells of forest soil and leaves.","events.1.text":"Your mother picks a fig from a tree, chews it, and feeds it to you.","events.2.text":"You slip off a branch, and your mother catches you. Your knee is scraped, and a little blood oozes out.","events.3.text":"A large male walks under the tree, and your mother presses your head against her chest until he is gone.","events.4.text":"For the first time, you see another chimpanzee's face over a mother's shoulder. It is grooming a young one, very gently.","events.5.text":"You copy your companions and try to fish for termites with a grass stem. Your first stem snaps, and no termites take the bait.","events.5.fact":"Chimpanzees using grass stems to fish for termites was first recorded by Jane Goodall at Gombe, Tanzania (Goodall, Nature, 1964)","events.6.text":"You follow the group to the edge of the forest and see a patch of freshly felled trees. The air smells of scorched earth.","events.7.text":"You all eat until your mouths are full under a large fig tree. Two young males are fighting beneath it, and you keep your distance.","events.8.text":"A female of your own age becomes your friend. You groom each other for a long time and do not separate.","events.9.text":"A roar of males comes from the group. You climb deep into the canopy and hold your breath.","events.10.text":"The footprint of a young leopard is pressed into the mud on the riverbank, very close to your nest.","events.11.text":"You find plenty of fruit on a tree. This year's fruit is especially sweet, and you eat for a long time.","events.12.text":"You lie in the nest all afternoon. Light filters through the leaves and rests, unmoving, on the back of your hand.","events.13.text":"You walk the border and stop beside a strange footprint. It was left by another group.","events.14.text":"You come into estrus. A male follows you, making a low rumbling sound.","events.15.text":"You give birth to an infant. Its face is pink, and it grips your fur without letting go.","events.16.text":"A leopard sniffs around under a tree in the distance. You stay perfectly still until it walks away.","events.17.text":"A male from a neighboring community crosses the border. The males of your group charge together and drive it back.","events.18.text":"A rusty wire wraps around your foot. You chew it free, but the wound swells a few days later.","events.19.text":"The air smells of smoke. Someone is cooking meat at the edge of the forest.","events.20.text":"Influenza spreads through the group. Several infants cough hard, and their mothers hold them tighter.","events.21.text":"Human and chimpanzee genomes are about 98.8% identical across sequences that can be directly compared. This estimate comes from the 2005 chimpanzee genome sequencing.","events.21.fact":"Sequencing of the chimpanzee genome shows that about 98.8% of directly alignable sequence is identical to humans (Chimpanzee Sequencing and Analysis Consortium, Nature, 2005)","events.22.text":"Your movements slow down. You stay in the nest longer and longer, coming out only at midday to bask in the sun for a while.","events.23.text":"Your teeth are worn down, and the hard fruit on the trees is too tough to bite now.","events.24.text":"You are the oldest member of the group. Young chimpanzees come to see you, and sometimes they groom you.","events.25.text":"The group has more than a hundred chimpanzees. At dawn the whole forest rings with grunts and calls, and you follow a band of young ones out to the edge.","events.26.text":"Your subgroup goes to a grove of fruit trees. Other subgroups shout from distant parts of the forest, and you can hear each other.","events.27.text":"Two males in the group are fighting for the top position. Females from several subgroups stop eating and watch them face off under the trees.","events.28.text":"Your little party has five or six juveniles. You chase each other through the branches, and when you fall you don't cry, you just get up and keep fighting.","events.29.text":"Your subgroup finds a tree full of fruit. Everyone eats together, and nobody fights over it.","events.30.text":"A female in the group takes her young and moves to another forest. You stay behind with a few males, guarding the fruit trees that remain.","events.31.text":"The group has only a dozen or so members, and few juveniles. Each day you see the same faces, and it is easy to find companions.","events.32.text":"The group is small, so there are fewer hands to share the fruit. You arrive late and pick up only what has fallen to the ground.","events.33.text":"For several days, males from a neighbouring group prowl the edge of the forest. There are too few males in your group to drive them off.","events.34.text":"Park rangers walk through the forest once a month. Over time you grow used to their footprints and the smell of their campfires.","events.35.text":"Researchers have placed wooden boxes in the forest, with bananas inside. You learn to take one out with one hand while holding a branch with the other as a lookout.","events.36.text":"A camera hangs on a tree trunk, a red light blinking on and off. You slap at it, but it does not respond, so you walk away.","events.37.text":"You cross a path that has just been cleared. Only stumps are left, and rainwater has pooled between them. You walk a long way before you find a tree that was not felled.","events.38.text":"A large truck drives along the new road, and its wheels crush the tree roots. The next day, three big trees are gone from your fruit grove.","events.39.text":"The voices of the logging workers carry from far away. The group retreats deeper into the forest, and you walk all day.","events.40.text":"Every morning the keeper at the rescue centre places fruit on wooden racks. You and your companions queue to take it, and sometimes you have to shove a little.","events.41.text":"A rope hangs in the enclosure, and someone has tied an old tyre to it. You swing on it for a long time.","events.42.text":"On the far side of the enclosure, a chimpanzee screams loudly. It was rescued from a poacher's cage. You listen for a long time.","events.43.text":"You are the first to climb a very tall tree and sway in the treetop until an adult shouts at you. Only then do you slowly climb back down.","events.44.text":"You wait a long time by the stream, watching your companions wade across one by one. You go last, and the water has reached your belly.","events.45.text":"You turn over a pile of dead leaves and find a beetle. You poke at it with a stick for a long time, until it burrows into the soil.","events.46.text":"You are the thinnest in the group. When the juveniles scramble for fruit, you always get yours last.","events.47.text":"Your arms are thicker than those of other males your age. In one scuffle you shove a female against a tree trunk, and she yells and walks away.","decisions.0.title":"Mother is pregnant again","decisions.0.body":"Your mother's belly is growing, and you spend less time clinging to her than before. She begins to set you down, and sometimes she pushes you away.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Go find playmates your age","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Learn to socialize · Alone","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You join a group of youngsters wrestling in the branches. They bite you, and you bite back.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Keep clinging to your mother","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Well fed · Newborn competes for milk","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You grip her fur tightly. The newborn cries in her arms, and you bury your nose in her armpit.","decisions.1.title":"A termite mound","decisions.1.body":"A termite mound sits right at your feet, and termites crawl out of its opening. Your companions have already taken out sticks and are poking them into the holes.","decisions.1.fact":"Chimpanzees using grass stems to fish for termites was first recorded by Jane Goodall at Gombe, Tanzania (Goodall, Nature, 1964)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Fish for termites with a stick","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Well fed · Needs practice","decisions.1.options.0.result":"Your first stick breaks twice. The third time, you catch a termite and chew it slowly.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Go pick fruit from the trees","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · Few termites","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You climb a tree and find several ripe fruits. The smell of the termite mound drifts away on the wind.","decisions.2.title":"Leaving your birth group","decisions.2.body":"No brothers or sisters of yours remain in your group. A young male in a neighboring group keeps watching you, and his group has many females. If you stay here, you may never have the chance to raise a family of your own.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Leave for the neighboring group","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"May find a mate · Risk on the way","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You cross the border at dawn. The males of the new group stare at you, but they do not drive you away.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay in your group","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Familiar · Few mates","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You keep following your mother. Your gaze often drifts across the border to the unfamiliar forest.","decisions.3.title":"Calls at the border","decisions.3.body":"The males of your group pace back and forth along the border, and their calls grow louder. Strange males on the other side are answering, and their voices carry hostility.","decisions.3.fact":"Between 1974 and 1978, the Kahama community at Gombe was gradually wiped out in conflicts with the neighboring Kasakela community (Goodall, 1986; Wilson & Wrangham, 2003)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Join the patrol and charge across","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Defend territory · May be killed","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You charge toward the border with the other males. The males on the far side retreat, but a bite tears a gash in your arm.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hide behind the others and wait for them to return","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Keep your strength · Low rank","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You hide behind a tree and watch your companions drive the others away. When they return, you have already eaten the last of the fruit.","decisions.4.title":"Coughing in the group","decisions.4.body":"Several chimpanzees in the group start coughing, and mucus runs from their noses. They only recently came back from near a human camp.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Leave the sick group for distant forest","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Protect yourself · Less food","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You travel far. In a forest with no people, you find a few fruit trees, and you count the days.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay and groom each other","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Company · Pathogens spread fast","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You huddle with your companions and groom one another's fur. A few days later, your throat begins to itch.","decisions.5.title":"A wire","decisions.5.body":"A taut wire stretches along the edge of the forest, its other end tied to a small tree. Beneath it, a monkey caught in the loop is still struggling. A cornfield is not far away.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Go around this path","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safe · Little fruit","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You make a wide detour and find a few scattered fruit trees on a steep slope.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Eat your fill at the edge of the cornfield","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ · Poachers may be nearby","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You eat until your mouth is full of sweet corn. Far off in the forest, something moves.","decisions.6.title":"Growing old","decisions.6.body":"Your teeth are beginning to loosen, and you cough more at night. Your daughters have built new nests nearby and come to see you every evening.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay near your daughters' nest","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Cared for · Little food to share","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You hear their voices every day. You lie under their tree and quietly watch the light move through the forest.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Go alone to the old nest on the ridge","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Little food · Alone","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You climb onto the ridge. The old nest has collapsed, but it still blocks the wind.","decisions.7.title":"The group is too small","decisions.7.body":"Only a dozen or so members are left, and the bonds between the few adult males are growing tense. Males from the neighbouring group patrol the border every week. You could follow them to the other side, or stay with your group.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Follow the neighbouring males across the border","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"New group · May be ganged up on","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You follow them across the border. The males on the other side surround you, and you bite one of them until they back off.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay with the group and guard the fruit grove","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay and guard the fruit grove with a few males. Your share of the fruit grows smaller and smaller.","decisions.8.title":"A new road","decisions.8.body":"The logging company's new road cuts through your forest. A bulldozer is parked at the roadside, and its driver leans against the door, smoking. Beyond the road the fruit trees are plentiful, but you have to cross the road to reach them.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Cross the road into the forest on the other side","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk on the road","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You cross the road. The fruit trees on the other side are heavy with fruit, and you eat until your mouth is full. A gunshot rings out in the distance, and you do not look back.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay in the old forest and eat less","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You search the old forest for fruit. Each day you walk farther, and the fruit on the trees grows scarcer.","opening.title":"Mother choosing a night nest","opening.body":"You are not yet born. During the last months of carrying you, your mother builds a new nest every evening. She has two places to choose from: high in a large tree, with a good view but a long climb, or among the roots of a bush, hidden but close to the ground, where snakes and leopards may pass.","opening.options.0.t":"A large tree, high up","opening.options.0.hint":"Good view · Long climb","opening.options.0.result":"You are born in the tree. The wind keeps blowing, and your mother holds you without letting go.","opening.options.1.t":"Among the roots of a bush","opening.options.1.hint":"Hidden · Close to the ground","opening.options.1.result":"You are born under the roots. The smell of grass is strong, and your mother's voice is always close to your ear.","actions.instinct.name":"Follow instinct","actions.instinct.desc":"Keep to the rhythm of the group and do what a chimpanzee usually does.","actions.forage.name":"Forage (fruit and termites)","actions.forage.desc":"Search the forest for fruit and leaves, or fish for termites with a stick.","actions.hide.name":"Hide quietly","actions.hide.desc":"Stay silent in the canopy or in a thicket until the danger passes.","actions.rest.name":"Rest in the nest","actions.rest.desc":"Lie in your night nest, or sun yourself on a branch.","actions.explore.name":"Patrol the territory","actions.explore.desc":"Walk the edge of the group's range, looking for strange groups, traps, or new fruit trees.","actions.mate.name":"Mating","actions.mate.done":"You have given birth to {n} young. Each is tiny, with its eyes closed, gripping your fur.","idle.0":"You shift slowly among the branches, your arms stronger than your legs.","idle.1":"The forest is damp, and drops from the leaves fall on your shoulder.","idle.2":"In the distance another group is calling, and your companions answer.","idle.3":"You scratch your armpit with your fingers, then go looking for food again.","idle.4":"Patches of sunlight move across your body as the canopy sways.","idle.5":"You hear termites gnawing inside a tree trunk.","idle.6":"A companion's hand rests on your back, and you sit quietly together.","idle.7":"A bird flies over your head, and you watch it for a long time.","actions.forage.text.0":"You find a ripe fig under a tree, take a bite, and the juice runs down your wrist.","actions.forage.text.1":"You slide a stick into a termite mound's hole, then slowly draw it out.","actions.hide.text.0":"You hold your breath and press your body behind a thick branch.","actions.hide.text.1":"A distant footstep passes, and you wait a long time more.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie in the nest you built, eyes closed, ears still listening to the sounds outside.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the border, stopping every few steps to test the response from the distance with a call.","actions.explore.text.1":"You stay for a long time beside a cut tree stump."},"copepod":{"name":"Copepod","tagline":"You may live only a year, yet you are one of the foundations of the North Atlantic food web","survivalNote":"Survival data for copepods at sea come from field sampling and laboratory estimates. Survival from egg to adult is typically only about 1%; this curve is a rough estimate.","epitaph":"Your fat ended up as part of a herring, and the herring became food for something else. There is no empty space in the sea.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Crustacea › Copepoda","size":"Adults are about 2 to 3 mm long","note":"Copepods and krill are both crustaceans, but they are not the same group. Copepods form their own class and are only about the size of a grain of rice; krill belong to the Malacostraca, closer to shrimp and crabs.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth water","birth.rolls.1.label":"Spring bloom","birth.rolls.2.label":"Mother's history","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are an egg about 0.15 millimeters across, drifting in {r0}. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs were laid in your batch.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the surface water of the Norwegian Sea, where algae first bloom in spring","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the water over a continental shelf in the western North Atlantic","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"an upwelling zone southwest of Iceland","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a murky water mass in the northern North Sea","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an on-time bloom, arriving as diatoms peaked in the weeks you hatched","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"an early bloom that had already passed before you hatched","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a late bloom, with clear water and hunger through your early life","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"a year with almost no bloom, only scattered phytoplankton at the surface","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a mother who came up from the deep in winter with thick fat reserves","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a mother who stayed at the surface all winter and was lean","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a mother who laid you in a dense egg cluster, crowded all around","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Nauplius (N1 to N6)","stages.2.name":"Copepodid (C1 to C5)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Eaten by a herring larva","causes.0.text":"A herring larva rises from below, its open mouth sucking you in along with the water around you.","causes.1.short":"Seized by an arrow worm","causes.1.text":"An arrow worm pins you with its grasping spines, then slowly draws you into its mouth.","causes.2.short":"Bloom over, starved","causes.2.text":"The spring bloom is over. The seawater has turned clear, and there are no diatoms left around you to eat.","causes.3.short":"Bitten by a fish while rising at night","causes.3.text":"You rise with the darkness toward the surface. A fish approaches in the dark, and you have just enough time to feel the water change direction.","causes.4.short":"Egg eaten by other plankton","causes.4.text":"An adult copepod swims past and swallows you along with the eggs around you.","causes.5.short":"Energy spent during winter dormancy","causes.5.text":"You survived the whole winter in deep water, living off your lipid sacs. Spring came too late, and your fat was used up.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"You swim more and more slowly, and finally you stop in the water and sink slowly.","events.0.text":"Lipids are stored in the yolk, fueling your first few days.","events.0.fact":"Copepod eggs and early nauplii depend mainly on yolk and lipid reserves, and early food conditions affect their survival (Mauchline, 1998)","events.1.text":"You wriggle out of the eggshell as a nauplius. Your body has a single eyespot, and three pairs of appendages paddle you through the water.","events.1.fact":"Copepod nauplii usually pass through six stages, swimming and feeding with three pairs of appendages (Mauchline, 1998)","events.2.text":"You eat a diatom, and its silica shell cracks apart in your mouthparts.","events.3.text":"You molt once, and your body is a little larger than before.","events.4.text":"A wind stirs the surface water all day, and the algae concentration rises as a result.","events.5.text":"You have eaten your fill, and the lipid sacs in your body start to turn pale orange.","events.6.text":"You rise and sink in the water with the change of day and night. At night you are near the surface; by day you are in the depths.","events.7.text":"A school of herring larvae swims by from above and eats several of your companions around you. You stay perfectly still.","events.8.text":"A cold current sweeps you into a patch of water that is much colder.","events.9.text":"The days are getting shorter. You begin storing more fat in your body.","events.10.text":"You shed your last skin and become an adult.","events.11.text":"You spawn for the first time. The eggs scatter one by one into the seawater and are soon impossible to find.","events.12.text":"You release another {n} eggs.","events.13.text":"A floating clump of debris holds several chains of diatoms. You eat them.","events.14.text":"An arrow worm swims toward you, its two grasping spines closing and opening beside you. It does not catch you.","events.15.text":"A storm at the surface sweeps you into the depths, and it takes you all night to find your way back to your original depth.","events.16.text":"You take in a fragment of microplastic. It stays in your gut for a long time.","events.16.fact":"Copepods ingest microplastics, as recorded in laboratory studies (Cole et al. 2013)","events.17.text":"Your ovaries are empty. Your body is lighter, and you swim more slowly.","events.18.text":"Another night comes, and you rise with your companions, but this time you are a few seconds late, and the ones ahead have already vanished.","events.19.text":"From the distant surface, the breathing of a whale drifts down like a low wind.","events.20.text":"You filter feed at the surface, your mouthparts opening and closing, and you trap a small chain of diatoms in your mouth.","events.21.text":"The diatoms at the surface are so dense that your gut glows.","events.22.text":"At dusk near the surface, the shadows of a school of herring larvae flicker above. You have not finished eating when the current pushes you down.","events.23.text":"The deep water is cold, and food is scarce. You stay here for a few days, and your body shrinks a little.","events.24.text":"You drift slowly beneath a boundary where cold and warm water meet, and the current around your body suddenly feels very light.","events.25.text":"You barely move your appendages and hang motionless in the water. Your body's energy use slows down.","events.26.text":"You cross a boundary where the water temperature changes sharply, and the water suddenly turns cold. Few companions are around you.","events.27.text":"You bump into a floating clump of seaweed debris, with swarms of diatoms trapped inside.","events.28.text":"Lights from a distant ship shine on the water, and a band of light reaches all the way down to you.","events.29.text":"A male swims close, grasps you with his appendages, and passes his sperm packet into your genital opening.","events.30.text":"You wait in the water for a long time, but no male comes close. The eggs in your body can only rely on your own reserves.","events.31.text":"You swim through a patch of diatoms, and every mouthful is food.","events.32.text":"You keep feeding through the tail end of the bloom. As the water warms, you are sturdier than your batchmates.","events.33.text":"You have just hatched, and most of the diatoms have already sunk into deep water. You chase the scraps that remain.","events.34.text":"By the time you reach the copepodid stage, the bloom is over. You slowly work your way down from the surface in search of food.","events.35.text":"The water stays clear, and you swim a long way without finding a single diatom. Your body starts to turn transparent.","events.36.text":"The bloom finally arrives. You settle on a freshly risen patch of diatoms and eat until your belly swells.","events.37.text":"Only scattered phytoplankton are left at the surface. You live off your lipid reserves for a long time.","events.38.text":"In deep water you find a small clump of diatoms that has sunk. It is the only full meal you get this year.","events.39.text":"Your lipid reserves were built by your mother in the deep water over winter. You do not rush to feed, and drift slowly through the water.","events.40.text":"Your lipid sac is large, like a drop of oil. A fish swims over you, but you only sink slowly.","events.41.text":"Your mother did not sink down to overwinter. She laid few eggs, and few of your companions are around you.","events.42.text":"You grew up feeding at the surface. When winter came, you had not yet learned to sink into deeper water.","events.43.text":"Your batch of eggs all hatched in the same patch of water. As soon as you start to swim, you bump into another body and bounce off.","events.44.text":"You swim among copepodids your own age. A fish plunges down from above, and the whole group scatters.","events.45.text":"You leave the stable deep water and swim into an unfamiliar cold water mass. There is more food there, and more fish too.","events.46.text":"A dark shape passes overhead. You hold still for a long time, until you are sure it is not coming back.","events.47.text":"Your lipid reserves are thick. On a night ascent you run into a cluster of fish larvae and eat every one.","decisions.0.title":"Nightfall","decisions.0.body":"The light at the surface fades, and your internal rhythm urges you upward. There is plenty of algae above, but there are fish too.","decisions.0.fact":"Diel vertical migration is a trade-off between feeding and avoiding predators (review by Hays, 2003)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Rise to the surface and eat your fill","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You fill your gut in the dark surface water. A shadow sweeps past in the distance, but you cannot make it out.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in the depths","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stop several hundred meters down and wait quietly for daylight.","decisions.1.title":"Winter is coming","decisions.1.body":"Daylight shortens by the day, and the algae at the surface are declining. Your lipid sacs can carry you for a while, but the path ahead is yours to choose.","decisions.1.fact":"C5 copepodids of Calanus finmarchicus sink to deep water, enter diapause, and largely stop feeding through the winter (Hirche, 1996)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Sink to deep water and enter diapause","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"No feeding · Calendar moves on, body does not age","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You sink hundreds of meters. The winter outside no longer concerns you, and your body slows down.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and keep feeding","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"A few algae remain at the surface. You stay, and every mouthful is a race against the winter.","decisions.2.title":"The spring bloom","decisions.2.body":"Diatoms at the surface are multiplying, and the seawater has turned pale green. Your ovaries are full of eggs, and your body grows heavier.","decisions.2.fact":"Egg production in Calanus depends on food conditions, and bloom periods are usually the peak of reproduction (Mauchline, 1998)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Spawn now","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Stamina↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You release your eggs at the surface. They drift apart in the seawater and soon vanish from sight.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Eat for a while longer","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Reproduction delayed","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You eat for a few more days. The eggs are still inside you, and there is no hurry.","decisions.3.title":"A school of herring","decisions.3.body":"A school of herring rises from below, and their shadow darkens the water around you. Food drifts along the edge of the school.","decisions.3.fact":"Calanus is one of the important food sources for Atlantic herring (Mauchline, 1998)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Drift into the edge of the school","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"The current carries you to the edge of the school. Fish mouths open and close.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Sink into deep water","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You sink, and the shadow of the school slides past above you.","decisions.4.title":"Thick green bloom","decisions.4.body":"You have grown several new pairs of appendages. The water ahead is a deep green, with so many diatoms you can see the color. On the other side the water is thin, with only scattered clumps of algae.","decisions.4.fact":"Nauplii gather in bloom areas to feed; when food is scarce, their development slows and some die (Mauchline, 1998)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Swim into the thick green bloom","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You swim into the dense green water and eat a great many diatoms. Many companions are doing the same thing around you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Wait in the thin water","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You wait in the thin water for several days. Algae slowly reappear, and your body recovers a little.","decisions.5.title":"Less algae at the surface","decisions.5.body":"Over several days, most of the diatoms at the surface have been eaten, and the water has faded from green to pale. Deeper layers still hold some algae, but they are far from the light, and you do not know how far you must swim to reach them.","decisions.5.fact":"Food for Calanus nauplii varies strongly with season and depth, and phytoplankton at the surface drop quickly after a bloom (Mauchline, 1998)","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Sink to a deeper layer with more chlorophyll","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You sink and slowly feed in the layer where some algae remain. The light there is very faint.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and eat what detritus is left","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Fish↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You feed on detritus at the surface. There is little of it, but you do not have to swim deep.","decisions.6.title":"The last rise","decisions.6.body":"Your ovaries are empty, your body is light, and your swimming is slow. Night falls, and your companions begin rising toward the surface. You can follow them, or stay where you are.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Rise with them and eat one last meal","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You rise with your companions and fill up in the dark surface water. Until dawn, your companions sink away one by one.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the depths and sink quietly","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ No more feeding","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay in the depths, moving more and more slowly. The water around you is quiet.","decisions.7.title":"The water is still clear","decisions.7.body":"You hatched only a few days ago, and the surface water is still clear. Some debris has sunk toward the thermocline, but it is far from you.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Wait at the surface","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Hungry, waiting for the bloom","decisions.7.options.0.result":"The bloom does not come. You stop right at the water's surface, and your body grows thinner day by day.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Sink to find debris","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being seen↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You sink for a long time. Debris is sparse, but you manage to eat some.","decisions.8.title":"Fat reserves can build a bit longer","decisions.8.body":"Your lipid sacs are still thick. You could molt into an adult now and start spawning early, or you could feed for a few more weeks and fill out your body.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Molt into an adult now","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Lifespan↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You soon begin spawning, and each batch of eggs draws on only a little of your fat.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Feed for a few more weeks before molting","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Offspring↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You feed at the surface for a while longer, and your body grows heavy.","decisions.9.title":"Rising at night","decisions.9.body":"You are a size smaller than your batchmates. When the swarm rises at night, you are pushed to the edge of the water mass, surrounded by bodies larger than yours.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Rise with the swarm","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Stamina↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You rise with the others, and the water around you shimmers. Before dawn the swarm sinks again.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay deep and forage alone","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Risk↓ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"In deep water you find a small patch of debris. The swarm does not wait for you.","opening.title":"Mother choosing the depth to release eggs","opening.body":"You have not yet left your mother's body. She swims between the bloom at the surface and the deep water, deciding at which layer to release her eggs. Above, there are many diatoms and many fish. Below, it is quiet, but far from food.","opening.options.0.t":"Release eggs in the surface bloom","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Many fish too","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs scatter in the green surface water. You hatch and soon find diatoms to eat, but there are just as many fish in this water.","opening.options.1.t":"Release eggs in deep water","opening.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Hungry","opening.options.1.result":"The eggs sink into deep water, and you hatch where there is no light. Here there is almost no algae, but few fish come this way.","actions.forage.name":"Rise and filter-feed","actions.forage.desc":"Rise through layers rich in chlorophyll, spreading your appendages to filter diatoms into your mouth.","actions.hide.name":"Sink into deep water","actions.hide.desc":"By day, hold still several hundred meters down, where there is no light, away from fish eyes.","actions.rest.name":"Hover motionless","actions.rest.desc":"Stop in the water with your appendages barely moving, to save energy.","actions.explore.name":"Cross a water mass","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the familiar layer and swim toward a water mass with different temperature and color.","actions.mate.name":"Meet a male","actions.mate.desc":"Mate when you meet a male, then release your eggs in batches into the seawater.","actions.mate.done":"After mating with a male, you release another {n} eggs.","idle.0":"You drift slowly through the water, your body density nearly matching the seawater.","idle.1":"The water is quiet. Only a distant ship makes it tremble.","idle.2":"The water temperature changes slowly; the difference between day and night is small.","idle.3":"Sunlight reaches the surface, and the water slowly deepens from pale green.","idle.4":"A clump of debris sinks slowly past you and soon vanishes into the dark.","idle.5":"Groups of zooplankton rise at night and sink again by day.","idle.6":"You do not know which sea area you are in, only that there are many of your kind here.","idle.7":"Wind over the sea stirs the water for a while, then slowly calms.","actions.forage.text.0":"You filter-feed in the green water, your appendages reaching out and pulling back.","actions.forage.text.1":"A chain of diatoms is swept into your mouth.","actions.forage.text.2":"The surface water is a little warmer than the depths, and you linger here a while longer.","actions.hide.text.0":"There is almost no light in the deep water, and your body slowly blends into the darkness.","actions.hide.text.1":"The water temperature drops little by little, and your movements slow down.","actions.hide.text.2":"A shadow passes above, and you do not move.","actions.rest.text.0":"You hang in the water, your appendages slowly folding in.","actions.rest.text.1":"The current nudges you gently, and then you stop again.","actions.rest.text.2":"You stop thinking about what to eat next and simply wait.","actions.explore.text.0":"You cross a boundary where the water temperature changes sharply, and a chill runs through your body.","actions.explore.text.1":"The water mass ahead is a different color from the one you know.","actions.explore.text.2":"You bump into a clump of debris and pause to look at it.","actions.mate.text.0":"You swim slowly through the water, looking for a companion to approach.","actions.mate.text.1":"A male comes close and grasps you with his appendages.","actions.mate.text.2":"Your body relaxes, and the eggs begin scattering into the seawater one by one."},"dog":{"name":"Dog","tagline":"Most of the world's dogs have no owner and no name","survivalNote":"This is an average curve across mixed populations. Free-roaming street puppies die far more often than pet puppies, and street dogs usually live shorter lives; pet dogs often live around ten years.","epitaph":"Most dogs on Earth have no name, and no one has ever written an epitaph for them.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Carnivora › Canidae","size":"Size varies enormously; a common range is 10 to 30 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Origin","birth.rolls.1.label":"Breed and build","birth.rolls.2.label":"City","birth.siblingsLabel":"Pups in the same litter","birth.intro":"Your eyes are still shut. {r0}. You are {sex}, and this litter has {siblings} pups in all.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"You were born in a grass heap at the edge of a village, and no one looks after this litter","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"You were born in a litter behind a rubbish bin on a city street","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"You were born in a family's living room, and your owners have already given you a name","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a village mongrel of medium build, with short mixed-color fur, one ear up and one ear down","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"the puppy of a toy breed, under three kilograms when grown","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"the puppy of a large working breed, possibly over thirty kilograms as an adult","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"the puppy of a short-nosed breed, flat-faced and snoring in sleep","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"an old city in northern India, with packs of dogs in the streets and hot summers","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"an old residential district of a mid-sized city in northern China, bitterly cold in winter","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a suburb in the American Midwest, with a wire fence and a doghouse in the yard","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"a small apartment in Tokyo, with the owner's laundry hanging on the balcony","stages.0.name":"Newborn (eyes closed)","stages.1.name":"Puppy","stages.2.name":"Young adult","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Hit by a car","causes.0.text":"A car turns out of the intersection. The squeal of brakes and your yelp are swallowed by the wall beside the road.","causes.1.short":"Canine parvovirus","causes.1.text":"You vomit for two days straight, and your body loses water. There is no vaccine, and no one takes you to a clinic in time.","causes.2.short":"Rabies","causes.2.text":"A rabid animal bites you. A few weeks later, you begin to drool, bark at empty air, and fail to recognize anyone.","causes.3.short":"Poisoned bait","causes.3.text":"You lick a rat poison by the road. It slowly makes your body bleed from within, and you find no one who can save you.","causes.4.short":"No food in winter","causes.4.text":"Heavy snow falls for several days, and the bins are frozen. You curl up in a corner until you stop shivering.","causes.5.short":"Fight with another dog","causes.5.text":"A strange dog rushes over and bites your neck. The wound is deep, and three days later it begins to fester.","causes.6.short":"Old-age illness, euthanasia","causes.6.text":"Your kidneys stop working. The vet says you can be allowed to go peacefully. One injection, and you close your eyes.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your back legs slow down more and more. The last time you sit down, you cannot get up again.","events.0.text":"You nurse among a heap of warm bodies. Your siblings grow a little more each day.","events.1.text":"Your eyes open. The world is a blur, and only the smells are clear.","events.2.text":"Your mother carries you to another corner, then carries you back. You don't understand why.","events.3.text":"You and your siblings dig through the rubbish heap. An adult dog charges in, and you scatter.","events.4.text":"Someone tosses a sausage onto the road. You crowd together, and each of you gets only half.","events.5.text":"A motorbike passes just beside your nose. You learn to dodge traffic for the first time.","events.6.text":"Your owner takes you for your first vaccine shot. The needle is thin, and you yelp only once.","events.6.fact":"Vaccines protect against canine parvovirus, distemper, and other infectious diseases (WSAVA canine and feline vaccination guidelines; Day et al. 2016)","events.7.text":"You romp on the lawn for the first time, carrying a ball back and forth. Your owner laughs.","events.8.text":"You are hungry enough for your stomach to ache for the first time. You learn to sit by a person's hand and wait.","events.9.text":"You start pulling on the leash during walks. Your owner pulls back hard, and you think this makes no sense.","events.10.text":"You fight an older male over a dead chicken and get bitten on the neck.","events.11.text":"A catch truck rolls into the neighborhood. A net falls over you, and you are shut into a cage. The dogs in the cage are taken away one after another.","events.11.fact":"The WHO Guidelines for Dog Population Management (1990) recommend combining capture, shelter, sterilization, and adoption to manage dog numbers","events.12.text":"You are sterilized and wake with a fresh incision, newly stitched. Your owner says you will be calmer from now on.","events.13.text":"You find a bone beside a rubbish bin, and the other dogs stare at you.","events.14.text":"A woman gives you a bowl of food and keeps talking to you while you eat.","events.15.text":"You go to the park with your owner. There are many people and dogs, and you slowly learn to greet strange dogs.","events.16.text":"It is very hot. You lie on the concrete, your body burning.","events.17.text":"You see snow for the first time. A flake lands on your nose and melts at once.","events.18.text":"A cat walks along the top of a wall. You chase it for a couple of steps, then watch it disappear on the other side.","events.19.text":"Your knees are getting worse, and your owner massages them every night.","events.20.text":"When winter comes, your joints ache. You have nowhere warm to lie, so you end up on a windy patch of ground.","events.21.text":"The vet says your kidney values are dropping. Your owner rests a hand on your head and says nothing.","events.21.fact":"Larger dog breeds tend to have shorter lifespans (Michell, 1999, UK breed lifespan survey)","events.22.text":"In a knocked-over rubbish bin, you find a rib bone that someone has not finished.","events.23.text":"Someone pours leftovers into a cardboard box by the road. A pack of dogs crowds around and eats quickly.","events.24.text":"At the drain behind a restaurant, someone has just poured out a pot of bone broth. You smell it and forget everything else.","events.25.text":"A motorbike drives past the mouth of an alley. You press yourself into the corner as far as you can until it is gone.","events.26.text":"The sound of the catch truck comes from the end of the street. You press against the base of the wall and do not move at all.","events.27.text":"Sunlight comes through the window, and you stretch out on the floor in a long shadow.","events.28.text":"You lie in the shade of an old tire, eyes closed, ears still pricked for sounds.","events.29.text":"You run into a market you have never visited before. The ground is covered with strange smells.","events.30.text":"You run a long way along the riverbank and come back with your paws covered in mud.","events.31.text":"You mate with a male dog. A few weeks later, a litter of {n} puppies appears beside you.","events.32.text":"You wrestle with your littermates on the threshing ground at the edge of the village. Village kids come to tease you, and you hide behind a haystack.","events.33.text":"Everyone in the village knows you. In the evening someone leaves leftover corn cakes at the door, and you eat them and move on.","events.34.text":"You spend the whole afternoon under the haystack, ears up, listening to the village roosters and a tractor somewhere far off.","events.35.text":"You are smaller than your littermates. Your brothers and sisters crowd the teats, and you always end up pushed to the outside.","events.36.text":"Your teeth are getting loose, and your owner takes you for a dental cleaning every few months.","events.36.fact":"Periodontal disease is among the most common chronic conditions in adult small dogs (American Animal Hospital Association, AAHA Dental Care Guidelines, 2019)","events.37.text":"Your knees sometimes slip without warning. Your back legs fold under you, and you need a few steps before you can walk normally again. Your owner carries you up the stairs.","events.38.text":"You grow fast, with longer legs than your littermates. Running around the yard, you often knock over chairs.","events.39.text":"As an adult, your joints ache on rainy days. Your owner buys you a thick cushion.","events.40.text":"Your nostrils are narrow, so you have to stop and catch your breath while nursing. Your mother pushes you aside, then picks you up in her mouth and brings you back.","events.41.text":"When the weather heats up, you pant with your mouth open, your tongue nearly hanging to the ground. You cannot run far, and after a short run you are gasping badly.","events.42.text":"At night your snoring is loud, and you wake yourself several times. Your owner says your airway is narrower than other dogs'.","events.43.text":"A three-wheeled cart rolls past you, and someone in it throws a stone. You and your littermates scatter.","events.44.text":"A street stall tips out a pot of soup as it closes, and a pack of dogs rushes in. All you manage to get is a bone with a little broth clinging to it.","events.45.text":"On a summer afternoon the pavement is hot enough to fry an egg. You find a shady drainage ditch, bury your body in it, and stay perfectly still.","events.46.text":"On a winter morning, ice coats the stairwell door. You find a corner beside the radiator and curl up to sleep until morning.","events.47.text":"You hear fireworks for the first time, and you bolt into a cardboard box by the road.","events.48.text":"Someone in the complex is doing square dancing, and the music goes on for a long time. You bark along to the rhythm for a while, until your owner laughs and leads you away.","events.49.text":"Outside the wire fence, a squirrel hops from branch to branch. You throw yourself at the fence, barking until your voice goes hoarse.","events.50.text":"On the weekend your owner grills meat in the yard. You lie on the grass, waiting for bones to fall.","events.51.text":"A neighbor's child tosses a frisbee over the fence. You run to catch it and bring it back.","events.52.text":"You live in a small apartment and can only spend a little time on the balcony each day. When a subway rumbles by in the distance, you lift your head to watch.","events.53.text":"When it rains, you curl up on the cushion by the window and listen to the drops hitting the balcony railing.","events.54.text":"People in the elevator greet you, and you wag in return. Your owner says you are the best-known dog in the building.","events.55.text":"You push into an unfamiliar patch of grass for the first time, and a hare bursts out right in front of your nose. You chase it a long way and never look back.","events.56.text":"Each time you pass a new doorway, you stop first and sniff, checking that no one is waiting for you, before you walk on slowly.","events.57.text":"You drag a stick into a corner, then drag it back out and chew it over and over. Your ears stay up, listening for other sounds.","events.58.text":"You are the smallest in the litter, so feeding takes you a long time. Your siblings have had their fill and walked off, but you are still nosing at the teat.","events.59.text":"You are the strongest of the litter. When an old male comes to grab a bone, you shove him aside.","decisions.0.title":"A piece of meat by the wall","decisions.0.body":"A piece of meat with a bone has dropped by the roadside. Another stray is staring at it, and you are a single step from the meat.","decisions.0.fact":"Most dogs worldwide are free-roaming street or village dogs (Hughes & Macdonald, 2013, Biological Conservation)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Dash in and grab it","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Fight risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You run off with the bone in your mouth, and behind you comes the sound of snarling. You do not look back.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Circle around behind the bin","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Safe","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You circle behind the rubbish bin and find a fish bone that someone has already gnawed.","decisions.1.title":"A road","decisions.1.body":"On the other side of the road, in the grass, there is a pile of food, but you have to cross a busy road to reach it. The traffic light has just turned green.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Wait until all the cars pass","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safe · Slow","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You wait by the roadside for a long time. Cars pass one after another, and finally you find a gap.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Dash across while the cars are still far away","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being hit↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You reach the grass. A car horn sounds behind you and quickly fades into the distance.","decisions.2.title":"The door is open","decisions.2.body":"The door has been left open, and outside wind blows in. A cat is sunbathing in the yard, flicking its tail.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Dash out and chase it","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Fun · Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You chase the cat out of the yard and run through two streets before someone finds you and brings you back.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Wait at the door for your owner","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You crouch obediently by the door. When your owner returns, you wag your tail and leap up at them.","decisions.3.title":"First heat","decisions.3.body":"A few drops of blood fall behind you. An unfamiliar scent drifts through the air, and a male dog is barking somewhere in the distance.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Follow the scent","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You follow that smell a long way. In the days that follow, your body feels different.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay where you are. The barking of the male dog slowly fades away.","decisions.4.title":"One more chance at treatment","decisions.4.body":"Your body is growing slower and slower. The vet says there are two paths: an expensive treatment, or letting you go peacefully.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Undergo treatment","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Expensive","decisions.4.options.0.result":"After treatment, you can walk around the yard a few times again. Your owner stays with you the whole time.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Euthanasia","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"End","decisions.4.options.1.result":"The vet murmurs a few words beside you. One injection, and you fall asleep peacefully.","decisions.5.title":"A winter night","decisions.5.body":"Wind slips through the gaps in the door. Nearby, a vent gives off warm air, but someone is sleeping there, and they will chase you off.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Go to the vent","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Warm · May be chased off","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You curl up beside the vent, warm air blowing over you in waves. At dawn, someone kicks you.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Cold · Little food","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You curl up in place. The wind never stops, and when you wake, frost covers your body.","decisions.6.title":"Your owner wants you sterilized","decisions.6.body":"The vet says that after sterilization you will roam less and fight less. But after surgery, your body will be different for a while.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Agree to surgery","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"No more heat · Surgery risk","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You sleep through most of the day after surgery. A round collar sits on your neck, and when the wound itches, you keep wanting to lick it.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Refuse","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Keep breeding · More roaming","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You are the same as before. The moment you hear the door, you want to bolt out.","decisions.7.title":"The catch truck is back","decisions.7.body":"A white van has stopped at the end of the street, and people with nets and long poles are getting out. Your packmates have already started running in every direction. You still get to choose where to go.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze into a storm drain","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ · Dirty","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You squeeze into a narrow storm drain. The shouting outside turns muffled inside the concrete pipe.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Slip into a family's yard","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ · Easy to spot","decisions.7.options.1.result":"The yard gate is open. You slip in, the dog inside barks once and then ignores you. Beneath the steps you find a bowl of leftover rice.","decisions.8.title":"The dog in the park","decisions.8.body":"On the park lawn, a strange big dog runs toward you with its tail wagging. Its owner calls from far away.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Rush over to say hello","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Make a friend · Risk of conflict","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You sniff each other and then run a lap together. By the time the owner arrives, you are both still panting.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Circle past, staying close to your owner's leg","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Missed chance","decisions.8.options.1.result":"Your owner leads you down a side path. The other dog is still barking behind you, its voice growing fainter.","decisions.9.title":"Old age, still going for walks?","decisions.9.body":"Your back legs are growing slower. Your owner stands at the door holding the leash. You watch them and wonder how far you will get before you have to stop today.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Go out for your usual walk","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Health↑ · Might fall on the road","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You slowly finish your usual route. When you return, you lie at the door and pant for a long time.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Sun yourself in the yard only","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safe · Muscles slowly waste","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You lie in the yard all afternoon. The sun warms your back little by little.","decisions.10.title":"A summer afternoon","decisions.10.body":"Outside it is over thirty degrees, and there is not a patch of shade in the yard. Your owner stands in the doorway holding a water bowl.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Stay indoors with the air conditioning","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Less exercise","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You lie on the cool tiles, and your breathing slowly evens out.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Go for a walk outside with your owner","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Fun↑ Heatstroke risk↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You cannot make it half a block before you stop by the road, panting hard with your tongue hanging down.","decisions.11.title":"After a meal","decisions.11.body":"You have just eaten, and your belly is swelling. You want to vomit but cannot. Your owner has already picked up the car keys.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Go to the vet right away","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Expensive","decisions.11.options.0.result":"The vet says your stomach has twisted. After surgery, you lie down for two days before you can stand.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Wait for it to get better on its own","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You lie on the floor with your belly hard as a drum. By midnight your breathing is getting shallower and shallower.","decisions.12.title":"A pack of dogs","decisions.12.body":"A pack from the street corner heads toward you. The leader bares its teeth, and the dogs behind it stop short.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Back up against the wall","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You press your back against the wall and hold still. The leader pauses, then walks away.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Go forward and bark a few times","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Rank↑ Fight risk↑","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You bark twice and charge forward. After a flurry of snapping, you each fall back to where you were.","decisions.13.title":"The gate is open","decisions.13.body":"The yard gate is unlatched, and cars pass on the road outside. The wind pushes the gate back and forth.","decisions.13.options.0.t":"Stay in the yard","decisions.13.options.0.hint":"Safety","decisions.13.options.0.result":"You lie in the grass all afternoon, watching the cars drive past one after another.","decisions.13.options.1.t":"Run out after the cars","decisions.13.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.13.options.1.result":"You chase the cars for two blocks before a passerby stops you.","opening.title":"First crawl toward your mother","opening.body":"You have just been born, and your eyes and ears are still closed. Your littermates are squeezed into a heap. The middle is warmest, and the pups at the edge will nurse first. Your mother lies on her side, with no free time to tend to you yet.","opening.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the warmest spot at the center of the litter","opening.options.0.hint":"Warm · Slow to nurse","opening.options.0.result":"You squeeze in between your littermates. You are warm all over, but your stomach stays empty.","opening.options.1.t":"Crawl first toward your mother's nipples","opening.options.1.hint":"Get colostrum · Hard to push in from the edge","opening.options.1.result":"You find a warm nipple and suckle it with your eyes closed. The colostrum is thick and yellow, and you drink it greedily.","actions.forage.name":"Forage through the trash","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the smells from rubbish bins and restaurant back doors. You eat well, but you have to compete with other dogs, and people often chase you off.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Squeeze into a wall corner, under a bed, or into a cardboard box. Don't bark, and don't wander. You eat less, but you are harder to spot.","actions.rest.name":"Lie down and sleep","actions.rest.desc":"Find a warm place to lie down, relax your body, and sleep until you wake on your own.","actions.explore.name":"Roam around","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your home turf for farther streets or the park. You see more, but there is more danger too.","actions.mate.name":"Find a mate","actions.mate.desc":"Follow the scent of a dog in heat. Sterilized dogs cannot do this.","actions.mate.done":"A litter of puppies is born, {n} in all.","idle.0":"Somewhere far off, someone calls a name, though it is not yours.","idle.1":"A pigeon lands a short distance away. You glance at it and do not move.","idle.2":"Dusk falls, and the lights in the alley come on one by one.","idle.3":"Traffic noise drifts over in waves from far away.","idle.4":"A scent drifts in, then slowly disperses.","idle.5":"The sound of the garbage truck rumbles from the mouth of the alley.","idle.6":"At night a lit window is open, and voices drift out of it.","idle.7":"A gust of wind passes, and things around you stir slightly.","actions.forage.text.0":"You push your nose into the gap of a trash bag and drag out a bone with some meat still on it.","actions.forage.text.1":"A rat darts out from under a vegetable crate. You pounce but catch nothing.","actions.forage.text.2":"The garbage truck has not come yet, and the pile at the mouth of the alley is scattered with cabbage leaves and fish heads.","actions.hide.text.0":"You curl up in the corner and rest your head on your front paws.","actions.hide.text.1":"The voices outside fade away, and you still do not move.","actions.hide.text.2":"You stay under the bed all afternoon, your tail tucked beneath your body.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie on the sun-warmed concrete, breathing slower and slower.","actions.rest.text.1":"You dream, and your legs kick in the air a few times.","actions.rest.text.2":"You roll over, belly up, one ear still listening to the sounds outside.","actions.explore.text.0":"You sniff along an unfamiliar alley, pausing at every corner.","actions.explore.text.1":"Someone is flying a kite on the park lawn, and you chase it for a while.","actions.explore.text.2":"The stones by the river smell of fish, and you linger there for a long time.","actions.mate.text.0":"You catch an unfamiliar scent and follow it for a long time.","actions.mate.text.1":"A female dog barks far away. You stop and prick up your ears.","actions.mate.text.2":"You sniff each other's tails and circle around several times."},"duck":{"name":"Farmed duck","tagline":"A batch of meat ducks is sent to market after about seven weeks. Many of these ducks never get a chance to swim between hatching and slaughter.","survivalNote":"Mortality under intensive rearing, including early deaths after hatching, is roughly 5 to 10%. The final-day cliff on the curve is slaughter at market, not natural death, and is recorded at the end of the curve.","epitaph":"Your life lasts just over seventy days, most of it in a barn without sunlight.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Rearing system","birth.rolls.1.label":"Hatching season","birth.rolls.2.label":"Hatchery","birth.rolls.3.label":"The trip to the farm","birth.siblingsLabel":"Ducklings hatched in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are an egg from {r1}. You will grow up in {r0}. You are {sex}. About {siblings} ducklings hatched in your batch.","birth.sex.0.0":"male","birth.sex.1.0":"female","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a flat barn with only a few water basins","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a barn with a shallow pool","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a semi-free-range yard with a patch of dirt and a drainage ditch","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a winter batch, when the brooder room must be heated","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a spring batch, when the temperature is steadier","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a large commercial hatchery, turning out batches of hundreds of thousands","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a small county hatchery, hatching only a few thousand at a time","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a farm family's own hatch, from ducks they kept for breeding","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a long ride in a cardboard box, jolted about on the road","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a short two-hour ride straight to the barn","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a truck stopped for hours on the road, the box stuffy and hot","stages.0.name":"Egg (in the incubator)","stages.1.name":"Duckling (brooding stage)","stages.2.name":"Growing duck","stages.3.name":"Fattening stage","causes.0.short":"Slaughter at market","causes.0.text":"At seven weeks, the caretaker herds you and your companions into crates and transports you to the slaughterhouse. Everything follows routine, and you have no choice.","causes.1.short":"Crushed by companions under the brooder lamp","causes.1.text":"At night, one side of the brooder lamp is packed with ducklings. The ones at the bottom are pinned under the rest and cannot climb out.","causes.2.short":"Coccidiosis","causes.2.text":"Coccidia in the intestines multiply in the droppings. Within a few days you pass bloody feces and can no longer stand.","causes.3.short":"Heat stroke","causes.3.text":"The barn temperature rises above 30°C, and one fan has broken. You gasp with your beak open until at last you stop moving.","causes.4.short":"Infection after pecking by companions","causes.4.text":"A companion's peck tears the skin on your back, and the wound becomes infected in the damp litter.","causes.5.short":"Leg disorder, unable to stand","causes.5.text":"Your legs cannot keep up with the weight of your body. In the end you lie beside the feeder and never stand up again.","causes.6.short":"Killed by a weasel","causes.6.text":"A weasel squeezes through a hole beneath the barn door and bites your neck.","events.0.text":"The incubator holds a steady temperature of about 37.5°C. A candling lamp checks you once a day, and blood vessels take shape in your body one by one.","events.1.text":"A worker lifts you from the egg tray, glances at you, and sets you back down, making a mark on the record sheet.","events.2.text":"A few eggs in the batch have stopped developing. They are picked out and tossed into a waste bucket.","events.3.text":"You break out of the shell, wet all over. The first thing you see is a red brooder lamp.","events.4.text":"You peck up a grain, swallow it, and bend down for the next. Fine powder is scattered around the feed tray, and it sticks to your beak with every touch.","events.5.text":"You push into the pile of companions under the lamp. Your body warms, and your eyelids soon droop.","events.6.text":"A companion in a corner has been pinned down, and its cries soon stop. The caretaker lifts it out and drops it into a bucket.","events.7.text":"A rat scurries under the feed tray. You duck beneath the straw bedding and do not come out for a long time.","events.8.text":"You walk toward the barn door. Outside is a small patch of concrete, very bright. You stand for a while, then walk back.","events.9.text":"You walk into the shallow pool until the water covers your chest. You bury your head, shake it off, and your feathers fluff up at once.","events.10.text":"You can only wet your beak in the water basin. Without a pool, the oil on your feathers cannot be spread, and you get dirtier and dirtier.","events.11.text":"Your companions crowd around the feeder, and you can only use your beak to push them aside and make room.","events.12.text":"You lie on dry bedding, warm underneath, with your legs tucked under your body.","events.13.text":"A companion has lost a large patch of feathers from its back. It sits in a corner, and the other ducks keep away from it.","events.14.text":"The caretaker comes in for the first time carrying a scale and lifts each of you up to weigh you.","events.15.text":"The feed switches to a high-energy formula. You grow quickly, and your steps begin to waver.","events.16.text":"You take a few steps toward the pool at the other end of the barn and find the water very dirty, with droppings floating in it. You turn back.","events.17.text":"On summer nights the fans turn without stopping, and their noise drowns out everything else. You gasp with your beak open, and each breath is hard work.","events.18.text":"Coccidiosis spreads through the barn again. There are bloody droppings on the floor, and companions fall one by one.","events.19.text":"A weasel comes in through a hole beneath the barn door. It drags a duck beside you out by the neck.","events.20.text":"You shrink into a corner and tuck your head under your wing. The breathing around you is heavy, but you do not want to look up.","events.21.text":"The scale beeps, and the needle stops at 3.1 kilograms. The caretaker writes a number on a sheet of paper.","events.22.text":"The sound of a transport truck comes from the road in the distance, and every duck raises its head.","events.23.text":"The caretaker shines a flashlight around, then shuts the door and leaves. Only the heat and your companions' breathing fill the barn.","events.24.text":"At noon the barn is stuffy. The caretaker turns on an exhaust fan, and a cool draft passes over your feathers.","events.25.text":"You hatch in a commercial hatchery alongside hundreds of thousands of ducklings. The door to the brooder room stays open, and new trucks come and go every day.","events.26.text":"The caretaker comes by once a day. He watches the clock, not you. The feed troughs and water basins are set out by number.","events.27.text":"A chart on the wall lists the market date for your batch. Your name is not on it, only a number.","events.28.text":"The hatchery owner comes by every day. He touches your back to check whether you are warm enough.","events.29.text":"There is a small ditch by the yard, and the owner says a soak will clean your feathers. You spend the whole afternoon in it.","events.30.text":"The farm dog wanders around the yard. It does not bite you, but now and then it barks at the group.","events.31.text":"You were hatched by a hen. She crouched over you until the last one had broken out of its shell.","events.32.text":"You follow the hen into the yard to look for insects. Dew on the grass soaks your feet.","events.33.text":"A weasel slips through a gap in the yard wall. The hen's cries are loud, and you all dive into the haystack.","events.34.text":"The cardboard box jolts on the truck all day. You are packed together, and some ducklings are pressed underneath.","events.35.text":"You reach the brooder room with your down tangled. The caretaker dips you one by one into a water basin to soak.","events.36.text":"You remember the fright from the road. Every time a big truck passes, you shrink into a corner.","events.37.text":"You reach the brooder room before you have found your footing, and you are pushed toward the pile of companions under the lamp.","events.38.text":"When the caretaker opens the box, the first thing you see is a red lamp, with spiderwebs hanging from the ceiling.","events.39.text":"You suffered no hardship on the road, and your feathers are a little shinier than those of your batchmates.","events.40.text":"The truck stops by the roadside for hours, and the box is stuffy. The breathing of a duckling beside you grows slower and slower.","events.41.text":"When you arrive, your feet cannot hold you for a while. Several ducklings beside you never get up again.","events.42.text":"You always eat a step behind the other ducks. By the time you reach the feed, they are already eating, and you are still gasping at the side.","events.43.text":"You are the first to crawl out of the pile of companions under the lamp. You walk to the water basin and put your head in.","events.44.text":"You always stay at the very edge of the shed and pull back into a corner when you see a person. The feed pan is close, but you wait for the others to finish before you go over.","events.45.text":"You walk the length of the shed and find a small hole. Outside is a little patch of grass. You poke your head out and look for a while.","events.46.text":"You are lighter than the other ducklings in your batch, and you always lose out when fighting for feed. Sometimes the caretaker pulls you aside so you can eat a little longer.","events.47.text":"Your chest is broad, and you grow fast. When companions crowd in, you hold your ground and do not get pushed away.","decisions.0.title":"The pile under the brooder lamp","decisions.0.body":"You have just learned to stand steady. The area under the red lamp is warmest and crowded with companions. A little farther from the lamp it is emptier, and the feed tray is over there too.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Push under the lamp","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Warm · Easy to be crushed","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You squeeze in among your companions, and warmth floods over you at once. In the middle of the night, something presses down on your back.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Go eat by the edge of the lamp","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Easy to catch a chill","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You eat at the edge of the feed tray for a long time. When you look up, your body has already grown cold.","decisions.1.title":"By the pool","decisions.1.body":"There is a shallow pool in the barn, and the water is dirty. It is deep at one end and shallow at the other. At the deep end, companions are shoving one another.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Bathe in the water","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Clean · Uses up stamina","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You soak in the water for a long time. Your feathers are caked with mud, but you feel lighter.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Eat in the dry feed area","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Feathers get sticky","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You eat your fill, but your feathers start to feel sticky.","decisions.2.title":"Between the feeder and the water basins","decisions.2.body":"There is no pool in the barn, only a few water basins. The basins are crowded with companions, and the feeder is at the other end. Your feathers get dirtier by the day in the litter.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Push to the water basin","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Drink · Get squeezed","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You push to a water basin and drink a few mouthfuls. A companion steps on your back.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay by the feeder","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Getting dirtier","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You eat your fill, but more and more filth clings to your body.","decisions.3.title":"A companion pecked and wounded","decisions.3.body":"In the barn, one duck's back has been pecked open, and blood drips onto the litter. The other ducks are all pecking at its wound. You are the closest to it.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Hide on the far side of the barn","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safe · Eat less","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You make your way to the other end of the barn. There is less feed there, but nobody pecks at you.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay by the feeder","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Risky","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep your head down and eat. After a while, your own back is pecked once.","decisions.4.title":"A stuffy summer night","decisions.4.body":"The barn is nearly 35°C, and only one fan is still turning. Your beak stays open, and your breathing is quick. There is a breeze at the door, but there are mosquitoes too.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stand by the fan near the door","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Cool · Door startles you","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You move close to the door, and the breeze ruffles your feathers. A noise from outside startles you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Push to the water basin","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Drink · Crowded","decisions.4.options.1.result":"The water basin is packed with companions. You get a drink, and you are jostled a little.","decisions.5.title":"High-energy feed","decisions.5.body":"The fattening feed has more energy in it, and the more you eat, the faster you grow. But your legs have begun to ache, and you sway from side to side as you walk.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Eat every last bit","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Weight↑ Legs↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You eat every pellet in the feeder. Your body grows heavier.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Eat less, lie down more","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Less weight","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You lie on the litter and hardly move for several hours.","decisions.6.title":"The transport truck has arrived","decisions.6.body":"The barn door opens, and light spills in. The caretaker's gloves move near the feeder, and companions are caught one by one and pushed into cages. You are in a corner. You can push toward the door, or stay where you are.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Push toward the door","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Caught first","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You are caught first. The cage is crowded, and your wings are pinned against the cage door.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the corner","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Caught last","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay in the corner and are not caught until the very end.","decisions.7.title":"The caretaker's scale","decisions.7.body":"The caretaker comes in with a scale to weigh you batch by batch and take you out. You can huddle together and wait to be caught, or hide in a corner.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Huddle together and wait","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Caught↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You are picked up one by one, weighed, and put back.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Hide in a corner","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You hide in the corner until the person leaves.","decisions.8.title":"A water basin","decisions.8.body":"You are still weak, and the water basin is not far. The other ducklings crowd in to drink. Do you push in, or wait?","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Push in and drink","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Trampled↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You shove into the crowd and take a few gulps of water, and someone steps on your foot.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Wait until it is less crowded","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Thirsty","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You wait a long time, and the basin finally empties. You lie down and drink slowly.","decisions.9.title":"The hole by the shed wall","decisions.9.body":"The hole leads to a small patch of grass outside. It is bright out there, and windy. You can squeeze out, or stay in the shed.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Squeeze out","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Weasel risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You squeeze out and peck at the grass a few times. A wind blows over from outside, and you slowly work your way back in.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay in the shed","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay in the shed and gaze at the hole for a long time.","opening.title":"The egg racks at the hatchery","opening.body":"You are still a fertile egg, waiting in the cold storage of a hatchery. The workers are deciding which tier of the egg cart this batch goes on. The top tier sits near the fans, where the air is strong and temperatures change quickly. The bottom tier is against the wall, where the temperature is steadier, but eggs go into the incubator later.","opening.options.0.t":"Place on the top tier","opening.options.0.hint":"Strong draft · Temperature swings","opening.options.0.result":"You are placed on the tier closest to the fan. For a while, the temperature in the incubator rises and falls.","opening.options.1.t":"Place on the bottom tier","opening.options.1.hint":"Steady · Enters the incubator late","opening.options.1.result":"You are placed on the tier against the wall. The temperature is steady, but your development falls behind by half a day.","actions.forage.name":"Pecking feed","actions.forage.desc":"Lower your head and peck at the grain in the feeder, one piece at a time.","actions.hide.name":"Tuck into a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in a corner or under the straw bedding, with your head pulled in.","actions.rest.name":"Lie down","actions.rest.desc":"Lie on the litter, with your legs folded under your body.","actions.explore.name":"Walk to another area","actions.explore.desc":"Take a few steps toward the pool, the door, or the water basins at the other end.","idle.0":"The barn is dim, and when the lights come on, you all lift your heads together.","idle.1":"The sound of feed in the trough rings through the barn, again and again.","idle.2":"The litter is warm, and some spots have already clumped.","idle.3":"You dip your beak into the water basin, drink a few mouthfuls, and lift your head again.","idle.4":"Outside, a rooster crows once, very far away.","idle.5":"A companion rolls over beside you and pins you down so you cannot move.","idle.6":"Fine powder is in your feathers, and a shake sends it flying.","idle.7":"The hum of the fans never stops.","actions.forage.text.0":"You bend your head to feed, your beak full of crumbs.","actions.forage.text.1":"Fine powder is scattered around the feed tray, and it sticks to your beak with every touch.","actions.hide.text.0":"You crouch, trying not to draw attention to yourself.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie down, and the ground beneath you is warm.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk slowly over, stepping on the litter with each step."},"earthworm":{"name":"Earthworm","tagline":"Darwin studied these animals for much of his life and wrote an entire book about them","survivalNote":"Lifespan data for wild earthworms are scarce; adults in the lab can live several years. This curve is a rough estimate based on predation by birds and moles, drought, and waterlogging.","epitaph":"You were an earthworm. Many years from now, the soil that was once your body will be eaten again by the next generation of earthworms.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Year","birth.rolls.2.label":"Species","birth.siblingsLabel":"Worms in the same cocoon","birth.intro":"You hatch from a cocoon {r0}, less than a centimeter long, translucent, with no eyes. You are {sex}. There are {siblings} worms in the same cocoon.","birth.sex.0.0":"hermaphroditic","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"beneath a layer of rotting leaves","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"in the tilled soil of a vegetable plot","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in the moist humus layer on the forest floor","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a warm, wet year, with plenty of rain in spring and summer","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a dry year, with almost no rain in summer","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a frozen-ground year, with the soil locked in frost for a long time in winter","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a Lumbricus terrestris cocoon, buried in an old burrow under the grass","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"an Eisenia fetida cocoon, laid in a compost heap","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a cocoon at the edge of a pile of cow dung","stages.0.name":"Cocoon","stages.1.name":"Juvenile worm","stages.2.name":"Adult worm","stages.3.name":"Old worm","causes.0.short":"Pulled from its burrow by a blackbird","causes.0.text":"After the rain, a blackbird hops up beside you on the grass. It grips you in its beak and pulls you out of the soil. You try to pull back, but it is already too late.","causes.1.short":"Seized from below by a mole","causes.1.text":"The soil suddenly shakes. A mole's forepaws grab you and drag you into its tunnel.","causes.2.short":"Burrow flooded by rain","causes.2.text":"After a heavy storm, the burrow fills with water. There is no air left, and in the dark you slowly stop moving.","causes.3.short":"Soil dried out, body dehydrated","causes.3.text":"No rain has fallen for more than twenty days, and the burrow walls have hardened. You huddle at the bottom of the burrow as moisture evaporates from your body, bit by bit.","causes.4.short":"Cut by a plow blade","causes.4.text":"A tractor passes, and the plow blade turns over the whole field. You are cut in two, and the front half is still wriggling.","causes.5.short":"Pesticide seeps into the soil","causes.5.text":"Pesticide is sprayed on the farmland, and rain carries it down through the soil. You keep secreting mucus in your burrow until finally you stop.","causes.6.short":"Cocoon split open in the soil","causes.6.text":"The cocoon has lain in the soil too long. Its shell turns brittle and cracks. Before you can grow, the moisture inside drains away.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your segments harden one by one, and your body grows lighter. On your last day, you lie still at the bottom of the burrow and do not move again.","events.0.text":"The cocoon slowly hardens in the soil. Inside it, your body divides into segments, one by one.","events.1.text":"You wriggle out of one end of the cocoon. The soil outside is wetter than you expected.","events.2.text":"In the leaf litter you find a half-rotted leaf. Soil particles are mixed in with it, and you swallow everything together.","events.3.text":"You take soil in at the front and push it out at the back. The small lump that comes out is castings.","events.4.text":"You pull a scrap of rotting leaf into the burrow and digest it slowly inside.","events.5.text":"You drag a fallen leaf to the mouth of your burrow, pushing it with your tail end, and inch it down to the bottom. Then you eat it slowly.","events.6.text":"Beneath a pile of leaves you find a rotting root, and you eat it for a long time.","events.7.text":"It rains at night. You crawl out of the burrow and move slowly across the wet grass, searching for new food.","events.8.text":"You crawl across a patch of stones. The dry surface draws away much of your body's moisture.","events.9.text":"You pull back to the bottom of the burrow and plug the opening with your body segments. Footsteps thud across the ground above, one after another.","events.10.text":"You burrow deeper into the soil, where the temperature is much steadier than at the surface.","events.11.text":"You curl up in a warm burrow, hardly moving.","events.12.text":"You lie at the bottom of the burrow and move very slowly. The warmth from outside does not reach this deep.","events.13.text":"You press head to head with a companion, each of you passing sperm into the other's storage sacs.","events.14.text":"After the rain, a blackbird hops around on the grass, its eyes fixed on the ground.","events.15.text":"The sun is strong for several days, and the walls of the burrow begin to dry. You huddle at the bottom and stay still.","events.16.text":"A faint tremor runs through the soil. A mole is passing right beside your tunnel.","events.17.text":"A tractor sounds in the distance, and the ground keeps trembling.","events.18.text":"Darwin wrote in his book that earthworms carry large amounts of soil up to the surface and bury stones and old bricks beneath it.","events.18.fact":"Darwin (1881), The Formation of Vegetable Mould through the Action of Worms","events.19.text":"Your segments are growing harder, and you have not passed castings for a long time.","events.20.text":"Your burrow has been home for many years. The passages left by earlier residents crisscross beneath you, and some have caved in.","events.21.text":"The rain does not stop through spring and summer. The fallen leaves on the surface are wet and soft, and you eat plenty of rotting leaves near the mouth of the burrow.","events.22.text":"Rainwater fills the mouth of your burrow. You climb slowly up the side wall, just keeping your head above the water.","events.23.text":"It hardly rains all summer. The burrow walls grow harder by the day, and you crowd at the bottom, slowly drying out.","events.24.text":"The grass on the surface has turned yellow. You find something damp beneath the dry, hard leaves and eat it for a long time.","events.25.text":"The winter ground stays frozen for a long time. A hard crust forms over the mouth of the burrow, and you can only inch along above the frost line.","events.26.text":"The first spring rain thaws the frozen topsoil. You crawl out of your frozen burrow, still a little stiff.","events.27.text":"You dig down an old vertical burrow. The walls are firm, left behind by earlier generations of earthworms.","events.28.text":"At night you push your head out of the burrow, grip a fallen leaf with your tail end, and pull it down to the bottom.","events.29.text":"Inside the pile it is hot, and the smell of rotting cabbage leaves and cow dung is strong. You eat quickly, and your body grows fast.","events.30.text":"The pile is turned over once. A shovel flings you into the air, and you land back in the damp muck.","events.31.text":"A crust has formed on the surface of the dung, so you can only enter from the edge. Inside it is warm and moist.","events.32.text":"A heavy rain washes the dung pile apart, and the water carries you out onto the grass. You wriggle back into the soil.","events.33.text":"You poke out of the burrow earlier than the other worms in your cocoon. A tremor in the ground makes you stop, and then you move on.","events.34.text":"You pause at the mouth of the burrow every time, pulling your body back in and then easing it out again. After a few rounds, the footsteps nearby pass by.","events.35.text":"You slip into a new side tunnel, where the soil has a faintly sweet smell. You keep pushing forward until you hit a thin white root.","events.36.text":"You are smaller than several of your cocoon-mates. When you crowd at the mouth of the burrow, you are always pushed to the back and can only eat the leaf scraps the others leave behind.","events.37.text":"Your segments are thick and sturdy. When a mole drags you toward the depths of its tunnel, you press your body against the walls, and it cannot pull you out.","decisions.0.title":"The night after rain","decisions.0.body":"The rain has stopped, and earthworms are crawling everywhere on the ground. Somewhere far off, a blackbird is calling, and the humidity in the air is changing.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Crawl out to the surface","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You crawl out of the burrow and wander through the wet grass for a long time. When you return, there is a fresh wound on your body.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in the burrow","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You listen to the rain from the bottom of the burrow until morning.","decisions.1.title":"Weeks without rain","decisions.1.body":"The surface has cracked, and fallen leaves have lost their moisture. You can burrow deeper, or stay near the surface to find something to eat.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Burrow deeper","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You keep going down until the soil turns damp. All around you is darkness.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay near the surface and eat fallen leaves","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"Under the dry, hard pile of leaves, you find a few bits that have not yet rotted.","decisions.2.title":"A companion at the neighboring burrow","decisions.2.body":"A companion slides over from the neighboring burrow. You can feel the mucus on its body.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Exchange sperm","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You press head to head until your bodies separate. In the days that follow, your clitellum begins to secrete a cocoon.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Let it go","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Keep things as they are","decisions.2.options.1.result":"It slides away along the soil crack. You return to the bottom of your burrow and keep eating your leaves.","decisions.3.title":"The tractor is coming","decisions.3.body":"The field is about to be plowed, and the ground is shaking. The plow blade will cut through the shallow burrows. You can burrow to a deeper layer, or wait at the entrance.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Burrow to a deeper layer","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You crawl down the vertical burrow, and the shaking fades as you go.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay in the shallow layer","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"The plow blade passes over your head. The soil is turned over, and you are half-buried in wet mud.","decisions.4.title":"The grass at night","decisions.4.body":"You are hungry. There are many fallen leaves on the grass, but blackbirds are hopping about on it too.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Crawl out and feed","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You eat for a long time. Far off, something moves between the blades of grass.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Guard the leaves at the burrow mouth","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little food","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You drag the leaves at the mouth of your burrow inside, bit by bit, and eat them slowly.","decisions.5.title":"Winter arrives","decisions.5.body":"The soil surface begins to freeze. The frost line moves lower each day.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Burrow below the frost line","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety↑↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You burrow below the frost line. The cold wind from the surface cannot reach you here.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay near the surface","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You curl up near the entrance of your burrow, waiting for the surface to warm again.","decisions.6.title":"No rain in summer","decisions.6.body":"The fallen leaves have dried into a brittle crust, and the burrow walls have hardened. You can dig deeper along an old root, or wait for dew at night.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Dig down along an old root","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You dig down along a dead grass root. Moisture slowly seeps back into the soil.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Crawl out at night to look for dew","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You find a few drops of dew under the grass roots. On the way back to the burrow, you pick up several dry, cracked wounds.","decisions.7.title":"The frozen ground has not thawed","decisions.7.body":"The ground is as hard as stone, and the frost line presses down just above your burrow mouth. You can dig below the frost line, or stay at the edge with a layer of half-frozen leaves.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Dig below the frost line","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You keep digging down, and the soil around you is still warm. The cold wind from above cannot reach you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay with the half-frozen leaves","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"The leaves are half frozen and half soft. You tug off a few mouthfuls, and then you have no strength left to eat.","decisions.8.title":"The dung pile is heating up","decisions.8.body":"The dung pile you live in is warmed by fermentation, and the rotting cabbage leaves are piled deep. But the air at the center of the pile is getting thinner, and the humidity is shifting.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Stay in the warm dung pile","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You feed in the warm muck for a long time, and your body grows heavy.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Move to the edge of the pile","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safer · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"The edge is cooler and drier. You eat slowly and do not dare go far.","decisions.9.title":"A night heavy with dew","decisions.9.body":"Dew hangs thick on the grass blades, and the lawn glitters. Something in your body will not let you stay in the burrow all the time.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Crawl out and take a look","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You crawl over several blades of grass. On the way back to the burrow, a bird's shadow sweeps over your head.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Hold back and stay in the burrow","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay pressed against the burrow wall until the dew dries, then slowly draw back down.","opening.title":"Mother burying the cocoon","opening.body":"You are not yet born. Your mother has laid a cocoon in a moist layer of rotting leaves. Its shell is hard, and one or two young worms are inside. Where the cocoon is buried is up to the mother.","opening.options.0.t":"Bury it beneath the rotting leaves on the surface","opening.options.0.hint":"Abundant food · Birds dig for it","opening.options.0.result":"The cocoon is buried under the rotting leaves. Rain soon soaks it through.","opening.options.1.t":"Bury it in moist soil more than ten centimeters deep","opening.options.1.hint":"Safer · Less food","opening.options.1.result":"The cocoon is buried deep in damp soil. It is dark and quiet all around.","actions.forage.name":"Drag leaves into the burrow","actions.forage.desc":"Pull fallen leaves into the mouth of the burrow and eat them slowly inside.","actions.hide.name":"Retreat to the bottom of the burrow","actions.hide.desc":"Withdraw to the deepest part of the burrow and plug the opening with your body.","actions.rest.name":"Curl up and stay still","actions.rest.desc":"Stay in the burrow to reduce water loss.","actions.explore.name":"Crawl up to the surface","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the burrow at night and move across the grass, searching for food and companions.","actions.mate.name":"Mating","actions.mate.done":"You exchange sperm with a companion. A few days later, your clitellum begins to secrete the shell of a cocoon.","idle.0":"You push slowly forward between damp crumbs of soil.","idle.1":"The soil is dark and quiet. You have no eyes, but you can feel changes in the light.","idle.2":"Every segment of your body ripples, pushing the soil forward.","idle.3":"A tiny pebble is pushed out of the burrow wall and drops to the bottom.","idle.4":"Far off, grass roots slowly spread through the soil.","idle.5":"Rain seeps into the soil, the air grows thinner, and you move up a little.","idle.6":"The soil you swallowed slowly turns into castings inside your body.","idle.7":"Footsteps thud across the ground above, one after another, and then pass.","actions.forage.text.0":"You drag a fallen leaf to the mouth of the burrow.","actions.forage.text.1":"You pin it down with your body segments.","actions.forage.text.2":"Soil and leaves mix together inside your body.","actions.hide.text.0":"You retreat to the bottom of the burrow.","actions.hide.text.1":"The sounds outside are muffled by the soil.","actions.rest.text.0":"You curl up in the burrow and do not move.","actions.explore.text.0":"You crawl out of the burrow entrance.","actions.explore.text.1":"Droplets on the grass blades make your body slick."},"elephant":{"name":"African savanna elephant","tagline":"An elephant spends its life remembering waterholes and routes, and every companion that has died","survivalNote":"Age structure in African elephants comes mainly from long-term individual tracking studies such as the Amboseli study. Calf mortality is high, adult mortality is very low, and poaching and drought can reshape the whole curve","epitaph":"Herds remember waterholes, routes and the companions they have lost. The paths you walked will be remembered by someone.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Proboscidea › Elephantidae","size":"Adult females about 3 tonnes, males about 6 tonnes","birth.rolls.0.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.1.label":"Family group","birth.rolls.2.label":"Population","birth.rolls.3.label":"That year's rain","birth.siblingsLabel":"Littermates","birth.intro":"You are a newborn calf, weighing about 100 kilograms, and still shorter at the shoulder than your mother's legs. You are {sex}. {r0}, and your mother leads you into {r1}.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"The rains have just begun, and the grass has not grown tall yet","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"Mid-rains, with the waterholes full","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"Late in the dry season, as the riverbed nears cracking dry","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A family group of mothers, aunts and cousins","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A newly reshuffled group, made up almost entirely of unfamiliar females","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Amboseli in southern Kenya, where elephants have been studied for more than half a century and each one has a name","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"the Okavango Delta in Botswana, where the wetlands spread into a sea every flood season","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Kruger National Park in South Africa, with mixed grassland and bush and many tourist vehicles","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"sparse grassland on the edge of the Sahel, where villages and farms lie close together","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a year when the rains came on time and the grass grew tall","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a year when the rains were late, and the dry season dragged into the next year","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a year with too much rain, when rivers scoured their beds and mud buried much of the grass","stages.0.name":"Calf (0–1 years)","stages.1.name":"Juvenile (1–12 years)","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Elder","causes.0.short":"Surrounded by lions","causes.0.text":"A pride of lions circles your group in the night. Your mother keeps you behind her, but you are still dragged into the grass.","causes.1.short":"Dragged into the water by a crocodile","causes.1.text":"You go down to the water to drink, and a shape beneath the surface suddenly moves. It bites your trunk and pulls you under.","causes.2.short":"Died of thirst in the dry season","causes.2.text":"Two dry seasons in a row, and the waterhole dries up. You walk for three days and find only a layer of cracked mud.","causes.3.short":"Killed by poachers","causes.3.text":"A gunshot cracks from far away. You have no time to turn before you fall in the grass. The poachers want your tusks.","causes.4.short":"Poisoned bait or electric fence at the farm edge","causes.4.text":"You catch the smell of maize and step into a farm. By the next day you are lying at the edge of the field.","causes.5.short":"Infection after a fight","causes.5.text":"The tusk of another bull pierces your flank. Pus forms in the wound, and you lie by the water for several days.","causes.6.short":"Worn-out teeth, starvation","causes.6.text":"Your last set of molars wears flat, and you can no longer chew grass. Your body thins day by day until even walking is a struggle.","causes.6.fact":"An elephant goes through only six sets of molars in its life. Once the last set wears down, it often dies because it can no longer get enough food (Sukumar 2003)","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"You slowly make your way to the shade of a tree and lie down. The mothers of the herd gather around you and stay for a long time.","events.0.text":"You stand up for the first time, your hind legs not yet strong. Your mother lifts you up with her trunk.","events.1.text":"You reach your trunk toward your mother's mouth and learn to graze.","events.2.text":"An older female keeps you close. For the first time you feel the warmth of another elephant's body.","events.3.text":"You see water for the first time. You push your trunk into it and blast yourself with spray.","events.4.text":"In the grass ahead, a lion lies motionless. The herd forms a ring and keeps you in the middle.","events.5.text":"You stumble into the mud, and when you get up, you are covered in it.","events.6.text":"The rains come, and the grass grows fast. You follow your mother and eat all day, and your belly rounds out.","events.6.fact":"An adult African elephant needs roughly 40–80 kilograms of dry matter and 150–200 litres of water a day (San Diego Zoo African elephant fact page)","events.7.text":"The herd stops at a waterhole, and you all soak in it together, reluctant to climb out for a long time.","events.8.text":"You hear a low rumble from far away. It is another herd, its calls so deep that you feel them more through your body than your ears.","events.9.text":"In the second year of drought, the waterhole dries up. Your mother leads you farther away.","events.10.text":"You begin shoving against young bulls your own age. Your mother watches from the side and does not stop you.","events.11.text":"You give birth to your first calf. It weighs about 100 kilograms, and it wobbles when it tries to stand.","events.11.fact":"African elephant gestation lasts about 22 months, and births are usually 3–5 years apart (San Diego Zoo African elephant fact page)","events.12.text":"A female in the herd collapses. The others stop, touch her body softly with their trunks, and stay with her for a long time.","events.12.fact":"Elephants linger beside dying or dead companions and touch them with their trunks (Douglas-Hamilton et al. 2006)","events.13.text":"An old bull comes into musth. Black fluid seeps from his temporal glands, and everyone keeps out of his way.","events.14.text":"Poachers' footprints appear at the water's edge. Your mother leads you deeper into the scrub.","events.15.text":"A farmer beats an iron drum at the field's edge and drives you away.","events.15.fact":"Fences of beehives hung around farm fields have been found to effectively deter elephants from raiding crops (King et al. 2011, African Journal of Ecology)","events.16.text":"You leave the herd and walk alone for three days, until you find a group of bulls.","events.17.text":"Your last layer of teeth has worn to the edges. You start picking out the soft leaves on the grassland.","events.18.text":"After the rain, the water rises again. You drink a great deal, and the water slowly returns to your body.","events.19.text":"You walk more and more slowly, and the line behind you slows down too.","events.20.text":"You hide in your mother's leg shadow. A bird flies past outside, and you do not lift your head.","events.21.text":"Your mother curls her trunk around you, and you fall asleep pressed against her body.","events.22.text":"You pull a branch from an acacia with your trunk. The bitter bark makes you sneeze.","events.23.text":"On a rain-soaked meadow you find a pile of tender grass. You eat slowly, deeply satisfied.","events.24.text":"Engine noise carries from far off. You stop and stand still in the scrub, not moving until the sound fades.","events.25.text":"At the edge of a muddy pool after the rain, you stand side by side with your mother, neither of you in a hurry to leave.","events.26.text":"You walk a day away from the herd and meet another group on an unfamiliar meadow. They glance at you once and carry on.","events.27.text":"You step to the edge of a field and catch the sweet smell of maize. The wire fence is badly rusted, but you squeeze through anyway.","events.28.text":"In the eleventh month of your pregnancy, you walk more and more slowly. The mothers begin to stop beside you and wait for you to catch up.","events.29.text":"Researchers sit in jeeps at a distance, recording the name of each elephant. They come every day, and you have grown used to the vehicles.","events.29.fact":"The Amboseli elephant research project began in 1972 and is among the longest-running studies of individually known wild elephants (Cynthia Moss et al., Amboseli Elephant Research Project)","events.30.text":"Snow on Kilimanjaro appears and vanishes in the clouds far away. You lower your head to graze on the grassland, watching the mountain as you eat.","events.31.text":"Your family has lived at the foot of the mountain for decades. You remember every path to water.","events.32.text":"In flood season, you wade through water up to your chest. The calves press close to their mothers, carried along by the current.","events.33.text":"After the flood recedes, the wetland is covered with tender new grass. You lower your head and eat mouthful after mouthful.","events.34.text":"You roll in the mud hole for a long time, and the heat of your body slowly drains away.","events.35.text":"A jeep stops by the road, and a person inside raises a camera. Your mother moves you to her other side.","events.36.text":"You approach a row of high-voltage power lines for the first time and smell an unfamiliar metallic odor.","events.37.text":"You walk along one trail after another, worn by tourists' feet. Beneath a tree by the path stands a sign.","events.38.text":"Barking drifts over from the direction of the village. Your family skirts a maize field that has just been harvested, walking slowly.","events.39.text":"At the edge of the farmland, people drive you off with torches and firecrackers. You and your companions turn back and head for another grassland.","events.40.text":"You wade into a small pond. The water is murky, but you are too thirsty to care.","events.41.text":"The rains are plentiful this season. You eat as you walk, and your belly soon grows round.","events.42.text":"The grass is tall. You lower your head, and one mouthful takes a big bunch.","events.43.text":"In the rainy season water fills the riverbed, and your whole herd plays by the water for a long time.","events.44.text":"The rain is slow to come. Your mother searches for water every day, and you follow her farther than you ever have before.","events.45.text":"The grass has been baked yellow. You bite into it, and it chews like dry hay.","events.46.text":"At a pond that is nearly dry, each elephant waits its turn in line.","events.47.text":"Floodwater spreads over the plain. Your mother holds you close beside her as you wade through a stretch of water up to your shoulders.","events.48.text":"Mud has buried the grass. You scrape the silt away and search for roots that have not yet rotted.","events.49.text":"When the floodwater recedes, fresh mud covers the riverbed. You walk through it for a long time.","events.50.text":"You walk at the very front of the family group and are the first to push your trunk into an unfamiliar thicket.","events.51.text":"Before every river crossing, you test the depth with your trunk, and only then will you step forward.","events.52.text":"You curl a branch around your trunk, swinging it back and forth, and play with it until dark.","events.53.text":"You are shorter than other calves your age, and you trail at the back of the family group. Your mother often looks back, waiting for you to catch up.","events.54.text":"A young bull challenges your place, and you press him with your tusks until he backs off a few steps.","decisions.0.title":"The dried-up waterhole","decisions.0.body":"Only a last small patch of mud is left in the waterhole. Another water source lies in the distance, but there is almost no grass on the way, and it is a long walk. Your mother stands in the mud and has not yet set off. You press close to her side and wait to see which way she goes.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Stay close to your mother and follow her toward the distant water","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑ on the road","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You walk right behind your mother for five days. Several calves fall along the way, but in the end you find new water.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay at the waterhole and wait for the rain with the others","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safety · Thirst↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You dig a small hollow in the mud and wait a long time. The rain finally comes, but you have lost a great deal of weight.","decisions.1.title":"Leaving the family group","decisions.1.body":"Your body has grown. Within your mother's herd, your place is becoming awkward. In the distance, a group of bulls is moving about not far away.","decisions.1.fact":"Young male elephants usually leave their birth family group in their teens (Lee et al. 2022, Behavioral Ecology)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Leave and seek out the bull group","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Independence · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You follow the bulls away. From now on, you no longer need to wait for your mother's steps every day.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Position grows awkward","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stay with the family group. Your mother still brushes your back with her trunk now and then.","decisions.2.title":"The smell of the maize field","decisions.2.body":"The sweet smell of ripe maize drifts on the wind. The fence at the field's edge looks old, but it is still standing.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Go in and eat","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being spotted↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You eat until you are full. Someone far away blows a whistle, and then a man comes running toward you.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Go around","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety · Slightly hungry","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You skirt the field. In the distance, the farmer is still beating his drum.","decisions.3.title":"Facing down another bull","decisions.3.body":"An older bull blocks the path ahead. His ears are spread wide, and he is staring straight at you.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Charge at him","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Win position · Injury risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You lock foreheads and tusks. In the end he backs off, but your flank is torn open by his tusk.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Step aside","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety · Lose position","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You step to one side and wait until he has moved on, then find your route again.","decisions.4.title":"A gunshot in the distance","decisions.4.body":"A gunshot echoes from a hillside far away. The mothers at once spread their ears and look in the direction away from the sound.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Lead the herd away at once","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety · Slightly hungry","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You all head north together without looking back. Days later, you find water on another plain.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Turn back to find where the sound came from","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Curiosity · Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You turn toward the direction of the shot. A patch of grass there has been trampled flat, and you never go back.","decisions.5.title":"A calf stuck in the mud","decisions.5.body":"A calf in the herd is trapped in a mud pit, its legs thrashing, sinking deeper with every push. The mothers stop and look over. You are the closest to it.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Go down and help push","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Rescue it · Stamina↓↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You brace your forehead against its body. The mud is slick, and your knees sink into the sludge. At last it gets to its feet, shakes its trunk and runs back to the herd.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Call out a low rumble to the mothers","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Call for help · Safety","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You give a low rumble. Several mothers come over and, with their trunks and bodies, lift the calf out of the mud, bit by bit.","decisions.6.title":"A mother goes down","decisions.6.body":"The old female in the herd collapses in the shade of a tree. Her breathing is slow, and her eyes are half closed. The others stop and touch her body gently with their trunks.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay and keep watch beside her","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Companionship · Left behind","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You stand beside her through the whole night. By morning she has stopped moving. The herd passes her one by one, touching her with their trunks.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Keep moving with the herd","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety · Leave regret behind","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You follow the herd onward. When you look back, only a grey outline remains in the shade of the tree.","decisions.7.title":"The place at the front","decisions.7.body":"The old female is very aged now, and she walks more and more slowly. The other mothers in the herd keep looking at you, as if waiting for you to take over the route.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Take the place at the front","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ · You must remember every waterhole","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You walk at the head of the herd, remembering the location of every pool and the longest route of last year's drought.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Keep following behind","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Less effort · You do not choose the route","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You follow in the wake of the others. Someone else chooses the route; you only have to walk along.","decisions.8.title":"The last row of teeth","decisions.8.body":"Your last set of molars has worn to the edge. Tough grass is too hard to chew, while soft leaves and salty mud are far away.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Go find soft grass and salty mud","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑ on the road","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You walk a long way and find soft leaves on a salt-crusted patch of mud. Your belly slowly warms.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Slowly chew the tough grass where you are","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Hunger↑↑ Life on the line","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You chew the tough grass mouthful by mouthful. Your gums bleed from the grinding, but you do not stop.","decisions.9.title":"The third dry season","decisions.9.body":"After two dry seasons, another rainless wet season arrives. The waterholes have cracked open. Far off, a small pool remains in a riverbed, but the way there crosses a stretch with no grass.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Follow the herd to the distant riverbed","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Water · Dangerous on the road","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You walk for five days. When you reach the riverbed, the water is only knee-deep. You drink for a long time, until your belly swells.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay where you are and dig for water in the mud","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety · Little water","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You dig a pit in the mud and wait three days. Only a trickle of water seeps into the bottom.","decisions.10.title":"The herd stops waiting for you","decisions.10.body":"You are old now, and you walk slowly. The young mothers in the herd no longer wait for you. At a waterhole in the distance, a few old elephants are standing alone.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Stay with the herd","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Safety · Cannot keep up","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You keep trailing slowly behind the herd. One day, when they stop, you are still a long way back.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Walk alone toward the waterhole in the distance","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Water · Leave the protection of the herd","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You walk for a long time and reach the water. A few old elephants are there, and they glance at you once.","decisions.11.title":"The edge of the farmland","decisions.11.body":"Ahead is a field where maize has just been planted. The field smells sweet, and barking drifts from a village in the distance.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Walk in and eat a few ears of maize","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Conflict risk↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You walk into the field and eat several mouthfuls of maize. Someone far off begins to shout.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Go around the field to the grassland beyond","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Safety · Slow","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You skirt the field, cross a patch of bush, and reach another grassland.","decisions.12.title":"The waterhole is drying up","decisions.12.body":"The waterhole has shrunk to a small pool at its center, and the mud around it is cracking. The family has to decide: head for a distant river valley, or wait for rain.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Go to the river valley for water","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Water · Long walk","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You walk for three days and finally find water in the river valley. Some of the young calves can no longer keep up along the way.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Wait for rain at the waterhole","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Close · Short on water","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You wait at the waterhole, but the rain never comes. The water keeps getting lower.","decisions.13.title":"The flooded riverbed","decisions.13.body":"The floodwater has not receded. The grass is all on the far bank, and you must cross the river to reach it.","decisions.13.options.0.t":"Follow the bank around by a longer route","decisions.13.options.0.hint":"Safety · Slow","decisions.13.options.0.result":"You follow the riverbank a long way before finding a shallow place to cross.","decisions.13.options.1.t":"Wade straight across","decisions.13.options.1.hint":"Fast · Risk↑↑","decisions.13.options.1.result":"You wade into the swift current. A crocodile surfaces beneath you, and you move away from it and climb onto the far bank.","opening.title":"Standing for the first time","opening.body":"You have just been born, weighing about one hundred kilograms, your legs as limp as wet wood. Your mother hums low beside you, and the members of the family group form a circle around you. Before long, the herd will move on.","opening.options.0.t":"Struggle to your feet and keep up with the family","opening.options.0.hint":"Keeps up with the herd · Stamina↓↓","opening.options.0.result":"You fall three times, and on the fourth you finally stand. You follow your mother, your legs trembling badly, but you do not fall behind.","opening.options.1.t":"Hide under your mother's leg and nurse first","opening.options.1.hint":"Well fed · Easily left behind","opening.options.1.result":"You suckle in the shadow of your mother's leg. By the time the herd has moved far ahead, you scramble to your feet in a panic.","actions.forage.name":"Graze","actions.forage.desc":"Most of the day is spent eating. Grass, leaves, bark, anything at all, tens of kilograms in a day.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in the scrub","actions.hide.desc":"Slip deeper into thick scrub or a river valley, away from open grassland and water. You eat less, but you are harder for poachers to find.","actions.rest.name":"Rest in the shade","actions.rest.desc":"Stay still in the shade of a tree or in a mud pit and let your body cool down.","actions.explore.name":"Explore unfamiliar grassland","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the familiar routes of the family group for new pasture and water. You may find better food, or run into other herds or people.","actions.mate.name":"Gestate and give birth","actions.mate.desc":"A female carries a calf for about 22 months and gives birth to one young elephant. Only adult females take this path.","actions.mate.done":"You have given birth to {n} more calves.","idle.0":"Your mother gently rests her trunk on your back.","idle.1":"The ground trembles softly under your feet. Another herd is moving somewhere in the distance.","idle.2":"You curl a dry clump of grass into your trunk and chew it slowly.","idle.3":"The sun beats down on your back, and you spread your ears to fan yourself.","idle.4":"The family group moves very slowly, and no one is in a hurry.","idle.5":"The night is quiet, with only the rustle of grass in the wind.","idle.6":"You probe the mud ahead with your trunk to make sure there is no hole.","idle.7":"A bird lands on your back, then flies away again.","actions.forage.text.0":"You scoop up a big bundle of grass, stuff it into your mouth and chew with a crunch.","actions.forage.text.1":"You pull a branch from a tree with your trunk, and leaves rain down all over you.","actions.forage.text.2":"Your mother walks ahead, and you follow, head down, eating all the way.","actions.hide.text.0":"You hide in your mother's leg shadow, and the outside sounds grow distant.","actions.hide.text.1":"It is dark in the scrub, and you stand motionless together.","actions.hide.text.2":"Vehicles rumble on a road in the distance, and you press your bodies deeper into the bushes.","actions.rest.text.0":"You and your mother stand side by side in the shade, your ears lifting and falling together.","actions.rest.text.1":"You curl your trunk together and doze off slowly.","actions.rest.text.2":"The water in the mud pit is cool. You sink your body into it, leaving only your back above the surface.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk into a grassland you have never seen, where the grass stands a head taller than at home.","actions.explore.text.1":"On a hillside in the distance, another herd is slowly making its way down.","actions.explore.text.2":"You stop at the edge of a mud pit and catch the scent of unfamiliar water.","actions.mate.text.0":"Through the days of your pregnancy, you walk more and more slowly, and the line behind you slows with you.","actions.mate.text.1":"The mothers stop beside you and wait for you to walk on together.","actions.mate.text.2":"At night you lie down on the grass, your belly rising and falling."},"farmed_carp":{"name":"Farmed grass carp","tagline":"Most grass carp raised in a pond are seined, weighed and loaded onto a truck within a year or two","survivalNote":"Farmed grass carp are usually marketed within 1–2 years. Most deaths come from fry-stage disease and pond oxygen shortage. This curve is a rough estimate, and reaching market marks the end, not a natural lifespan","epitaph":"You grew up in a pond, were netted, weighed, poured into a plastic tub and carried to market. This is the life of a farmed grass carp.","taxon":"Chordata › Actinopterygii › Cypriniformes › Cyprinidae","size":"About 1–2 kilograms at harvest","birth.rolls.0.label":"Farming region","birth.rolls.1.label":"Farming method","birth.rolls.2.label":"Pond water source","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs from the same artificial spawning","birth.intro":"You are an egg, drifting in {r0} and slowly swelling as you take in water. You are {sex}. Your artificially spawned batch numbered {siblings} eggs, and only a few of them will grow into adults in the pond.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a fry hatchery in a farming county of the middle and lower Yangtze","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a nursery beside a fishpond in the Pearl River basin","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a fishpond in the hills of the Sichuan Basin","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a fish-farming area by a reservoir in the northeast","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a traditional polyculture pond, with grass carp, silver carp and bighead carp raised together on natural plankton","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a pond stocked with grass carp alone, fed heavily and crowded with fish","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a net-cage farm with good water flow, though wild waterbirds can land on the netting","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a pond fed by rain and river water","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a pond fed by groundwater, cooler than most","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a pond supplied by drainage from rice paddies in a closed loop","stages.0.name":"Egg (drifting hatch)","stages.1.name":"Fry (nursery pond)","stages.2.name":"Fingerling (fingerling pond)","stages.3.name":"Growing grass carp","stages.4.name":"Adult at market size","causes.0.short":"Grass carp haemorrhagic disease","causes.0.text":"Red bleeding patches appear on both sides of your body. Two or three days later, you stop swimming and just turn slowly on the pond bottom.","causes.1.short":"Oxygen shortage at dawn","causes.1.text":"Before daybreak, the oxygen in the pond is used up. You rise to the surface with the other fish and gape. You cannot sink again before the light comes.","causes.2.short":"White spot disease","causes.2.text":"Ich parasites cling to your gills and skin, and white spots spread across your body. Your breathing grows quicker and quicker.","causes.3.short":"Water mould","causes.3.text":"Water mould spreads over your egg membrane and gills. Little by little, your body is wrapped in white filaments.","causes.4.short":"Snatched by a waterbird","causes.4.text":"A cormorant dives from the bank, its beak opens and closes, and you are gone.","causes.5.short":"Oxygen shortage in transit","causes.5.text":"You are poured into a plastic tub, and the water in it grows less and less. The truck drives for a long time, and in the oxygen-starved water your breathing stops.","causes.6.short":"Harvested and sold","causes.6.text":"You are hauled onto the bank, weighed and put into a plastic tub bound for market. At a live-fish stall, someone scoops you out of the tub and drops you into a basin.","events.0.text":"The egg drifts slowly in the current, taking in water until it swells.","events.1.text":"You hatch into a fry. Your body is only a few millimetres long, and you still live on the nutrients in your yolk sac.","events.2.text":"You begin eating zooplankton in the pond, and your body grows a little at a time.","events.3.text":"The pond water turns green. The workers add fertiliser once, and plankton multiplies.","events.4.text":"Ich parasites in the nursery pond attach to your body, leaving a few small white dots.","events.5.text":"At night, the oxygen in the pond is used up quickly. You breathe near the surface until morning.","events.6.text":"You are moved into a fingerling pond, where the fish around you are much bigger. The feed is gobbled up in no time.","events.7.text":"The workers scatter pellet feed, and you rush up with your companions to snatch it.","events.8.text":"The pond water turns murky overnight, and the oxygen is low. You rise to the surface, then slowly sink again.","events.9.text":"For the first time, a seine net moves you into another pond. You are crowded among a mass of fish, and the water is cloudy.","events.10.text":"Your body is now as long as a hand. You swim faster, and your back has darkened.","events.11.text":"The workers scatter feed, and every fish in the pond surges toward the edge. You get a good mouthful.","events.12.text":"Some water plants grow along the bank. You bite off a piece; its taste is faint, but it is one of your foods.","events.13.text":"A cormorant lands at the pond's edge, then flies away. Your school dips briefly into deeper water.","events.14.text":"A heavy rain clouds the pond. The workers switch on the aerator, and a band of white foam forms on the surface.","events.15.text":"Haemorrhagic disease breaks out among the grass carp in the pond. Your flanks turn red, and for several days you eat nothing.","events.16.text":"The workers pen the fish in a net cage, weigh them and return them to the water. You struggle for a while before going still.","events.17.text":"Your weight is approaching the market standard. The workers start seining every week to check.","events.18.text":"Part of the pond water is pumped out, and you crowd into what is left.","events.19.text":"The sky above the pond is bright, and a flock of birds crosses the treetops in the distance.","events.20.text":"In the pond you catch a swarm of water fleas. They thrash in the water, and you swallow them quickly.","events.21.text":"The workers scatter pellet feed, and you push to the front for a bite.","events.22.text":"Wind blows the water plants at the bank into the pond, and a leaf drifts up in front of you. You take a bite.","events.23.text":"The workers scatter feed, and you and your companions compete for it. The water is murky, and you nearly bump into the pond wall.","events.24.text":"You hold still in the shade of the water plants. The shadow of a cormorant skims over the surface and then is gone.","events.25.text":"You hide among the water plants. A layer of duckweed has settled on the leaves, and your scales itch a little.","events.26.text":"You stop on the pond bottom while the sediment stirred up around you drifts back down slowly.","events.27.text":"You swim halfway across the pond and meet another group of fish. Their scales are a slightly different colour.","events.28.text":"You swim to the far end of the pond, where the water is clearer, but the feed is far scarcer.","events.29.text":"You glide into the deep water of the pond, where it is cooler than at the edge and holds less oxygen.","events.30.text":"The silver carp and bighead carp in the pond clean up the plankton, and the water stays fairly steady.","events.31.text":"Grass carp and common carp churn the mud at the bottom together. The water clouds for a while, and you have to stay near the surface.","events.32.text":"The pond is full of grass carp. When feed is thrown in, they rush for it together, and you grow quickly.","events.33.text":"The pond is crowded, and oxygen is used up fast at night. All of you rise to the surface.","events.34.text":"In the net cage you swim back and forth, and the water passes through the mesh, clean.","events.35.text":"A waterbird lands on top of the net. You sink inside the cage and dodge its shadow.","events.36.text":"After heavy rain, river water rushes into the pond, bringing many zooplankton. You eat your fill at the surface.","events.37.text":"River water brings in other fry, which mix with you, and the pond suddenly gets crowded.","events.38.text":"The water is pumped up from underground, so it is cool. Your movements slow down.","events.39.text":"In a pond fed by well water, the water is very clear, but there is little zooplankton, so you have to rely on feed.","events.40.text":"Drainage from the rice paddies flows into the pond, and the water turns a pale brown. Your gills feel uncomfortable.","events.41.text":"Many zooplankton grow in the paddy runoff. You eat your fill, and your color darkens.","events.42.text":"You are a size smaller than the other fry in the pond. Each time feed is thrown in, you cannot get to the front.","events.43.text":"You are sturdier than the rest of your batch. When the net is hauled, you struggle free several times, but in the end you stay in the pond.","events.44.text":"You are the first to swim to the feed, and you get a bruise from being bumped. You still get the first mouthful.","events.45.text":"The shadow of a cormorant skims across the water. You have barely stopped before you slip into the shade of the water plants.","events.46.text":"You hover by the aquatic plants at the edge of the pond and find a locust that has fallen into the water. You eat it.","decisions.0.title":"Surfacing before dawn","decisions.0.body":"The oxygen in the pond is used up before daybreak. You rise to the surface with the other fish in the pond, gaping, and wait for the light.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Float at the surface and wait for daylight","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You keep breathing at the surface until the sun rises. Several fish around you never wake up.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Sink to the bottom to conserve energy","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Hypoxia risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You sink to the pond bottom and slowly stop moving. The water is cold, and there is very little oxygen.","decisions.1.title":"Red patches on the body","decisions.1.body":"Some fish have red patches on their bodies, and a few have stopped swimming. The workers are feeding more often and checking the water quality.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Keep eating to keep your strength","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You keep eating pellets. Within days the red patches spread, and your body feels heavier and heavier.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Leave the crowd and hide by the water plants","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Slightly hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You hide by the water plants, away from the crowd. The water there is clearer.","decisions.2.title":"The sound of feeding","decisions.2.body":"The workers' boat drifts up to the bank, and the splash of feed hitting the water crackles across the surface. The pellets are gone in moments.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Push to the front and grab","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You shove your way to the front of the feed and eat a great deal. The fish around you jostle you too.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Eat slowly from the back","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You eat what is left at the back. The pellets sink slowly, and there is still enough for you.","decisions.3.title":"Seining","decisions.3.body":"The workers begin to seine. The net gathers slowly along the pond bottom, and the fish ready for market will be picked out and put into plastic tubs.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Crowd toward the middle of the school as the net closes","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You push into the middle of the school as the net tightens step by step. You are hauled onto the bank.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hide in the deep water by the bank","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You hide in the deep water by the bank. The net sweeps past you and, for a moment, does not catch you.","decisions.4.title":"By the weeds or mid-pond","decisions.4.body":"You can now eat zooplankton. Near the bank, among the water plants, plankton is plentiful, but water mould spreads in the still water there. In mid-pond the current runs fast and food is scarce, but the water is cleaner.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stay near the water plants by the bank","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Water mould↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You swim to the water plants, where zooplankton is abundant. Water mould threads slowly spread across the leaves.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay in mid-pond","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Steady · Slightly hungry","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You stay in mid-pond, where the current carries you along. Food is scarce, but your gills stay clean.","decisions.5.title":"Silt on the pond bottom","decisions.5.body":"The silt on the pond bottom keeps building up. In summer the water warms, dissolved oxygen shifts faster, and at dusk the oxygen drops especially quickly.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Rise to the surface to breathe","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Hypoxia risk↓ Safety↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You rise to the surface and gape to take in air. The wind ruffles the water, and your body rocks with the waves.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay on the bottom and move as little as possible","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Hypoxia risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You stay on the bottom and keep still as much as you can. The water there barely moves, but you can last the night.","decisions.6.title":"Nearing market size","decisions.6.body":"Your weight is approaching the market standard. The workers have not seined yet, and the feed in the pond is running low. You could keep eating and try to grow heavier, but the fish in the pond are getting more crowded.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Keep eating and try to grow heavier","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Disease risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You keep eating, and your weight creeps up. A few fish at the edge show red patches, and you carefully keep away from them.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Save your strength and stay on the bottom","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Steady · Slightly hungry","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay on the pond bottom and wait for the workers to come and seine.","decisions.7.title":"The net has a tear","decisions.7.body":"A corner of the net cage was torn by waves, and a gap has opened in the mesh. Your body could fit through it, but the water outside is swift.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Swim out through the gap, back into the pond","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Freedom · Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You swim out through the gap. The water is swift, and a waterbird is watching you from above.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the cage and wait for repairs","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety · Less food","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay in the cage. The repairman comes late, and you wait inside for several days.","decisions.8.title":"The pond is too crowded","decisions.8.body":"Grass carp fill the pond with barely any room to move. At night oxygen is being used up faster than last year. A worker has set up an aerator at the edge of the pond, but it is not switched on yet.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Rise to the top and wait for the aerator","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety · Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You float near the surface. Far off, the aerator finally starts to hum.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Sink to the bottom to save energy","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Low-oxygen risk↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You sink to the bottom, and the water grows stuffier and stuffier.","decisions.9.title":"Rice paddy drainage","decisions.9.body":"Water from the rice paddies is draining into the pond, and there is a faint chemical smell. The fish by the edge have all risen to the top.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Swim with the current toward the middle of the pond","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You swim to the middle of the pond, and the current carries you along. The smell fades a little.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay near the inlet until the water clears","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safer · Slightly hungry","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay beside the inlet, and the water slowly clears.","opening.title":"Currents in the hatchery","opening.body":"You are still an egg, drifting in the water of a nursery pond. Near the aerator the current runs smoothly and the oxygen is plentiful. At the still water by the bank it is quiet, but water mould is more likely to take hold there.","opening.options.0.t":"Drift with the current toward the aerator","opening.options.0.hint":"Oxygen-rich · Many fry","opening.options.0.result":"The current stirred up by the aerator pushes you into the pond. The water is full of oxygen, and you slowly swell as you take in water.","opening.options.1.t":"Drift into the still water by the bank","opening.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Lots of water mould","opening.options.1.result":"You come to rest in the still water by the bank. No one crowds you there, but water mould spreads quickly in that spot.","actions.forage.name":"Eat feed and water plants","actions.forage.desc":"Look for pellet feed, zooplankton or leaves by the water plants in the pond.","actions.hide.name":"Hide among the water plants","actions.hide.desc":"Stay in the shade of the water plants, away from the crowd and the waterbirds.","actions.rest.name":"Sink to the pond bottom","actions.rest.desc":"Sink to the bottom, drift slowly or hold still to save energy.","actions.explore.name":"Swim to the far end of the pond","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the crowded school and go see the other side of the pond.","idle.0":"The pond water is a pale green, and you can see only a short way into it.","idle.1":"A layer of sediment lies on the pond bottom, and now and then the current stirs it up.","idle.2":"The aerator hums in one corner of the pond, and the current pushes the duckweed toward the bank.","idle.3":"Workers walk along the bank, and their shadows fall across the water.","idle.4":"The current carries you along slowly. You drift with it for a while, then stop.","idle.5":"When the water is cold, everything slows down. You simply stay where you are.","idle.6":"The grass on the bank rustles in the wind, and a few leaves drop into the water.","idle.7":"You do not know where the edge of this water is, only that it is large.","actions.forage.text.0":"You bite a leaf off a water plant. It tastes faint.","actions.forage.text.1":"Pellets scattered by the workers sink slowly through the water, and you swim over for a bite.","actions.forage.text.2":"When zooplankton is plentiful, you swallow it mouthful by mouthful.","actions.hide.text.0":"The water plants wrap around you, and the current slows.","actions.hide.text.1":"The shadow of a cormorant skims across the surface, and you hold perfectly still.","actions.hide.text.2":"A layer of duckweed has settled on the leaves, and you shift your body a little.","actions.rest.text.0":"You rest on the pond bottom, your fins fluttering gently.","actions.rest.text.1":"Sediment on the pond bottom is stirred up, then slowly settles again.","actions.rest.text.2":"When the water is cold, you barely move.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim halfway across the pond and meet another group of unfamiliar fish.","actions.explore.text.1":"The water at the far end of the pond is clearer, but the feed is far scarcer.","actions.explore.text.2":"You glide into the deep water of the pond, where it is cooler than at the edge."},"farmed_shrimp":{"name":"Farmed penaeid shrimp","tagline":"Most farmed shrimp in the world belong to this kind","survivalNote":"Mortality is high during the larval stage, and during grow-out it depends on disease and water quality. This curve is a rough estimate. Farmed shrimp are usually harvested within 3–4 months, and harvest marks the end, not a natural lifespan","epitaph":"You lived in a pond for about four months. On the day of harvest, you were poured onto ice, packed into boxes and shipped to market.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Crustacea › Malacostraca › Decapoda › Penaeidae","size":"About 10–15 centimetres in length at harvest","birth.rolls.0.label":"Farming region","birth.rolls.1.label":"Farming method","birth.rolls.2.label":"Seed source","birth.siblingsLabel":"Fertilised eggs from the same hatchery tank","birth.intro":"You are a fertilised egg, hatching in {r0}. You are {sex}. This batch holds {siblings} eggs, and the hatchery sorts by batch, so only some will be moved into the grow-out ponds.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a hatchery on the coast of Southeast Asia","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a hatchery on the southern coast of China","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a hatchery on the coast of Ecuador","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a hatchery on the Pacific coast of Mexico","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a traditional extensive earth pond, stocked lightly, where the shrimp live on algae and bottom-dwelling animals","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a semi-intensive pond, fed daily and water changed on a schedule, with sparse stocking","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a high-density intensive pond, kept oxygenated by aerators and daily feeding, with water quality checked every day","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"a factory greenhouse biofloc tank, rarely changed, with bacteria breaking down the waste","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"ordinary broodstock, with breeders chosen for growth rate","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a disease-selected line, with breeders chosen for resistance to white spot disease","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"postlarvae from specific-pathogen-free (SPF) broodstock, from a hatchery whose water is strictly disinfected","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"postlarvae from wild-caught broodstock, with few records kept by the hatchery","stages.0.name":"Egg (hatchery tank)","stages.1.name":"Nauplius and zoea larvae","stages.2.name":"Postlarva (PL)","stages.3.name":"Juvenile shrimp in the grow-out pond","stages.4.name":"Adult shrimp nearing harvest","causes.0.short":"White spot syndrome","causes.0.text":"White spots appear on your shell. Within days, the shrimp in your pond stop feeding one after another, and the water at the edge grows still.","causes.1.short":"Acute hepatopancreatic necrosis","causes.1.text":"Your hepatopancreas shrivels and discolours, and you stop eating. In a pond, this disease tends to spread quickly.","causes.2.short":"Oxygen shortage at dawn","causes.2.text":"Before dawn, the dissolved oxygen in the pond falls very low. The aerator hums in the distance, but not fast enough. You do not last until daylight.","causes.3.short":"Bitten by a pond mate while moulting","causes.3.text":"Your body is still soft after moulting. A shrimp from your pond comes close and bites through your new shell.","causes.4.short":"Died during the larval stage","causes.4.text":"The water quality in the hatchery tank fails, and bacteria spread through your group. You do not survive to the day you would have been moved to the grow-out pond.","causes.5.short":"Snatched by a waterbird","causes.5.text":"A waterbird dives from the bank. The shrimp in the pond sink together into deeper water, and you do not sink quickly enough.","causes.6.short":"Dead after chilling at harvest","causes.6.text":"On harvest day, you are scooped out of the pond and tipped into a box full of crushed ice. The cold quickly brings your movements to a stop.","events.0.text":"The egg floats in the hatchery tank, where the water is kept above twenty degrees Celsius.","events.1.text":"You hatch into a nauplius. Your body is tiny, and you live on the nutrients in your yolk sac.","events.2.text":"You begin eating the microalgae and feed the hatchery provides, and your body grows a little more each day.","events.3.text":"You moult once. The hatchery workers check the water quality and your larvae's activity every day.","events.4.text":"The water in the tank turns a different colour, and the hatchery adds a disinfectant once. You feel unwell for a while, but you survive.","events.5.text":"The hatchery stage usually passes through nauplius, zoea and mysis larval periods. You are in one of them now.","events.5.fact":"Penaeid shrimp pass through several larval stages in the hatchery, and disease and water management are critical at this point (Lightner 1996, A Handbook of Shrimp Pathology and Diagnostic Procedures)","events.6.text":"You become a postlarva (PL), already close in shape to an adult shrimp. The hatchery packs you by batch into boxes and ships you to a farm.","events.7.text":"You are released into a grow-out pond. The water is a pale green, and there are several feeding points along the edge.","events.8.text":"The water temperature in the pond drops suddenly by several degrees. You stop and eat very little.","events.9.text":"You have eaten your fill. Pellets drift slowly down around you.","events.10.text":"Before dawn, the dissolved oxygen in the pond falls. The aerator hums on the far side, and you swim up near the surface.","events.11.text":"You moult for the first time as a juvenile.Once the old shell comes off, your body is soft for several hours and you cannot eat.","events.11.fact":"During the soft-shell stage after moulting, a shrimp's body is fragile and easily injured or attacked by conspecifics (Albalat et al. 2022, Frontiers in Marine Science)","events.12.text":"Leftover feed piles up in front of the feeding tray. You and your companions crowd together to eat, with so many shrimp around you that there is barely any room.","events.13.text":"Organic matter builds up on the pond bottom, and the water quality worsens. Your gills feel uncomfortable.","events.14.text":"The shrimp in the pond vary widely in size, and the bigger ones take a larger share of the feed.","events.15.text":"The shadow of a waterbird slides across the edge of the pond, and the shrimp suddenly sink into deeper water.","events.16.text":"The sound of the aerator does not stop all night. You crawl slowly along the pond bottom.","events.17.text":"Your shell hardens, and your body is nearing harvest size.","events.18.text":"A few shrimp in the pond stop feeding. Their hepatopancreas has paled, and you do not know whether you are next.","events.19.text":"Harvest is drawing near. Every day the workers measure the shrimp at the pond's edge.","events.20.text":"Water quality has been good these past few days, and you have been eating well. Your body grows a little sturdier each day.","events.21.text":"The workers drain part of the pond, and the current carries you into another pond.","events.22.text":"On the pond bottom you find a piece of feed that has sunk, and you take a bite.","events.23.text":"Pellets are scattered across the pond bottom, and you compete with several shrimp for them. The water is murky, and you bump into another shrimp.","events.24.text":"You turn up a leftover piece of bait on the bottom. It has already blackened, but you eat it anyway.","events.25.text":"The current pushes you into the shallows near the edge, where the water runs warmer.","events.26.text":"You hide in a corner of the pond bottom. A shrimp steps on you, and you do not move.","events.27.text":"There is a lot of sediment on the bottom, and a layer of grime settles over your body.","events.28.text":"The aerator stops for a few minutes, and the water goes still. You rest on the bottom and wait for it to start up again.","events.29.text":"You swim along the wall to the other side. The water is a little clearer, and a small heap of feed has not yet been found by anyone.","events.30.text":"A waterbird lands on the railing at the pond's edge. You swim back to the middle, while the shrimp around you are all drifting back too.","events.31.text":"A water pipe at the pond's edge bursts, and the flow rushes in from there and pushes you across half the pond.","events.32.text":"The pond is large and shallow. The bottom is covered with algae and small bottom-dwelling animals, and you crawl along as you eat.","events.33.text":"No feed has been thrown in for a long time. The shrimp in the pond are uneven in size, and you have to compete with the larger ones for food.","events.34.text":"A run of torrential rain dilutes the pond, and the salinity drops. It takes you several days to adjust.","events.35.text":"Workers scatter feed on schedule. The pellets sink slowly through the water, and you and the other shrimp gather around them.","events.36.text":"Workers drain part of the old water and refill with fresh water. The temperature changes, and you pause for a while before you recover.","events.37.text":"The aerator turns day and night, and its sound on the surface never stops. Your gills are full of fresh water.","events.38.text":"The pond is packed with shrimp, and you nearly touch every one around you. The shoving underwater is clearly audible.","events.39.text":"The power goes out at night, and the aerator stops for twenty minutes. All of you rise to the surface and fight for oxygen.","events.40.text":"Flocs drift in the water, clumps of bacteria and organic matter. Your walking legs grab them, and your mouth fills with a fermented taste.","events.41.text":"The pond has not been drained for months, and ammonia slowly rises. Your antennae start to tingle, so you stay near the top.","events.42.text":"Workers draw the sludge from the bottom of the pond with a hose, and the water suddenly turns cloudy.","events.43.text":"Water quality in the nursery tank gets worse by the day. The larvae around you stop moving one after another. You are still moving.","events.44.text":"You grow faster than most shrimp in the pond, and your body gets heavier day by day.","events.45.text":"An illness spreads through the pond. Several shrimp beside you stop eating, and your body starts to turn white as well.","events.46.text":"White spots appear across the pond, but your shell has none. You keep eating, and your body stays steady.","events.47.text":"The hatchery ran a rapid test before stocking, and every batch came back negative. Workers carry the boxes gently onto the truck.","events.48.text":"Shrimp in the next pond have collapsed, but only a few are lost from yours.","events.49.text":"The hatchery water is disinfected with ozone and UV light, so there is almost no foam on the surface. Your body is clean and transparent.","events.50.text":"At stocking, workers check every box of juveniles before sealing it.","events.51.text":"The shrimp in this pond have grown evenly. The workers say this batch has a higher survival rate than last year's.","events.52.text":"The juveniles in your batch vary in size, and the hatchery record notes only a single collection date.","events.53.text":"The postlarvae are packed tightly in the transport box, and several beside you stop moving before they reach the pond.","events.54.text":"You grow slowly, and the shrimp in the pond keep pushing you into the shallow water at the edge.","events.55.text":"You are a size smaller than your batchmates. You always stay in a corner of the nursery tank, and it is hard to get your share of feed.","events.56.text":"Your color is darker than your batchmates', and your body is sturdy. When you swim, the shrimp around you make way.","events.57.text":"You are always the first to swim to the feeding tray, and you pick up several bruises from collisions.","events.58.text":"When the current changes, you retreat into a corner at the bottom. You eat less than the others and are rarely bumped.","events.59.text":"You keep circling near the pond wall and find a silt corner no one visits, with a small pile of uneaten feed.","decisions.0.title":"When to moult","decisions.0.body":"Your old shell begins to split. Moulting leaves your body soft, you cannot eat for several hours, and you are vulnerable to attacks from your own kind.","decisions.0.fact":"After moulting, penaeid shrimp pass through a soft-shell period in which their bodies are fragile and easily injured (Albalat et al. 2022, Frontiers in Marine Science)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Moult now","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Stamina↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You wriggle out of the old shell. The new one is still soft, and you slowly make your way to a corner at the pond's edge.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Eat first, then moult","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Slower","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You eat for a while, and once your body has enough reserves, you slowly moult.","decisions.1.title":"The feeding tray","decisions.1.body":"The feeding tray is packed with shrimp. Those that get to the feed grow fast, but crowding together also makes it easier for them to injure one another.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Push in and grab","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You squeeze to the front of the feeding tray and grab a large mouthful. The shrimp around you are shoving too.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Back off to the edge and wait","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Slightly hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You back off to the edge of the pond. By the time the feed has spread out, it has already sunk far away.","decisions.2.title":"Hypoxia before dawn","decisions.2.body":"The dissolved oxygen in the pond drops before dawn, and you feel short of breath. The aerator hums in the distance, but not fast enough.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Swim up to the surface, toward the aerated current","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You move close to the current stirred by the aerator. There is a bit more oxygen, but the light on the surface is harsh.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Sink to the bottom and breathe slowly","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Hypoxia risk↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You sink to the bottom and swim less. The low-oxygen water slides slowly past your gills.","decisions.3.title":"White spots","decisions.3.body":"A few white spots have appeared on your shell. Several shrimp in your pond have stopped eating, and the workers at the edge are speeding up water changes.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Keep eating and wait","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You keep eating, and within days the spots spread.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hide in the shallows by the edge","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Somewhat safer · Hungry","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You hide in the shallows by the edge. The water there is a little warmer, and there are fewer shrimp around.","decisions.4.title":"Just released into the pond","decisions.4.body":"You have been released into the grow-out pond, where the water is pale green. The temperature has suddenly dropped by several degrees. The shrimp around you crowd into the corners, while the central feeding tray keeps releasing pellets.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Crowd into a corner","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You squeeze into a corner. The water there is slow, and there is little feed, but fewer shrimp are around you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Follow the shrimp in the middle to the feeding tray","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Crowded","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You swim toward the feeding tray and catch a few pellets. Many shrimp are shoving, and a few times you are knocked off course.","decisions.5.title":"Water flow during an exchange","decisions.5.body":"The workers open a drain at one corner of the pond. The exchange current suddenly stirs up the sediment on the bottom, and the new water is at a temperature you are not used to.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Swim into the new current","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stress↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"The current carries you into the new water. The temperature change leaves you uneasy for a while, but the new water holds more dissolved oxygen.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Hide in a corner of the old water","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Steady · Poor water quality","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You hide in a corner of the old water. Its quality keeps getting worse, but you are not swept into the eddies of the new water.","decisions.6.title":"The last days before harvest","decisions.6.body":"A few shrimp in the pond have stopped feeding. The workers begin to drain water and measure sizes, and the feed at the edge has been switched to a better pellet. You could eat for a few more days, or hide on the bottom and wait.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Keep eating and try to grow bigger","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Disease risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You keep eating, and your body grows a little more. The few shrimp at the edge that stopped eating do not appear at the feeding tray again.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Hide on the bottom and wait","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You hide on the bottom. The feed above is eaten away bit by bit, and your body slowly grows lighter.","decisions.7.title":"The aerator has stopped","decisions.7.body":"A breaker tripped in the night, and the aerator stopped for twenty minutes. Oxygen in the water is falling, and several shrimp have already risen to the surface.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Stay by the aerator's outflow","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Oxygen↑ Stamina↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You stay close to the bubbles from the outflow until the aerator starts up again.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Sink to the bottom and stay still","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Saves energy · Low-oxygen risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You sink to the bottom and stay still. The water grows stuffier, and you do not make it to dawn.","decisions.8.title":"The floc is getting thick","decisions.8.body":"The flocs in the water are growing thick and turning gray. A worker has fitted a suction hose at the pond's edge, ready to draw off part of it.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Stay and feed in the flocs","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You eat your fill among the flocs, but the smell of ammonia grows stronger.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Hide in clean water at the pond's edge","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ A bit hungry","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You swim to the edge, where the water is still clean, but there is little to eat.","decisions.9.title":"The shipping box","decisions.9.body":"The shipping boxes have been sitting on the dock in the sun, and the water inside has warmed. You are crowded at the bottom, and the water above is cloudy.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Crowd into the cooler water at the bottom","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safer","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You push down to the bottom, where the water is cooler. The shrimp beside you still have not moved.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Climb to the top, near the air vent","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Oxygen↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You move close to the air vent. The airflow is weak, but you can still breathe.","decisions.10.title":"A gap in front of the feed tray","decisions.10.body":"A patch in front of the feed tray suddenly clears. A bird's shadow is at the pond's edge, and the shrimp are all sinking toward the deep water.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Rush to the feed tray and grab the first mouthful","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You snatch a mouthful of feed and nearly get covered by the bird's shadow.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Back away slowly along the bottom","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You inch backward along the bottom until the bird flies away.","opening.title":"Your place in the hatchery tank","opening.body":"You are still a fertilised egg, floating in the hatchery tank. On one side of the tank, an aeration pipe sends up fine bubbles. On the other side is the feeding spot, and the middle is crowded with eggs and larvae from the same batch.","opening.options.0.t":"Move close to the aeration pipe","opening.options.0.hint":"Oxygen-rich · Fast current","opening.options.0.result":"The current from the aeration pipe pushes you all in the same direction. The water is full of oxygen.","opening.options.1.t":"Move close to the feeding spot","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Crowded","opening.options.1.result":"Feed is scattered on this side every day. You quickly get a taste of microalgae, but the eggs and larvae crowded here also foul the water faster than elsewhere.","actions.forage.name":"Forage on the bottom","actions.forage.desc":"Search the pond bottom for leftover feed pellets and settled organic matter.","actions.hide.name":"Stay on the bottom","actions.hide.desc":"Stay in a corner of the pond bottom, away from the surface, the feeding spot and the shrimp crowd.","actions.rest.name":"Hold still","actions.rest.desc":"Stop on the bottom, let your antennae sway gently, and conserve energy.","actions.explore.name":"Swim along the wall","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the crowd in the middle and swim along the wall toward the other end, to see what else the pond has.","idle.0":"The pond water is very pale, and light filters down through it, its brightness changing over time.","idle.1":"Workers walk along the edge every day, and their shadows fall across the water.","idle.2":"The concrete at the pond's edge is clean, but there are many unseen bacteria under the water.","idle.3":"The water slowly circulates, pushing feed and sediment to one side together.","idle.4":"Individuals just like you crowd around, leaving almost no gaps between one another.","idle.5":"In the sediment on the bottom, you can sometimes see a pellet that was not finished.","idle.6":"The water temperature changes very slowly, so slowly you can barely feel it.","idle.7":"At night the hatchery lights stay on, and a patch of white light reflects off the surface.","actions.forage.text.0":"You search the pond bottom for leftover feed pellets.","actions.forage.text.1":"A clump of sunken feed is caught by your walking legs.","actions.forage.text.2":"There is some organic matter on the bottom, and you push through the crowd of shrimp to reach it.","actions.hide.text.0":"You lie still against the pond bottom, with only water flowing past.","actions.hide.text.1":"The aerator's current stirs up the sediment, and you slowly return to your corner.","actions.hide.text.2":"A shrimp crawls past you, and you do not move.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stop on the bottom, your antennae swaying gently.","actions.rest.text.1":"The pond is very quiet, and you can barely sense the passing of time.","actions.rest.text.2":"Sediment settles slowly onto your body, and you do not bother to shake it off.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the wall, and the water grows shallower.","actions.explore.text.1":"The water on the far side of the pond looks different, and there is less feed.","actions.explore.text.2":"The shadow of a waterbird slides across the edge of the pond, and you turn back."},"frog":{"name":"Dark-spotted frog","tagline":"In spring, every male frog in the paddy fields is calling.","survivalNote":"Lifespan data for wild frogs are scarce. This curve is a rough, unverified estimate, based on the high mortality of eggs and tadpoles and an annual adult mortality of roughly 40–60%","epitaph":"You leave no name, only a pond full of tadpoles. Next spring they will start calling from the same water's edge.","taxon":"Chordata › Amphibia › Anura › Ranidae","size":"Adults about 5–9 centimetres long","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Rainfall this year","birth.rolls.2.label":"Frogs in this water","birth.siblingsLabel":"Same egg mass","birth.intro":"You are an egg, in {r0}, wrapped in a clear jelly along with your clutch. You are {sex}. There are {siblings} others in the same egg mass as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a shallow puddle of rainwater at the edge of a paddy field","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a clump of water plants at the edge of a pond","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a shallow ditch on the bank of a reservoir","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A wet year, with deep water in the pond and an early spring","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"An ordinary year","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"A dry year with little spring rain, and the pond shrinks well below its usual level","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"A warm winter brings an early spring and early hatching, but a late cold snap drops the water temperature","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Only dark-spotted frogs in this water","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Water shared with another pond frog, whose calls mix with yours","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A pond newly stocked with fish fry","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Tadpole","stages.2.name":"Young frog","stages.3.name":"Adult frog","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Snatched by a heron","causes.0.text":"The long bill of a little egret stabs down from above the water. You leap into the water, but its bill is faster than your jump.","causes.1.short":"Swallowed by a snake","causes.1.text":"A grass snake pokes its head out of the grass. It bites your hind leg first, then slowly works you toward its mouth.","causes.2.short":"Eaten by a carp","causes.2.text":"A carp swims up from the pond bottom, and its mouth closes on you before you can dodge.","causes.3.short":"Egg mass eaten by aquatic insect larvae","causes.3.text":"A water beetle larva bites from the edge of the egg mass, and the eggs in the jelly are swallowed one by one.","causes.4.short":"Skin infected with chytrid fungus","causes.4.text":"Grey-white patches grow on your skin. The skin loses its ability to regulate water and ions, and your heart slowly stops.","causes.5.short":"Pond dries up","causes.5.text":"Several weeks without rain, and the pond grows shallower each day. Before you ever become a frog, you are baked dry in the mud.","causes.6.short":"Frozen while hibernating","causes.6.text":"You bury yourself in the silt to hibernate, but that winter is especially cold. The surface of the silt freezes, and you never wake.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your movements grow slower and your calls fainter, until there is no sound of you left in the grass by the pond.","events.0.text":"The egg mass drifts beneath the surface, each black egg held in clear jelly. Light filters down, and your colour deepens day by day.","events.1.text":"A spring rain falls on the pond, and the water warms all at once. Your embryos develop a little faster.","events.2.text":"Your tail wags back and forth in the water. You taste algae for the first time, and your mouth fills with a green flavour.","events.3.text":"You scrape a green film of algae off the water plants, and your gut fills up at once.","events.4.text":"You bury yourself in the silt, leaving only your eyes exposed. The water is murky, and nothing outside can make you out.","events.5.text":"You rest on the bottom, almost motionless, your tail trembling now and then.","events.6.text":"You swim out of the shallows to the middle of the pond. The water is deeper there, and there is more algae.","events.7.text":"A carp passes along the bottom, and the silt it stirs up blocks your view.","events.8.text":"Your hind legs grow out, and your body begins to absorb your tail. For the first time, you feel you can leap a long way.","events.9.text":"You flick out your tongue, and a mosquito is curled straight into your mouth.","events.10.text":"A shadow sweeps over from above. You dive into the water and hide beneath the water plants.","events.11.text":"At night, you wait in the grass by the water. A moth lands in front of you, and your tongue shoots out.","events.12.text":"You sit by the water basking in the sun, and your body slowly warms.","events.13.text":"You leave your old pond, hop across a field bank and reach another pool.","events.14.text":"A male calls from the water's edge, the sound carrying far through the night. You leap into the water and clasp a female.","events.14.fact":"In many frog species, males attract females by calling, and the rate and intensity of the call influence female choice (Gerhardt and Huber 2002)","events.15.text":"A little egret strides along the bank, and you freeze without even blinking.","events.16.text":"A grass snake pokes its head out of the grass. Your hind legs kick, and you leap into the water.","events.17.text":"The pond water has turned murky, with a thin film on the surface. A companion on the bank has died, its skin grey-white.","events.17.fact":"The chytrid fungus Batrachochytrium dendrobatidis caused mass die-offs of frogs in rainforests of Australia and Central America (Berger et al. 1998, PNAS)","events.18.text":"Your skin has become very dry, and its colour has dulled. You rarely jump anymore.","events.19.text":"The calls at night are growing fainter. You hardly call at all now, just stay by the water.","events.20.text":"The pond is deep and the algae grows thick. Your body grows faster than the other tadpoles in the pond.","events.21.text":"Spring rain keeps falling, and the pond stays full. You spend a long time in the water.","events.22.text":"The water is neither deep nor shallow. There is enough algae and tiny insects to eat, and you grow at an ordinary pace.","events.23.text":"The weather is normal. You hunt insects in the grass, and your body grows sturdier year by year.","events.24.text":"The pond shrinks a little more every day, exposing mud along its edges. You crowd into the deepest water.","events.25.text":"A strong summer wind blows across, and the pond loses another stretch of water.","events.26.text":"After a warm winter, the egg masses swell in the water early. Then a late cold snap drops the water temperature, and the eggs stop developing.","events.27.text":"You hatch early, but the water temperature has not caught up. You move slowly on the bottom and eat very little.","events.28.text":"The tadpoles in this pond are all of one species, and they do not compete for space. You swim freely.","events.29.text":"At night, only frogs of your species call from the edge of the pond, and their voices carry clearly across the water.","events.30.text":"Several frog species share this pond, and their calls pile on top of each other. You cannot find a place of your own at the water's edge.","events.31.text":"You call from the water's edge, and a frog of another species joins in. You are not sure whether any female heard you.","events.32.text":"Many silver carp and common carp fry have been put into the pond. They stir up silt on the bottom and block your view.","events.33.text":"There are many fish in the pond, and anglers come at night with lanterns. The light falls on the water, and you hide among the grass roots.","events.34.text":"You are smaller than the tadpoles from your batch, and you swim at the back. By the time you arrive, the water plants have already been eaten bare.","events.35.text":"Your hind legs are stronger than those of other young frogs. In one leap you clear a clump of grass.","events.36.text":"A dragonfly lands in front of you. You lunge at it, land in the water instead, and choke on a mouthful.","events.37.text":"You sit at the water's edge for a long time, with only your eyes above the surface. Only after an egret has walked past do you start to eat.","events.38.text":"You keep darting between the water plants and find a small patch of shallow water with no tadpoles in it. The algae there is clean.","decisions.0.title":"Water plants or the shallows","decisions.0.body":"You are now a tadpole. The water-plant area is thick with algae, but it also has fish. The shallows are safer, but there is little to eat.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Go to the water plants and graze on algae","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You graze your fill among the water plants. A carp passes close to you, and you do not stop.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in the shallows by the bank","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safety · Little food","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You swim slowly through the shallows by the bank. You eat little, but it is quiet.","decisions.1.title":"The water is shrinking","decisions.1.body":"The pond gets smaller each day. There is still deep water in the middle, but you would have to cross a large stretch of shallows to reach it.","decisions.1.fact":"Pond drying is one of the main causes of tadpole death, and many tadpoles are baked dry before they metamorphose (Wells 2007, The Ecology and Behavior of Amphibians)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Head for the deep water in the middle","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Risk↑ Food↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You cross the shallows. The water is scorching, but you reach the deep water in the middle.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay where you are and wait for rain","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stay put. The water recedes bit by bit, and the mud beneath you grows harder.","decisions.2.title":"Going ashore","decisions.2.body":"Your hind legs have formed, and your tail is almost absorbed. The grass on the bank is full of insects, but on land there is no water to protect you.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Go ashore and hunt insects in the grass","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You climb out onto land for the first time. The grass is full of insects, but a heron's shadow passes over your head.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay in the water","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety · Little food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay in the water and wait for your body to grow stronger.","decisions.3.title":"Winter is coming","decisions.3.body":"The weather keeps getting colder, and mist begins to rise off the water. Your body needs warmth, but the insects outside are already scarce.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Burrow into the silt to hibernate","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ No lifespan gain","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You burrow into the silt at the pond bottom, and your body slowly cools. You close your eyes, and you hear nothing more.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep moving in the water","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Hungry Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep swimming in the water. The insects grow fewer, and your body grows lighter each day.","decisions.4.title":"Shadows at the pond's edge","decisions.4.body":"At night, a heron is stalking the pond's edge. On the other side of the grass, a snake's shadow moves. You must decide whether to leave the water's edge or stay in the grass to look for food.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Hide beneath the water plants","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You hide under the water plants and wait for the heron to walk away.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Leap to the bank and hunt","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You jump onto land and eat a whole cluster of flying insects. Suddenly the heron turns its head.","decisions.5.title":"The chorus","decisions.5.body":"On a spring night, the males at the pond's edge call one after another. Your vocal sac swells, and you must decide whether to join in.","decisions.5.fact":"A male's call attracts females of its species but also draws predators, so chorusing is itself a risk (Gerhardt and Huber 2002)","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Join the chorus","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You call until dawn. A female swims toward you.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Keep quiet on the bottom","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Offspring↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You stay quiet on the bottom and listen to the other males calling.","decisions.6.title":"Where to lay the eggs","decisions.6.body":"Your eggs have matured inside you. You must decide whether to lay them on the water plants in the middle of the pond or in the shallows by the bank.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"On the water plants in mid-pond","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You lay your eggs on the water plants. The egg mass slowly swells in the water, and the eggs are black.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"The shallows by the bank","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Offspring↓ Safer","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You lay your eggs by the bank. The water is warm here, and the eggs hatch quickly, but the pond will shrink sooner.","decisions.7.title":"Mud in the shallows","decisions.7.body":"The water to the south of the pond has dried up, leaving a stretch of mud. There is still a small puddle to the north, and the deep water in the middle has not gone. You have to decide where to swim.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Swim toward the small puddle to the north","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↓ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You cross a stretch of mud and reach the small puddle. The water is warm.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the deep water in the middle","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay in the deep water in the middle. There is not much of it, but it is enough to live in.","decisions.8.title":"The fisherman's lantern","decisions.8.body":"A fisherman by the pond has hung his lantern in a tree. The light falls on the water, and the fish around you sink into the dark.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Hide under the water plants and wait for the light to go out","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You hide beneath the water plants, and the lantern stays lit above for a long time.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Jump onto the bank and eat insects under the lantern","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"Insects swirl around the lantern, and you eat a great many. A heron swoops down from the tree.","opening.title":"Mother looks for a place to spawn","opening.body":"You have not yet been born. Your mother is a female frog. After an early spring rain, she clasps a male and must decide where to lay her eggs. Among the water plants the water is deep and quiet, but there are many fish and water insects. By the muddy edge of the shallows the water is warm, and the eggs will hatch fast, but the water can easily dry up.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay in the deep water at the edge of the water plants","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"The egg mass hangs from the water plants. You grow slowly inside the jelly, with plenty to eat around you, and plenty of eyes watching.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay in the shallows at the muddy edge","opening.options.1.hint":"Safer · Little food","opening.options.1.result":"The egg mass clings to the muddy edge. The water grows warmer by the day, but your pond is also starting to shrink.","actions.forage.name":"Eat algae or catch insects","actions.forage.desc":"Tadpoles scrape algae film, while adult frogs catch insects with their tongues.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in mud or water plants","actions.hide.desc":"Burrow into the silt or hide beneath the water plants, with only your eyes showing.","actions.rest.name":"Rest by the water and bask","actions.rest.desc":"Stay still, bask in the sun or rest on the bottom to conserve energy.","actions.explore.name":"Head for another pond","actions.explore.desc":"Leave familiar waters and look for new food and water.","actions.mate.name":"Call to attract a mate","actions.mate.done":"The female you clasped laid her eggs in the water, and you fertilised them. Afterward, {n} eggs appeared on the water plants.","idle.0":"You stay by the water, now and then inflating your throat sac.","idle.1":"The pond water is green, with a thin film across its surface.","idle.2":"Frogs call from somewhere far off, the sound coming in waves.","idle.3":"Your eyes are beneath the surface, and all you can see is the cloud in the sky.","idle.4":"The water temperature follows the daylight, and your movements slow along with it.","idle.5":"The grass on the bank sways in the wind, and something creeps beneath its roots.","idle.6":"A dragonfly lands on a water plant, its wings catching the light.","idle.7":"Your skin is wet, and it takes in air and ions from the water.","actions.forage.text.0":"You scrape a green film of algae off the water plants.","actions.forage.text.1":"Your tongue shoots out, and a flying insect is curled into your mouth.","actions.hide.text.0":"You burrow into the silt, with only your eyes showing.","actions.hide.text.1":"The water plants sway around you, and you stay perfectly still.","actions.rest.text.0":"You bask by the water, and your body slowly warms.","actions.explore.text.0":"You hop across a field bank and reach another pool."},"goat":{"name":"Domestic goat","tagline":"Over a billion goats live in the world, most of them in the flocks of smallholder farmers. They eat grass and shrubs that other livestock often leave behind.","survivalNote":"Mortality in smallholder flocks is high among kids (pneumonia, diarrhoea, wolves and feral dogs) and, once adult, comes mainly from drought, parasites and slaughter before festivals. This curve is a rough field estimate, and it varies widely from place to place.","epitaph":"Your milk, meat, hair and hide were all put to use. Goats were among the first animals to be domesticated, and your life belongs to that long chain.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Purpose","birth.rolls.2.label":"Breed","birth.rolls.3.label":"Conditions in the birth year","birth.siblingsLabel":"Kids in the same litter","birth.intro":"You are born in {r0}. You are {sex}. {r1}. There are {siblings} kids in your litter, including you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a goat pen at a farmhouse in the mountains of northwest China","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a herder's flock on the edge of an East African grassland","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a village courtyard in a dry region of India","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a kidding shed at a dairy goat farm in the European mountains","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A future milking doe","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"Raised for slaughter, and will be killed within about a year","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"A future breeding buck","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Local mountain goat, small and able to eat coarse fodder of any kind","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Saanen dairy goat, which yields a lot of milk and needs feed with soybean meal","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Boer meat goat, which grows fast and has a round body","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"A year with good rains, and the grass grows tall","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"A spring with heavy snow, which buries large areas of pasture","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"A year with many wolves in the mountains, and the village posts someone to guard the goat pen every night","stages.0.name":"Kid (nursing)","stages.1.name":"Weaned young goat","stages.2.name":"Yearling","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Kid pneumonia","causes.0.text":"The winter pen is damp. You cough for three days, and a yellow-green discharge runs from your nose. No one gets up in the night to give you an injection.","causes.1.short":"Starved in drought","causes.1.text":"The rains do not come this season. The grass on the hillside has dried, and the goats ahead have stripped the shrub leaves bare. You walk a long way, and in the end you lie down by the road.","causes.2.short":"Killed by a wolf or feral dog","causes.2.text":"Just after dark, a wolf bursts up from below the slope. The flock scatters, and after a few steps it has your hind leg in its jaws.","causes.3.short":"Barber's pole worm infection (anaemia)","causes.3.text":"Worms in your stomach feed on your blood. The lining of your eyelids slowly turns pale, your steps grow slower and slower, and in the end you collapse in the grass.","causes.4.short":"Bloat","causes.4.text":"You gorge on a big handful of tender alfalfa, and the gas in your stomach cannot escape. Your belly swells like a ball, and your breathing stops.","causes.5.short":"Fell to your death from a cliff","causes.5.text":"You climb the hillside after a tuft of grass, and the stone under your hoof gives way.","causes.6.short":"Slaughtered before a festival","causes.6.text":"The village is holding a feast, and the butcher picks you out. The knife is quick, and you do not cry out.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth wear shorter and shorter, until you can no longer chew dry hay. One winter you lie down in a corner of the pen, and you do not get up again.","events.0.text":"Your mother licks the amniotic fluid from your body. You stand for the first time, your legs give way, and you tumble back into the grass.","events.1.text":"You find your mother's teat for the first time and suckle for a long while. Your stomach grows warm.","events.2.text":"There is a damp smell in the corner of the pen, and the dung on the floor has not been cleared. You cough twice.","events.3.text":"You lie beside your mother's belly, listening to the other goats chewing their cud, a steady, rhythmic gurgle.","events.4.text":"You nibble a clump of tender locust leaves, dew still on them. Goats can climb onto the rocks to reach them.","events.5.text":"You hook your forehooves around a young tree and yank down its topmost tender shoots.","events.6.text":"You follow the flock up a ridge, and the view suddenly opens out. In the distance lies a meadow no one has grazed.","events.7.text":"A dog barks without stopping at the foot of the mountain. You squeeze into a crevice between the rocks, pressed tight.","events.8.text":"Villagers are sharpening knives on the roofs. One goat in the flock has been chosen and is led into the courtyard.","events.9.text":"In the dry season, you dig a few dry leaves out from under the shrubs.","events.10.text":"You lie in the shade of an old walnut tree and chew your cud the whole afternoon.","events.11.text":"The goat beside you gives birth to a kid. The smell of fluid and blood reaches you, and your stomach tightens.","events.12.text":"In the rainy season the grass grows too fast, and you eat too much. Your belly swells, and you walk for a long time before you can finally belch the gas out.","events.13.text":"Mosquitoes swarm in the pen, and their bites have swollen your ears.","events.14.text":"One of your molars has broken. You can only chew grass on the other side of your mouth.","events.15.text":"Your movements slow down, and you have to stop halfway up the slope to catch your breath.","events.16.text":"A small patch of skin beneath your belly has turned red, and there are ticks on your legs. You rub against a tree for a long time.","events.17.text":"Spring arrives. You give birth to {n} kids and lick the amniotic fluid off them.","events.18.text":"In the flock, a buck chases a doe, their horns knocking together with a dull thud.","events.19.text":"One of your ears droops and no longer stands up. The young goats in the flock begin to keep their distance from you.","events.20.text":"A hailstorm comes. You and the flock huddle under a rock wall, your coat soaked through.","events.21.text":"A wolf howls somewhere far off in the night. Every goat in the flock pricks its ears, and not one of them moves.","events.22.text":"A herder walks over, counts the goats and writes something down in a notebook.","events.23.text":"Another doe gives birth. You are squeezed into a corner of the pen, grooming each other.","events.24.text":"You can climb steep rocks and nibble tender leaves that the other goats cannot reach.","events.25.text":"Local mountain goat kids are thin but sturdy. The first time you follow your mother up the slope, your legs shake, but you do not fall.","events.26.text":"The villagers say goats like you eat coarse food and will eat almost anything. You have chewed a thorny shrub, and your mouth did not bleed.","events.27.text":"Your mother has plenty of milk, and you eat your fill. But your belly sometimes swells, and you have to walk a few steps before you feel better.","events.28.text":"Dairy goats need concentrated feed. The trough holds feed with soybean meal, and you eat it quickly, but you do not much like climbing the hills.","events.29.text":"Every day you are led off to be milked. The milker has a strong grip, and once you kicked him.","events.30.text":"You grow faster than the other kids in your litter, and your body quickly becomes round.","events.31.text":"The people feed you plenty, and you grow fast and fat. Sometimes your legs can barely hold up your body.","events.32.text":"The villagers say your meat will be plentiful. They tie a red cloth around your neck.","events.33.text":"The rains are good this year, the grass grows tall, and you and your mother eat your fill.","events.34.text":"New grass covers the hillside. You finish one clump and move on to the next.","events.35.text":"This year all the goats in the village grow plump and round.","events.36.text":"Spring snow falls for several days, burying the grass. All you can do is strip the bark from the trees.","events.37.text":"When the snow melts, the ground is a sea of mud. Your hooves are caked with it, and every step sinks in.","events.38.text":"The villagers carry dry hay into the pen, bundle by bundle. You crowd beside the haystack and get a fair share.","events.39.text":"At night someone stands guard at the pen gate, holding a fire stick. Howls sound from far off, and all the goats crowd into the corner by the wall.","events.40.text":"The village dogs bark all night. Wolf prints are left in the snow below the slope, and people find them the next morning.","events.41.text":"The night watchmen take turns, and sometimes you can hear them talking softly.","events.42.text":"You are the first to jump onto the big rock. The wind in the valley ruffles your beard.","events.43.text":"You always stand at the back of the flock, looking ahead for any movement before you step forward.","events.44.text":"You climb into the fork of an old tree and see the village and a river in the distance from up high.","events.45.text":"You are smaller than the other kids in your litter. They have taken your mother's teats, and you can only wait behind.","events.46.text":"Your horns are thick and long. In winter you butt the other does aside and claim a dry spot for yourself.","decisions.0.title":"The pen gate is open","decisions.0.body":"In the morning the gate opens, and the does file out one after another to graze. You are still small, and your steps are wobbly.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Follow them out","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Graze · Easily left behind","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You follow your mother out, and on a dirt slope you nearly tumble all the way down.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in the shed and nurse","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Warm · Eat less","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stay in the shed, pressed against your mother's belly, and remain there until dusk.","decisions.1.title":"Less milk","decisions.1.body":"Your mother's milk is thinning, and your stomach is often empty. The shrubs on the pasture are within reach now, but they lie some distance from the pen.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go to the shrubs to find food","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Eat more · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You gnaw at the shrubs for a long time, and when you return, your legs have several new scratches.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Push in beside another doe to nurse","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · Get butted","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You squeeze in under another doe's belly. She butts you twice, but still lets you nurse.","decisions.2.title":"The pasture dries up","decisions.2.body":"The rains have not come this season. The grass near the village has been eaten bare. There is still some green in the mountains, but it is a long way off.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Go farther into the mountains","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Eat more · Wolves and falls","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You follow the lead goat over two ridges. There is a patch of grass in the mountains no one has visited, but your legs tremble with exhaustion.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay near the village and eat dry hay","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Close · Little grass","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay by the village, eating the dry hay your keeper scatters, and you feel hungry every day.","decisions.3.title":"The village is holding a feast","decisions.3.body":"The butcher is sharpening his knife in the yard. Your group is the nearest one.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Keep your head down and graze, away from the crowd","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Calm · Cannot choose","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You keep your head down and eat mouthful by mouthful. The man picking animals walks over and leads you out.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hide in the maize field","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Hide for now · Can be found","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You slip into the maize field, and the leaves scrape your face raw. After a long while, someone parts the leaves and finds you.","decisions.4.title":"Breeding season","decisions.4.body":"You are old enough to breed. Not far away, a buck in the flock calls out, his voice loud.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Move toward the buck","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You go to him. Months later, you are walking around the pen with a swollen belly.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Go to the far side of the hill","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Quiet · No offspring","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You walk to the far side of the hill and graze on grass no one has touched.","decisions.5.title":"Winter feed buried in snow","decisions.5.body":"Snow has buried the feed under a layer. You have lost a lot of weight, and you can feel each rib one by one.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Paw at the snow to find grass","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Eat more · Cold","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You dig all afternoon, your cloven hooves aching with cold. You find a few handfuls of dry grass.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Squeeze into the pen and wait for someone to bring feed","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Warm · Long wait","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You wedge yourself among the flock and wait for someone to bring hay. It comes slowly.","decisions.6.title":"Too old to kid","decisions.6.body":"You have not given birth for many years. The keeper writes your name in his notebook and draws a question mark beside it.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay on the grass a little longer","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Calm","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You linger on the grass all afternoon. The next day you are still led back to the pen.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Walk to the front of the flock","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Conspicuous · Noticed sooner","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You walk to the front of the flock. Someone sees you and makes another mark in the notebook.","decisions.7.title":"Milking time","decisions.7.body":"The milker comes every morning and takes your udder in hand. You can stand still and let him milk you, or run off and make him chase you.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Stand still and be milked","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Eat more · Health↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You stand still while he fills a bucket with milk.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Run up the hillside and hide","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Eat less","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You run up the hillside, and the milker shouts from the bottom for a long time.","decisions.8.title":"Grass under the snow","decisions.8.body":"Snow presses down on the grass, and a little green shows at the edges of the blades. You can paw at the snow with your hooves, or go to the pile of bark nearby.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Paw at the snow with your hooves","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Eat more · Frostbite↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You dig all afternoon, and your hooves ache with cold. You uncover only a little grass.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Gnaw at the bark","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Health↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"The bark is bitter and hard. You gnaw at it for a long time and swallow only a little.","decisions.9.title":"The night watchman","decisions.9.body":"The watchman has lit a fire and keeps his eyes on the pen. You can stay in the middle of the flock, or slip off to the grass slope to look for food.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Stay in the middle of the flock","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Eat less","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You squeeze in among the flock and go hungry all night.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Slip off to the grass slope to look for food","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Risk of wolf attack↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You slip up onto the grass slope and graze for a long time. Far away a wolf howls, and you cannot tell where it is.","decisions.10.title":"A steep, big rock","decisions.10.body":"On the far side of the big rock there is a clump of tender leaves, but the stone is slippery. You can climb up, or go around.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Climb up","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Eat much more · Risk of falling↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You climb up and nibble the tender leaves. On the way down, your foot slips.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Go around","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Eat less","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You take a long detour and arrive beside another clump of shrubs.","opening.title":"The mother chooses where to kid","opening.body":"You are still inside your mother's belly, listening to snow strike the roof outside. Tonight your mother will give birth to you. She can go to the goat pen, or to the lee side of a rock on the hillside.","opening.fact":"Goats were domesticated about 10,000 years ago in the Zagros Mountains (Zeder and Hesse 2000, Science)","opening.options.0.t":"Give birth in the pen","opening.options.0.hint":"Sheltered · Disease spreads easily","opening.options.0.result":"You are born in a pile of dry hay in the pen, right beside the other does.","opening.options.1.t":"Give birth behind the rock","opening.options.1.hint":"Clean · Cold, close to wolves","opening.options.1.result":"You are born behind the rock. The wind wraps around the stone and it is bitterly cold.","actions.forage.name":"Browse shrubs","actions.forage.desc":"Stand up, hook a branch with your forehooves, and strip the tender leaves and shoots.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a rock crevice","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in a gap between rocks or behind a clump of trees, out of sight of the flock.","actions.rest.name":"Lie down under a tree","actions.rest.desc":"Lie in the shade with your legs folded beneath you and chew your cud.","actions.explore.name":"Climb the hillside","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the flock and climb the slope or ridge in search of new pasture.","actions.mate.name":"Breed","actions.mate.done":"You have given birth to {n} kids.","idle.0":"You chew your cud, the corners of your mouth moving in a steady rhythm.","idle.1":"The wind on the hillside blows through your coat.","idle.2":"A doe bleats somewhere far off, and you lift your head at once.","idle.3":"You paw the ground, sniff it, and wander off again.","idle.4":"The flock crowds together beside the rocks, rubbing ears with one another.","idle.5":"A bird lands on your back and flies off again.","idle.6":"Your stomach gurgles, and the grass inside you is still being digested.","idle.7":"A herder's whistle drifts in from far away, and the flock slowly turns back.","actions.forage.text.0":"You brace your forehooves against a small tree and chew its leaves.","actions.forage.text.1":"Dew wets your muzzle.","actions.hide.text.0":"You squeeze into a crevice between the rocks, and the outside wind cannot reach you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie under the tree, chewing your cud slowly.","actions.explore.text.0":"You climb up along the rocks, your hooves slipping with every step."},"greenland_shark":{"name":"Greenland shark","tagline":"The longest-lived known vertebrate, which takes about 150 years to reach adulthood","survivalNote":"No field data exist for wild Greenland shark mortality. The upper lifespan limit comes from the estimate of Nielsen et al. 2016 (392 ± 120 years), and this curve is a rough estimate","epitaph":"No one alive on land the year you were born has seen you die.","taxon":"Chordata › Chondrichthyes › Squaliformes › Somniosidae","size":"Adults about 4–5 metres long","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth waters","birth.rolls.1.label":"Depth","birth.rolls.2.label":"Fishing pressure","birth.siblingsLabel":"Pups born in the same litter","birth.intro":"You are born in {r0}, about 40 centimetres long, in water near 0°C. Your mother gives birth to roughly {siblings} pups at once, then swims away. You are {sex}. At your pace, you will not reach adulthood until about 150 years old.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the deep end of a fjord on Greenland's east coast","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the cold water layer of Baffin Bay","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the continental slope off Svalbard, Norway","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"the deep sea north of Iceland","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Cold water tens of metres below the surface, under the winter ice","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A continental slope several hundred metres deep, where the water stays close to 0°C","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"The seafloor more than a thousand metres down, where almost no light reaches","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A sea area with almost no fishing boats, where seals and fish are still plentiful","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A sea area crossed year-round by bottom trawlers","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A longline fishing ground, with rows of baited hooks hanging in the water","stages.0.name":"Pup","stages.1.name":"Juvenile","stages.2.name":"Subadult","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Eaten by a larger shark or seal","causes.0.text":"While you are still small, a bigger member of your kind slams into you in the darkness.","causes.1.short":"Caught by fishing gear","causes.1.text":"You bite into a bait on a longline. Fishermen haul you onto the deck, cut the line and throw you back into the sea. By the time you sink, you no longer move.","causes.2.short":"Tangled in a bottom trawl","causes.2.text":"A bottom trawl set for Greenland halibut sweeps across the seafloor and scoops you up with it.","causes.3.short":"Long-term starvation","causes.3.text":"For several years, there are few seals and few fish. Your liver shrinks little by little, until you finally stop swimming.","causes.4.short":"Infection","causes.4.text":"A wound from many years ago never fully healed.","causes.5.short":"Old age","causes.5.text":"You slowly come to rest on the frigid seafloor. Since you were born, more than a dozen generations of people on land have lived and passed.","events.0.text":"You swim slowly near the seafloor and catch a small fish.","events.1.text":"A hooded seal glides over you, far larger than you are. You sink into deeper water.","events.2.text":"You cruise at a little over thirty centimetres a second, slower than a person walking.","events.2.fact":"Greenland sharks swim at an average speed of about 0.34 metres per second, the slowest of fish of comparable size (Watanabe et al. 2012)","events.3.text":"You find the carcass of a whale on the seafloor and feed on it for many days.","events.4.text":"You quietly approach a sleeping ringed seal and bite down on it.","events.4.fact":"Seals are often found in the stomachs of Greenland sharks, and researchers suspect the sharks prey on seals asleep in the water (Leclerc et al. 2012; Watanabe et al. 2012)","events.5.text":"A parasitic copepod clamps onto the corner of your eye and never lets go. Your vision blurs more and more, though at this depth there was little light to begin with.","events.5.fact":"Many Greenland sharks carry the parasitic copepod Ommatokoita elongata on their eyes (Borucinska et al. 1998)","events.6.text":"You eat a reindeer that has drowned after falling into the sea. No one knows how it came to be here.","events.7.text":"This year you grow less than a centimetre.","events.7.fact":"Greenland sharks grow only about 1 centimetre a year, or less (Nielsen et al. 2016)","events.8.text":"In the year {year} CE, summer sea ice is far thinner than it was when you were young.","events.9.text":"The sound of a ship's propellers carries across the surface for several days.","events.10.text":"You give birth to {n} pups, then swim away.","events.10.fact":"Greenland sharks are ovoviviparous, and a single litter is known to number about 10 pups (Koefoed 1957)","events.11.text":"In the deep water you meet a female, and the two of you mate.","events.12.text":"A longline hook snags your mouth. You struggle for a long time before the hook finally tears free at the corner of your lips.","events.13.text":"Some of the proteins in your body have not been replaced since the year you were born.","events.13.fact":"Researchers dated the core of the eye lens by carbon-14, and that tissue had already formed before birth (Nielsen et al. 2016)","events.14.text":"You swim more slowly now. You have lived through about {years} winters.","events.15.text":"A school of polar cod passes in front of you, and you eat a few.","events.16.text":"You follow the scent of decay and swim to a whale carcass.","events.17.text":"You smell a school of polar cod, and you open your mouth and swallow two of them.","events.18.text":"You sink into the soft mud, leaving only your eyes exposed. A shadow above slowly drifts away.","events.19.text":"The soft mud is very cold, and fine sediment slides over your gills. You hold perfectly still.","events.20.text":"You stop in the water and let the current carry your body slowly along. You do not eat a thing.","events.21.text":"You rest on the seafloor. Your old wound itches for a while, then the feeling settles down again.","events.22.text":"You head toward a layer of water you have never visited. The temperature drops a little.","events.23.text":"You swim down along the slope, and a light from above shines down behind you, then goes out quickly.","events.24.text":"In the deep water you meet another of your kind. Its body is cold, and it swims slowly. You draw close, then part again.","events.25.text":"In the darkness you find a female. You stay together for a while, then swim off in separate directions.","events.26.text":"In winter the ice above you slowly closes over. You swim in the cold water beneath it, and now and then the shadow of a seal passes overhead.","events.27.text":"In summer the ice cracks open. Beneath a crack you find a school of cod, packed tightly together, and you eat them one mouthful at a time.","events.28.text":"You swim close to the ice. Footsteps and the barking of sled dogs carry down from above. You do not go up to look.","events.29.text":"Dead fish drift down the edge of the slope, sinking slowly, and land one by one around you.","events.30.text":"You swim along the slope for a long time. The water is a little colder than you remember, but still comfortable.","events.31.text":"A research vessel lowers a very long cable above you. You swim over to look at it, then swim away.","events.32.text":"There is no light here. You sense your surroundings through pressure and vibration, moving slowly, and go several days without a bite to eat.","events.33.text":"You rest at the edge of a patch of seafloor mud, marine snow settling on your back. You stay still for a long time.","events.34.text":"The carcass of a whale has settled on the seafloor. You smell it, swim a long way, and manage to eat a mouthful.","events.35.text":"No boats have come to this area for a long time. You follow a wounded seal and eat the meat that falls from it.","events.36.text":"The water is very quiet, with only the sound of currents. You have lived here for many years without once meeting a fishing net.","events.37.text":"Now and then a ship crosses the surface far above, and the vibration of its propellers slowly spreads through the water.","events.38.text":"A bottom trawl drags over you. The sediment kicked up by its mouth turns the whole water column a murky yellow. You press against the seafloor and slip away from it.","events.39.text":"The sound of the trawl echoes back and forth across the seafloor. You bury yourself in the mud and wait several days before coming out.","events.40.text":"A trawl line has snapped on the seafloor, one end still tangled around a dead fish. You smell the fish and swim over, and the line catches you again.","events.41.text":"A longline hangs down from above, with a row of baited hooks along it. You bite one hook, the line goes taut, and you twist free.","events.42.text":"A half-dead salmon hangs on a hook. You bite into it, and a length of steel wire ends up in your mouth.","events.43.text":"The floats of the longlines bob on the surface in rows. You swim around them and keep your distance.","events.44.text":"You are the first to leave the group of young sharks. You swim toward a faint glow of light and bump into a drifting ice floe.","events.45.text":"You stay still against the seafloor for a long time. Only when the shadow of a large shark glides overhead do you move at all.","events.46.text":"You swim into a deep pit you have never visited before. At the bottom lies a cold stone, covered in white sponges.","events.47.text":"You are the smallest of your litter. The other pups get to the fish first, and you can only wait beside the bones they leave behind.","events.48.text":"Your body is thicker than that of sharks your age. When you fight other sharks over a whale carcass, you push them away with your body.","decisions.0.title":"Shallow or deep","decisions.0.body":"The shallows of the fjord have plenty of food, but larger sharks and seals roam there. The deep part of the slope is cold and dark, with almost nothing in it.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Stay in the shallows and forage","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You settle in the fjord.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Sink to the depths","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safer · Hungrier","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You sink all the way down to more than a thousand metres.","decisions.1.title":"A meat that does not move","decisions.1.body":"On the seafloor hangs a string of bait on a rope, its smell strong. Fishermen are after Greenland halibut.","decisions.1.fact":"Greenland sharks are a common bycatch of longline and bottom trawl fisheries in the North Atlantic (NAFO 2020)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go and eat","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Might get hooked","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You slip past the hook and bite off half of the bait.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Ignore it","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Keep searching slowly","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You swim away.","decisions.2.title":"The sea is warming","decisions.2.body":"The water layer you often visit has warmed, and the fish no longer come there. Reaching the colder seas to the north would take several years of swimming.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Migrate north","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Long way · Food may be more plentiful","decisions.2.options.0.result":"It takes you several years, but you reach colder water.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Familiar place · Little food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay, eating less than before.","decisions.3.title":"A giant carcass","decisions.3.body":"The body of a bowhead whale has sunk to the seafloor. Several of your kind have already gathered around it, some larger than you.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Push in and eat","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Injury risk","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You take a bite and get jostled, but you eat well enough to last a long while.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Wait for them to finish","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Not much left","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You wait for several days and eat what remains.","decisions.4.title":"Light beneath the ice","decisions.4.body":"The underside of the sea ice is coated with a thin layer of algae, and light leaks down through cracks in the ice. Small fish gather there, but the water under the ice is colder and darker.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Swim along the ice edge","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You swim along the ice edge for years and eat many small fish.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Sink to deep water","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You sink to the deep water, where the light grows distant until only darkness is left.","decisions.5.title":"A trawl track on the seafloor","decisions.5.body":"A long furrow runs across the seafloor, the mark left by a trawl's ploughing. Scattered fish and scraps of flesh lie in the furrow. You could follow it for food, or steer clear of it.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Follow the track for food","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being netted↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You eat several fish that were dragged loose. The trawl passes overhead in the distance, but it does not touch you.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Steer clear of the track","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You swim around the furrow and head off to the other side.","decisions.6.title":"Eyes that cannot see","decisions.6.body":"Your eyes see less and less clearly, and the parasites still cling to them. You can search for carrion by smell, or sink to greater depths and move as little as possible.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Hunt carrion by smell","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Health↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You follow the scent to a rotting fish and eat it slowly.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Sink to greater depths","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You sink into deeper water. There is no light there, and no carrion either.","decisions.7.title":"An old wound","decisions.7.body":"A wound from many years ago has never healed, and its edges have turned white. You could rest in the cold water layer and let it mend slowly, or keep chasing whale carcasses to feed.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Rest in the cold water and heal","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You stay in the cold water for a long time, and a thin film slowly forms over the wound.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Go to the whale carcass to feed","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You bite off a large piece of flesh, but the wound grows warm and throbs.","decisions.8.title":"A school of polar cod under the ice","decisions.8.body":"A school of polar cod swims beneath the ice, large in number. You are already slow. You could chase after them, or lie on the seafloor and wait for them to swim close.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Chase after them","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stamina↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You catch a few and eat your fill. For a long time afterward, you swim very slowly.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Lie on the seafloor and wait","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Stamina↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You lie on the seafloor and wait. Most of the cod have already swum far away.","decisions.9.title":"Light in the shallows","decisions.9.body":"You are very old now. The shallows hold many fish, and also the sound of many fishing boats. You could follow the slope up into the shallows, or stay quietly on the seafloor.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Swim toward the shallows","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk of bycatch↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You reach the shallows and eat a few fish. In the distance, a longline rope sways in the water.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay on the seafloor","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay on the seafloor and hold still, slowly.","decisions.10.title":"Whale bones on the seafloor","decisions.10.body":"You have found the carcass of a whale that sank into the mud of the seafloor. Its blubber has not fully decayed, but several large sharks are heading this way.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Fight them for it","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk of fatal bites","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You bite into a big piece of blubber, and the sharks nearby bite in too. In the dark you collide with each other several times.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Wait until they finish, then eat the leftovers","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safety · Little food","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You wait several days until they have all swum off, then gnaw at the remaining bones and scraps.","decisions.11.title":"The sound of the trawl","decisions.11.body":"The engine of the trawler gets closer each day. In the seafloor mud you see a furrow that has been ploughed open, with a broken line still hanging from it.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Leave the trawler's route and head for deep water","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You swim into the depths, and the sound of the trawl slowly fades above you.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Follow the trawl and eat the fish that fall through","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk of getting caught in the net","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You eat a fish that falls from the back of the net. The net line slides across your back, and you tear free, leaving a bloody gash on your body.","opening.title":"Which layer to sink to","opening.body":"Your mother swam away after giving birth to you and your littermates, leaving nothing behind. You have only just left her body, and the water around you is dark and cold. In the shallows above, there are small fish, and also larger shadows.","opening.options.0.t":"Sink to the seafloor and stay by the soft mud","opening.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Little food","opening.options.0.result":"You rest against the soft mud of the seafloor, barely moving. The light overhead grows more distant until only cold water remains.","opening.options.1.t":"Swim toward the shallows of the fjord to find small fish","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Large predators↑","opening.options.1.result":"You swim toward the shallows and eat a few small fish along the way. There are larger shadows there too, one flicking past at your side.","actions.forage.name":"Patrol the seafloor","actions.forage.desc":"Swim slowly along the seafloor, and when you catch the smell of carrion or fish, follow it.","actions.hide.name":"Sink into the soft mud","actions.hide.desc":"Sink to the seafloor and bury your body in the soft mud, leaving only your eyes showing.","actions.rest.name":"Hover motionless in the water","actions.rest.desc":"Stay still in the water, letting the current carry your body slowly, using almost no energy.","actions.explore.name":"Head for a new water layer","actions.explore.desc":"Leave a familiar layer of water and head for somewhere farther away or shallower.","actions.mate.name":"Find a mate in the deep","actions.mate.desc":"Meet a member of your kind in the deep water, draw close, and then bear pups.","actions.mate.done":"You have given birth to {n} pups, then swum away.","idle.0":"You swim slowly through the darkness.","idle.1":"The water temperature is 1°C. One year passes like this.","idle.2":"Marine snow drifts slowly down from above.","idle.3":"You have not seen one of your kind in a long time.","idle.4":"In winter the ice cover above closes, and in summer it cracks open again.","idle.5":"For several months of the year, you do not eat a single thing.","idle.6":"Your heartbeat is very slow.","idle.7":"Somewhere far off, a whale sings, its voice carrying a long way through the cold water.","actions.forage.text.0":"You swim slowly along the seafloor, your nose sniffing the current.","actions.forage.text.1":"The smell of decay drifts in from ahead, and you follow it.","actions.forage.text.2":"You open your mouth and swallow a small fish, then find a second one in the soft mud.","actions.hide.text.0":"You sink to the seafloor and bury your body in the soft mud.","actions.hide.text.1":"The shadow above slowly drifts away.","actions.hide.text.2":"The soft mud is cold, and fine sediment slides across your gills.","actions.rest.text.0":"You hover in the water, your body drifting slowly with the current.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your heartbeat is slow, so slow you can barely feel it.","actions.rest.text.2":"You have no idea how much time has passed before you move again.","actions.explore.text.0":"You head toward a layer of water you have never visited. The temperature drops a little.","actions.explore.text.1":"You bump into the underside of a large floe, and a little light falls under the ice.","actions.explore.text.2":"You swim down along the slope, and the light behind you goes out quickly."},"honeybee":{"name":"Western honeybee worker","tagline":"You spend your whole life working for one colony, and never see a flower open under your own name","survivalNote":"Survival from egg to emergence is fairly high. A summer worker lives about 3–6 weeks after emerging, and death rates are highest during foraging. The curve is a rough estimate based on these figures, not day-by-day measurement.","epitaph":"You never laid a single egg, but you helped hundreds of flowers set fruit.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Insecta › Hymenoptera › Apidae","size":"Worker body length about 1.2 cm","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Season","birth.rolls.2.label":"Strain","birth.rolls.3.label":"Beekeeping","birth.siblingsLabel":"Workers emerging from the same comb","birth.intro":"You are {sex}, but your reproductive organs are underdeveloped and you would not normally lay eggs. For now you are an egg, laid in {r0}. It is {r1}. About {siblings} workers hatched in the same batch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a brood comb in the center of the brood area","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the edge of a comb full of nectar","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a comb near the edge of the hive","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a newly split swarm","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"summer, when nectar is plentiful","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"late autumn, as the colony prepares for winter","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Italian bee (Apis mellifera ligustica), with bright yellow bands on the abdomen and a gentle temperament","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Carniolan bee (A. m. carnica), grey-black, clusters tightly in winter and builds up fast in spring","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Buckfast bee, a hybrid line bred at an English abbey for gentleness and disease resistance","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"African-hybrid bee, highly defensive of its nest; at any disturbance the whole guard force rushes out","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"A settled colony in a traditional wooden box, checked by the beekeeper once a year","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"A colony at a large commercial apiary, trucked around each year to pollinate crops","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"A wild colony in a tree hollow in the forest, with no one looking after it","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva","stages.2.name":"Pupa","stages.3.name":"Cleaning and nursing","stages.4.name":"Wax work and guarding","stages.5.name":"Foraging","causes.0.short":"Dies after stinging an intruder","causes.0.text":"Your barbed sting sinks into its skin and catches. When you try to fly away, your abdomen tears off, leaving the venom sac and sting behind in it.","causes.1.short":"Pesticide poisoning","causes.1.text":"You are gathering nectar in a field that was just sprayed. On the way back to the hive, your nerves begin to twitch, and you finally drop onto the stone slab in front of the entrance.","causes.2.short":"Worn out from foraging","causes.2.text":"Your wings are badly worn from a full day of flying. You return to the hive at dusk, crawl to the entrance, and never get up again.","causes.3.short":"Varroa mites and virus","causes.3.text":"Varroa mites and the viruses they carry weaken your body. Your wings deform, and you can no longer fly properly.","causes.4.short":"Drowned at a water source","causes.4.text":"You are drinking at a trough when the algae on the edge gives way under your feet, and you fall in. Your wings soak through, and you cannot take off again.","causes.5.short":"Eaten by a spider or bird","causes.5.text":"A spider web catches your wings. The spider slowly moves in, and your struggling soon stops.","causes.6.short":"Temperature too low","causes.6.text":"The temperature in the hive falls below what the cluster can maintain. You huddle with your sisters, but your muscles slowly stop.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your wings beat more and more slowly, and you finally stall on a foraging trip. You have made many trips this summer.","events.0.text":"You stand upright at the bottom of a hexagonal cell, like a grain of millet. The queen has just laid you here.","events.1.text":"3 days later your shell splits, and you become a white larva.","events.2.text":"The workers squeeze a milky food from glands in their heads and feed it to you, mouthful by mouthful.","events.3.text":"For the first few days you eat royal jelly. After that, it is a mixture of pollen and honey.","events.4.text":"The cell is capped with wax. Inside, you spin a silk cocoon, and your body slowly turns into a pupa.","events.5.text":"A varroa mite crawls into your cell and settles on your body to feed on your fluids.","events.5.fact":"Varroa mites originally parasitized Asian honeybees. In the first half of the 20th century they switched to the western honeybee and spread worldwide through colony trade (Rosenkranz et al. 2010)","events.6.text":"The virus enters your body through the mite's bite. It will deform your wings.","events.6.fact":"Deformed wing virus (DWV) is spread mainly by varroa mites, and infection causes wings to deform (de Miranda & Genersch 2010)","events.7.text":"The first thing you do after emerging is clean out the empty cells, dragging the debris and dead bees outside the hive.","events.7.fact":"Worker bees take on jobs by age. They clean and nurse inside the hive first, and only later forage outside (Seeley 1982)","events.8.text":"You lie over the brood area, feeding larvae from the glands in your head. Your head is hot.","events.9.text":"The brood area must stay above 30°C. You fan your wings to blow heat onto the cells that need it.","events.10.text":"The queen crawls past you. A knot of workers gathers around her, grooming and feeding her.","events.11.text":"Wax scales grow on the underside of your abdomen. You bite them off, soften them, and press them onto the edges of the cells.","events.12.text":"A wax moth larva crawls into the comb. You charge at it and push it out of the entrance.","events.13.text":"Your sisters at the entrance are beating their wings. A sharp, pungent scent drifts in, and every guard raises its abdomen.","events.14.text":"A spider has spun a web at the entrance. The guards tear its silk and drive it away.","events.15.text":"You make your first flight outside the hive. You circle in front of the entrance, memorize its position, and then fly off toward distant flowers.","events.16.text":"You forage in a field of rapeseed. Your honey sac is full, and two pellets of pollen hang from your hind legs.","events.17.text":"A swallow swoops overhead. You skim low over the leaves for a long stretch before returning to the hive.","events.18.text":"A hornet hovers over the flowers. You circle around it, but you fly in the wrong direction and lose a lot of time.","events.19.text":"Your wings are worn, and flying has become a struggle.","events.20.text":"A beekeeper opens the hive and lights a smoker. The cool smoke calms your sisters. He pulls out several combs full of honey and leaves enough for winter.","events.21.text":"The colony is loaded onto a truck and driven to a faraway orchard, where the fruit trees are about to bloom and need pollinating.","events.22.text":"You are caught in a spray mist over a freshly mown alfalfa field. You fly out as fast as you can, with droplets still clinging to your fuzz.","events.23.text":"The weather turns cool. You and your sisters gather around the queen at the center and keep the cluster warm with the heat of your beating wings.","events.24.text":"You push your proboscis into a rhododendron bloom and taste a sweet drop. Your honey sac slowly swells.","events.25.text":"You roll through a pile of pollen, and before long your hind legs are covered in pollen pellets.","events.26.text":"You slip into a dark gap between two combs, where the light is faint and the temperature is steady.","events.27.text":"A large bumblebee flies past the entrance. You press against the comb wall until it is gone.","events.28.text":"You rest at the edge of a cell, wings trembling slightly. Slowly your body warms.","events.29.text":"You pause on the stone slab at the entrance, your abdomen pressed against its cool surface.","events.30.text":"You leave your familiar flowers and fly to an orchard you have never visited. Over a meadow you lose your way, and you circle for a long time before finding home.","events.31.text":"You fly over a small river, its surface glinting brightly. You nearly hit a power line and swerve sharply.","events.32.text":"Your abdomen has bright yellow bands. The guard at the entrance sees you and simply moves aside instead of stopping you.","events.33.text":"This colony is laying heavily. Rows of cells in the brood area are packed with larvae, and your brood-food glands are soon empty.","events.34.text":"In a rapeseed field you fly with your sisters, and pollen quickly coats your hind legs.","events.35.text":"Spring has just arrived and the colony starts to expand. Its brood area is a week ahead of other colonies, and you are among the first workers to emerge.","events.36.text":"Cold rain falls for three days. The cluster tightens, and you and your sisters press against the comb and beat your wings to keep warm. No one leaves.","events.37.text":"You gather nectar in a freshly flowering alfalfa field, flying fast. Back at the hive, your honey sac is soon empty.","events.38.text":"The beekeeper lifts the lid of the hive. You do not sting him. You simply crawl to the other side of the comb and go on cleaning cells.","events.39.text":"A rapeseed flower is crowded with your sisters, but you do not fight for it. You move to a flower no one is using and forage there slowly.","events.40.text":"A varroa mite crawls to the edge of your cell. It cannot get inside, and the beekeeper brushes it out during an inspection.","events.41.text":"A raccoon pries off the lid of the hive. There are far more guards than usual, and you all rush at it together. It runs off, and the entrance is still buzzing.","events.42.text":"When the beekeeper passes by, you all rise at once and chase after his veil. The smoke from his smoker fails to calm this swarm.","events.43.text":"A wax moth larva has burrowed into the comb. The guards quickly surround it, and it cannot get out.","events.44.text":"In spring the beekeeper adds a layer of foundation, and the gaps in the wooden frames are sealed with propolis. There is enough space in the nest that you never need to squeeze.","events.45.text":"At the end of summer the beekeeper moves the hives up onto a hillside. Forage there is scarcer than at the foot of the hill, so you have to fly farther to find flowers.","events.46.text":"The beekeeper leaves a small bowl of syrup in the hive. You empty it quickly, and the nest is soon full of sweet scent.","events.47.text":"You are loaded onto a truck and jolted all night. When you reach the orchard, the honey stores in the nest have already shrunk noticeably.","events.48.text":"In the new location the guards do not yet know their way around. A few workers fly back to where the old hive stood and cannot find their own nest.","events.49.text":"The orchard is covered in apple blossom. You fly among the flowers all day, pollinating them one by one, and your wings wear down quickly.","events.50.text":"Your colony built this nest in a tree hollow, with a thick wax layer, and the wind outside cannot get in. In the dark you calmly clean the cells.","events.51.text":"At the edge of the forest you find a linden tree in full bloom. You fly back and forth every day, and your honey sac fills quickly.","events.52.text":"A woodpecker drums on the trunk all morning, and the vibrations travel down into the nest one after another. The guards stay at the entrance and do not leave.","events.53.text":"You are a little smaller than your sisters. When you squeeze into the cells, you always come last, and the heat of the brood area never quite reaches you.","events.54.text":"You pause at the entrance for a long time, looking several times before you dare to fly out. Your sisters have long since flown away, and your honey sac is still empty.","events.55.text":"A sideways gust blows you off course. Your wings are thick and strong, and you push against the wind all the way to the flowers.","events.56.text":"You leave the flowers you usually visit and circle to a water trough you have never seen before. You rest on the petals floating on the water for a long time before flying back.","decisions.0.title":"An intruder at the entrance","decisions.0.body":"A giant hornet lands at the entrance, twice your size. Its mandibles clamp onto the wing of one of your sisters.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Sting it","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Defend the entrance · You will die","decisions.0.options.0.result":"Your barbed sting goes in. You try to fly away, but your abdomen tears off. Its wings are still beating as your body drops in front of the entrance.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Fall back into the hive","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · Give up the entrance","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You and your sisters crowd into the doorway and let it have the entrance. It pauses there for a while, then flies away.","decisions.1.title":"A chemical smell on the wind","decisions.1.body":"Far off lies a large field of rapeseed, rich with nectar. But the wind carries a sharp smell: someone is spraying pesticide near the field.","decisions.1.fact":"Sublethal doses of neonicotinoid pesticides reduce bees' foraging success and survival (Henry et al. 2012, Science)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Fly to the big field","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Plenty of nectar · Pesticide risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"There is plenty of nectar, and your honey sac fills. On the way back your flight turns erratic, and you still end up landing on the stone slab in front of the entrance.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Go to the wild flowers at the forest edge","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less nectar","decisions.1.options.1.result":"There are not many wildflowers, but they are clean. You fill half your honey sac and return to the hive safely.","decisions.2.title":"A patch of abundant flowers","decisions.2.body":"You have just unloaded your nectar, and sisters crowd around you in the hive. You find a bare spot on the comb. You must decide: tell the others where the flowers are, or quietly go alone.","decisions.2.fact":"Karl von Frisch showed that honeybees use the waggle dance to tell nestmates the direction and distance of food. He received the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for his work on animal behavior (von Frisch 1967; Nobel Prize 1973)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Do the waggle dance","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Tell the others direction and distance · Stamina↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"Your abdomen sways from side to side, and the straight run points a little off the sun's direction. You dance more than 20 circuits, and the bees around you begin leaving the entrance.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Go quietly on your own","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Share nothing · Keep your nectar","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You don't dance. You fly straight back to that flower patch.","decisions.3.title":"A shallow puddle by the trough","decisions.3.body":"You are thirsty. Beside the trough a stone rises out of the water. The water beneath it is deep, but the algae around it is slippery.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Drink from the stone","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Close · Risk of slipping","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You stretch out your tongue and take a sip. The algae shifts under you, but you lift off just in time.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Fly to a distant water source","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Far · Stamina↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You fly a long way and find a clean puddle. On the way back you are very tired.","decisions.4.title":"The weather keeps getting colder","decisions.4.body":"Late autumn. The temperature inside the hive is dropping, and your body has no spare energy to waste. You must decide: stay in the cluster for warmth, or go out and look for the last flowers.","decisions.4.fact":"Winter bees emerge in autumn and can live for several months, while summer workers live only a few weeks (Winston 1987)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stay in the cluster for warmth","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Lifespan↑ Risk↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You squeeze into the center of the cluster and tremble your wings, warming the queen and the larvae.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Go out to find the last flowers","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You fly out. Most of the flowers along the roadside have closed up, but you still find a small patch.","decisions.5.title":"The brood area is too hot","decisions.5.body":"The brood area has risen above its proper range, and the cells holding larvae are heating up. You can go to the edge of the comb and fan, or crawl into a honey-storing cell and eat more honey.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Go and fan to cool it","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Protect the larvae · Stamina↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You lie along the edge of the brood comb and beat your wings fast, blowing hot air away from the larvae.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Eat honey in a storage cell","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Leave the brood area","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You crawl into a honey-storing cell, fill your honey sac, and then return to the brood area.","decisions.6.title":"A web among the flowers","decisions.6.body":"A spider has spun a web at the edge of a flower patch, with a few petals caught in it. There is plenty of nectar, but the web sits right below the flowers.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Land on the flower and drink","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being caught↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You drink your fill and fly back to the hive. The web sways gently behind you.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Go to another patch of flowers","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less nectar","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You steer around the web and spend a short while on another patch.","decisions.7.title":"Worn wings","decisions.7.body":"The edges of your wings are chipped and ragged, and you wobble in flight. You can keep foraging, or return to the hive and rest between the cells.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Keep foraging","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stamina↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You fly two more trips. On the last flight home, you nearly fall into the entrance on momentum alone.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Rest in the hive and tend the larvae","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Foraging↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You go back inside and spend a few days at the edge of the brood area, helping to feed the larvae.","decisions.8.title":"The guards' alarm","decisions.8.body":"The buzzing at the entrance suddenly changes pitch. The guards rush at each shape that approaches, and even workers from the comb come pouring out. You can join them, or hide deep in the cells.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Join the guards and attack the approaching shapes","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Nest defence · Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You rush to the entrance with the guards and sting several of the intruders. The smell of their venom clings to your body.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Hide deep in the cells","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Nest defence↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You back into the deepest cells and listen as the commotion outside slowly dies down.","decisions.9.title":"An unfamiliar hive","decisions.9.body":"The truck sets you down between a row of hives beside the orchard. In a neighbouring hive, the sweet smell of honey drifts out, and strange bees come and go at its door. You have to decide: slip inside, or hold your own doorway.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Slip into the neighbouring hive","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Disease risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You squeeze through the gap of a strange hive. There is plenty of honey inside, but you smell the scent of another colony.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Guard your own doorway","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Cautious · Little food","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay at your original entrance. A few days later the truck takes the hives away again, and you do not follow the other bees.","decisions.10.title":"Swarming in the tree hollow","decisions.10.body":"The tree hollow is crowded with workers, and every cell is full of larvae. A large group of workers has already gathered around the queen, ready to fly out with her in search of a new nest. You can follow them, or stay in the old nest.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Follow the swarm out of the tree hollow","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"New nest · Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You pour out of the hollow with a large crowd of sisters and settle on a branch, where you cluster together to rest.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay in the old nest","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Cautious · Crowded cells","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You stay in the old tree hollow. A new queen slowly grows up elsewhere, and a large stretch of cells is left empty in the old nest.","decisions.11.title":"The sweetness next door","decisions.11.body":"A sweet smell drifts from the entrance of the neighbouring hive, and guards patrol back and forth at its door. Your flowers are still far away, and their nectar is much the same as usual.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Raid the neighbouring hive for honey","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You slip through a strange entrance and fill your honey sac. By the time a guard spots you, you are already flying out, carrying several bite marks.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Go to your own flowers","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Cautious","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You fly to a familiar patch of flowers, gather ordinary nectar, and return quietly to the nest.","opening.title":"The queen chooses a cell","opening.body":"You are still an egg, standing upright at the bottom of a hexagonal cell. The queen circles the comb, deciding which cell to lay in next. The center of the brood area has the most stable temperature, so larvae develop on schedule. The edge of the comb is colder, sits against the wall of the hive, and is closer to the entrance, where cold wind and intruders are more likely.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay in a cell in the center of the brood area","opening.options.0.hint":"Stable temperature · Crowded","opening.options.0.result":"You are laid in a cell surrounded by warmth. The cells around you are full of larvae, and workers come to feed you soon.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay in a cell at the edge of the comb","opening.options.1.hint":"Colder · Close to the entrance","opening.options.1.result":"You are laid in a cell toward the outside. It is cooler here than in the center, and the workers arrive late, so your development is a little slower.","actions.forage.name":"Fly out to forage","actions.forage.desc":"Leave the entrance to find nectar and pollen, fill your honey sac, and fly back.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a dark gap in the comb","actions.hide.desc":"Slip between cells to avoid hot air and intruders.","actions.rest.name":"Rest by the cells","actions.rest.desc":"Stop and let your wings and body rest.","actions.explore.name":"Fly far from the hive","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your usual flower patches and search a new place for flowers.","idle.0":"The hive is dim, with only a little light coming through the entrance. The air smells of honey and beeswax.","idle.1":"A low hum rises from the hive, and all your sisters are beating their wings.","idle.2":"The cells are warmer than the outside, and the temperature stays steady.","idle.3":"In the distance, flowers sway in the wind, and their scent comes and goes.","idle.4":"The queen's pheromone hangs in the air, and your sisters gather around her.","idle.5":"Hexagonal cells line the comb in rows, some capped and some still empty.","idle.6":"At the far end of the comb, a worker is cleaning out a cell.","idle.7":"Wind blows past the entrance, then soon dies down.","actions.forage.text.0":"You fly back and forth among the flowers, looking for the next one.","actions.forage.text.1":"The sweetness of the nectar grows stronger, and your honey sac slowly swells.","actions.forage.text.2":"Two pellets of pollen hang from your hind legs, and your flight grows heavy.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip into a dark gap between two combs, where the light is faint.","actions.hide.text.1":"The buzz of wings from outside the entrance reaches you in waves.","actions.hide.text.2":"The temperature in the cell is steady, and you wait quietly against the wax.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie along the edge of a cell, wings trembling faintly.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your body slowly warms, and your movements slow down.","actions.rest.text.2":"The sisters around you are busy with their own tasks, and nobody disturbs you.","actions.explore.text.0":"You leave your usual flowers and fly toward where the wind is coming from.","actions.explore.text.1":"Below, the fields turn into an unfamiliar patchwork of colors.","actions.explore.text.2":"You circle a meadow for a while before you find the way back to the hive."},"housefly":{"name":"Housefly","tagline":"In a summer kitchen, a housefly lives from egg to death in only 2–3 weeks","survivalNote":"Field data on housefly lifespan is scarce; adults in the lab can live for several weeks. This curve is a rough estimate based on summer temperatures, swatting, and predation pressure","epitaph":"You lived only a few weeks, but you laid several hundred eggs. In a summer kitchen, neither you nor your offspring were ever remembered.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.2.label":"Pest control in this place","birth.rolls.3.label":"Eggs laid on the same heap","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the same clutch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, laid in {r0}. You are small, white and under 1 mm long. You are {sex}. The clutch you came from holds {siblings} eggs in total.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"an uncovered kitchen bin","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a heap of fermenting cow dung","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the edge of a forgotten plate of leftovers","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A week in midsummer, when the dung heap is hot enough for you to grow quickly","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A mild late spring, with the dung heap fermenting slowly","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"Early autumn, when nights have turned cool and your development slows","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A farm that sprays pyrethroid insecticide year-round, where your population has already developed resistance","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A cowshed where no one has sprayed insecticide for decades","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"The corner of a city kitchen where fly bait has just been put down","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"Several hundred eggs packed onto the same dung heap","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"Several dozen eggs scattered along the edge of the dung heap","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"A small scrap of leftover food, shared with only a few other eggs","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (maggot)","stages.2.name":"Pupa","stages.3.name":"Adult fly","stages.4.name":"Old fly","causes.0.short":"Swatted","causes.0.text":"A palm comes down with a rush of air. You have no time to take off before you are pressed into a smear of grease.","causes.1.short":"Caught in a spider web","causes.1.text":"You crash into a web in the corner of a window. The silk sticks to your wings, and the more you struggle, the tighter it binds. A spider slowly creeps out of the shadows.","causes.2.short":"Killed by an insect zapper","causes.2.text":"The blue tube flickers, and a brief crackle fills the air. Your body stops.","causes.3.short":"Ate insecticide","causes.3.text":"You land at the edge of a sugar syrup. There is a thin film of oil on it. A few minutes later, your feet can no longer grip anything.","causes.4.short":"Parasitized by a pupal wasp","causes.4.text":"A tiny black wasp drills into your pupal case. It lays its eggs on you, and its larvae will slowly eat you from the inside.","causes.5.short":"Dung dried in the sun, larvae dehydrated","causes.5.text":"The dung heap dries out under the midday sun. You crowd together, and your bodies lose water bit by bit.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your wings have worn ragged, and you fly lower and lower. One evening you drop from the windowsill and never get up again.","events.0.text":"Eggs are packed in rows on the surface of the dung heap, white and slender, like a small pile of rice grains.","events.1.text":"You wriggle out of your eggshell and burrow into the warm dung heap.","events.2.text":"You feed in the fermenting dung. Your mouthparts can only take in liquid, so you first spit out saliva to dissolve the food.","events.3.text":"The dung heap grows hotter and hotter. You and your siblings burrow deeper.","events.4.text":"A beetle larva digs a tunnel in a nearby dung heap, churning it into a mess.","events.5.text":"You stop feeding. Your body becomes shorter and harder, and its color begins to darken.","events.6.text":"You turn into a pupa, protected by a hard shell. You no longer move. You can only wait.","events.7.text":"Inside the pupal case, your body slowly takes the shape of an adult fly.","events.8.text":"You land at the edge of the kitchen sink, spit out a little saliva, and dissolve a drop of sugar syrup before sucking it up.","events.9.text":"A piece of leftover food has a thin film of oil on its surface. You walk around it once, then dissolve it.","events.10.text":"At the edge of a dish you find a trace of leftover sugar, and you eat it slowly.","events.11.text":"You fly out the window and circle the yard, until you find a fresh pile of droppings.","events.12.text":"You fly into the kitchen, drawn to the light at the door, and crash into the glass. You have to turn back.","events.13.text":"You hide in a corner of the ceiling, gripping tightly with your feet. People walk back and forth below.","events.14.text":"You squeeze into a crack in the wall, where the outside light cannot reach.","events.15.text":"You rest on the window glass, perfectly still.","events.16.text":"You rest on a warm wall, and your abdomen slowly soaks up the heat.","events.17.text":"A male chases you down and grabs your back with his forelegs.","events.18.text":"A spider has spun a web in a corner of the kitchen. You can smell the scent of its silk.","events.19.text":"The door opens and closes again. The draft spins you around, then sets you back down where you were.","events.20.text":"Someone in the room is swinging a fly swatter. The air is crowded and buzzing.","events.21.text":"You find a damp cloth, push your abdomen into it, and lay {n} eggs.","events.22.text":"You look for another spot to lay. This time you only produce {n} eggs.","events.23.text":"Your wings grow more and more tattered, and you fly lower and lower.","events.24.text":"At night you rest under the ceiling, no longer searching for food.","events.25.text":"In a life of only a few weeks, you grew from an egg into an adult fly.","events.25.fact":"In warm summers, a housefly can develop from egg to adult in as little as 7–10 days. At lower temperatures it can take several weeks (UF/IFAS IN205)","events.26.text":"The surface of the dung heap steams. You feed together in the heat, faster than seems possible.","events.27.text":"It is hot inside the pupal case. You change a few days faster than the other pupae.","events.28.text":"On the day you emerge, the sun outside the window falls right into the kitchen.","events.29.text":"The dung heap slowly warms and ferments evenly. You eat steadily, and your body grows in proportion.","events.30.text":"A spring breeze blows through the gap in the window, and for the first time you feel strength in your wings.","events.31.text":"It is still cool, and the other flies outside the heap emerge later than you.","events.32.text":"At night the dung heap cools, so you stop moving and wait for the warmth to come back.","events.33.text":"Pupation takes much longer than in summer. You wait in the pupal case for a long time before you feel your body begin to change.","events.34.text":"When you emerge, the wind outside is already cool. Your wings unfold more slowly than the others'.","events.35.text":"A mist drifts out of the farm sprayer. Your companions topple over one after another, and you keep walking.","events.36.text":"You find a bit of sugar on the cowshed dung heap. The mist has not cleared, and you have already eaten it all.","events.37.text":"The farm workers have switched to a different spray, and your kind drop one after another. You are still alive, waiting in the shadow of the wall corner.","events.38.text":"The dung heap in the cowshed is large, and no one has ever sprayed it. You all eat until you are full.","events.39.text":"You rest on a beam in the cowshed. It is quiet here, and no one is spraying.","events.40.text":"During a routine spraying, the mist creeps closer. You fly fast, but for a moment you are still caught in a layer of it.","events.41.text":"From the kitchen bin drifts the sweet smell of a bait box. Your companions crowd over, and some never move again.","events.42.text":"You land on a sweet piece of bait with a film of fine granules on its surface. You take one sip and spit it back out.","events.43.text":"Someone tidies up the kitchen and sets a box of fly bait in the corner by the wall.","events.44.text":"You and your companions press together, bumping into one another. Before you can eat, others have taken the food.","events.45.text":"The surface of the dung heap is drying out fast, because you all draw moisture from it. Your body grows drier and drier.","events.46.text":"You are slower than the other larvae in your batch. They have already pupated, and you are still crawling.","events.47.text":"You claim a small piece of dung heap all to yourself, eating slowly and quietly.","events.48.text":"There is plenty of food around you, and your body slowly grows bigger.","events.49.text":"Undisturbed inside the pupal case, your development proceeds steadily.","events.50.text":"The broth of this scrap of leftover food is full of sugar. You eat fast, and your body swells.","events.51.text":"The leftovers dry out quickly, and the broth vanishes bit by bit. You suck up the very last drop.","events.52.text":"You are closer to people than the other larvae are. Footsteps keep sounding overhead.","events.53.text":"You are smaller than the other larvae in your batch, squeezed at the edge of the heap. The larvae ahead of you have eaten the food first.","events.54.text":"You are sturdier than the larvae around you. You push them aside and dig a tunnel through the heap.","events.55.text":"You rest on the windowsill for a long time, testing every landing spot with your feet before you take a sip of sugar water.","events.56.text":"You circle a strange box three times, then probe into a crack and catch a new smell.","decisions.0.title":"The dung heap is getting hotter","decisions.0.body":"The dung heap is fermenting in the sun, and its temperature climbs higher each day. Your body begins to want to leave it.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave the dung heap and pupate in dry soil","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You crawl out of the dung heap and burrow into a patch of dry soil. The outside heat soon fades.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in the dung heap and keep feeding","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You feed once more. The surface of the heap starts to dry out, and it becomes harder and harder for you to move.","decisions.1.title":"The first flight","decisions.1.body":"You climb out of your pupal case, and your wings have just unfolded. Outside the window is light; inside the kitchen is food.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Fly toward the light outside","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You bump into the glass several times before finding a half-open window, and fly out into the sunlight.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay by the kitchen bin","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You settle at the edge of the bin. The smell of food is strong, and soon your wings are coated in grease.","decisions.2.title":"A palm","decisions.2.body":"Someone in the room is walking toward you, holding a fly swatter. The shadow of the swatter falls on the wall first.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Take off immediately","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safe↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You take off, and the swatter lands where you were just sitting.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay put and wait for the hand to drop","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You don't move. The swatter comes down with a loud crack.","decisions.3.title":"A drop of sugar water by the sink","decisions.3.body":"There is a drop of sugar water by the sink. By the door hangs a zapper, its blue light flashing on and off.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Go and drink the sugar water","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You feed for a long time. Only when you fly toward the door do you notice how bright that lamp is.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Go look for other food","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Less food","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You leave the sink and head for a pile of rotten fruit.","decisions.4.title":"A male comes close","decisions.4.body":"A male circles you a few times, and his forelegs have already settled on your back.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Accept him and let him mate","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"After mating, you store his sperm. Over the next few days, your eggs keep multiplying.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Fly away","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You wriggle free from his forelegs and fly into the light of the kitchen.","decisions.5.title":"A female lands beside you","decisions.5.body":"A female settles on a patch of grease next to you. Her abdomen is already swollen.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Chase her down and mate with her","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You seize her back. After mating, you fly for a few more minutes before finding the next place to rest.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Keep feeding","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Offspring↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You keep sucking at the grease. The female flies away.","decisions.6.title":"A dry summer","decisions.6.body":"The water on the windowsill has dried up. Your body needs water, and it needs sugar too.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Find a damp spot to drink","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You drink from a damp cloth, but that is also a place people wipe often.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Keep looking for sugar in the grease","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You suck up a lot of sugar from an old patch of grime. Your feet no longer seem to obey you.","decisions.7.title":"The farm sprayer is back","decisions.7.body":"The cowshed door is open, and someone walks in carrying a sprayer. The mist smells of pyrethroid. Your companions drop from the air one after another, but you can still fly.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Stay in the corner and ride it out on your resistance","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Risk↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You stop in the corner. The mist settles on you, and your feet tremble slightly, but you hold on.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Fly deep into the dung heap, out of the spray","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You dive into the depths of the dung heap. It is stuffy in there, but the mist cannot reach you.","decisions.8.title":"The bait box in the corner","decisions.8.body":"In a black plastic box in the corner there is a sweet piece of bait. The lid does not close tightly, and the scent of sugar drifts out.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Walk over and take a bite","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You push your mouthparts into the gap. The sweetness is intense. A few minutes later, your feet begin to stiffen.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Turn away and look for other food","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You turn away from the box and head for a pile of rotten fruit.","decisions.9.title":"A dung heap crowded with larvae","decisions.9.body":"More and more larvae are crowding onto the dung heap, and the food is suddenly not enough to share. Your body is squeezed soft, and the companions ahead keep backing into you.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Climb to the top and fight for the surface that has not been worked over yet","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You climb to the very top, where the food has not yet been churned. But the surface of the heap is already beginning to dry out.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Burrow to the bottom, away from the crowd","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You dig downward. The bottom is darker and damper, but the food there is already gone.","decisions.10.title":"Cold weather, inside the pupal case","decisions.10.body":"Nights are already cold this season. Inside your pupal case it is quiet, while the outside temperature keeps dropping.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Wait a few more days in the pupal case, until it warms up","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ More time","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You keep waiting inside the shell while the wind blows through the gap in the window, gust after gust.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Emerge as soon as possible, while there is still light","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You push hard against the pupal case until it splits. It is cold outside, your wings are still damp, and they unfold slowly.","decisions.11.title":"A piece of meat on the dinner table","decisions.11.body":"The people at the table have just got up to answer the phone, and a piece of greasy meat is still on the plate. Your companions have already landed on it.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Drop down and eat right away","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You land and suck up the grease, mouthful by mouthful. Footsteps return from the doorway, and you do not have time to fly off.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Circle above the table until the people leave","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Cautious","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You circle in the air several times until the footsteps fade, then land on the edge of the plate.","opening.title":"A mother fly looks for a place to lay","opening.body":"You have not hatched yet. Your mother lands on the edge of a kitchen bin, her abdomen trembling. The bin holds plenty of food, but someone clears it out every day or two. The heaps of cow dung out in the distance are never cleaned up, but they are farther from people.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay in the kitchen bin","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being cleared↑","opening.options.0.result":"You hatch under a pile of rotten vegetable leaves. There is plenty of food here, but within a few days someone lifts the bag and throws it out.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay on a heap of cow dung far away","opening.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","opening.options.1.result":"You hatch on the surface of the cow dung. There is plenty of food, and people rarely come here. The heap is only a little warm.","actions.forage.name":"Lick up sugar water","actions.forage.desc":"Spit saliva onto sugar water, leftover food and rotting matter to dissolve it, then suck it up.","actions.hide.name":"Hide under the ceiling","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in a corner or under the ceiling, away from human activity.","actions.rest.name":"Rest on the wall","actions.rest.desc":"Settle on a flat surface and rest without moving.","actions.explore.name":"Buzz around","actions.explore.desc":"Wander around the room, searching for new smells.","actions.mate.name":"Mate","actions.mate.done":"The male grips you with his forelegs. After mating, you store his sperm inside your body, and your later eggs will be fertilized with it.","idle.0":"You are on the kitchen wall, feeling the temperature of the surface with your feet.","idle.1":"Your compound eyes split the room into countless small squares.","idle.2":"You spit out a drop of saliva and dissolve a small piece of food in front of you.","idle.3":"Light flickers at the window, and your wings make a faint buzz in the air.","idle.4":"People are busy in the kitchen, and the sound of pots and bowls comes in waves.","idle.5":"Fine hairs on your feet pick up the scent of every place you have landed.","idle.6":"As you fly, you keep a mental map of where the sugar is and where the people are.","idle.7":"Dust in the corner of the ceiling drifts slowly through the sunlight.","actions.forage.text.0":"You land on a lump of sugar.","actions.forage.text.1":"You spit saliva onto it to dissolve it.","actions.forage.text.2":"You suck the dissolved food into your mouthparts.","actions.hide.text.0":"You duck into the shadow of a corner.","actions.hide.text.1":"People keep walking back and forth below.","actions.rest.text.0":"You rest on the wall, perfectly still.","actions.explore.text.0":"You fly across the windowsill, then loop back into the kitchen.","actions.explore.text.1":"You catch a familiar smell of rot."},"human":{"name":"Human","tagline":"Hundreds of thousands of people are born on Earth every day. You are one of them, in a place you did not choose","survivalNote":"Rough order-of-magnitude estimate based on global averages published by the UN and WHO (under-5 mortality about 3.7%, UN IGME 2023 report, 2022 data). Differences between regions are enormous; infant and child mortality in sub-Saharan Africa is far above this level","epitaph":"Every day many people are born, and many people leave. Your life passed through the numbers, quietly, and it was real.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Primates › Hominidae","size":"Adult height about 1.6–1.7 m","birth.rolls.0.label":"Region of birth","birth.rolls.1.label":"Family income","birth.rolls.2.label":"Birth order","birth.rolls.3.label":"Parents' education","birth.siblingsLabel":"Brothers and sisters at home","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}. {r1}. You are {sex}, with {siblings} brothers and sisters at home.","birth.sex.0.0":"a girl","birth.sex.1.0":"a boy","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a rural area in sub-Saharan Africa","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a county town in South Asia","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a city in East Asia and the Pacific","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a small town in Latin America and the Caribbean","birth.rolls.0.options.4.0":"a city outskirt in the Middle East and North Africa","birth.rolls.0.options.5.0":"a city in Europe or Central Asia","birth.rolls.0.options.6.0":"a suburb in North America","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Your family is very poor, spending less than US$2.15 a day","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"Your family earns just enough to get by, with a house that has running water","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"Your family is comfortable, and you have a room of your own","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Eldest child, with younger siblings to look after","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Middle child, with older and younger siblings","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Youngest child, with older brothers and sisters","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"Neither parent finished primary school","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"Parents finished middle school and know some basic hygiene and nutrition","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"Both parents went to university, and there are some books at home","stages.0.name":"Infant (0–1 years)","stages.1.name":"Toddler","stages.2.name":"School-age child","stages.3.name":"Young adult","stages.4.name":"Adult","stages.5.name":"Older adult","causes.0.short":"Dehydration from diarrhea","causes.0.text":"You have had diarrhea for 3 days. There is no oral rehydration salt in the house and no money for a clinic, so your mother spoons water into your mouth a little at a time.","causes.1.short":"Pneumonia","causes.1.text":"You run a high fever, and your breathing grows faster and faster. The nearest hospital is a full day's walk away.","causes.2.short":"Malaria","causes.2.text":"An Anopheles mosquito bites you. 3 days later you start to shiver, then the fever comes, and at last you cannot say a word.","causes.3.short":"Childbirth complications","causes.3.text":"You bleed heavily during labor. The birth attendant does not arrive in time, and the hospital is too far away.","causes.4.short":"Road traffic accident","causes.4.text":"A motorbike swings around the corner. You do not have time to get out of the way.","causes.5.short":"Cardiovascular disease","causes.5.text":"One morning you collapse in the kitchen doorway. By the time someone finds you, it is too late.","causes.6.short":"Cancer","causes.6.text":"Your checkup report shows a shadow. You never get to finish the rest of the tests.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"You fall asleep in bed and never wake up. Your family is all around you.","events.0.text":"You lift your head for the first time and see shadows moving across the ceiling.","events.1.text":"A neighbor brings an old blanket. Your mother wraps you in it and carries you to the window to sit in the sun.","events.2.text":"You receive your first vaccine. The measles vaccine is usually given at around 1 year old.","events.2.fact":"Measles vaccination is estimated to have prevented about 57 million deaths between 2000 and 2022 (Minta et al. 2023, MMWR)","events.3.text":"You have diarrhea, and water is draining from your body day after day. At home, the only thing you can be given is sugar water.","events.4.text":"You start grabbing whatever is near you and pushing each thing into your mouth.","events.5.text":"You start going to school. The desks are planks nailed together, and one teacher has to teach dozens of children at once.","events.5.fact":"UNESCO estimates that about 272 million children and young people worldwide are out of school (UNESCO GEM 2025)","events.6.text":"You go with the other children to the fields to gather firewood. There is no time for school.","events.7.text":"In summer the village well runs dry. You and your family walk a long way to fetch water.","events.7.fact":"Joint monitoring by WHO and UNICEF estimated that in 2024 about 2.1 billion people lacked safely managed drinking water (JMP 2025)","events.8.text":"You are 12, and you are sent to work in a small workshop for 10 hours a day.","events.8.fact":"ILO and UNICEF estimated that about 160 million children were in child labor in 2020 (ILO & UNICEF 2021)","events.9.text":"You and a friend go to play by a pond. On the way home, mosquito bites cover one of your arms.","events.10.text":"You start looking for work. You earn only a few dozen US dollars a month, as many people do.","events.10.fact":"The ILO estimates that about 58% of workers worldwide were in informal employment in 2024 (ILO 2025)","events.11.text":"Your phone connects to the internet for the first time. Pictures from far away appear on the screen.","events.11.fact":"The ITU estimates that about 65% of the world's population was online in 2023 (ITU 2024)","events.12.text":"You leave your hometown for the first time to look for work in the city.","events.13.text":"You get married. The wedding spends everything the family saved over a year, and everyone says it was a lovely celebration.","events.14.text":"Your child runs a high fever in the night. You carry him to the clinic and queue at the door for the whole morning.","events.15.text":"A crash happens at the intersection. You dodge it, but your friend does not.","events.16.text":"You have your first medical checkup, and your blood pressure is higher than you expected.","events.17.text":"At the end of the year, you and your family share a rich meal.","events.18.text":"Your checkup report says your blood sugar is high. The doctor says this is very common everywhere in the world.","events.18.fact":"The IDF estimates that about 590 million adults worldwide had diabetes in 2024 (IDF Diabetes Atlas 2025)","events.19.text":"Your memory is starting to slip. You forget where you put your keys.","events.19.fact":"The WHO estimates that about 57 million people worldwide had dementia in 2021 (WHO dementia fact sheet)","events.20.text":"Your grandchild says your name for the first time. You stare for a long while.","events.21.text":"The weather grows hotter day by day. You lie indoors all afternoon and do not go out.","events.22.text":"Your pay comes through, and the amount is a little more than last month.","events.23.text":"You work half a day at a small village shop, and the owner gives you a handful of coins.","events.24.text":"A late shift makes you miss your child's parents' evening at school. The next day you feel you owe him an apology.","events.25.text":"You dig out your old clothes, alter them, and wear them through another winter.","events.26.text":"You split your savings into 3 parts: one under your pillow, one in the bank, and one kept for emergencies.","events.27.text":"In the afternoon you doze on the sofa, and in your dream someone calls your name.","events.28.text":"You board a long-distance bus. Outside the window, field after field goes by, and you know no one.","events.29.text":"At the entrance of a big hospital you run into a fellow villager. He says city wages are double what they are back home.","events.30.text":"You quarrel over the money for the wedding, and neither of you gives way.","events.31.text":"Your child is born. You hold him and look at him for a long time, unable to say a word.","events.32.text":"You are the eldest at home. When your mother goes out to work, you carry your younger siblings to the river to wash clothes.","events.33.text":"Your schoolbooks are brand new, the first ones the family has ever bought for a child.","events.34.text":"You manage the family's money. Each month you split your wages into shares: one for your parents, one for your younger siblings' school fees, and the rest for yourself.","events.35.text":"You are always caught between the older and the younger ones. The older ones can go out and play, and the little ones need to be carried, so you are often left in the middle.","events.36.text":"Your clothes are usually hand-me-downs from your older brothers and sisters, their colour already faded. You do not mind, but it is the first thing you remember.","events.37.text":"You go to the city to work with your older brother for the first time. You sleep crowded together in one room, and he says this is how life was for their generation.","events.38.text":"You are the youngest son at home, and your older brothers and sisters all fight to hold you. Formula is often short, and your mother saves her own share for you.","events.39.text":"Your older brothers and sisters have all gone to school, and at home there is only you and your mother. You play alone in the yard each day, waiting for them to come back.","events.40.text":"Your textbooks are old books someone else used. Your older brother's name is written on the cover, and you rub at it many times but cannot get it off.","events.41.text":"Your mother does not know how to feed you, or that water must be boiled first. Your stomach often swells, and you cry for a long time.","events.42.text":"No one at home can help you with your homework. When you cannot work it out, you sit at the desk until it gets dark.","events.43.text":"On your payslip, every line has to be read aloud to you. You do not dare to sign, afraid of signing the wrong thing.","events.44.text":"Your mother remembers something she was taught at the hospital: pat the baby's back after every feed. She keeps doing it, and you rarely choke.","events.45.text":"Your father helps you look up words in the dictionary, and after supper he tells you about the books he has read.","events.46.text":"You find a free course online and spend several months of evenings finishing it.","events.47.text":"Your mother adds solid food according to the schedule in a book. She records your weight each day, and the notebook fills up quickly.","events.48.text":"There is a bookshelf at home. The first words you learned came from your parents' books.","events.49.text":"You go to school in the city. Your parents' old classmates live there, and they help you find a cheap room.","events.50.text":"After school you dare to run to the river and jump in with the older kids. The water is cold, but you laugh loudly.","events.51.text":"Whenever a car passes at the crossroads, you look both ways before crossing. The adults laugh that you are timid, and you don't mind.","events.52.text":"You take apart the family radio and find the thin wire inside. You want to know why it makes sound.","events.53.text":"You are lighter than other children your age, and your cry is faint. The doctor says you need to eat more, so your mother keeps feeding you.","events.54.text":"Your body has always been strong, and you rarely get sick. Your classmates say you have a lot of strength.","decisions.0.title":"Go to school, or stay home","decisions.0.body":"The adults at home talk it over for a long time. The village school is not far away, but the walk takes 2 hours each way. The family also needs someone to look after your younger brother.","decisions.0.fact":"Many school-age children still do not attend school because of poverty, transport costs and other barriers (UNESCO GEM 2025)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Go to school every day","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Knowledge↑ Household help↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You walk a long way to school every day. When you come home in the evening, your younger brother is already asleep.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay home and work","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Family better off · Knowledge↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stay home to look after your brother, and you learn all the farm work.","decisions.1.title":"Work in the city, or stay in the village","decisions.1.body":"A factory in the city is hiring, and its wages are higher than at home, but going means being apart from your family. The family's fields may not be enough to feed everyone.","decisions.1.fact":"Workers in informal jobs mostly have no stable income or social protection (ILO 2018)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go to the city to work","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Income↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You join a factory. By the end of the month, you have saved more than you did in a whole year at home.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay and farm at home","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Stable · Less income","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You spend the whole year in the fields. The harvest is not great, but the family still has enough to eat.","decisions.2.title":"A relationship","decisions.2.body":"Someone wants to build a life with you. You want a home, but you worry that money will get even tighter.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Marry and have children","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Family · Spending↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You move into a small house together. The year your child is born, money flows out of the family like water.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Live alone","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Freedom · Loneliness","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay on your own. After work each day, you buy a bowl of noodles at the corner and eat it slowly.","decisions.3.title":"A checkup","decisions.3.body":"Both your blood pressure and your blood sugar are high. The doctor says you must take medicine every day and change your diet.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Take medicine on time and change your diet","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Spending↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"Every morning you take a pill, and you eat far less salty food. Slowly your body begins to feel better.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Ignore it","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Easier · Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep eating and working as usual, and you don't take the doctor's words to heart.","decisions.4.title":"A phone call from home","decisions.4.body":"On the other end of the line, your father has been in the village hospital for a week. The doctor says someone needs to sit by his bed. You are in the busiest stretch of work, and your boss does not want to let you take leave.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Take leave and go home to care for him","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Family at ease · Income↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You go back to the village and keep watch at his bedside for 3 weeks. Only on the day he can get out of bed do you return to the city.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay in the city and earn money","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Income↑ · Uneasy at heart","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You send money home each day and call the nurses every day. Your father's condition slowly settles.","decisions.5.title":"Borrowed money","decisions.5.body":"A small house on the edge of the city is for sale. The down payment means borrowing a large sum from relatives, and the monthly repayments will weigh heavily on you.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Borrow money and buy it","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Security↑ · Spending↑↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You move in. The walls have just been painted. On the day each repayment is due, you check your account first.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Keep renting","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Spending↓ · Unstable","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You move house again. In an old building on the corner, you find another small room.","decisions.6.title":"Chest tightness at night","decisions.6.body":"You wake in the middle of the night with a tight feeling in your chest, and your left arm tingles a little. You are not sure whether it is just tiredness.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Get checked at the hospital the next day","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Health↑ · Takes a day","decisions.6.options.0.result":"The doctor runs an ECG and takes a blood sample. While you wait for the results, you sit in the corridor.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Wait it out and ignore it","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Easier · Risk↑↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You lie down and wait for it to pass. The next morning you go to work as usual, feeling you can still cope.","decisions.7.title":"Whether to stay in the old house","decisions.7.body":"Your legs are getting weaker, and the stairs take longer each day. Your children want you to move in with them, but you are more used to this old house, with its light and its neighbors.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Move in with your children","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Someone to look after you · Leave the familiar street","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You move into a small room at your children's home. Every morning you can hear people talking downstairs.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the old house and cook for yourself","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Familiar · Living alone","decisions.7.options.1.result":"Every day you buy your own groceries and cook your own meals. The stairs are steep, and you always hold the wall as you go down slowly.","decisions.8.title":"A shadow on the checkup","decisions.8.body":"Your follow-up results are in. The doctor says you need further tests as soon as possible, and you may need surgery or long-term treatment.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Accept treatment","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Health↑ · Spending↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"Treatment goes on for a long time. You go to the hospital once a week, and after each visit you have to lie down for a long while.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Skip treatment and go home","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Keep your routine · Risk↑↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You go home and carry on cooking and sitting in the sun. You do not want your last days to be spent in the smell of disinfectant.","decisions.9.title":"A winter cough","decisions.9.body":"Since winter set in, your cough has not stopped, and there are traces of blood in your sputum. The doctor says you can be admitted to hospital or take medicine at home.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Be admitted to hospital","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Health↑ · Leave home","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You stay in the ward for 2 weeks. Outside the window the plane trees have dropped their leaves, and each day you count the birds on the branches.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Take medicine at home","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Comfortable · Risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You lie in bed with a cup of warm water beside you. The cough gradually eases, and you think you have recovered.","decisions.10.title":"The family needs another worker","decisions.10.body":"Your father has hurt his back, and the family fields need someone to work them. School expects you there every day, but the fields need someone too.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Take leave and work the family fields","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Fields planted · Fall behind in class","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You take a semester off and plant the family's fields. When school starts again, you have fallen far behind.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Keep going to school","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Knowledge↑ Tiring","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You get up before dawn every day, finish the farm work, and then go to school. In class you often doze off, leaning on your desk.","decisions.11.title":"High fever in the middle of the night","decisions.11.body":"You run a high fever in the night, and your forehead is frightening to touch. Your mother does not know what to do, and the village clinic has already closed.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Wait until morning to go","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Less trouble · Risk↑↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You burn with fever all night. By dawn, you have stopped crying.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Carry you to the county hospital overnight","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Health↑ · Long way","decisions.11.options.1.result":"Your mother borrows a tricycle and walks through the night for two hours. The doctor gives you an injection, and your fever slowly breaks.","decisions.12.title":"The frozen river","decisions.12.body":"The river has frozen over, and across the bank a group of children is skating. The ice looks thin. You could try it, or take the long way home.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Step onto the ice","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Close · Risk of falling through the ice","decisions.12.options.0.result":"The ice cracks with a sharp sound. You crawl to the bank on your belly, soaked through.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Take the long way home","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Far","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You take a long detour, and by the time you get home it is already dark.","decisions.13.title":"A friend wants to borrow money","decisions.13.body":"You have saved up some money. A friend says he has a chance that could multiply it several times over, but it could also lose all of it.","decisions.13.options.0.t":"Put the money in the bank","decisions.13.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Little return","decisions.13.options.0.result":"You deposit a little every month, and the figure grows slowly.","decisions.13.options.1.t":"Lend the money to your friend","decisions.13.options.1.hint":"Goodwill↑ · Risk↑","decisions.13.options.1.result":"The money is lent out. The next year your friend says it was all lost, and his face stayed red for a long time.","decisions.14.title":"A book with no cover","decisions.14.body":"At a second-hand market you find a book with no cover. The things it describes are things you have never heard of in your village.","decisions.14.options.0.t":"Buy it and take it home to read slowly","decisions.14.options.0.hint":"Knowledge↑ Low cost","decisions.14.options.0.result":"You read it through page by page, and for a long time afterwards you keep thinking about the world inside it.","decisions.14.options.1.t":"Spend the money on a bowl of hot noodle soup","decisions.14.options.1.hint":"Full stomach","decisions.14.options.1.result":"You eat a bowl of hot noodle soup, and it is very good. A few days later you have forgotten the book.","decisions.15.title":"A job that needs strength","decisions.15.body":"A construction site is hiring loaders, who must carry heavy cement bags all day. The pay is half as much again as other work.","decisions.15.options.0.t":"Take the loading job","decisions.15.options.0.hint":"Income↑↑ Health↓","decisions.15.options.0.result":"You carry dozens of bags of cement each day, and your shoulders are rubbed raw. A month later you have saved a sum of money.","decisions.15.options.1.t":"Find an easier job","decisions.15.options.1.hint":"Income↑ Health↑","decisions.15.options.1.result":"You work in a small shop, taking payments at the till. The pay is less, but your hands are not so worn out.","decisions.16.title":"The school race","decisions.16.body":"The teacher is picking people for a running race. You are small, and when you run you always finish at the back.","decisions.16.options.0.t":"Sign up to take part","decisions.16.options.0.hint":"Stamina cost↑","decisions.16.options.0.result":"You finish the whole course. Your legs tremble for the rest of the day, but nobody laughs at you.","decisions.16.options.1.t":"Stay in the classroom and read","decisions.16.options.1.hint":"Quiet","decisions.16.options.1.result":"You finish reading a whole book in the classroom.","opening.title":"The family's first decision","opening.body":"You have not been born yet. The family must decide where you will be delivered: at the nearest clinic, or at home with a familiar birth attendant to help.","opening.options.0.t":"Give birth at the nearest clinic","opening.options.0.hint":"Risk↓ · Takes time on the road","opening.options.0.result":"You are born at the clinic. The attendant is skilled, and after cutting the cord she wraps you in a clean cloth.","opening.options.1.t":"Give birth at home","opening.options.1.hint":"No travel · Risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"The birth attendant is an elderly woman from the house next door. The room is dim, and her hands are steady, but there are no instruments and no medicine.","actions.forage.name":"Work","actions.forage.desc":"Go to school or to a job to earn money and learn skills. It is tiring, but the family depends on it to eat.","actions.hide.name":"Live frugally","actions.hide.desc":"Spend little, go out little, and avoid possible risks. You eat less, but life stays steady.","actions.rest.name":"Spend time with family","actions.rest.desc":"Stay at home with your children or parents, cook and eat together, and put money worries aside.","actions.explore.name":"Go out and try your luck","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your hometown or a familiar job for a new city or a new trade. There are more chances, and more risks.","actions.mate.name":"Start a family","actions.mate.desc":"Find a partner, settle down and have children. Children cost a lot of money and a lot of time.","actions.mate.done":"You have a child, and your household grows by {n}.","idle.0":"The street outside is noisy, and the sound comes through the window in waves.","idle.1":"From the kitchen comes the sound of oil in a wok. Someone is cooking supper.","idle.2":"The room is warm, and someone is humming softly.","idle.3":"The neighbor's children run around the yard, and their laughter never stops.","idle.4":"A water pipe groans once in the night, then goes quiet.","idle.5":"After a light rain the street shines, and people slow down.","idle.6":"Somewhere far off, firecrackers go off with a muffled sound, then stop.","idle.7":"As evening comes, the streetlamps come on one by one, and the people outside the windows thin out.","actions.forage.text.0":"You spend a whole day carrying bricks on the building site, and blisters rise on your palms.","actions.forage.text.1":"The factory bell rings, and you follow the crowd into the workshop.","actions.forage.text.2":"You stand at the cash register for 10 hours, and your feet no longer feel like your own.","actions.forage.text.3":"The night shift ends, and you ride your bike home with only the streetlamps for company.","actions.hide.text.0":"You write every expense in a small notebook.","actions.hide.text.1":"You skip your friends' get-together and put away another bit of money.","actions.hide.text.2":"You draw the curtains. The room is dim and very quiet.","actions.rest.text.0":"You help your child with homework, the lamp lighting both your heads.","actions.rest.text.1":"The smell of supper drifts from the kitchen. The table is small, but everyone squeezes in.","actions.rest.text.2":"You massage your parents' backs, and your father says your grip is getting stronger.","actions.explore.text.0":"You ride a long-distance bus for the first time, to a city you have never seen.","actions.explore.text.1":"You ask for directions in an unfamiliar shop, and you cannot quite follow the accent.","actions.explore.text.2":"At night, in your new little room, you spread out your bedding and think about where to go tomorrow.","actions.mate.text.0":"The two of you go to register your marriage, and you are both smiling in the photo.","actions.mate.text.1":"You try cooking in the kitchen. The first pot comes out a little too salty.","actions.mate.text.2":"Wedding firecrackers sound in the distance, and a relative holds your hand."},"krill":{"name":"Antarctic krill","tagline":"A small transparent crustacean that sits at the center of the Antarctic food web","survivalNote":"Antarctic krill typically live 5–6 years, and mortality in the larval stages is extremely high. This curve combines field estimates with a rough view of predation pressure","epitaph":"You survived several winters beneath the Antarctic ice. Your body was once a small store of food. In the end you became part of seabirds, seals and whales.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Crustacea › Malacostraca › Euphausiacea","size":"Adult body length about 6 cm","note":"Krill are not copepods. They belong to the Malacostraca, the same class as shrimp and crabs, and are 20 to 30 times longer than copepods.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth waters","birth.rolls.1.label":"Sea ice and algae in the year you were born","birth.rolls.2.label":"When your mother released her eggs","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs released by the same mother","birth.intro":"You are an egg, under 1 mm across, slowly sinking through {r0}. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs were released in your batch.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the open waters off the western Antarctic Peninsula","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the continental shelf near South Georgia","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the sea-ice edge of the Ross Sea, where algae bloom in spring","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"the deep waters of the Weddell Sea","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A strong year: thick sea ice and a thick algae layer at the ice edge","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"A typical year: sea ice and algae close to the long-term average","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"A weak year: little sea ice and a thin algae layer","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"A batch released in early summer","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"A batch released in midsummer, at the peak of the algal bloom","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"A batch released in late summer, when the season is already short","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (nauplius, calyptopis and furcilia stages)","stages.2.name":"Immature","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old","causes.0.short":"Swallowed whole by a baleen whale","causes.0.text":"A baleen whale opens its mouth and sucks you in together with thousands of your fellows. The filter plates keep the water in and let you go.","causes.1.short":"Eaten by a seal or penguin","causes.1.text":"A seal surges up from under the ice, and the swarm scatters. It only needs to bite you once.","causes.2.short":"Starved in winter","causes.2.text":"The algae under the ice have been eaten away through winter. Your stomach has been empty for months, your body shrinks bit by bit, and at last it stops.","causes.3.short":"Caught in a trawl","causes.3.text":"A large net opens from the stern of a ship, and its mouth draws in the water where you are. You are carried into the freezer hold of a processing vessel.","causes.4.short":"Eaten by copepods and fish","causes.4.text":"You are still sinking when a small fish plunges down from above, and you never get to eat a single alga.","causes.5.short":"Failed molt","causes.5.text":"Your old shell catches on your body, and you cannot pull yourself out of it. A few days later your body stops moving inside the shell.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"You swim more and more slowly, and at last you sink with the current. You settle into the soft mud of the seafloor and become part of other life there.","events.0.text":"The egg sinks slowly through the seawater. The deeper it sinks, the fewer things there are to eat it.","events.1.text":"You hatch. You cannot yet find food on your own, so you rely on the yolk inside your body.","events.1.fact":"Antarctic krill develop through several larval stages, relying on yolk in the early stages (Marr 1962)","events.2.text":"You eat a layer of green algae that hangs in the water like a thin mist.","events.3.text":"You swim in the shade of the algae layer beneath the ice. The light is faint, but the algae are plentiful.","events.4.text":"Winter arrives, and the sea surface freezes. Ice algae cling to the underside of the ice, and you move with the ice edge.","events.5.text":"You molt once, and your body grows a little larger.","events.5.fact":"Juvenile Antarctic krill molt many times before reaching adulthood (Marr 1962)","events.6.text":"You swim together with a large crowd of your kind. They roll their bodies together, and from a distance the swarm looks like a silver cloud.","events.7.text":"A seal bursts up from under the ice, and the swarm scatters. You dive into the densest part of the swarm.","events.8.text":"Food grows scarcer through winter. Your body is shrinking, and your energy use drops too.","events.9.text":"A blue whale surges up from below into the krill swarm, its open mouth swallowing the whole patch of water.","events.10.text":"You spawn for the first time, releasing several hundred eggs.","events.11.text":"At night you rise beneath the ice to feed on the algae layer, then sink back into the depths by day.","events.12.text":"The sound of a trawler reaches you from above, louder than anything you have heard before.","events.13.text":"A flock of penguins dives into the water. The krill around you scatter, and you drift down with the current.","events.14.text":"The seawater cools a little. You move more slowly, and you use up less energy.","events.15.text":"A piece of floating ice breaks above you, and the shards scatter ice algae into the water.","events.16.text":"Your abdomen has thinned, and your shell feels loose.","events.17.text":"Your swimming slows bit by bit, and your night-time rises grow shorter.","events.18.text":"You stop in a layer of algae beneath the ice, and your body slowly goes still.","events.19.text":"Whale calls sound in the distance, and your swarm turns together as if it were one body.","events.20.text":"You scrape off a small patch of ice algae. It is chewed in your mouthparts, and its green juice flows into your stomach.","events.21.text":"A line of your fellows lines up and takes turns scraping the algae from the underside of the ice. You squeeze into the line and take a bite.","events.22.text":"At night you rise beneath the ice, where the algae layer is thick. The shadow of a seal above moves too.","events.23.text":"The deep water is dark. Your energy use drops, but winter algae are far scarcer here.","events.24.text":"You stay in the deep water. Above the ice there is no sound.","events.25.text":"The swarm hangs in the water, and you can barely feel the current. Your energy use slows.","events.26.text":"You swim through an upwelling. Nutrients from the deep are carried up, and diatoms nearby suddenly multiply.","events.27.text":"Beneath an iceberg you find a patch of broken ice covered in ice algae.","events.28.text":"You release your eggs near the surface. They develop as they sink, and soon you cannot see them anymore.","events.29.text":"There are very few males this season, and only some of the eggs you release are fertilized.","events.30.text":"The ice-edge algae layer is very thick this year. As you scrape, the ice algae pile up in your mouth layer after layer, and your stomach fills quickly.","events.31.text":"Your batch has a great many companions, a dense mass beneath the ice that looks from afar like a silver blanket.","events.32.text":"You and your companions line up to scrape ice algae. This year the layer is enough to feed everyone.","events.33.text":"The ice-edge algae layer is very thin. You search the underside of the ice for a long time before you find a small mouthful.","events.34.text":"Many of your batchmates are gone. You have swum a long way and seldom meet another krill.","events.34.fact":"Krill abundance in parts of the Southern Ocean has been recorded to decline markedly over recent decades (Atkinson et al. 2004, Nature)","events.35.text":"You scrape off little ice algae, and your stomach is often empty. Your body grows lighter bit by bit.","events.36.text":"The algae at the ice edge are neither thick nor thin. You get a little to eat every day, and your body grows slowly but steadily.","events.37.text":"This winter is not a long one. The spring ice melts slowly, and the current carries you into a new layer of water.","events.38.text":"Your eggs sink through the early summer seawater. The water is still cold and development is slow, but the food around them has not yet been eaten up.","events.39.text":"You hatch earlier than many of your batchmates and catch the first algal bloom.","events.40.text":"You molt just as an upwelling passes by, bringing nutrients close to you.","events.41.text":"The eggs this season are numerous. A fine film of eggs drifts across the sea surface, and you sink along with them.","events.42.text":"The algal bloom this season is large. Whenever your mouthparts touch the algae, there is more food than you can eat.","events.43.text":"Your batchmates are numerous. You crowd into the same algae layer and bump into one another, swimming through what looks like a green fog.","events.44.text":"You were released late in the summer. As you sink, the water grows cooler day by day and your development slows.","events.45.text":"By the time you hatch, the algae layer has thinned. You can find only a little leftover algae at the edge of the ice, and your body quickly grows thin.","events.46.text":"You are a step behind your batchmates. They have already claimed the algae layer, so you have to go around to the edge to feed.","events.47.text":"You leave the group earlier than the others and swim to an empty algae edge, where you feed quickly.","events.48.text":"A strange sound comes from beneath the ice. Your companions are still hesitating when you sink into deep water.","events.49.text":"You notice a patch of light you have never seen before and move closer. It is a melting shard of ice, coated with a film of green algae.","events.50.text":"Your body is a little smaller than your companions'. In the swarm you are always pushed to the edge, so you feed where the current is weakest.","events.51.text":"Your shell is thick and your body strong. In one collision with a companion, you push it aside a short distance.","decisions.0.title":"The food under the ice is running out","decisions.0.body":"The algae at the ice edge have mostly been eaten through the winter. Open water farther out may be deeper, but food there is even scarcer.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Stay at the ice edge and shrink","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Energy use↓ Starve through winter","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You stay beneath the ice. Your body shrinks bit by bit, as if waiting for a spring that may never come.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Migrate to open water","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You leave the ice edge and swim toward the deeper water in the distance.","decisions.1.title":"The old shell splits","decisions.1.body":"A crack has opened in your old shell. Molting is necessary for growth, but molting now leaves your body vulnerable.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Molt immediately","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You pull yourself free of the old shell. The new shell is still soft, and you swim slowly.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait until food comes, then molt","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · The shell may split further","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You hold still and wait for more algae. The old shell splits another crack.","decisions.2.title":"A pod of baleen whales","decisions.2.body":"A pod of baleen whales comes charging from afar. Their mouths open, and the water rolls toward you like a wall.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Scatter","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You and your fellows scatter in every direction. A whale brushes past your side without opening its mouth.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Huddle together and move toward the surface","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You bunch into a dense ball and drift toward the surface. The whale's mouth opens.","decisions.3.title":"A net mouth is approaching","decisions.3.body":"The mouth of a trawl net is moving toward your patch of water, and the sound of the flow grows louder.","decisions.3.fact":"Annual Antarctic krill catches are in the hundreds of thousands of tonnes (CCAMLR catch statistics, 2022)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Swim ahead with the swarm","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safe · May be netted","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You swim ahead with the swarm. The current at the net's mouth sweeps you all inside.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Dive deep, away from the swarm","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You dive down alone. The sound of the swarm grows distant.","decisions.4.title":"The breeding season","decisions.4.body":"The spring algae have bloomed, and your ovaries are full. Releasing eggs now will cost you some strength.","decisions.4.fact":"Antarctic krill eggs hatch as they sink, and the larvae then swim back upward (Marr 1962)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Spawn now","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Stamina↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You release your eggs in the surface waters. They develop as they sink, and soon you cannot see them anymore.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Delay spawning and eat first","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Spawning delayed","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You feed for several more weeks. The eggs are still inside you, and there is no hurry.","decisions.5.title":"A thin algae layer at the ice edge","decisions.5.body":"You are a larva, living on the thin layer of ice algae at the ice edge. Spring water is warming, the ice is starting to melt, and the algae will scatter with it. You do not know where the next layer is.","decisions.5.fact":"Ice algae under sea ice are an important food source for Antarctic krill larvae (Flores et al. 2012, PLoS ONE)","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Stay at the ice edge and eat the thin algae","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Predators under the ice↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You stay at the ice edge, eating the thin ice algae. Now and then the shadow of a seal passes overhead.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Swim out into open water","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You leave the ice edge. There is almost no algae in the open water, but no seals come lunging up from beneath the ice either.","decisions.6.title":"The season of shortening days","decisions.6.body":"The light is shortening through the year. Algae in the upper waters are declining. The deep water is steadier in temperature, but food there is sparser, and you must live on what your body has stored.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay in the upper waters and keep feeding on algae","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You stay in the upper waters. The algae grow scarcer, and every mouthful is a race against the winter.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Dive into deep water to cut energy use","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Energy use↓ Starve","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You dive into the deep water. The water there is stable, and your movements slow.","decisions.7.title":"The last rise with the swarm","decisions.7.body":"Your body grows thinner, and your shell feels loose. The swarm is still rising at night. You can follow it, or stay in the depths and slowly sink.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Follow the swarm on its last rise","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You rise with the swarm and get a meal. A baleen whale comes charging from afar, the swarm scatters, and you slip past its flank.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the depths and never rise again","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe · No more feeding","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay in the depths. The water is cold, and your movements slow until they are barely visible.","decisions.8.title":"The algae at the ice edge is thinning","decisions.8.body":"The algae layer at the ice edge where you live is getting sparser. Farther out there is another bloom in open water, but it is a long swim, and you will lose more companions on the way.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Swim toward the distant bloom","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You head for the distant bloom. The journey is long, and fewer and fewer companions stay beside you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay put and wait for new algae to grow at the ice edge","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Steady · Less food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You stop beneath the ice edge and wait for new ice algae to grow. The waiting itself wears you down.","decisions.9.title":"The last layer of algae before winter","decisions.9.body":"Summer is almost over, and the algae layer at the ice edge is pulling back. You were born late, and your body has little in reserve.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Rush to the last algae beneath the floating ice","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You dash into the last algae layer under the floating ice and eat in a hurry. The shadow of a seal slides past the edge of the ice.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Dive to deep water and get through winter early","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Energy use↓ Steady","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You dive into deep water, and slowly your body needs less food.","decisions.10.title":"The fishing boat's lights","decisions.10.body":"The lights of a fishing boat flicker on the water, and food scraps drift in their glow. Bolder members of your kind have already swum over.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Move toward the lights and grab scraps","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You swim to the edge of the light and grab a small mouthful of scraps. Water rushing through a net sounds in the distance.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Keep away from the lights and return to dark water","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You leave the light and sink back into the dark water. There is no food there, but there is no net either.","opening.title":"The mother chooses a depth to release eggs","opening.body":"You are still an egg. Your mother is releasing you all into the waters at the ice edge. The eggs will slowly sink, and after they hatch, the larvae will slowly swim upward. Where you begin depends on the depth at which the eggs leave the mother's body.","opening.options.0.t":"Release them in the shallow waters at the ice edge","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Upper-level predators↑","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs spread out beneath the ice edge. The algae on the floating ice are close, but seals and fish are swimming nearby too.","opening.options.1.t":"Release them in deep water","opening.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","opening.options.1.result":"You are released into deep water. The eggs sink for a long time before they hatch, and by then the upper-level predators are far away.","actions.forage.name":"Scrape ice algae","actions.forage.desc":"Scrape a layer of ice algae off the underside of the sea ice, or filter-feed on phytoplankton in the upper water.","actions.hide.name":"Dive into deep water","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the shallow water at the ice edge and sink to a lightless layer hundreds of meters down, away from seals and birds.","actions.rest.name":"Hover with the swarm","actions.rest.desc":"Hover in the water with your swarm, drifting slowly with the current and using almost no energy.","actions.explore.name":"Swim toward new algae","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your swarm and look for an algae bloom beneath the ice somewhere else.","actions.mate.name":"Release eggs","actions.mate.desc":"Females release eggs in the upper water, and the eggs develop as they sink.","actions.mate.done":"You release {n} eggs in the upper water, and they develop as they sink.","idle.0":"The water around you is cold, though your body does not register much about it.","idle.1":"The light here is faint and tinged with green.","idle.2":"The seawater flows slowly, carrying you in different directions.","idle.3":"All you can sense is a slight change in water temperature and current.","idle.4":"A cluster of tiny creatures you cannot make out brushes past you and is gone.","idle.5":"Far above, the shadow of the sea ice presses lightly on the surface.","idle.6":"You do not know where you are, only that many of your kind are around you.","idle.7":"The difference between day and night is nearly impossible to feel here.","actions.forage.text.0":"You cling to the underside of the ice and scrape a layer of green ice algae into your mouth.","actions.forage.text.1":"The algae layer flickers in front of your mouthparts.","actions.forage.text.2":"You filter-feed in the upper water, your body stretching and contracting.","actions.hide.text.0":"The light in the deep water is faint, and your color slowly darkens.","actions.hide.text.1":"You drift slowly in the shade, using less energy than during the day.","actions.hide.text.2":"The whale calls overhead are farther away than you expected.","actions.rest.text.0":"You drift slowly with your fellows.","actions.rest.text.1":"The swarm turns slowly like a silver cloud.","actions.rest.text.2":"You stop swimming and let the current carry you.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim beneath a shadow of ice, and the light ahead suddenly brightens.","actions.explore.text.1":"You find a new layer of ice algae and eat quickly.","actions.explore.text.2":"The swarm in the distance is much larger than yours.","actions.mate.text.0":"You swim up to the surface waters at night, your eggs now ripe.","actions.mate.text.1":"A male comes close and delivers his spermatophore to you.","actions.mate.text.2":"The eggs drift out of you one by one and slowly sink into the water."},"lanternfish":{"name":"Lanternfish","tagline":"Possibly one of the most numerous vertebrates on Earth. By day you stay in dark deep water; at night you rise toward the surface","survivalNote":"Mesopelagic fish typically live 1–4 years, and early mortality is very high. This curve combines estimates from predation pressure and fisheries research","epitaph":"Most of your life was spent in water without sunlight. Your light-producing organs were the only way you communicated with the world around you, and almost no other eyes could see them.","taxon":"Chordata › Actinopterygii › Myctophiformes","size":"Most are 2–15 cm long","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth sea area","birth.rolls.1.label":"Sea conditions in the year you were born","birth.rolls.2.label":"Season of birth","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, drifting near the surface in {r0}. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs were laid in your batch, and most of them will be eaten by something else.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the open subtropical waters of the North Pacific","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the temperate waters on the northern edge of the Southern Ocean","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the plankton-rich waters near the Canary upwelling","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"above a deep basin in the western Indian Ocean","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"A La Niña year, when upwelling brings deep nutrients up and plankton is dense","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"An El Niño year, when warm surface water suppresses upwelling and plankton is sparse","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"An ordinary year, with nothing unusual about the sea","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"Spring, when phytoplankton has just begun to bloom","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Summer, with warm water, and the eggs hatched quickly","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Winter, with cold surface water and little plankton","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (surface)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (begins vertical migration)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old","causes.0.short":"Seized by a squid","causes.0.text":"A squid reaches out of the darkness with its tentacles and wraps around you. Its suckers clamp down in an instant.","causes.1.short":"Chased down by tuna","causes.1.text":"A school of tuna rushes down from above, and your group is scattered. You do not have time to find the cover of your light.","causes.2.short":"Snatched from the surface by a seabird","causes.2.text":"A seabird dives from the air, and its wings barely raise a splash. You vanish into its beak.","causes.3.short":"Eaten as an egg","causes.3.text":"You are still an egg when a copepod swallows you, along with the eggs around you.","causes.4.short":"Starved","causes.4.text":"On the way up at night, you cannot find enough zooplankton. Your body grows lighter bit by bit, until at last you stop in the water.","causes.5.short":"Chased by dolphins","causes.5.text":"A pod of dolphins leaps up beneath your school, and the sound carries far through the water. You scatter in every direction, but only one of you fails to escape.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your glow grows dimmer, and your swimming grows slower. At last you sink into water without light.","events.0.text":"The egg drifts near the surface, like a small transparent bead.","events.1.text":"You hatch into a larva. You are very small, with no light organs yet, and you can only drift with the current.","events.2.text":"In the surface waters you catch a copepod larva and crunch it up, bite by bite.","events.3.text":"By day you swim in sunlight. Your shadow falls clearly beneath the surface, and you are easy to see.","events.4.text":"A cloud of drifting phytoplankton detritus passes slowly, and you eat part of it.","events.5.text":"The first light organs begin to appear along your sides. Their glow is faint and only visible in the dark.","events.6.text":"By day you dive several hundred meters. The light fades layer by layer, and the water grows colder.","events.7.text":"At night you rise to a layer tens of meters deep, feeding with other fish on zooplankton.","events.8.text":"The light organs on your belly flicker, matching the brightness of the moonlight overhead. Your shadow disappears.","events.8.fact":"Lanternfish and other mesopelagic fish use ventral photophores to produce light similar to the light from above, which erases their shadows (Herring 2002, The Biology of the Deep Ocean)","events.9.text":"A squid reaches out of the darkness with its tentacles, and you flash and dart away just in time.","events.10.text":"You spawn for the first time, and the eggs drift in the upper waters.","events.11.text":"At dawn the light in the water begins to brighten. You sink with your school into deeper water.","events.12.text":"At night your school rises. You eat one copepod, then another.","events.13.text":"A school of tuna rushes down from above, and your group is scattered.","events.14.text":"Dolphins leap out of the water far away. Their calls travel through the deep water and reach you faintly.","events.15.text":"Your light organs flash in the darkness, like a small patch of moving stars.","events.16.text":"A warm current carries you to a place rich in prey.","events.17.text":"Another school of fish passes nearby. You avoid each other and each swim toward your own depth.","events.18.text":"Your swimming slows, and the nightly rises grow shorter.","events.19.text":"Some of your scales have fallen off, and your light organs have dimmed.","events.20.text":"Your ovaries are empty. You no longer rise toward the surface.","events.21.text":"At night, near the surface, you bump into a cluster of krill larvae, and your body jerks as you bite into one.","events.22.text":"At night you rise to a layer tens of meters deep, your mouth full of copepods.","events.23.text":"At dusk you feed happily in the shallows. A school of silver herring presses down from above, and you are scattered.","events.24.text":"In the deep water, a small glowing jellyfish drifts by, and its light mixes with yours until you can no longer see each other clearly.","events.25.text":"You stay in deep water for a long time. A scale drops off, and the water around you is cold.","events.26.text":"You hover in a layer of warmer water. Your body's light slowly fades, then slowly returns.","events.27.text":"A squid's tentacle reaches out of the darkness, then pulls back.","events.28.text":"You swim into a cold water mass, where several unfamiliar small fish move in schools through the shadows.","events.29.text":"You come across a murky patch of water. Plankton is abundant there, and the water is darker.","events.30.text":"You release a batch of eggs. They drift in the upper waters, carried by the sea in different directions.","events.31.text":"Phytoplankton is dense in the surface water. Every stretch you swim brings you to a clump of food.","events.32.text":"In the layer you rise to at night, krill and copepods are more plentiful than in other years. You finish one meal and can eat another.","events.33.text":"The surface water is warm and thin. You swim a long way and find only a few mouthfuls to eat.","events.34.text":"Water temperature stays high all year. Your metabolism speeds up with it, and you grow hungry sooner than other fish.","events.35.text":"In spring the surface water turns green with phytoplankton, patch by patch. You eat more than the other larval fish, and your body grows fast.","events.36.text":"You crowd together with fish born in the same season. When you rise at night, your light organs join into one glowing band that is easy to see.","events.37.text":"The water is warm and you grow fast, but the surface food grows scarcer by the day.","events.38.text":"The summer surface is bright, and there are more seabirds than usual. For several days you dare only approach the surface at night.","events.39.text":"The surface water is cold and zooplankton is scarce. You slow down and take each mouthful with great care.","events.40.text":"In winter the water layer is deep and almost no light reaches it. Your light organs glow in the darkness, the only bright spot in the area.","events.41.text":"A school of tuna rushes down from above. You do not dive deeper. Instead you slip along the edge of the school and snap up a copepod that was startled into the open.","events.42.text":"You wait a long time in deep water, until the fish and seabirds above have moved far off, before you slowly rise.","events.43.text":"Far off, a jellyfish's tentacles flash. You swim over for a look, and its light blends with yours until you lose track of time.","events.44.text":"You are a little smaller than the companions around you. When the group rises, you always fall to the back, chasing whatever plankton is left.","events.45.text":"Your body is sturdier than other fish. When the group charges into a patch of krill, you push to the front and eat more than the others.","decisions.0.title":"Dusk","decisions.0.body":"Night falls, and your school begins to rise. The surface holds abundant food, and it is also where hungry tuna hunt.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Rise with the school","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You feed until full in the dark surface waters. Somewhere in the distance a patch of silver splashes, but you do not look.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay in deeper water","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · A little hungry","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stay in deeper water, your light flashing, until dawn.","decisions.1.title":"A school of tuna","decisions.1.body":"The shadow of a school of tuna presses down from above. They are swimming fast, and your group begins to scatter.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Dive into the dark deep water","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You plunge into the darkness. The tuna's shadow glides over you without stopping.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Blend into the school and flash your lights","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Danger↑↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"Your light organs flash together in the water. The tuna turn, but they crash straight into the middle of you.","decisions.2.title":"Light at the surface","decisions.2.body":"Daylight brightens the surface. Seabirds wheel overhead, and their shadows slide back and forth across the water.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Stay at the surface and feed","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Seabirds↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You find a clump of food at the surface. A seabird drops down above you, then takes off again.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Sink into the lower layer","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You sink, and the sunlight behind you slowly fades.","decisions.3.title":"Time to spawn","decisions.3.body":"The eggs in your body are ripe. The upper waters are warm enough for the eggs to develop, but seabirds and fish schools gather there too.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Spawn in the upper water","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You release your eggs into the upper water. They drift apart and are soon out of sight.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay in the depths and delay spawning","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Offspring↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep to the deep water. The eggs are still inside you, and there is no hurry.","decisions.4.title":"The first dive","decisions.4.body":"Your body begins to sense changes in light. The daytime surface is bright, and seabirds circle above it. You can learn to sink to deeper water by day, or stay at the surface and keep feeding.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Dive several hundred meters by day","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You sink, and the light fades layer by layer behind you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and feed","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Tuna↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You stay near the surface to feed. A school of tuna rushes down from above, and you sink just in time.","decisions.5.title":"How bright to glow","decisions.5.body":"Adult lanternfish can match their light to the daylight overhead, making their shadows vanish. But when they glow, they are also easier for other eyes to see in the dark.","decisions.5.fact":"Lanternfish and other mesopelagic fish use ventral photophores to produce light similar to the light from above, which erases their shadows (Herring 2002, The Biology of the Deep Ocean)","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Keep glowing and rise with the school","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Seen↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You rise together with the school, glowing. The lights of the school join into one band, and you catch a lot of food.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Dim your lights and swim alone","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Stealth↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You dim your lights and swim alone at the edge of the school. It is dark around you, and no one notices you.","decisions.6.title":"The last few rises","decisions.6.body":"Your body grows lighter, and the nightly rises grow shorter. The school is still rising, and you can follow it, or stay in the depths and let your body slowly come to rest.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Rise with the school for one last meal","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You feed until full in the dark water layer. A squid's tentacle brushes past you, and you turn just in time toward the middle of the school.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the depths and stop rising","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe · No more feeding","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stop in the darkness of the depths and no longer rise. The water around you is still, and now and then a faint light flashes.","decisions.7.title":"The thin surface","decisions.7.body":"The surface water is warm and thin, and plankton is sparser than usual. You can stay at the surface and hunt slowly, or sink into colder water and conserve what you have.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Stay at the surface and keep hunting","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You swim back and forth in the warm water and find a few mouthfuls. The shadow of a seabird skims the water, and you do not look at it.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Sink into cold water and conserve","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You sink, and your body slows. The color of the warm water fades above you.","decisions.8.title":"Krill carried up by upwelling","decisions.8.body":"Upwelling has carried deep nutrients here, and a large swarm of krill is rising. You can go all out to feed, or eat only some and save your strength.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Feed all out","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You keep eating until your belly glows. The light of the swarm joins into one band around you, and you do not notice the water around you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Eat about seven-tenths full and stay in the middle of the group","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Safe↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You eat part of the krill, then swim back to the middle of the group. The krill are still rising, and you stop chasing them.","decisions.9.title":"Cold water in winter","decisions.9.body":"You have stayed in cold water for a long time, and your movements slow a little more each day. There is a little food higher up, but the water there is brighter and more dangerous.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Stay in cold water and cut back on energy use","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You stop in the cold water, your fins barely moving. All winter you hardly move.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Rise to warmer water to look for food","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You swim up a short distance and eat a small clump of copepods. A squid's tentacles flash in the dark.","decisions.10.title":"The edge of the group","decisions.10.body":"The group is rising, and at its edge a lone juvenile has lost its glow. Krill are drifting in front of it, one patch after another. You can rush to the front, or follow the group.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Rush to the front and grab that patch of krill","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You grab a mouthful. Other fish are pushed aside, and one swerves sharply beside you without catching up.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Follow the group and do not push ahead","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safe↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You rise with the group. You leave the lone juvenile behind and do not look back.","opening.title":"The mother chooses the layer to spawn in","opening.body":"You are still an egg. Your mother is swimming in the upper waters. By day she stays several hundred meters down, and only at night does she rise. The layer where eggs are released should be near the light of the surface, or deeper, farther from seabirds.","opening.options.0.t":"Release eggs in the surface waters","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Seabirds↑","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs drift apart in the upper water and are soon wrapped in the color of plankton. Seabirds circle over the surface in the distance.","opening.options.1.t":"Release eggs in a deeper layer","opening.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Less food","opening.options.1.result":"The eggs drift apart in deeper water. There are no seabirds there, but plankton is also scarce, and you must rely on your yolk for a long time.","actions.forage.name":"Rise to feed at night","actions.forage.desc":"After dark, rise with your school to a layer tens of meters deep and eat copepods and krill. Do not do this by day.","actions.hide.name":"Dive by day","actions.hide.desc":"By day, dive into dark water several hundred meters deep to escape birds and tuna.","actions.rest.name":"Hover still","actions.rest.desc":"Stop in one layer of water, flicking your fins gently to save energy.","actions.explore.name":"Swim to another layer","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your school and head for a layer where the temperature and light are different.","actions.mate.name":"Spawn in the upper water","actions.mate.desc":"Mature females release eggs in the upper water, where the eggs float near the surface, held up by the warm water.","actions.mate.done":"You release {n} eggs in the upper water.","idle.0":"The water temperature is slowly changing. You can feel it, but you cannot say why.","idle.1":"The water around you brightens and darkens, as if it were slowly breathing.","idle.2":"A jellyfish drifts past, its tentacles wrapping around you briefly before loosening.","idle.3":"The current pushes you in one direction for a while, then slowly lets go.","idle.4":"You cannot tell whether it is day or night above you; you can only judge time by changes in the water temperature.","idle.5":"A cluster of tiny creatures drifts in the water and is soon eaten by something else.","idle.6":"In the distance a faint light appears, then fades again.","idle.7":"Food in this water comes and goes, and you do not pay much attention to it.","actions.forage.text.0":"In the dark water layer you eat a copepod, then swim a little farther.","actions.forage.text.1":"The water at night is dark, and the light organs on your belly flicker.","actions.forage.text.2":"A cluster of zooplankton slides past in front of you, and you open your mouth and swallow it.","actions.hide.text.0":"The light fades layer by layer, and your body's color darkens with it.","actions.hide.text.1":"Your light organs glow faintly, matching the light overhead.","actions.hide.text.2":"The water grows colder, and your movements slow.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stop in the water, flicking your fins gently.","actions.rest.text.1":"The current pushes you toward the depths, then slowly lets go.","actions.rest.text.2":"Your light slowly fades, then flickers back on after a while.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim into a warm current, where there are more zooplankton than elsewhere.","actions.explore.text.1":"In the distance on the water's surface there is a blurred shape, and you do not know what it is.","actions.explore.text.2":"You cross a boundary where the water temperature changes suddenly, and a chill runs through your body.","actions.mate.text.0":"You swim toward the warm water above.","actions.mate.text.1":"The eggs in your body are ripe, and you slowly relax.","actions.mate.text.2":"The eggs drift apart in batches, carried by the sea in different directions."},"layer_hen":{"name":"Laying hen","tagline":"Your wild ancestors laid only a dozen or so eggs a year. You are expected to lay about 300","survivalNote":"Laying hens are usually culled at 70–80 weeks of age, once egg production falls. Early mortality and accidental deaths during laying are approximated from average commercial farm figures, so this is a rough estimate","epitaph":"You lived about a year and a half. The eggs you laid far outnumber the eggs your wild ancestors laid in their whole lives.","taxon":"Chordata › Aves › Galliformes › Phasianidae","size":"About 1.5–2 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Housing system","birth.rolls.1.label":"Line","birth.rolls.2.label":"Conditions in the year you hatched","birth.siblingsLabel":"Chicks hatched in the same batch","birth.intro":"You break out of your shell in a hatchery, covered in yellow down. You are {sex}. The place where you will live is {r0}. About {siblings} chicks hatched in your batch.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a large battery-cage house, where each hen has a little less room than an A4 sheet of paper","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a barn with floor housing, where you can walk about and there are nest boxes and perches","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a free-range farm, where you can walk on grass outside during the day","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a White Leghorn line: light-bodied, with a high egg count","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a brown-egg line: bigger-bodied than the white hens, and a bigger eater","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a crossbred line from local village chickens: lower egg count, but hardy","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a stable year, with cheap feed","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a poor maize harvest, with expensive feed and farms cutting costs","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"avian flu in a neighboring province, with every farm guarding against outside visitors","stages.0.name":"Chick","stages.1.name":"Rearing period","stages.2.name":"Laying period","stages.3.name":"Before culling","causes.0.short":"Culled and sent to slaughter","causes.0.text":"Once egg production drops, the farm loads you into a transport crate and sends you to the slaughterhouse. Your last use is as chicken meat products.","causes.1.short":"Keel fracture","causes.1.text":"Your breastbone cracks in a fall. After that you cannot keep your footing, and you slowly starve.","causes.2.short":"Cloacal prolapse","causes.2.text":"Years of intense laying push part of your oviduct out of your body. No one on the farm comes to deal with it.","causes.3.short":"Pecked to death by other hens","causes.3.text":"A group of hens corners you and pecks at your head and back. You cannot escape from the corner.","causes.4.short":"Respiratory or intestinal infection","causes.4.text":"You cough and lose your appetite. After a few days you no longer get up.","causes.5.short":"Foot caught in the wire floor","causes.5.text":"Your toes get caught in the gaps of the wire floor. You struggle all night and never manage to pull free.","causes.6.short":"Taken by a bird of prey","causes.6.text":"On the grass of the free-range yard, a hawk dives from the sky. You do not reach the shelter in time.","events.0.text":"The male chicks from your batch are sorted out after hatching, put into another box, and quickly disposed of. The chicks around you are still calling.","events.0.fact":"Since 2022, German law has prohibited killing male chicks soon after hatching without a justifiable reason (German Animal Welfare Act)","events.1.text":"You are packed into a cardboard box with dozens of other chicks and jolted along to the farm.","events.2.text":"During rearing you practice pecking, flapping your wings, and rolling in sand. Many farms have no room for these instincts.","events.3.text":"Hens crowd around the drinker. A larger hen pecks your head, and you duck to let her pass.","events.4.text":"The feed trough is filled automatically. You eat quickly, and something catches in your throat.","events.5.text":"You lay another egg. It rolls down the sloped nest and lands on a conveyor belt, which carries it away quickly.","events.6.text":"You lay your first egg. Before that, calcium in your body was mobilized bit by bit. Your wild ancestors laid about 12 eggs a year in the wild. You are expected to lay about 300.","events.7.text":"The wire floor is hard, and your toes slip on it again and again.","events.8.text":"The farm has withheld feed for several days, in an attempt to induce a molt. Once you molt, laying starts again.","events.9.text":"Your breastbone aches dully after a fright. You begin to avoid the other hens.","events.9.fact":"EFSA (2023), scientific opinion on laying hen welfare: keel bone fractures are common in all housing systems","events.10.text":"In a corner, a hen is pecking at her own feathers, and small feathers lie scattered on the floor.","events.11.text":"The lights in the house stay on. You cannot tell where day ends and night begins.","events.12.text":"Egg production has clearly dropped. Farm workers walk among the flock, counting the hens one by one.","events.13.text":"You are pulled out and put into a cramped transport crate.","events.14.text":"In the next cage, a hen's foot is caught. She calls for a long time, and no one comes.","events.15.text":"You perch on a bar for the first time. It is more comfortable than the wire floor.","events.16.text":"You walk out onto the grass of the outdoor yard, pecking at grass roots and insects. The weather is fine, and the grass is green.","events.17.text":"The cage is crowded, and your wings can barely open. Every day you can only stand or crouch.","events.18.text":"You bend down and peck at the pellets in the trough. A hen pushes in, and you step aside.","events.19.text":"The feed trough in front of the cage is narrow. You stretch your neck to reach the few pellets there.","events.20.text":"On the grass you peck up an insect. It wriggles a few times, and you swallow it quickly.","events.21.text":"You tuck yourself into a corner of the shed, with a wall at your back. From here you cannot see the door.","events.22.text":"You squeeze into a corner of the cage and tuck your head under your wing. The wire floor presses against your feet.","events.23.text":"You crouch on the wire floor, and your body slowly relaxes. The lights stay on, and you sleep lightly.","events.24.text":"You crouch with a few other hens, pressed together. It is warm under your wings, and you can hear the breathing of the others.","events.25.text":"You walk along the aisle to the far end of the house. The floor there is a little damp, and a few hens are pecking at it.","events.26.text":"You take a few steps back and forth in the cage, and you are soon blocked.","events.27.text":"You walk to the fence of the free-range yard. There are many insects in the shade of the trees, but birds are always calling overhead, and you do not dare to stay long.","events.28.text":"Your eggshell is white, easy to spot on the conveyor belt. Your daily egg count is written on a whiteboard.","events.29.text":"You are light, so you can reach the feed trough quickly among the flock.","events.30.text":"The White Leghorn's laying period is concentrated. In the weeks before culling, you almost stop laying.","events.31.text":"Your eggshell is brown. Your body is heavier than the white hens', and laying takes more effort.","events.32.text":"You eat more than the white hens in your batch. The workers add a little extra feed at the trough, but you always feel it is not enough.","events.33.text":"The brown hens' feathers came in fully during rearing. You grow taller by the day, and the hens in your house watch you.","events.34.text":"Your body is sturdy and your legs are strong. The other chicks are dozing, but you are already digging in the sand for insects.","events.35.text":"You lay few eggs, and your shells are thick. On the record sheet, a special mark appears beside your name.","events.36.text":"You scratch for a while on a small patch of ground outside the house and find a few earthworms. This is how your ancestors survived.","events.37.text":"Feed is cheap this year, and the trough is almost always full.","events.38.text":"The farm has never stopped the feed. You do not go hungry, but the house is more crowded.","events.39.text":"The farm bought a new batch of feed. On the day it was changed, the hens lowered their heads and sniffed the feed in the trough, one grain at a time.","events.40.text":"The feed price has risen, and the farm has cut costs. The feed in the trough shrinks a little more each day.","events.41.text":"To save money, the farm has lengthened the periods without feed. Your weight drops noticeably.","events.42.text":"The hens next to you are pecking at scraps on the floor. You lower your head too and pick them up one by one.","events.43.text":"News arrives of avian flu in a neighboring province. A disinfection pool appears at the house door, and everyone entering must step through the disinfectant.","events.44.text":"The house door stays shut tight and is not opened once all winter. The air is stuffy, and you cough a few times.","events.45.text":"A few hens suddenly stop moving. Workers in masks come in and put them into bags.","events.46.text":"You are the first to jump out of the cardboard box and stumble around the floor of the brooding room.","events.47.text":"The moment you see a person, you shrink into a corner. When the keeper reaches for a hen, you are always the last one caught.","events.48.text":"You wander around the house and look over every corner. You remember where the dust is and where the wire is.","events.49.text":"You are lighter than the other chicks in your batch, and they keep pushing you away from the feed tray.","events.50.text":"Your breastbone is thick and strong. When the keeper lifts you to weigh you, the scale reads higher than that of the other hens.","decisions.0.title":"At the drinker","decisions.0.body":"There is only one drinker, and you and several hens reach it at the same time. A larger hen lowers her head and signals you to move aside.","decisions.0.fact":"The 'pecking order' was first described in hens by Schjelderup-Ebbe in 1922 (Zeitschrift für Psychologie)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Step aside","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Less water","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You back off to one side and wait a long time before you get a drink.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Fight back","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Injury↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You peck back. The hen charges, and your head is pecked open.","decisions.1.title":"No nest","decisions.1.body":"You want to find a quiet corner to lay, but the cage has no nest, only a wire floor.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Lay on the wire floor","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"The egg may break","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You crouch down, and the egg rolls across the wire and falls through.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Hold on and wait","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Stress↑ Laying delayed","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You do not lay. The egg inside you slowly builds up.","decisions.2.title":"A perch","decisions.2.body":"The house has a row of high perches. Up there you can get away from the other hens, but jumping down from a height can injure you.","decisions.2.fact":"EFSA (2023): perches benefit the behavior of laying hens, but falling from perches can also cause keel bone fractures","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Climb up","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Risk of falling","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You jump up onto the perch and stand steady. On the way down your claws slip, and your chest strikes the metal.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Huddle in the corner","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Stress↑ Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You huddle in the corner and can only hear the hens above jumping up and down.","decisions.3.title":"The door is open","decisions.3.body":"The small door to the free-range yard is open. Outside there is grass and a patch of shade. Far above, a red-tailed hawk is circling.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Walk outside","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk of predation↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You peck at grass roots and find insects. A gust of wind passes, and only then do you notice the shadow overhead.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay in the shed","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay in the shed, pressed together with your flock mates. The grass outside stays green.","decisions.4.title":"The feed suddenly stops","decisions.4.body":"The farm has stopped feed for several days. Only a thin layer of crumbs is left in the trough, and hens in your shed are pecking along its edge. This may be meant to force a molt, or the feed truck may simply be late. None of these choices changes the day you will be culled.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Eat sparingly and wait for feed","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↓ Health↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You peck a few times, then crouch down and wait. The feed truck does not come.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Fight for another hen's feed","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Conflict↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You squeeze in beside another hen's trough and eat a few pellets. That hen pecks at you once.","decisions.5.title":"A hen pecking at her own feathers","decisions.5.body":"In a corner, one hen is pecking at her own feathers, one by one. Other hens nearby have crowded in and started pecking at her back. You can move closer, or walk away.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Move closer to her","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safe↓ Health↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You peck at a few of the feathers on her back. She shrinks away, and the gap between you narrows.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Walk away","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You walk to the other side, where hens are also pecking, but none of them peck you.","decisions.6.title":"The lights are still on","decisions.6.body":"The lights in the house stay on, even at night. The corners are darker, but more hens crowd there. The area near the feeder is brighter and noisier. None of these choices changes the day you will be culled.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Hide in the corner","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You tuck yourself into the corner and close your eyes. The feed trough in the distance is never approached.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Go to the feeder under the light","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Health↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You eat a few mouthfuls of feed under the light. The glare makes your eyes ache.","decisions.7.title":"Eggs are getting fewer","decisions.7.body":"You have been laying fewer eggs lately. The farm workers walk among the hens, and you can feel them watching. You can push to lay a few more, or lay less and save your strength. None of these choices changes the day you will be culled.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Push to lay more","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Eggs↑ Health↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You lay a few more eggs, and for the next few days even standing up feels exhausting.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Save your strength","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Health↑ Fewer eggs","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You lay a few fewer eggs, and your body slowly recovers a little.","decisions.8.title":"Places at the trough","decisions.8.body":"You are bigger than the white hens in your batch and eat more. Room at the trough is limited, and the white hens are already crowding both sides.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Push into the middle of the trough","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Pecked↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You shove your way into the middle of the trough and eat your fill. The hens around you peck at you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Step aside and wait for feed to come to the front","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Safe↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You step back to the side and wait for fresh feed to come down the conveyor.","decisions.9.title":"The disinfection pool at the door","decisions.9.body":"A basin of disinfectant has been placed at the door. The house door is shut, and inside it is stuffy and warm. When the door opens, the cold outside air rushes in.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Stay in the house and do not go out","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You do not go near the door once all winter.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Walk to the door and look at the grass outside","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You walk to the door and breathe in a lungful of cold air from outside. A few days later, you begin to cough.","decisions.10.title":"The feed stops again","decisions.10.body":"After the feed price rose, the farm stopped the feed again, for longer than last time. Only a layer of crumbs is left in the trough.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Eat sparingly and wait for feed","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↓ Health↓","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You peck a few times, then crouch down and wait. The feed truck is slow to come.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Squeeze in at another hen's trough","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Conflict↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You push into another trough and eat a few grains of feed. That hen pecks you once.","decisions.11.title":"A hole in the corner wall","decisions.11.body":"There is a hole in the corner wall, and light from outside comes through it. You could squeeze through to take a look, or stay in the house.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Squeeze through and take a look","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Curious · Chilled","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You squeeze through the hole. The grass outside is damp and cold. You stand there a long while before going back into the house.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Stay in the house","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Safe · Bored","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You stay in the house and watch the light in the corner slowly move away.","decisions.12.title":"An unfamiliar nest box","decisions.12.body":"In a corner of the house is an unused nest box, lined with straw and higher than your cage. You would have to jump up to see inside.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Jump up and look","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Curious · Chest injury↑","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You jump up, your claws slip, and your chest strikes the edge of the box.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Stay on the ground","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You walk a circle on the ground and return to where you were.","decisions.13.title":"Beneath the feed trough","decisions.13.body":"The edge of the trough is filled by other hens, so you can only pick up the feed that has fallen beneath it.","decisions.13.options.0.t":"Keep picking up scraps underneath","decisions.13.options.0.hint":"Food↓ Health↓","decisions.13.options.0.result":"You pick up scraps underneath for a long time, one grain at a time into your beak.","decisions.13.options.1.t":"Push up to the top","decisions.13.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Pecked↑","decisions.13.options.1.result":"You squeeze to the top and eat a few good mouthfuls. The hen beside you butts your head with its beak.","opening.title":"A place in the cardboard box","opening.body":"You have just hatched, covered in yellow down, and you are being packed with dozens of other chicks into a cardboard box for the trip to the farm. The middle of the box is crowded and warm, and the edges are cooler, but no one is pressing on you there.","opening.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle of the box","opening.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Risk of being crushed↑","opening.options.0.result":"You push into the middle of the pile of chicks, and soon you warm up. On the bumpy road, several chicks underneath are crushed, and you do not look.","opening.options.1.t":"Stay at the edge of the box","opening.options.1.hint":"A little cool · Not crushed","opening.options.1.result":"You lean against the cardboard, and cold air comes through the gaps. No one presses on you during the trip, but you shiver the whole way.","actions.forage.name":"Eat at the feed trough","actions.forage.desc":"Walk along the feed trough and peck at the feed. In a cage, you can only stretch your head out to reach it.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the aisle, where people walk, and tuck yourself into a corner of the shed or cage.","actions.rest.name":"Crouch and stay still","actions.rest.desc":"Crouch on the ground or the cage floor, moving as little as possible to rest your body.","actions.explore.name":"Walk around the house","actions.explore.desc":"Walk along the aisle to see the other parts of the house. In a cage, this is only a very short walk.","idle.0":"It is dim here, with light falling only from above.","idle.1":"Your claws step across the floor, one at a time, and it feels a little cold.","idle.2":"A hen in the corner preens her feathers, then stands up again.","idle.3":"Someone walks by in the distance, and the footsteps slowly fade away.","idle.4":"The feed smells faint. You lower your head, eat a few mouthfuls, and look up again.","idle.5":"You crouch down, and your body tingles a little.","idle.6":"You do not know what day it is.","idle.7":"Wind taps the tin roof of the shed softly.","actions.forage.text.0":"You bend down and peck at the pellets in the trough.","actions.forage.text.1":"A hen pushes in, and you step to the side.","actions.forage.text.2":"The trough is shallow, and the feed is soon eaten up again.","actions.hide.text.0":"You tuck yourself into a corner of the shed, with a wall at your back.","actions.hide.text.1":"The footsteps outside slowly fade away.","actions.hide.text.2":"You tuck your head under your wing and stay quiet for a while.","actions.rest.text.0":"You crouch down, and your body slowly relaxes.","actions.rest.text.1":"The light stays on, and you sleep lightly.","actions.rest.text.2":"Your toes feel numb, so you shift your weight to the other foot.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the aisle, your claws stepping on hard ground.","actions.explore.text.1":"You take a few steps back and forth between the cages.","actions.explore.text.2":"Somewhere nearby, someone is changing the feed, and a layer of feed spills across the floor."},"lion":{"name":"African lion","tagline":"The lion is the only cat that lives in groups","survivalNote":"Cub mortality is high; most studies suggest nearly half of all cubs do not survive their first year. Males are often driven out of the pride at three to four years old. Adult lifespan is roughly 10 to 14 years. This curve is a rough estimate.","epitaph":"You slept through countless afternoons on the plains. The grass will grow back, and the pride will take in new lions.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Carnivora › Felidae","size":"Body length (excluding tail): males about 1.7–2.5 m and 150–200 kg; females about 120–150 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"State of the pride","birth.rolls.2.label":"Population","birth.rolls.3.label":"Pride size","birth.siblingsLabel":"Cubs in your litter","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}, your eyes still shut. {r1}. You are {sex}, and there are about {siblings} cubs in your litter.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the tall grass of the Serengeti plains","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a thicket beside the Mara River","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the reedy swamp edge of the Okavango Delta","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"grassland outside a national park, near a cattle pasture","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a settled pride, with a male still holding the territory","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a new male has just driven out the old one, and the pride is unsettled","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"the Serengeti grassland, where herds of wildebeest and zebra migrate through each year","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"Kruger National Park, where prides roam between the thornveld and the river valleys","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"Gir Forest in Gujarat, India, the last wild population of Asiatic lions","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"a fenced reserve, where people supplement the pride's food","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a large pride of more than a dozen lions, with several lionesses and a few males","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a small pride of five or six lions, with two or three lionesses","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a lone lioness with her cubs, after an outside male drove her from her old pride","stages.0.name":"Cub (hiding in the grass)","stages.1.name":"Cub (following the pride)","stages.2.name":"Subadult (preparing to leave)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Killed by an intruding male","causes.0.text":"A strange male charged into the pride. He bit you twice and threw you into the grass. Your mother's call was far away, and you could not crawl back to her.","causes.1.short":"Surrounded by spotted hyenas","causes.1.text":"A pack of spotted hyenas closed in at the edge of the grass. You ran, and they dragged you into the tall grass, where your cries were quickly drowned out by their laughter.","causes.2.short":"Gored by a buffalo","causes.2.text":"A Cape buffalo turned on the pack and drove its horn into your flank. The wound became infected within days, and you never got back on your feet.","causes.3.short":"Killed by a male fighting for territory","causes.3.text":"A coalition of males pushed you to the edge of a cliff above the river. You tried to run, but they caught your hind leg in their teeth.","causes.4.short":"Herder's revenge (poisoned bait)","causes.4.text":"The herders had lost cattle. They left a poisoned carcass beside the cattle pen. After you ate it, you soon felt your body go cold.","causes.5.short":"Shot by a poacher","causes.5.text":"In the night a gunshot rang out in the distance. Before you could run, the lioness beside you had fallen, and a bullet struck your shoulder.","causes.6.short":"Starved (drought, little prey)","causes.6.text":"Months of drought drove the game off the plains. Your ribs showed more and more, and you did not survive the next rainy season.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"A tooth fell out, and your bite grew weaker. You lay down beneath a tree and never got up again.","events.0.text":"You lie hidden deep in the grass. Your mother comes back to feed you only every few hours, then goes off again.","events.1.text":"Your mother carries you back to the pride. Your eyes have just opened, and the light is harsh.","events.2.text":"You drink your mother's milk, warm and rich. The other cubs push and shove around you.","events.3.text":"Footsteps of another lion approach through the grass. Your mother presses you down into the earth.","events.4.text":"Hyenas call in the distance, their cries sounding like human laughter. You make yourself smaller.","events.5.text":"The pride surrounds a zebra in the morning light. You follow your mother, learning to lower your body and creep closer.","events.6.text":"The days grow hot, and you rest together in the shade. A lion may sleep 15 to 20 hours a day.","events.6.fact":"Lions spend most of the day resting in the wild and can sleep 15 to 20 hours a day (Schaller, The Serengeti Lion, 1972)","events.7.text":"A lioness presses you flat in the grass and tells you not to move, waiting for the buffalo herd to pass.","events.8.text":"A spotted hyena catches the scent of prey and trails the pride. Its laughter sends you scattering.","events.9.text":"You follow the pride across a dry riverbed. The mud is full of elephant tracks.","events.10.text":"A young cub's leg is struck by a hoof. It cries on the ground all night. By morning, it has stopped crying.","events.11.text":"You catch your first gazelle on your own. Its struggle soon ends. You bite slowly, as if you are still learning.","events.12.text":"You leave the pride and walk for two days before realizing you have entered another male's territory.","events.13.text":"You and your brothers bite at each other's necks across the grassland, practicing for fights.","events.14.text":"A buffalo charges from the riverbank, and its horn tears open your foreleg.","events.15.text":"In the rainy season the grass grows tall. You lie in it while flies gather on you, and no amount of shaking drives them off.","events.16.text":"The pride ambushes a buffalo at night. Its horn strikes your shoulder, and you hold on to its nose and do not let go.","events.17.text":"The riverbank is hot at midday. You lie beside your mother, your tail flicking now and then at flies.","events.18.text":"A male shadows you through the grass, growling low every few minutes.","events.19.text":"You give birth to three cubs. Their eyes are still closed, and they hide deep in the thicket. You dare to return only once a day.","events.20.text":"The pride walks the edge of the rainy-season range. In the distance you see a large herd of cattle.","events.21.text":"A herders' party passes at night with torches held high. The lions hold their breath in the grass.","events.22.text":"A study says the number of African lions has fallen considerably over the past twenty years. In your pride, no new males have been seen for a long time.","events.22.fact":"In 2023 the IUCN listed the African lion as Vulnerable, based on a range decline of about 36% over roughly 21 years, or three generations (CMS, 2023)","events.23.text":"A tooth falls out, and your bite grows weaker. You can eat only the scraps the pride leaves behind.","events.24.text":"A young male watches where you lie. He is waiting for you to leave.","events.25.text":"You watch the newborn cubs of the pride. Their eyes are still blue.","events.26.text":"A herd of wildebeest comes surging across the far side of the grassland, like a broad river. You crouch with the pride beside the road and wait for them to come closer.","events.27.text":"The wildebeest on the grassland number in the tens of thousands. You pick out a lone one and bring it down in the grass by the river.","events.28.text":"Far off, the grassland has been burned, and black stubble stretches to the horizon. You walk along the edge of the burn and wait for the herds to return.","events.29.text":"The brush along the river valley is dense. You hide in it through the afternoon. Far away an elephant walks between the trees, and you stay where you are.","events.30.text":"There are many water buffalo in the scrub. You lie behind a tree and wait for them to walk into range.","events.31.text":"A jeep stops by the road, and the tourists inside raise their cameras. You ignore them and keep sleeping in the grass.","events.32.text":"At night the village dogs bark without stopping. You move slowly along the forest edge and do not enter the village.","events.33.text":"At the forest edge you chase down a deer. Gir has fewer deer than the grassland, so each chance takes a long wait.","events.34.text":"A forest guard rides a motorbike past on a distant track. He comes every day to count you.","events.34.fact":"Gir Forest is the only wild habitat of the Asiatic lion. A 2020 census recorded about 674 lions (Gujarat Forest Department 2020)","events.35.text":"The reserve fence is high, and the hyenas outside cannot get in. You grew up inside it and seldom hear the calls of the wild.","events.36.text":"Each evening staff leave a dead cow on a wooden platform, and the pride gathers below it. You eat well, but you have not hunted on your own for a long time.","events.37.text":"You walk slowly along the fence and find no way out. The grassland outside looks very far away.","events.38.text":"Several lionesses in the pride look after the cubs together. You lie among the cubs while one lioness licks your fur.","events.39.text":"The pride chases down a water buffalo together. You bite its hind leg while the other lions close in from all sides, and you get a share of the meat.","events.40.text":"The lionesses drive off a hyena together in the afternoon. They bite hard, and you stay to the side and do not join in.","events.41.text":"Five or six lions take turns watching the cubs. One lioness goes out to hunt, and you sleep in the shade all day.","events.42.text":"When you hunt, there are not enough of you, and the zebra runs off into the grass. All you can do is gnaw at bones others have left behind.","events.43.text":"Far off, a male lion growls once. The lionesses in the pride lift their heads, glance over, then lie down to sleep again.","events.44.text":"Your mother hunts alone while you wait in the grass. When she comes back, there is no meat at her mouth, only grass.","events.45.text":"A new male walks up and drives you and your mother deep into the grass. He roars from a distance, and you stay hidden.","events.46.text":"You hide beneath a dry riverbed. The grassland grows brighter every day, and you grow hungrier every day.","events.47.text":"You are the first to break out of the grass and pounce at a lizard. It runs fast, and you roll twice on the ground.","events.48.text":"You lie in the den for a long time, until the wind blows from the other side and you confirm there is no hyena scent outside.","events.49.text":"You walk onto a patch of grass you have never visited. An ant climbs onto your paw, and you look at it for a long time.","events.50.text":"You are the smallest of your litter. Your mother feeds your siblings first, and you can approach only after they have eaten.","events.51.text":"Your shoulders are broad and your mane is thick. In a squabble over food with a companion, you shove it into the grass.","decisions.0.title":"Leaving your father's pride","decisions.0.body":"You are nearly three years old. The males of the pride have begun to growl at you and no longer let you near the females. Your mane is coming in, and your voice is deepening. You can stay, or you can leave now.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave to look for a new pride","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"May find a mate · Risk on the way","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You and your brothers walk out of the grassland. The roars behind you fade, and ahead lies unfamiliar grass.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay and form a coalition with your brothers","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Holds ground · Shares prey","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You and the other males lie together, licking each other's necks. They will be your most reliable companions from now on.","decisions.1.title":"A new male arrives","decisions.1.body":"A coalition of males calls out across the grassland, moving toward the pride. The old male has already been driven off. If the newcomers take over the pride, they may kill the cubs.","decisions.1.fact":"After taking over a pride, new males often kill the previous male's cubs. Females resist by hiding their cubs and mating with the new male (Packer & Pusey, American Naturalist, 1983)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Hide the cubs deep in the grass","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Cubs safer · Less Food","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You lead the cubs into a dense patch of grass. Your body covers their cries.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay with the pride and feign submission","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Close to the new males · Risk of being killed","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You move toward the males. They sniff your body, then walk toward the cubs.","decisions.2.title":"A buffalo herd","decisions.2.body":"At the far side of the grassland, a herd of Cape buffalo is moving slowly toward the river. At the back of the herd walks an old bull that has fallen behind. His horns are large, and he looks dangerous.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Ambush the straggler","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk of a horn wound","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You rush in together and pull him down into the mud by the river. His horn grazes your flank.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Wait while the females attack, then scavenge what is left","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little Food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"The lionesses drive the buffalo into a mud pit. You are left with a single hind leg.","decisions.3.title":"Drought","decisions.3.body":"The rains have not come. The river shrinks by the day, and game on the plains grows scarcer. The pride must decide whether to follow the water or hold its current territory.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Follow the pride toward the river","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Water · Long way","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You walk for five days. The grass by the river has been trampled flat, but a few buffalo are still drinking there.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hold the current territory","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Familiar · Food shrinks daily","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep watch over the dry riverbed. Every day you wait, and every day the rain does not come.","decisions.4.title":"The herders' cattle","decisions.4.body":"Inside a cattle pen at the edge of the grassland, many cattle are penned behind wooden fences at night. The herders have built a fire nearby, and dogs bark without stopping.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Raid the pen at night","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk of being poisoned by herders","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You bite through one of the fence posts and drag out a calf. The herders' torches flare up behind you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay with the wild prey","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little Food","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You leave the pen and return to the heart of the grassland. There is far less game there than before.","decisions.5.title":"The old male's place","decisions.5.body":"You can now stand beside a coalition of males as an equal. But another coalition is moving toward your territory, and you must decide whether to hold it.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Defend the territory and fight for it","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Keeps the females · May be killed","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You bite at each other across the grassland until the rivals retreat. A cut is left on your ear.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Go to the edge of the territory as a lone male","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"No fight · No pride","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You leave the pride and sleep alone in a distant thicket for several days.","decisions.6.title":"Old, but still in the pride?","decisions.6.body":"Several of your teeth have fallen out, and you can no longer keep up on the hunt. The young males of the pride are watching you, waiting for your place to open.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay on the edge of the pride","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Secure · Less Food","decisions.6.options.0.result":"Each day you eat only what others leave behind. You are still awake, and you are still in the pride.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Walk alone into the heart of the plains","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Less Food · Not driven out","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You walk into a grassland with no lions in it. It is quiet there, but there is nothing left to eat.","decisions.7.title":"The gate of the enclosure","decisions.7.body":"A staff member is repairing the fence beside the enclosure, and the gate has not been locked properly. Outside is grassland, with no one bringing food, but with plenty of wildebeest and zebra.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze out through the gap and hunt on the grassland","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Risk of being shot by poachers","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You squeeze through the gap, and the grassland wind rushes into your nose. Somewhere in the distance a shot cracks, and you lie flat and wait for a long time.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the enclosure and wait for food","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe · Well fed","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You return to the middle of the enclosure and lie down. In the evening the staff leave a dead cow on the wooden platform.","decisions.8.title":"A new male lion","decisions.8.body":"Your pride has been taken over by an outside male. He roars from the grass, and your mother lowers her head and keeps you behind her. You can follow your mother away, or stay and watch him.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Follow your mother to another grassland","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ · Less food","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You walk for several days. Only a few vultures circle in the sky over the grassland.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay and wait for the male to leave","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food assured · May be bitten","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You hide in the grass and listen to the male growling in the distance. When he finally walks away, your body has several new scratch marks.","opening.title":"Where your mother chooses to give birth","opening.body":"You are not yet born. While your mother carries you, she must find a place to hide her cubs. Tall grass on the plains is close to water, but male lions pass through it often. A thicket is hidden, but far from the river, and there is less prey nearby.","opening.options.0.t":"Among the tall grass","opening.options.0.hint":"Close to water · Males pass often","opening.options.0.result":"You are born in the tall grass. Your mother's body presses over you, and far away a male lion growls low.","opening.options.1.t":"The edge of the thicket","opening.options.1.hint":"Hidden · Far from prey","opening.options.1.result":"You are born in the thicket. Leaves and branches screen you well, and the outside sounds suddenly grow distant.","actions.instinct.name":"Follow instinct","actions.instinct.desc":"Keep to the rhythm of the pride and do what lions usually do.","actions.forage.name":"Hunt","actions.forage.desc":"Ambush prey with the pride, or catch small prey on your own.","actions.hide.name":"Lie low in the grass","actions.hide.desc":"Lie still in the tall grass, waiting for prey to come near or for danger to pass.","actions.rest.name":"Sleep in the shade","actions.rest.desc":"Find the shade of a tree during the day and save your strength for hunting at night.","actions.explore.name":"Patrol the territory","actions.explore.desc":"Walk the boundary of the territory and check for other prides or herds.","actions.mate.name":"Mate","actions.mate.done":"You give birth to {n} cubs. They hide deep in the thicket and will be a long time before they can follow the pride.","idle.0":"You slowly roll over in the grass, your ears turned toward the wind.","idle.1":"A fly lands on your nose, and you shake your head.","idle.2":"The grassland stretches to the horizon, without a single tree.","idle.3":"The females lie around you, now and then reaching out a paw to tap you.","idle.4":"The grassland is noisy at night. You hear hyenas, antelope, and fires in the distance.","idle.5":"You yawn, showing two rows of long canines.","idle.6":"A flock of starlings flies past you and settles on a buffalo's back.","idle.7":"The river shines in the sun, and you squint at it for a long time.","actions.forage.text.0":"You lower your body and creep closer across the grass, inch by inch.","actions.forage.text.1":"The prey's tail twitches, and the pride rises all at once.","actions.hide.text.0":"You press your body into the grass, showing only your eyes.","actions.hide.text.1":"Footsteps pass in the distance, and you wait another hour.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie in the shade of a tree, your tail flicking now and then at flies.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk the boundary slowly, stopping every so often to sniff the ground.","actions.explore.text.1":"A hyena calls in the distance, and you both stop to look.","birth.rolls.0.options.4.0":"the bushveld of Kruger National Park in South Africa","birth.rolls.0.options.5.0":"the dry woodland of the Gir Forest in India","birth.rolls.0.options.6.0":"the grassland of a fenced reserve"},"mayfly":{"name":"Mayfly","tagline":"Most of its life is spent underwater; the adult usually lives only about a day","survivalNote":"Nymph mortality underwater is very high. This curve is a rough estimate based on the annual cycle of temperate species and predation pressure from fish and aquatic insects. Adults live only about a day.","epitaph":"You lived underwater for nearly a year, and in the air for about a day.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Insecta › Ephemeroptera","size":"Adult body length about 1–2 cm (excluding tail filaments)","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"River conditions","birth.rolls.2.label":"Year of emergence","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid by the same mother","birth.intro":"You are an egg, about half a millimeter long, wrapped in jelly and anchored {r0}. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs were laid in the same batch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"beneath the gravel of a mountain stream","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"on a patch of sediment in soft mud at a lake's edge","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"on the bottom of a pond thick with water plants","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"on a silty shallow of a murky riverbed","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a clear mountain stream with clean stones","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a river that takes in farmland drainage, with some nutrients in the water","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"below a reservoir, where the water temperature is steadier than elsewhere","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"a recently polluted river with very low dissolved oxygen","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a warm, humid evening with a huge swarm of adults","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a delayed emergence season, with the river still cold","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a drought year, when the water retreated to very low places","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Nymph (underwater)","stages.2.name":"Subimago (winged)","stages.3.name":"Adult","causes.0.short":"Eaten as an egg","causes.0.text":"A small aquatic insect crawled out from under a stone and swallowed you along with the jelly around you.","causes.1.short":"Eaten by a stonefly nymph","causes.1.text":"A stonefly nymph seized your abdomen in its mouthparts. In the struggle, several of your gill plates broke off.","causes.2.short":"Swallowed by a trout","causes.2.text":"A trout leapt from below the surface. Its mouth opened, and you were swept into its throat along with a mouthful of water.","causes.3.short":"Caught by a swallow","causes.3.text":"A swallow turned sharply low over the water. You could not get out of its way.","causes.4.short":"Suffocated in polluted water","causes.4.text":"Sewage released upstream used up the oxygen in the river. Your gills spread wide, but found nothing to take in.","causes.5.short":"Riverbed dried up","causes.5.text":"For several weeks no rain fell, and the river shrank to a few pools. You could not crawl to the water that was left.","causes.6.short":"Caught in a spider's web","causes.6.text":"Your wing struck a fine thread and stuck fast. The spider crept slowly toward you from the other side of the web.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your wings slowly stopped beating. You landed on the water and drifted away with the current.","events.0.text":"Inside the eggshell, the embryo's cells divide again and again. You are wrapped in jelly, anchored beneath a stone.","events.1.text":"Water flows over the stone, bringing a little dissolved oxygen.","events.2.text":"A small aquatic insect crawls past and brushes your jelly layer, without stopping.","events.3.text":"You scrape a thin layer of algae and debris from the stone and pass it to your mouth with your mouthparts.","events.4.text":"Thin, leaf-like gills line the sides of your abdomen, stirring gently in the water and constantly moving it across your body.","events.4.fact":"Mayfly nymphs have leaf-like gills on the abdomen (Brittain 1982)","events.5.text":"You molt. The old skin stays on the stone, translucent, with the shapes of its gills and legs still visible.","events.6.text":"A stonefly nymph peeks out from behind the stone. You loosen your grip on the rock and sink a little.","events.7.text":"The current quickens and sweeps you away from your perch. You grab hold of another stone.","events.8.text":"A thin layer of mud at the edge of the riverbed dries in the sun. You move to the wet side.","events.9.text":"A trout swims slowly down from upstream, a single flick of its tail carrying it past. You stay still beneath the stone.","events.10.text":"Winter comes, and the water temperature drops sharply. Your movements slow, and you almost stop feeding.","events.11.text":"In spring the water warms, and algae grow quickly. You eat far more than in winter.","events.12.text":"Your wing buds swell slowly along your sides. With each molt they grow a little larger.","events.13.text":"A factory downstream releases a foaming discharge. The oxygen in the river thins, and your gills keep opening and closing.","events.14.text":"You climb out of the water and cling to a reed. Your back splits open, and a thin pair of wings unfolds.","events.15.text":"Your wings are still damp. You rest on a leaf and wait for them to dry.","events.16.text":"At dusk your companions gather above the water at the bank, thousands of them rising and falling together.","events.17.text":"A thin mist settles over the water, and the moonlight turns it pale. You fly low over the surface for a stretch.","events.18.fact":"Adult mayflies have no functional mouthparts and do not feed (Brittain 1982)","events.18.text":"You open your mouth, but there is nothing. Your gut has been empty for a long time.","events.19.text":"A swallow skims past the edge of the swarm. You scatter together, then slowly gather again.","events.20.text":"A spider's web hangs where you were about to fly. You go around it, and nearly collide with another.","events.21.text":"Your abdomen swells, and the cluster of eggs inside shifts slowly.","events.22.text":"A bat slices through the swarm. It scatters suddenly, then slowly gathers again.","events.23.text":"You scrape a thin layer of dark diatoms from the stone. They taste faintly of earth.","events.24.text":"Your mandibles extend and scrape a fuzzy layer of organic debris from the stone into your mouth.","events.25.text":"You slip into a crack in the stone, your body pressed against the cold rock wall. The sound of the current outside suddenly recedes.","events.26.text":"You rest on the underside of a reed, your wings folded over your back. Wind sweeps across the water, and you stay perfectly still.","events.27.text":"You rest on the stone, barely moving, your gills stirring slowly.","events.28.text":"You rest on a leaf and wait for your wings to dry.","events.29.text":"You drift along the current over a stretch of gravel and find a new stone. Its underside has a fresh layer of algae.","events.30.text":"You leave the swarm and fly downstream along the river, with only a few companions left beside you.","events.31.text":"A male seizes you. You join in the air, then drop together toward the water. Your cluster of eggs is pushed out of your abdomen one by one.","events.32.text":"You catch a female. You hang together in the air for a moment, then fly apart again.","events.33.text":"The water is cold, and the algae film on the stones is thin. You sway your gills in the water and feed slowly on tiny debris.","events.34.text":"A warm current comes down from upstream, and the river is so clear you can see the pebbles on the bottom. You molt once, and the old shell stays clinging to a stone.","events.35.text":"Runoff from the farmland brings a smell of fertilizer. Green algae cover the stones, and you eat your fill every day.","events.36.text":"Many dead animals lie in the mud at the bottom of the river. You feed on them, but also smell a sour odor you cannot name.","events.37.text":"When the reservoir releases water, the river suddenly shallows. You wait a long time on a wet stone until the water returns.","events.38.text":"The water temperature in this stretch is steadier than anywhere else. When you emerge, you are barely touched by the cold.","events.39.text":"The river grows darker day by day. Your gills move more and more slowly, until at last you can only breathe by clinging to a stone.","events.40.text":"An oily sheen floats on the river, and your companions drift out in great numbers. You hide beneath a stone and do not go out.","events.41.text":"At dusk, thousands upon thousands of adults rise at once and block the light on the water. You find a gap among them.","events.42.text":"You have just flown out of the water, and all around you companions are taking off. A few of them strike your wings.","events.43.text":"The wind is cold on the day you emerge. As your wings unfold, a gust blows you onto the water.","events.44.text":"Adults fly slowly in the cold. You land on a reed and wait for the sun to come out.","events.45.text":"The river has receded, and the stone you were on is almost out of the water. You shuffle along the wet edge of the stone for a long time.","events.46.text":"The riverbed has half dried. When the adults take flight, the water surface smells of sand everywhere.","events.47.text":"You leave the crevice where you usually stay and swim into an unfamiliar rapid. The current tumbles you over a few times, but you find a patch of algae that no one has eaten.","events.48.text":"The shadow of a trout slides over the water. You press tightly under the stone and wait until the shadow is completely gone.","events.49.text":"You are smaller than the nymphs of your batch. The algae film on the stone was claimed by others first, so you have to nibble at the hard, thin growth on its underside.","decisions.0.title":"Depth and temperature","decisions.0.body":"On the riverbed, shallows are warm and fast, with abundant algae, but also plenty of fish. The deep water is cold and slow, but safer.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Stay in the cold water of the depths","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Grow slowly · Safe","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You crawl deeper along the riverbed. The water cools, and your movements slow.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Swim into the warm shallows among the water plants","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Grow fast · Lots of fish","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You climb to the roots of the water plants, where algae grow all around you. Trout know you are here too.","decisions.1.title":"The water at dusk","decisions.1.body":"Night is falling. You have just climbed out of the water, and your wings are still wet. Beneath the reeds on the bank, there is a patch of shade, where your companions are gathering above the surface.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Fly into the dusk swarm","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Find mates · Birds and fish nearby","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You mix into a large cluster of companions. Swallows are there too.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Hide under the plants on the bank until dark","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · Miss the swarm","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You cling to the underside of a leaf until the swarm breaks up, then slowly lift into the air.","decisions.2.title":"A female flies into the swarm","decisions.2.body":"A female crashes into your swarm. Her wings glow in the night, only a few centimeters from you.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Catch her and mate","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Mating · Higher risk of falling into water","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You join together in the air, then slowly sink together. The water is right below you.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Fly to the grass on the bank","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · No mating","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You let her go, and the air current carries her back into the swarm. You rest on the grass until dawn.","decisions.3.title":"A swarm of males above you","decisions.3.body":"Males dance above the water, their faint calls merging into one sound. One male flies toward you.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Fly into the swarm and let him catch you","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Mating · Higher risk of falling into water","decisions.3.options.0.result":"He seizes you. You hang together in the air for a moment, then drop down together.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Fly to the bank and wait for the next swarm of males","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Miss the chance","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You settle on a grass stem. Another swarm of males flies past in the night, and you do not move.","decisions.4.title":"Falling onto the water","decisions.4.body":"Your body is very light now. You can feel the cluster of eggs in your abdomen. Below is a calm stretch of water, and in the distance a trout leaps.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Drop onto the water and lay your eggs","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ · Higher risk of falling into water","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You dip toward the surface several times. The eggs scatter and sink into the cracks of the stones. A trout opens its mouth beneath you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Fly back to a branch on the bank and look for another patch of water","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Offspring↓ · Safe","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You return to a branch on the bank and lay your eggs above a patch of shallow water.","decisions.5.title":"The river turns murky","decisions.5.body":"Upstream, the water has turned cloudy, and layers of foam drift past. The dissolved oxygen has thinned. Your gills spread wide, but the water does not seem to be enough.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Slip into the gaps between the gravel","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You sink into the gaps between the gravel, where the current is slow and there is a little more oxygen.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and graze on algae","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Low-oxygen risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You eat a few mouthfuls of algae from the stone. Foam keeps drifting past, layer after layer.","decisions.6.title":"Trout hovering in the current","decisions.6.body":"A trout hangs in the current, its tail moving gently, waiting for whatever the water carries down. It is only a few stones away from you.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Hide behind a stone and stay still","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"The trout waits a while with its mouth open, then swims away.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Keep feeding in the current","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being eaten↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You scrape at the algae in the current a while longer. The trout does not come back.","decisions.7.title":"A stonefly behind the stone","decisions.7.body":"A stonefly nymph peeks out from behind the stone and creeps toward you. You can let go and sink to the bottom, or keep clinging to the stone and feeding.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Let go and sink to the bottom","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You settle onto the sand of the riverbed, and the stonefly crawls over the stone above you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Keep clinging to the stone and eating","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being bitten↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You eat a few more mouthfuls, but the stonefly has already crept up beside you. It does not bite. It just stops and watches for a while.","decisions.8.title":"A pile of rotting algae by the water","decisions.8.body":"Algae from farmland drainage have piled up in a thick layer by the stones, and the bottom has begun to smell. Fish are swimming back and forth there too.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Burrow into the algae pile and eat","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You burrow into the algae pile and eat your fill. A few days later, your body is coated with a sticky layer of mud.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Wait under a stone","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You hide beneath a stone, and wait until the fish have swum far away before slowly crawling out.","decisions.9.title":"The wind is cold and your wings are still wet","decisions.9.body":"You have reached the subimago stage and can feel the wind on your body. The water surface is cold, and your wings have not yet fully dried.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Fly up right away","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You spread your wings, and the wind carries you toward the grass on the bank.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Rest on driftwood until the wind drops","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You wait on the driftwood until the wind falls away.","decisions.10.title":"A flash of light on the water","decisions.10.body":"A bright light flickers on the water. You can feel that it is close to you, but you do not know what it is.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Swim toward the light","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You swim up to the surface and discover it was only a reflection.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay under the stone","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You do not go up. The light soon disappears.","opening.title":"Which stretch of water your mother chooses","opening.body":"You are still an egg, wrapped in jelly. Your mother flies back and forth above the water, choosing a stone where she can set you down. Fast shallows carry plenty of oxygen, but a single flood can wash the eggs away. Calm, deep pools are stable, but the soft mud at the bottom holds little oxygen.","opening.options.0.t":"Attach to a stone in the fast shallows","opening.options.0.hint":"Plenty of oxygen · Flood risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"Your jelly sticks to the underside of a stone, and the current keeps rushing over it. Your egg breathes well, but every flood tests it.","opening.options.1.t":"Place it in the soft mud of a calm, deep pool","opening.options.1.hint":"Steady current · Low oxygen","opening.options.1.result":"You sink into the soft mud, where the water barely moves. It is quiet here, but the mud holds little oxygen, and your jelly stays stifled.","actions.forage.name":"Scrape algae from stone surfaces","actions.forage.desc":"Use your mandibles to scrape diatoms and organic debris from the surfaces of stones. Adults have no functional mouthparts and do not feed, so this is only possible as a nymph.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a crack or under reeds","actions.hide.desc":"Nymphs slip into cracks between stones. Adults rest on the underside of reeds, out of reach of fish and birds.","actions.rest.name":"Rest on stone or leaf","actions.rest.desc":"Stop and stay still until the current or the wind passes.","actions.explore.name":"Drift downstream to a new stretch","actions.explore.desc":"Nymphs crawl along the current to another stone. Adults leave the swarm and fly downstream.","actions.mate.name":"Find a mate in the swarm","actions.mate.desc":"Adults look for a partner in the swarm above the water. An adult lives only about a day, so mating and egg-laying must both happen within that time.","actions.mate.done":"You mate in the air, and afterward you lay {n} eggs.","idle.0":"Light filters down from above and breaks into patches.","idle.1":"Water plants sway gently at the riverbank in the breeze.","idle.2":"A wind blows across the surface of the river, carrying a little moisture.","idle.3":"Somewhere in the distance, running water never stops.","idle.4":"A fallen leaf drifts across the water, its shadow moving over the stones.","idle.5":"On a nearby stone hangs an old molted shell, translucent.","idle.6":"The water level of this river rises and falls through the year.","idle.7":"Most of your life is spent along this riverbank.","actions.forage.text.0":"You lie on the stone face, scraping steadily with your mandibles.","actions.forage.text.1":"The algae are dark in color and taste faintly of earth.","actions.forage.text.2":"The current brings debris your way, and you gather it into your mouth.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip into a crack in the stone, your body pressed against the cold rock.","actions.hide.text.1":"The sound of the current outside suddenly recedes.","actions.hide.text.2":"A shadow passes overhead, and you stay perfectly still.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stop and do nothing at all.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your gills move slowly, as if you were breathing.","actions.rest.text.2":"The water flows past you, and you sway gently with it.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim along the current over a stretch of gravel.","actions.explore.text.1":"You find a new stone with a fresh layer of algae on its underside.","actions.explore.text.2":"You leave the swarm and fly downstream along the river."},"mite":{"name":"Oribatid mite","tagline":"One of the most numerous arthropods in forest leaf litter, often tens of thousands per square meter","survivalNote":"Survival rates of oribatid mites are rarely measured in the wild. This curve is a rough estimate based on soil predation, fungal infection, and drought stress. Lifespan is estimated at the scale of one to two years.","epitaph":"Your shell will stay among the fallen leaves and slowly become part of the soil.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Arachnida › Oribatida","size":"Body length about 0.2–1 mm","note":"Mites and spiders belong to the same class, Arachnida. They have eight legs and are not insects.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.2.label":"Layer of leaf litter","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, not yet hatched, with a very thin shell. You were laid {r0}, in {r1}. About {siblings} eggs were laid in the same batch as you, and most of them will never reach adulthood.","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"beneath a thick layer of fallen leaves","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"at the base of a moss cushion","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in the humus around an old oak","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"in the dry topsoil of a meadow","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"early spring, just as the snow melts","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"late autumn, just as the leaves have fallen","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"midsummer, when the ground has dried out completely","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a block of wood softened by white-rot fungi","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a thick layer of pine needles from a coniferous forest","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"damp, dark ground beneath the roots of a moss clump","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"dead leaves beneath a trampled lawn","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (six legs)","stages.2.name":"First nymph (eight legs)","stages.3.name":"Second nymph","stages.4.name":"Adult","stages.5.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Preyed on by a gamasid mite","causes.0.text":"A gamasid mite caught you with its chelicerae and pinned you to the ground with its walking legs. It sucked out your fluids and left behind an empty shell.","causes.1.short":"Eaten by a ground beetle","causes.1.text":"The mandibles of a ground beetle closed on you. It ran fast, and you had no time to pull into your shell.","causes.2.short":"Infected by an entomopathogenic fungus","causes.2.text":"A fungal hypha pierced your cuticle. It grew slowly inside you until your whole body had turned into white threads.","causes.3.short":"Eggs carried off by ants","causes.3.text":"An ant mistook your egg for food and dragged it back to its nest.","causes.4.short":"Soil dried out and dehydrated","causes.4.text":"For several weeks no rain fell, and the leaves crumbled to powder. You grew lighter every week, until finally you stopped.","causes.5.short":"Starved","causes.5.text":"The fungi on the leaves were eaten by other animals. Your reserves ran out, and you no longer moved.","causes.6.short":"Leaf litter flooded","causes.6.text":"A heavy rain filled the leaf litter with water. Your breathing pores were sealed by the water, and you slowly stopped breathing underwater.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your leg segments grew stiffer and stiffer. After your last crawl, you stopped on the same fallen leaf.","events.0.text":"The eggshell is thin, and the cells inside divide slowly. You wait for a season when the humidity is right.","events.1.text":"A fungal hypha grows past the egg. Your shell is not damaged.","events.2.text":"You climb onto a small patch of mold and scrape at its hyphae with your mouthparts. With only six legs, you move slowly.","events.3.text":"Your cuticle is still soft. A grain of sand presses down on you, and you stay still for a while before moving.","events.4.text":"You molt for the first time. The old exoskeleton stays among the leaves, and a fourth pair of legs has grown.","events.5.text":"On the underside of a dead leaf you find bacteria and fungi, and you feed on them for a long time.","events.5.fact":"Oribatid mites are among the most numerous arthropod groups in forest litter and soil (Krantz & Walter 2009)","events.6.text":"After the rain, the film of water in the leaf litter thickens. You crawl slowly through it for a stretch.","events.7.text":"A frost drops the surface temperature below zero. You pull deeper into the leaf litter and wait for it to pass.","events.8.text":"Fungi take some of the sugars the leaves release as they decompose, so you can eat only what is left.","events.9.text":"A gamasid mite passes beside you, its walking legs long. It pauses, then moves on.","events.10.text":"You graze on a rotting leaf. Its fibers are being broken down by fungi, and you eat the parts already broken down.","events.11.text":"A layer of moss covers the soil where you are. You crawl slowly between the damp roots.","events.12.text":"You lay your first eggs, tucked beneath the fallen leaves. There are {n} in all.","events.12.fact":"Many oribatid mite species reproduce by parthenogenesis, so females can lay viable eggs without mating (Krantz & Walter 2009)","events.13.text":"You lay another batch of eggs, only a few this time. Your body feels lighter.","events.14.text":"A ground beetle runs across the soil surface, and the tremor reaches your legs.","events.15.text":"A cluster of white hyphae pushes up from under a dead leaf. You move toward it and bite off a small mouthful.","events.16.text":"After a long drought, your shell hardens. You reduce your movements.","events.17.text":"Your leg segments stiffen, and the distance you can crawl grows shorter.","events.18.text":"You rest on an old pile of leaves for a long time. There are no new hyphae left around you.","events.19.text":"You tuck your legs beneath your body as rain flows over the surface. You wait quietly.","events.20.text":"You find a piece of leaf covered in mold and scrape its surface off, bit by bit.","events.21.text":"You feed for a long time on a damp moss root. Rain washes bacteria and fungi to your feet.","events.22.text":"You retreat into the deep layers of the leaf litter, your body pressed against damp debris. Footsteps pass overhead, then fade.","events.23.text":"You squeeze into a narrow crack and tuck your legs beneath your body. Time passes slowly, but this place is safe.","events.24.text":"You rest on a dry leaf. Your metabolism slows, and your body loses a little weight.","events.25.text":"You stay in the shadow of the leaves for a long time. Your body cools, then slowly warms again.","events.26.text":"You cross a bare patch of soil and then burrow into another pile of leaves. The journey is long, and your body loses water little by little.","events.27.text":"You climb a fallen log covered with a large patch of lichen. You take a bite and then hurry back down.","events.28.text":"You lay your eggs in a damp place beneath the fallen leaves. There are {n} in all.","events.29.text":"You lay your eggs among the roots of a moss clump. The eggs are tiny, almost invisible.","events.30.text":"You graze in the cracks of a rotten log. Fungi break the wood down into fine powder, and you eat your fill every day.","events.31.text":"One side of the log collapses, and you are pinned under the falling debris for a while. When you climb out, your legs are covered in wood dust.","events.32.text":"Pine needles are stiff and narrow. You can only push slowly through them and eat little, but almost no companions compete with you for it.","events.33.text":"The smell of resin is strong, and it coats your body. For several days you keep combing yourself with your claws.","events.34.text":"Beneath the moss it is damp and dark. Without moving much, you can eat the fungal threads that drop from the moss.","events.35.text":"A springtail walks across the moss and leaves a line of wet footprints. You retreat to a deeper layer.","events.36.text":"A footstep lands on the leaves above, and the whole layer shakes. You are pressed under the fallen fragments and take a long time to crawl out.","events.37.text":"The lawn is watered once. The litter you live in is soaked and churned, but it also spreads out.","events.38.text":"You climb onto a wet, dripping root and crawl into a layer of leaves you have never visited. It is cold there, and no other mites live there.","events.39.text":"You stop beneath a dry leaf and wait for the ants outside to pass. They do not notice you.","events.40.text":"You are a little smaller than the nymphs of your age. They reached the new fungal colony first, so you squeeze through a gap to get a bit of debris.","decisions.0.title":"The soil surface begins to crack","decisions.0.body":"It has not rained for three weeks. The leaves have turned brittle, and the thin film of water on the surface breaks into fragments. Beneath you lies a layer of damp moss roots, and deeper down there is still moist humus.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Burrow into the damp root zone beneath the moss","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You slowly squeeze between the damp roots, where the air is no longer so dry. You stay there for three weeks.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay under the dead leaves and keep feeding","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Dehydration risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"Fungi still grow under the dead leaves, and you keep grazing. Outside, the air grows drier every day.","decisions.1.title":"A patch of new hyphae","decisions.1.body":"Beneath the leaves, a layer of white hyphae has just spread out. The hyphae are tender and carry a film of sugar. On the nearby leaves, several of your kind are already gathering.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Eat it in big bites","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You eat your fill, and your body swells slightly. For the next two weeks you move sluggishly.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Nibble only at the edges","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You touch only the edge of the hyphae, then retreat into a gap among the dead leaves.","decisions.2.title":"The second batch of eggs","decisions.2.body":"Mature eggs are still inside your body. Laying them costs you energy. Keeping them may let you live a little longer.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Lay the second batch","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Stamina↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You lay your eggs beneath the leaves. Afterward your movements slow noticeably.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Conserve your energy","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Lifespan↑ Offspring↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You do not lay eggs. Your energy stays in your body, and you continue crawling slowly.","decisions.3.title":"A ground beetle approaches","decisions.3.body":"A ground beetle runs across the fallen leaves, its antennae probing ahead. You can stay still or crawl toward the edge of the leaf litter.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Pull into your shell and stay still","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"The ground beetle pauses beside you, then walks away.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Crawl toward the edge of the leaves","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Leave the litter · Risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You crawl out of the leaves. Ahead lies a small open patch with nowhere to hide.","decisions.4.title":"The first frost","decisions.4.body":"A thin sheet of ice has formed on the ground overnight. The soil temperature has dropped below zero, and the fluids in your body are beginning to thicken.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Go deeper into the humus","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Stable temperature · Little Food","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You crawl downward. It is a little warmer there than at the surface, and darker.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and eat quickly","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You feed one last time before the frost, and afterward your body begins to stiffen.","decisions.5.title":"A patch of fresh white hyphae","decisions.5.body":"A layer of fresh white hyphae has just appeared on the underside of a fallen leaf. It carries a little sugar. On the nearby leaves, several of your kind are already gathering.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Eat it in big bites","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You eat your fill, and your body swells slightly. For the next few weeks you move sluggishly.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Nibble only at the edges","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You touch only the edge of the hyphae, then retreat into a gap among the dead leaves.","decisions.6.title":"A heavy rain","decisions.6.body":"A heavy rain fell overnight. Water is rising higher and higher in the leaf litter, and your body feels the pressure of the damp. You can climb to the top of a small clump of moss, or stay beneath the dead leaves where you are.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Climb to the top of the moss","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You climb to the top of the moss, and water flows past beneath you. Only after the rain stops do you slowly come down.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay beneath the dead leaves and eat","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Flood risk↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You keep eating. By the time the water reaches your breathing pores, you realize it is already too late.","decisions.7.title":"Your leg segments grow stiffer","decisions.7.body":"Your leg segments have grown so stiff that they no longer obey you, and after each short crawl you must rest for a long time. The pile of leaves is still there, but the hyphae have become scarce.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Move less and save your energy","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Lifespan↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You stop in the shade of an old leaf, moving more and more slowly.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Keep crawling to look for the last hyphae","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You crawl a long way and find a small patch of hyphae. On the way back, your legs can barely lift.","decisions.8.title":"The wood is falling apart","decisions.8.body":"The rotten log you live in has loosened, and the gaps in the middle keep growing. There are still plenty of fungal threads inside, but the leaf litter outside is slowly getting damp too.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Burrow into the heartwood and eat the remaining hyphae","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being trapped↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You move through the inside of the rotten log. You find plenty to eat, but the gaps are widening, and you could be crushed at any moment.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Leave the log and go elsewhere","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You climb out of the log and slowly crawl along a layer of leaves toward the other side.","decisions.9.title":"Footprints on the lawn","decisions.9.body":"A line of footprints has been left on the lawn, and the vibrations come down through the ground in waves. The layer of leaves you live in is thin and close to the edge.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Hide under the deeper leaves","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You squeeze into a thicker layer of leaves, and the footsteps outside slowly fade away.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Crawl to the edge to eat at night","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You crawl to the edge after dark and eat a good meal. The next day, the lawn has been trodden again.","decisions.10.title":"Tangled hyphae","decisions.10.body":"A strand of hyphae is wrapped around a broken twig, bright in color, and you can feel it slowly growing. Near where it reaches, there is a small clump of something you have never eaten.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Follow the hyphae to take a look","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You crawl along the hyphae and reach a new cluster of spores.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Take another route","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You go around the strand of hyphae.","opening.title":"Where your mother chooses to lay her eggs in the leaf litter","opening.body":"You are still an egg, with a very thin shell. Your mother is searching for a place in the leaf litter. The top layer of leaves is the richest, but it dries out first. Beneath the moss roots it is damp and dark, but there is almost no food.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay the eggs in the damp zone beneath the moss roots","opening.options.0.hint":"Steady humidity · Little food","opening.options.0.result":"You are laid among the roots of the moss. It stays damp here, and once you hatch you will have to search slowly for food.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay the eggs in the richest top layer of leaves","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Drying risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"You are laid on a layer of decomposing leaves. There is plenty of food, but after the next sunny days, it quickly dries out.","actions.forage.name":"Graze on fungi on fallen leaves","actions.forage.desc":"Use your mouthparts to scrape hyphae and bacteria from the surface of fallen leaves, one mouthful at a time.","actions.hide.name":"Pull deep into the leaves","actions.hide.desc":"Squeeze into the damp debris beneath the leaf litter and wait for the disturbance outside to pass.","actions.rest.name":"Stay where you are","actions.rest.desc":"Stop and lower your metabolism to a minimum, waiting for rain and food.","actions.explore.name":"Crawl toward a new pile of leaves","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the familiar leaves and look for new hyphae and damper places.","actions.mate.name":"Lay eggs","actions.mate.desc":"Many oribatid mites can produce viable eggs without mating. You hide your eggs beneath the fallen leaves.","actions.mate.done":"You hide your eggs beneath the fallen leaves and lay {n} in all.","idle.0":"It is dark in the gaps among the fallen leaves. You stay where you are and do not move.","idle.1":"The air is damp, and the smell of mold drifts slowly over.","idle.2":"A thin root passes through the soil beside you and touches nothing of you.","idle.3":"It is dark beneath the leaves. Only changes in temperature and humidity tell you what is happening outside.","idle.4":"In the soil in the distance, an earthworm is turning the ground.","idle.5":"Rain slowly seeps down, and the water film between the leaves thickens and thins again.","idle.6":"Outside, the leaves yellow and fall one after another, crumbling into fine powder.","idle.7":"This small patch is very quiet, with only the decomposers slowly at work.","actions.forage.text.0":"You crawl slowly across the underside of a dead leaf, your mouthparts scraping constantly.","actions.forage.text.1":"At the edge of the mold spots there is a thin film of sugar, and you eat slowly.","actions.forage.text.2":"You can only eat what is left behind after the leaves have broken down into nutrients.","actions.hide.text.0":"You pull deep into the leaves, your body pressed against the damp debris.","actions.hide.text.1":"Footsteps pass over you again and again, and you do not move.","actions.hide.text.2":"The humidity here is steady, and the changes in the weather cannot reach you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You rest on a dry leaf, almost motionless.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your metabolism slows, and your body loses a little weight.","actions.rest.text.2":"You wait in the shade of the leaves and do nothing at all.","actions.explore.text.0":"You crawl a long way along the edge of a dead leaf and cross a bare patch of soil.","actions.explore.text.1":"You climb over a fallen log, where the lichen is dry.","actions.explore.text.2":"You find another pile of leaves, where the moisture slows you down."},"mosquito":{"name":"Asian tiger mosquito","tagline":"Begins in a ring of standing water and lasts only a few weeks. Females that bite during the day feed on blood.","survivalNote":"Field survival data for mosquitoes are scarce. This curve is a rough estimate based on laboratory lifespan (about 30–45 days) and the high mortality of the larval stage.","epitaph":"You were one of countless Asian tiger mosquitoes in the city. Those few mouthfuls of blood bought a batch of eggs waiting for rain.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Insecta › Diptera › Culicidae","size":"Body length about 5 mm","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.2.label":"Lineage","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid at the same time","birth.intro":"You are a slender black egg, stuck to {r0}, just above the waterline. You will become {sex}. About {siblings} eggs were laid in the same batch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the inner wall of a discarded tire","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the inner wall of a flowerpot saucer","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the inner wall of a rooftop rain barrel","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"the side wall of a drainage ditch","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"midsummer rainy season, with plenty of standing water, warm and quick to develop","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a cold rain at the end of autumn, as the days grow shorter","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a summer of repeated drought, when the sun dried up the standing water again and again","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"an old local lineage that has bred for many generations","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a lineage brought in from elsewhere in a load of used tires","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a lineage resistant to pyrethroid insecticides","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (fourth instar)","stages.2.name":"Pupa","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Slapped to death by a hand","causes.0.text":"Your mouthparts had only just pierced the skin when a palm came down. Your abdomen was crushed flat before you had finished feeding.","causes.1.short":"Caught by a dragonfly nymph","causes.1.text":"A dragonfly nymph reached out its labium beneath the water and scooped you from under the fallen leaves.","causes.2.short":"Swallowed by a mosquitofish","causes.2.text":"A mosquitofish shot up from beneath the surface. Before you could sink, it sucked you into its mouth.","causes.3.short":"Killed by Bacillus thuringiensis toxin","causes.3.text":"A bacterial crystal you swallowed dissolved in the alkaline fluid of your midgut. The toxin released there tore through the wall of your gut.","causes.4.short":"Water dried up, no food","causes.4.text":"The last small puddle dried out too. You crowded into the debris, found nothing to eat, and grew lighter and lighter.","causes.5.short":"Caught in a spider's web","causes.5.text":"You flew into a spider's web. The silk stuck to your wings and legs, and you struggled for a long time until the spider crawled over to you.","causes.6.short":"Snatched from the air by a swallow","causes.6.text":"A swallow shot through the air beneath you with its beak open. Before you could turn, you vanished into its bill.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your wings wore thin, and your legs no longer obeyed you. You stopped in a corner of the wall and slowly stopped moving.","events.0.text":"Your shell hardens in the dry air, but the embryo inside keeps developing slowly.","events.1.text":"The night is cool. Together with dozens of other eggs, you wait quietly for the next time the water rises.","events.2.text":"Rain falls into the container. The water level slowly climbs and spills over the ring where you rest.","events.3.text":"You hang beneath the surface, your body upside down, with your breathing tube reaching up to take in a breath of air.","events.4.text":"You sweep bacteria and algae in the water with your mouthparts, filtering them in mouthful after mouthful.","events.5.text":"You molt for the first time and grow from first instar to second.","events.6.text":"A leaf falls onto the water, and the light around you suddenly dims. You sink a little toward the bottom.","events.7.text":"The water is shrinking. You crowd into the last small puddle and feed on the debris that settles.","events.8.text":"You make your final molt and become a fourth-instar larva. Your body is thicker now, and its color is darker.","events.9.text":"A dragonfly nymph crawls slowly across the bottom. When it opens its jaws, several larvae near you vanish.","events.10.text":"You stop eating. Your body curls into a comma shape and floats just beneath the surface, supported by two breathing tubes.","events.11.text":"The light on the water changes, and the shell on your back slowly splits open.","events.12.text":"You climb out of the pupal case and rest on a leaf at the water's edge, waiting for your wings to harden.","events.13.text":"You perch on a flower and push your mouthparts into its nectar. The sugar warms your body.","events.14.text":"At dusk, a swarm of males circles around a bamboo pole. You join them and wait for the females to fly in.","events.15.text":"A swallow dives from overhead. You turn almost by instinct, your wings tracing a sharp curve.","events.16.text":"The sun is bright, and you rest for a while in the shade of a leaf.","events.17.text":"You have fed on blood. Your abdomen swells and turns deep red, and for the next few days all you want is a quiet place.","events.18.text":"You lay eggs on the inner wall of an old tire holding water, each one stuck just above the waterline. You have laid {n} eggs.","events.19.text":"You lay your first eggs, {n} in all.","events.20.text":"A spider's web hangs across your flight path. You fly into it, and your legs are coated in silk.","events.21.text":"It rained all afternoon. You hide in a crack under the eaves and listen to the drops fall one by one.","events.22.text":"Your wings have worn down, and each takeoff is a little slower than the last.","events.23.text":"You have finished laying your eggs. You rest quietly in a corner of the wall and wait for the sky to darken.","events.24.text":"You find a tiny blood vessel beneath a patch of skin, push your mouthparts in, and blood surges up in spurts.","events.25.text":"You had just landed when the skin was slapped. You shake your wings and fly to another leg without getting a drop of blood.","events.26.text":"You hide in the shadow of the eaves. The heat of the night cannot escape, so you stay there.","events.27.text":"You rest in the shadow behind a window screen. Light from inside the room filters through, flickering.","events.28.text":"You rest in the shadow of a corner, your wings folded over your back, perfectly still.","events.29.text":"You stay for a long time by a towel hanging out to dry, and your body slowly cools.","events.30.text":"You fly out of the yard and across a street. The smells of the city roll over you in waves, and your antennae keep turning.","events.31.text":"You fly into a green belt where many flowering shrubs grow. The sweet scent reaches you from far away.","events.32.text":"You lay {n} eggs beside an old tire that holds rainwater.","events.33.text":"You choose a flowerpot saucer and stick your eggs just above the waterline. The eggs are tiny and soon impossible to see.","events.34.text":"It has rained several times in this area, and the water in the containers has hardly stopped. All the eggs of your batch hatched at once, and the water quickly filled with larvae.","events.35.text":"The water grows warmer each day, and your molts come sooner than those of larvae of the same age elsewhere.","events.36.text":"A downpour fills the container, and the overflow carries leaves and bacteria down with it. You eat your fill among the debris.","events.37.text":"The nights grow dark sooner. The ring of water you live in grows colder, and your development slows, as if you are waiting for something.","events.38.text":"The water grows colder, and you eat more slowly. A larva beside you stops wriggling and slowly sinks beneath the debris.","events.39.text":"It frosted on an autumn night. You rest in a crack of roof tile by the wall, a thin film of moisture on your wings, moving as slowly as if you were still in the water.","events.40.text":"It has not rained for several days, and the water level drops every day. The ring of water you live in pulls back, and the wall around you is exposed, dry and white.","events.41.text":"The water is almost gone, and you struggle in the damp debris. You stop feeding, and your body becomes thin and dry.","events.42.text":"Someone empties the container, and the next day it is filled with water again. You roll over in the fresh water and take in a breath of air.","events.43.text":"You came from far away with a pile of old tires. During the journey, your eggs lay in the dry gaps of the rubber for several weeks.","events.44.text":"The larvae here are not from your lineage. They eat slowly and seldom fight you for debris, so you actually eat more than you did elsewhere.","events.45.text":"You fly into an unfamiliar neighborhood, where people pay you no attention. You land on a lower leg and draw your first mouthful of blood.","events.46.text":"The neighboring garden was sprayed with pesticide, and the spray washed into your container with the rain. An oily film floats on the water, and the larvae beside you flip over one by one. You are still moving.","events.47.text":"Insecticide was sprayed in the house, and white mist slowly spreads out under the lamp. You stop in a corner and do not move. The mist settles on you, but your legs can still tighten.","events.48.text":"A companion drops beside you, twitching. You fly past its body, and not a single wing is disturbed.","events.49.text":"You land directly on a person's hand, and before you can pierce the skin, the hand swings toward you. You fly back into the air and settle again.","events.50.text":"You linger by a person's breath for a long time, until they turn over, and then you slowly fly off.","events.51.text":"A colorful oil film floats on the water. Curious, you touch it, and your body sticks to it. It takes a long struggle to get free.","events.52.text":"You are a little smaller than the companions around you. When debris washes into the water, you always fail to grab it.","events.53.text":"You are sturdier than your companions. The larvae crowding in are pushed aside by you, and you claim the best piece of debris.","decisions.0.title":"A fish in the water","decisions.0.body":"A mosquitofish swims back and forth beneath the surface, its tail flicking. The surface is bright, and the fallen leaves on the bottom are dark.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Dive beneath the fallen leaves on the bottom","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You press against the underside of a leaf and stay perfectly still until the fish swims away.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and breathe","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You take a few breaths and feed on bacteria for a while. The fish does not come back.","decisions.1.title":"An arm","decisions.1.body":"You smell a scent of lactic acid and body heat. Someone is sitting in the yard enjoying the cool air, with one arm bare. A dog lies in the shade nearby.","decisions.1.fact":"Only female mosquitoes bite. The blood proteins are used to develop their eggs. Males feed only on plant nectar (Clements 1992)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Land on the arm and bite","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Large blood meal · Higher risk of being slapped","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You feed until you are full, then slip away before the person reacts.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Turn toward the dog","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Small blood meal · Safer","decisions.1.options.1.result":"The dog's coat is thick, but you find a small patch of skin and take a little blood.","decisions.2.title":"A flowering shrub","decisions.2.body":"The shrub by the road is covered with small white flowers, and its nectar smells strong and sweet. Your body is still light, and the moisture on your wings has just dried.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Stop and feed on the nectar","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Stay a little longer","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You stay among the flowers and feed for a long time, until your abdomen is round.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Fly on","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Keep moving","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You do not stop, and fly toward the direction the wind is blowing.","decisions.3.title":"A container that holds water","decisions.3.body":"A flowerpot saucer full of rainwater, with clear water and a damp inner wall. Your ovaries still hold a batch of eggs waiting to be laid.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Lay your eggs here","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk of being discovered","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You stick a batch of eggs just above the waterline, then fly away.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep searching for a better container","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Waiting · Risk↑ Hunger↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You fly a little farther and find a deeper barrel, but its water has already dried in the sun.","decisions.4.title":"Midday sun","decisions.4.body":"At noon the light outside is harsh, and hot wind makes the window screen hot to the touch. You see a room with the lights off, and its corners are cool and dark.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stay inside and keep still","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You rest in the shadow of a corner all afternoon, until the sun goes down.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Fly outside in search of a target","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Chance↑ Risk of predation↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You circle outside the window, find a new target, and bite down.","decisions.5.title":"Rain that has not stopped","decisions.5.body":"The evening rain fell for a while and then stopped, but the moisture has not cleared, and the air is heavy and muggy. You can stay under the eaves, or go out after dark to look for food.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Stay under the eaves until morning","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You spend the whole night under the eaves while raindrops fall one by one from above.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Go out after dark to look for food","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being slapped↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You fly to the window and smell warm air. You take a sip, then quickly fly away.","decisions.6.title":"Worn wings","decisions.6.body":"The edges of your wings have worn into notches, and every takeoff takes more effort. A quiet crack in the corner would be a place to rest, but there is no food there either.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Make one last trip to feed on blood","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You manage to take a little more blood. On the flight back to the corner, your legs can barely obey you.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Rest in the corner","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You rest quietly in the corner, and your wings slowly fold.","decisions.7.title":"A spider's web in the corner","decisions.7.body":"A spider's web hangs in the corner, with water beads on its threads. On the other side of the web, a flower is in bloom, and its nectar drifts over in waves.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Go around it and fly through a gap","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Steady · Little food","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You circle to the far side of the web and fly through an open gap.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Slip past the edge of the web to get at the nectar","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being stuck↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You brush against the web and fly in, and the silk catches one leg. You struggle, and in the end you pull free.","decisions.8.title":"The water grows colder","decisions.8.body":"The water has dropped to a cold you have never felt before, and your molting is slowing. You can grow up before the water turns fully cold, or stay beneath the debris and wait for it to warm again.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Molt into a pupa before the water freezes","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Development↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You keep eating and molt twice in a single day. Your body turns a little stiff.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Wait beneath the debris","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Development stalls · Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You curl up in the debris, and your body stops growing. The light on the water changes day after day, and you do not move.","decisions.9.title":"The last puddle","decisions.9.body":"Only a ring of water is left, and the waterline has already disappeared. Beneath the fallen leaves there is a small puddle some distance away, with dry debris between you and it.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Crawl to the small puddle beneath the leaves","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk on the way↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You move over there. The water under the leaves is cold, and plenty of debris drifts in it.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Wait for the next rain where you are","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Still · Food↓↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You cling to the wet wall without moving, waiting for water to fall from the sky.","decisions.10.title":"Two unfamiliar yards","decisions.10.body":"You do not know the smells of this area. The yard on the left has many people, and the one on the right has only a dog lying in the shade. Carbon dioxide is strong from both.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Fly to the yard with people","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being slapped↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You land on the first person's arm and draw blood, then fly off just before a palm comes down.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Fly to the yard with the dog","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Safer","decisions.10.options.1.result":"The dog's coat is thick, but you find a thin patch of skin behind its ear and take a small sip.","decisions.11.title":"A fogging truck","decisions.11.body":"A fogging truck is crawling toward the corner, white mist pouring from its back and wrapping the streetlamps and leaves. The flowers where you are resting lie right beside the road it will pass.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Hide in the eaves","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Safe↑ Food↓","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You squeeze into a crack under the eaves, and the white mist drifts slowly past outside.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Feed in the mist","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Poisoning risk↑","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You find an arm in the mist and draw a mouthful of blood. For the next few hours your legs keep shaking.","opening.title":"Where the mother looks for water to lay her eggs in","opening.body":"You are still a slender black egg, not yet hatched. Your mother hovers at the edge of a flowerpot, choosing a place where water will gather. The water in an old tire is deep and sheltered, but may hold no food. The water in a flowerpot saucer is shallow and full of bacteria, but it may dry out within days.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay the eggs just above the waterline, on the inner wall of an old tire","opening.options.0.hint":"Deep water · Little food","opening.options.0.result":"You are laid on a damp wall, just above the waterline. The next rain that falls in will hatch you.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay the eggs by the shallow water of a flowerpot saucer","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Water dries first","opening.options.1.result":"You are laid on the wall of the saucer. Bacteria are plentiful, but the water is shallow, and within a few days it may have dried up.","actions.forage.name":"Find a person to bite","actions.forage.desc":"Females follow carbon dioxide, body heat, and the smell of sweat to find skin, then pierce it. Only females bite; males do not take part in this action.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in the shade","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in the shade of eaves, a wall corner, or the underside of a leaf, and wait for the light to change.","actions.rest.name":"Rest in a corner","actions.rest.desc":"Stop and wait for your body to recover.","actions.explore.name":"Fly to the neighborhood","actions.explore.desc":"Leave this patch of water and fly into the city in search of flowers and people.","actions.mate.name":"Lay eggs","actions.mate.desc":"Females lay eggs on the inner walls of containers holding water, each egg stuck just above the waterline.","actions.mate.done":"You lay {n} eggs on the inner wall of a container holding water.","idle.0":"A thin layer of light sits on the water, reflecting the ceiling lamp.","idle.1":"Sounds of the city drift over in waves, far away from you.","idle.2":"Someone opens a door in the distance, and a gust of warm air drifts over.","idle.3":"Asian tiger mosquitoes are mostly active in daylight. At this hour, the city outside is heating up.","idle.4":"The water marks on the container wall slowly dry, then are covered again by the next rain.","idle.5":"The wings of a mosquito make a faint buzzing in the air.","idle.6":"The night is quiet, with only the sound of water droplets falling into the container.","idle.7":"The ring you live in is very damp, and the waterline changes day by day.","actions.forage.text.0":"You fly along the scent of carbon dioxide. The air is damp, and warm breath drifts out of the house in gusts.","actions.forage.text.1":"You circle in the air a few times, then move toward a warm spot.","actions.forage.text.2":"A palm swings near you, and you dodge sideways at once.","actions.hide.text.0":"You hide in the shadow beneath the eaves, where the outside heat cannot reach you.","actions.hide.text.1":"Someone opens a door in the distance. The light flares and dims again.","actions.hide.text.2":"Your wings are folded over your back, and you stay perfectly still.","actions.rest.text.0":"You rest in a patch of shade in the corner, your wings folded on your back.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your body cools slowly, and your movements grow slower.","actions.rest.text.2":"The city sounds drift in from outside, then slowly fade away.","actions.explore.text.0":"You fly out of the yard and across a street.","actions.explore.text.1":"The smells of the city roll over you in waves.","actions.explore.text.2":"You fly past a row of laundry hung out to dry, then settle back by a streetlamp."},"nematode":{"name":"Soil nematode","tagline":"For every person on Earth, there are roughly 57 billion nematodes in the soil beneath them","survivalNote":"Field survival data for soil nematodes are almost nonexistent. Laboratory lifespan is about 2–3 weeks. This curve is a rough estimate based on predation and drought stress.","epitaph":"You were one of 4.4 quintillion nematodes in the soil.","taxon":"Nematoda","size":"Adult body length about 1 mm","note":"Nematodes are not earthworms. Earthworms are annelids, with segmented bodies. Nematodes have no body segments, and are usually too thin to see with the naked eye.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Strain","birth.rolls.2.label":"Season of birth","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid by the same mother","birth.intro":"You hatched from an egg inside {r0}. You are about 0.25 mm long, transparent, with 302 neurons. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs came from the same mother as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"hermaphroditic","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"the flesh of a rotting apple","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a decaying plant stem","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"the topsoil of a temperate grassland","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"the edge of a compost heap","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a standard lab strain, kept on agar plates for hundreds of generations","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a wild strain isolated in Hawaii","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a wild strain highly tolerant of drying out","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"the week after rain, when the water film is still thick","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"late autumn, when fallen leaves are rotting in large numbers","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a dry summer, when the water film is thin and bacteria are scarce","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (L1–L4)","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Eaten by a predatory mite","causes.0.text":"A predatory mite clamped you in its chelicerae. From the moment it noticed you to the moment it swallowed you took less than a second.","causes.1.short":"Strangled by a nematode-trapping fungus","causes.1.text":"You slid into a fungal loop. In a tenth of a second it swelled and tightened, and the hyphae then grew into your body.","causes.2.short":"Bitten to death by another nematode","causes.2.text":"A Pristionchus nematode bit through your cuticle with its teeth.","causes.3.short":"Dried out in dry soil","causes.3.text":"For several days no rain fell, and the water films between soil particles broke apart. You could not swim to a wet place.","causes.4.short":"Intestinal infection","causes.4.text":"Bacteria you swallowed took root in your gut and consumed you from the inside.","causes.5.short":"Eaten as an egg","causes.5.text":"You had not yet hatched when a springtail swallowed the egg along with the bacteria around it.","causes.6.short":"Hatched inside the mother (eaten from within)","causes.6.text":"With no food left, you could no longer lay eggs. The eggs hatched inside you, and the larvae ate you from within before crawling out.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your pharynx pumped more and more slowly until it finally stopped. For a nematode, this is a natural end.","events.0.text":"Inside the eggshell, your cells divide in a fixed order. Each division is identical to those of every other nematode.","events.0.fact":"The full embryonic cell lineage of Caenorhabditis elegans, from fertilized egg to hatched larva, has been completely traced (Sulston et al. 1983)","events.1.text":"You squeeze out of the eggshell.","events.2.text":"You find a mass of bacteria, and your pharynx pumps more than two hundred times a minute.","events.3.text":"You follow the gradient of an odor upstream and find a new patch of bacteria.","events.4.text":"You molt. The old cuticle is left behind you.","events.4.fact":"Nematodes molt four times between L1 and adulthood","events.5.text":"A springtail passes overhead, and its vibration spreads through the soil particles around you. You back away at once.","events.6.text":"It rains. The water film in the soil thickens, and you can swim farther.","events.7.text":"The sun has beaten down all day, and the topsoil has dried. You burrow deeper.","events.8.text":"You are surrounded by others of your kind, and pheromones are everywhere.","events.9.text":"You begin laying eggs. On the first day you lay {n}.","events.9.fact":"A self-fertilizing Caenorhabditis elegans hermaphrodite lays about 300 eggs in its lifetime (Hodgkin & Barnes 1991)","events.10.text":"You lay {n} more eggs.","events.11.text":"You come across bacteria that make your neighbors sick. You take one bite and then turn away.","events.11.fact":"After eating pathogenic Pseudomonas aeruginosa, nematodes learn to avoid its odor (Zhang, Lu & Bargmann 2005)","events.12.text":"The bacteria around you have grown sparse. You trace small circles in place, searching.","events.13.text":"A water bear crawls slowly nearby. You ignore each other.","events.14.text":"A virus called Orsay infects the cells lining your gut. You eat a little less.","events.14.fact":"The Orsay virus, found in wild nematodes in Orsay, France, in 2011, was the first natural virus shown to infect Caenorhabditis elegans (Félix et al. 2011)","events.15.text":"Your movements slow, and your pharynx pumps less often.","events.16.text":"You have finished laying your eggs. You feed slowly among the bacteria.","events.17.text":"An earthworm plows past, turning the entire patch of soil over.","events.18.text":"You lie on a mass of bacteria, your pharynx pumping more than two hundred times a minute, your belly swelling bit by bit.","events.19.text":"You eat too fast, and your gut feels bloated. You stop and wait for it to digest a little.","events.20.text":"You slip into a narrow gap between soil particles, your body nearly pressed against all sides. The vibrations from outside travel down, then soon stop.","events.21.text":"You squeeze deep into a clump of humus. It is dark there, and you can only tell which way to go by the smell of the water film.","events.22.text":"You rest at a bend in the water film, your body curled into a ring, your pharynx pumping much more slowly.","events.23.text":"You stay behind a grain of soil for a long time, like a broken thread.","events.24.text":"You swim into a soil crevice you have never visited. The water film there is cold and thin. You circle several times before you find your way back.","events.25.text":"You swim a long way along a root and come across a rotting leaf you have never seen, holding an entire mass of bacteria.","events.26.text":"You lay your eggs one by one between soil particles. There are {n} in all.","events.27.text":"You lay another batch, stuck to a rotting piece of fruit.","events.28.text":"Your ancestors multiplied for many generations on agar in culture dishes. Wild smells were unfamiliar to you, and you had to try several times before you found bacteria.","events.29.text":"Your ancestors were isolated from a mushroom compost heap in Bristol, England. You still recognize the smell of that heap.","events.29.fact":"The standard lab strain N2 came from a mushroom compost heap in Bristol, England (Brenner 1974)","events.30.text":"When the temperature rises, you swim faster than other nematodes. Your companions are still crawling slowly, and you have already looped around to the front.","events.31.text":"In summer soil your offspring grow fast. You find plenty to eat in a rotting fruit, with bacteria all around you.","events.32.text":"Your eggshell is thicker than most. When the soil dries, you curl up and wait for the water to return.","events.33.text":"It has not rained for three days. The creatures around you are dehydrating, but you can still wriggle for a while where the water film is thinnest.","events.34.text":"The water film is thick enough for you to swim a long way. With every stretch, you run into a fresh patch of bacteria.","events.35.text":"It has been three days since the rain stopped, and the water film is starting to thin. You move early to deeper soil and escape the surface dryness.","events.36.text":"Fallen leaves pile up in layers, and inside them bacteria are breaking everything down. With every bite, your gut warms up.","events.37.text":"Fungal hyphae have crept through the rotting leaves. You carefully steer around the white, soft threads.","events.38.text":"The water film on the surface has nearly broken. Your companions crowd into a small patch of damp soil that is left, and you squeeze in with them.","events.39.text":"This summer you smell moisture deep in the soil for the first time. You swim there and survive.","events.40.text":"You do not wait for the surface bacteria to run out. You swim straight into an unfamiliar root crack. It is narrow, and you squeeze through for a long time.","events.41.text":"A clump of soil is trembling. You stop moving until the vibration has completely passed.","events.42.text":"You are thinner than the other nematodes from your batch. The larvae pushed onto the bacteria first, so you can only nibble scraps at the edge.","decisions.0.title":"A tempting mass of bacteria","decisions.0.body":"Ahead lies a large patch of bacteria, its smell very strong. Beside it is a small, thinner patch, which smells ordinary.","decisions.0.fact":"Nematodes are first attracted to Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and only learn to avoid it after contact (Zhang et al. 2005). This bacterium can kill nematodes within days (Tan et al. 1999)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Rush into the dense patch","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You eat until your belly shines. A few hours later, something in your gut begins to go wrong.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Go to the thinner patch","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You eat a plain meal.","decisions.1.title":"Too crowded, and too hungry","decisions.1.body":"Food is nearly gone, and pheromones from your own kind are everywhere. Your body is waiting for a decision: keep growing, or change into another form.","decisions.1.fact":"When crowded and starved, L2 larvae can enter the dauer diapause: the mouth closes, they stop feeding, and they can survive for months (Cassada & Russell 1975)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Enter dauer diapause","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Do not eat or age, wait for the next chance","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You seal your mouth and curl into a thin, hard rod, and begin to wait.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Keep developing","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Grow hungry · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You decide to gamble and keep molting.","decisions.2.title":"A woodlouse passes by","decisions.2.body":"After a long dormancy, you feel the ground tremble. A woodlouse is slowly crawling past above you. You can raise your body, balance on your tail, and wave, trying to cling to it.","decisions.2.fact":"Dauer larvae perform nictation, standing on their tails and waving to hitch a ride on slugs, woodlice, and insects to new places (Lee et al. 2011)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Stand up and wave","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"A new place · May fall off","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You catch hold of its leg. After a few meters, you drop into a rotting pear that has just landed. Bacteria are everywhere.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Wait in place for rain","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"The woodlouse wanders off. Two days later, rain falls, and the nearby leaves begin to rot. You wake up.","decisions.3.title":"A pleasant smell","decisions.3.body":"You smell a sweet scent, a mix of food and the odor of your own kind, drifting from beyond a cluster of white threads.","decisions.3.fact":"The nematode-trapping fungus Arthrobotrys oligospora releases odors that mimic food and sex pheromones to lure nematodes (Hsueh et al. 2017)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Go and look","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"There may be food","decisions.3.options.0.result":"There is nothing there. You carefully back out through the middle of the fungal loop.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Turn and leave","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"A little hungry","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You swim off in the opposite direction.","decisions.4.title":"A male","decisions.4.body":"A male nematode has found you. Among nematodes like you, males are only about one in a thousand.","decisions.4.fact":"After mating with males, hermaphrodites shrink noticeably and their lifespan is shortened (Shi & Murphy 2014)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Mate","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Lifespan↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"Over the next few days, you lay far more eggs than before. Your body begins to shrink.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Swim away","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Use your own sperm","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You continue to fertilize your eggs with your own sperm.","decisions.5.title":"A predatory mite approaches","decisions.5.body":"A faint tremor comes through the soil. A predatory mite is slowly crawling toward you between the soil particles. You can slip into a narrow crack, or keep going for the mass of bacteria ahead.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Slip into the narrow crack and stay still","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You press against a soil particle and shrink into the narrow crack. The chelicerae brush the soil beside you, then withdraw.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Keep going for the bacteria","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being pinched↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You bury yourself in a few mouthfuls. The mite stops behind you, and you do not notice until its chelicerae close.","decisions.6.title":"The water film is thinning on the surface","decisions.6.body":"It has not rained for several days. The water film on the soil particles grows thinner, and the bacteria are retreating into the depths. Your body feels tight, again and again.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Burrow into the moist soil deeper down","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You work your way down through the gaps between soil particles, where a layer of water film still remains.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay at the surface and feed on the last of the water","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Dehydration risk↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You lie on the last layer of water film and feed. Your body dries a little more each day.","decisions.7.title":"The last batch of eggs","decisions.7.body":"Your ovaries are more than half empty, and the remaining eggs are still waiting. Laying them will make your body lighter faster.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Lay all the remaining eggs","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Stamina↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You lay the remaining eggs into the soil one by one. Afterward your movements slow to the point of being almost invisible.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Keep them, and feed slowly","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Lifespan↑ Offspring↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You lay no eggs and continue crawling slowly among the bacteria.","decisions.8.title":"An unfamiliar smell","decisions.8.body":"Your ancestors lived for many generations in culture dishes and recognize only a few kinds of bacteria. Now a strange, sweet smell drifts toward you from ahead, and you do not know what it is.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Follow the unfamiliar smell","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"It is a bacterium your ancestors never smelled. You feed on it, and afterward your gut feels heavy for a long time.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Follow the familiar route","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You crawl slowly along the route you remember. You find little food, but it is a safe trip.","decisions.9.title":"The water film is breaking","decisions.9.body":"No rain has fallen this summer. The surface water film breaks more every day. You can still feed on it, but time is running out.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Feed a few more mouthfuls on the remaining water film","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Dehydration risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You feed for a few more mouthfuls on the last layer of water film. Then the film breaks.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Burrow deeper early","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You reach the deeper soil earlier than the other nematodes, where a little water still remains.","decisions.10.title":"A new scent","decisions.10.body":"A smell you have never encountered drifts out of a crack in the soil beside you. It is faint but clear, and you feel an urge to move closer.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Go closer and look","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Maybe food · Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"It is a patch of white fungus that has just sprouted. You smell it, but you do not get too close.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Steer around the smell","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You take a long detour and leave the smell behind you.","opening.title":"Where your mother chooses to lay her eggs","opening.body":"You are not yet born. Your mother is crawling along the edge of a rotting apple. The center holds the most bacteria and the most of her kind. The edges have few bacteria, but few creatures pass through them.","opening.options.0.t":"Lay eggs in the center, where bacteria are most plentiful","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Crowded, with more predators","opening.options.0.result":"You hatch in the middle of a large mass of bacteria, surrounded by your brothers and sisters.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay eggs at the quiet edge","opening.options.1.hint":"Safer · Less food","opening.options.1.result":"You hatch at the edge of the peel. It is quiet all around, and there are few bacteria.","actions.forage.name":"Eat bacteria","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the odor to where the bacteria are thickest, and feed with your head down.","actions.hide.name":"Burrow into the deep soil","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in the narrow gaps between soil particles, out of reach of predators and away from the dryness at the surface.","actions.rest.name":"Curl up and stay still","actions.rest.desc":"Stop foraging and moving, and let your body slowly recover.","actions.explore.name":"Swim toward a new soil layer","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the familiar bacteria and look for food in distant soil you have never visited.","actions.mate.name":"Lay eggs","actions.mate.desc":"A hermaphroditic individual can fertilize its eggs without a male, and lays them between soil particles.","actions.mate.done":"You lay {n} more eggs.","idle.0":"You wriggle slowly in the water film.","idle.1":"Between the soil particles it is dark and quiet.","idle.2":"You have no eyes. You have only odor, temperature, and touch.","idle.3":"You eat, excrete, and eat again.","idle.4":"The temperature has dropped a little, and your movements slow with it.","idle.5":"Hairs nearby secrete sugars, and bacteria gather there.","idle.6":"You move toward a place with less carbon dioxide.","idle.7":"In the distance, something is digging through the soil, and you feel the tremors.","actions.forage.text.0":"You feed with your head down on a mass of bacteria.","actions.forage.text.1":"Your pharynx pumps in rhythm, and bacteria are sent into your gut.","actions.forage.text.2":"You crawl slowly along the edge of the rotting fruit, and every bite finds something.","actions.hide.text.0":"You squeeze into a narrow crack, your body nearly pressed against the soil particles.","actions.hide.text.1":"The vibrations from outside travel down again and again, and you do not move.","actions.hide.text.2":"It is wetter and darker here than at the surface.","actions.rest.text.0":"You curl up in the water film and stop wriggling.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your pharynx slows its pumping, and your body warms up little by little.","actions.rest.text.2":"It is quiet around you, and you do nothing at all.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim deeper along a root.","actions.explore.text.1":"The water film between the soil particles has become cold and thin.","actions.explore.text.2":"You smell an unfamiliar odor and crawl toward it."},"octopus":{"name":"Common octopus","tagline":"Three hearts, blue blood, and a life of about two years.","survivalNote":"Wild data are scarce. Mortality during the floating stage is very high. Lifespan is about 1–2 years, estimated from catch ages and laboratory observation (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005).","epitaph":"You had three hearts and blue blood, and you lived only two years. One of the eggs you guarded will hatch in a crevice of the reef.","taxon":"Mollusca › Cephalopoda › Octopoda › Octopodidae","size":"Adults usually weigh 1–3 kg, with an arm span of about 1 m","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Sea area","birth.rolls.2.label":"Sea conditions that year","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid by the same mother","birth.intro":"You hatch inside {r0}. You are {sex}, no bigger than a sesame seed. About {siblings} eggs came from the same mother as you, hanging from the cave ceiling like strings of white grapes.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a shallow reef crevice, where your mother guarded a cluster of eggs with her arms","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a pile of empty shells at the edge of a seagrass bed","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a gap among the rubble beneath a breakwater","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a rocky bay on the western Mediterranean, warm in summer so you grow fast","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a cold-water bay in the North Atlantic, where you grow slowly","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a bay beside a fishing harbor, its pier strewn with old nets and clay pots","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"the edge of a tropical coral reef, with plenty of food and moray eels","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a year of many winter storms, with the seafloor churned up and den mouths often murky","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a calm year, with clear water where you can see the bottom of your den","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a year of unusually warm seawater, with plenty of plankton but less and less oxygen","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Planktonic larva","stages.2.name":"Benthic juvenile","stages.3.name":"Adult octopus","stages.4.name":"Senescence","causes.0.short":"Bitten by a moray eel","causes.0.text":"A moray eel poked its head from the mouth of the den, and its teeth closed on your arm. You wrapped your arm tightly around a stone, but it did not let go.","causes.1.short":"Swallowed by a grouper","causes.1.text":"A grouper burst out from behind the reef. You squirted ink, but its mouth was faster.","causes.2.short":"Caught in an octopus pot","causes.2.text":"You reached your arm into a clay pot, and it was hauled up. The neck of the pot was narrow, and your arm was jammed inside.","causes.3.short":"Egg cluster eaten by a crab","causes.3.text":"Your mother was away. A crab slipped into the den through the entrance and dragged out the cluster of eggs where you were.","causes.4.short":"Eaten while drifting as a larva","causes.4.text":"You drifted out of the den on the current, your body as small as a speck of dust. A jellyfish's tentacle curled around you.","causes.5.short":"Died after mating","causes.5.text":"After mating, your body began to decline. Male octopuses usually die at this stage.","causes.6.short":"Infection","causes.6.text":"White spots appeared on your skin, your arms grew slower and slower, and then you stopped.","causes.7.short":"Exhausted after laying eggs","causes.7.text":"You guarded your eggs and ate nothing for a long time. When the eggs hatched, your body stopped too.","events.0.text":"Your mother fans seawater at the mouth of the den, bringing oxygen to every egg. You can feel the current arrive in waves.","events.1.text":"Your mother neither eats nor drinks, but simply guards the cluster of eggs. Her color grows paler and paler.","events.1.fact":"Removing the optic glands of a brooding female octopus causes her to abandon her eggs, resume feeding, and live longer, suggesting that hormones from the optic gland are linked to reproduction and aging (Wodinsky 1977, Science)","events.2.text":"You drift out of the den with the current, your body transparent and nearly invisible. You have no color, only eyes that flash.","events.3.text":"You swim among the plankton, now and then pushed toward the surface by a current.","events.4.text":"You find a crab in a crevice of the reef, pin it with your arm, and slowly drag it into the den.","events.5.text":"A grouper swims past. You adjust your body to match the color of the reef, and it does not stop.","events.6.text":"You crawl across a stretch of sand, your arm leaving a curved trail behind. Far away there is a new rocky outcrop.","events.7.text":"You push an arm into a shell and slowly pull out the clam meat inside.","events.8.text":"A dangerous shadow passes overhead. You squirt a cloud of ink and retreat to the back of the den.","events.8.fact":"The common octopus squirts ink and changes its body color at the same time to escape predators, a multisensory defense (Hanlon & Messenger 2018)","events.9.text":"You wait quietly in the den. Your color lightens and darkens with the changing light.","events.10.text":"You leave your familiar den for a new patch of rocks. There are many shellfish there, but no cover.","events.11.text":"A male octopus approaches the entrance of your den. One of his arms slips in beside you, and your arms wrap around each other.","events.12.text":"A moray eel pokes its head from the den entrance. Your arm tightens around the stones at the opening, waiting for it to leave.","events.13.text":"Your skin is slow to change color now, and the patterns begin to blur, as if melting in the water.","events.14.text":"You stop eating. Your digestive glands have begun to decline, and your arms no longer reach out to grab things.","events.15.text":"Your eggs pour out of your body. You hang them in strings from the ceiling of the den, arranging each one in place.","events.16.text":"You guard the eggs and do not leave the den for a single step. The water temperature changes a little every day.","events.17.text":"At night you crawl out of the den, your color changing to match the rocks. You creep slowly across the moonlit reef.","events.18.text":"You wait in the shadow of the den entrance, extending only your arms to feel the direction of the current.","events.19.text":"You guard the entrance. Your arms sway slowly in the cracks of the rock, and your color gradually blends into the stones.","events.20.text":"A storm churns up the stones on the seafloor, and sediment blocks half the entrance of your den.","events.21.text":"The rocky shore of the Mediterranean is full of small crabs and shellfish. You catch a crab under a stone, and the suckers on your arm wrap around it at once.","events.22.text":"Summer water heats up high, and you move faster than other octopuses your age. Your body fills out well.","events.23.text":"A summer heatwave lasts a long time, and the seawater turns murky. You hide in your den for half a month.","events.24.text":"Cold water slows you down. It takes much longer to drag a crab back into your den.","events.25.text":"The winter sea is cold. You stay in your den for several weeks, extending only an arm now and then to test the water temperature.","events.26.text":"Your body slowly cools, and the color of your skin fades more slowly than that of other octopuses. You can still stay in the den for a while.","events.27.text":"After the fishing boats unload, small fish and bits of shrimp drift under the pier. You slip into a gap in an old net and eat a whole meal.","events.28.text":"You reach an arm into a clay pot, and a crab is inside. The mouth of the pot is narrow, and it takes real effort to pull your arm back out.","events.29.text":"A fishing boat's propeller passes overhead, churning the seawater into white foam. You pull back into the den, and a humming fills the water around you.","events.30.text":"In the gaps of the coral reef, small fish swim past in shoals. You sweep one arm through them and catch two.","events.31.text":"A moray eel lives in a rock crevice next to your den. Each day you both move carefully within each other's range.","events.32.text":"Your skin takes on the color of the coral, and the patterns across your body flicker in the light.","events.33.text":"Storms have churned the sand on the seafloor for three days straight. Your den mouth is half buried, and you dig the sand loose a little at a time with your arms.","events.34.text":"You hide deep in the den while the waves outside shove the stones back and forth with a dull knock.","events.35.text":"The storm pulls you into an eddy in the shallows. You tumble with the seawater and are carried far away.","events.36.text":"For a whole month the sea stays calm. Sunlight passes through the surface and dapples the stones at your den mouth with small patches of light.","events.37.text":"Beneath the calm surface, you travel far, all the way to the edge of a beach you have never visited.","events.38.text":"The sea is flat, and the floating larvae drift slowly through the water. The current carries you farther than you expected.","events.39.text":"The sea is much warmer than in other years. The egg strings develop faster in the warm water, and the mother guarding them weakens faster too.","events.40.text":"Plankton in the warm water is astonishingly abundant. You eat your fill, but your body also burns through energy faster.","events.41.text":"You grow unusually fast this year, but there is less and less oxygen in the water, so you have to push an arm out of the den again and again to draw fresh water over it.","events.42.text":"You refuse to stay in the den. You feel over every rock on the reef, and sand flies everywhere.","events.43.text":"Before you leave the den each time, you reach an arm out to make sure no fish is waiting, and only then do you crawl out.","events.44.text":"You are smaller than your companions. Crabs run too fast across the rocks for you to catch, so you can only pick up the shell fragments they drop.","events.45.text":"A moray eel bites your arm. You fling it hard, sending the eel into the sand, and it lets go.","events.46.text":"You carry an empty shell to the mouth of your den and nudge it with your arm, again and again, until it sits where you feel it belongs.","decisions.0.title":"Leaving the den for the first time","decisions.0.body":"You have just grown into a benthic juvenile octopus. Crabs are crawling over the rocks outside the den, and the sea at night is darker. You must decide when to go out and look for food.","decisions.0.fact":"After the planktonic stage, juvenile common octopuses settle in dens and crevices on the seafloor (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave the den at night to forage","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You slip out under cover of darkness and catch a crab. On the way back, a fish swims past in the distance.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Hide in the den during the day","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You spend the whole day in the den, your color matching the stone. Your belly stays empty.","decisions.1.title":"The crabs are growing scarce","decisions.1.body":"You have eaten most of the crabs near the entrance of the den. Outside, on the sand, there is a bed of shellfish, but nothing to hide behind, and more fish around.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go out to the sandy area","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You crawl across the sand and eat a great many shellfish. On the way, you see the shadow of a moray eel.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait in the den","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You keep waiting in the den. Your food slowly runs out, and your body grows lighter.","decisions.2.title":"A female octopus approaches","decisions.2.body":"A female octopus is swimming near the entrance of your den. Her arms feel along the rocks, as if telling you she is ready.","decisions.2.fact":"Male common octopuses die soon after mating, the hectocotylus being inserted into the female's mantle cavity during copulation (Hanlon & Messenger 2018)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Approach and mate","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Lifespan↓↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You extend your hectocotylus, and mating is complete. Over the next few days, your body slowly declines.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay away and remain in the den","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Lifespan↑ Offspring↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay in the den, and the female slowly swims away.","decisions.3.title":"Where to hang the eggs","decisions.3.body":"Your ovaries have matured. You need to find a safe den and hang your egg strings there. The quieter the den, the better the chance the eggs will hatch. But the deeper the den, the harder it is to come out and forage.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Lay the eggs now and guard them","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Hunger","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You hang the strings of eggs from the ceiling of the den and begin to guard them. From then on you hardly eat.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hunt for a while longer","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Offspring↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You feed for a few more days before hanging the eggs.","decisions.4.title":"A moray eel at the entrance","decisions.4.body":"A moray eel has pushed its head into your den. Its eyes gleam in the water, and your arm has already wrapped around the stones at the entrance.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Retreat to the back of the den and stay still","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safer↑↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You withdraw to the back of the den. The moray waits at the entrance for a long time, then slowly leaves.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Squirt ink and camouflage yourself","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"The ink spreads, and your color turns to that of the reef. The moray snaps at nothing, and you slip to the far side of the den.","decisions.5.title":"Whether to leave the den to forage","decisions.5.body":"The eggs still hang from the ceiling, and your body has become very light. There are some shellfish on the sand outside, but once you leave the den, no one will be guarding the eggs.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Leave the den to forage","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You leave the den and find some shellfish. When you return, a layer of gray sand has settled on the strings of eggs.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay in the den and guard the eggs","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Offspring preserved · Hungry","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You keep guarding the eggs. Beside your body, they turn transparent one by one.","decisions.6.title":"A clay pot under the pier","decisions.6.body":"A crab is inside a clay pot, mouth up, wedged between the wooden piles. You smell it, and its legs tap against the wall of the pot again and again.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Reach in and grab the crab","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Predation risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You push your arm into the mouth of the pot and seize the crab. The opening is narrow, your arm gets stuck, and the pot is dragged up along with you.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Leave it and search the reef crevices","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You give up on the crab and creep along the piles toward another rock.","decisions.7.title":"The moray next door","decisions.7.body":"A moray eel lives in the rock crevice next to your den. Each evening it pokes its head out, its eyes bright in the water.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Move to the other side of the rock","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Little food","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You pull yourself across with your arms, inch by inch, stopping after each move to look around.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Guard your den and wait for it to leave","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"The moray lingers at your den mouth for several nights. You hardly leave at all.","decisions.8.title":"The den mouth after the storm","decisions.8.body":"The storm has piled sand at your den mouth into a wall. Inside, the air is stuffy, and water seeps slowly through the gaps in the stone.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Dig the sand out with your arms, a little at a time","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety↓ Food↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You dig all day, and the sand finally opens a single gap.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Move to another den","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Hungry","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You feel your way through the rock crevices and find a cleaner den. On the way, you come across an unfamiliar crab.","decisions.9.title":"The water keeps warming","decisions.9.body":"The shallow water has grown warmer than in past years. There is plenty of food in the shallows, but less and less oxygen. Deep water is cooler, and food is farther away.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Stay in a den in the shallows","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Oxygen↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"There is plenty of food in the shallows. Every so often you have to push an arm out of the den to refresh the water.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Dive into deeper water","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Oxygen↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"The deep water is cooler. You sink slowly, and your body darkens as you go.","opening.title":"Where your mother looks for a den to lay her eggs","opening.body":"You have not yet been born. Your mother has circled the reef crevices of the shallow sea for a long time, deciding which wall of which den to hang the eggs on. A den facing the current gets more oxygen, but crabs and fish are more likely to spot it.","opening.options.0.t":"Hang them at the mouth of the den, facing the current","opening.options.0.hint":"Oxygen↑ Risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs hang at the mouth of the den. Water keeps flowing across them, but many eyes are watching this place too.","opening.options.1.t":"Hang them in a corner deep inside the den","opening.options.1.hint":"Safer · Less oxygen","opening.options.1.result":"The eggs hang deep inside the den. It is dark here, and very little current reaches them.","actions.forage.name":"Hunt","actions.forage.desc":"Search the reef and sand for crabs, shellfish, and small fish, then drag them back into the den with your arms.","actions.hide.name":"Camouflage and hide","actions.hide.desc":"Change your body color and texture, stay motionless against the rocks, and squirt ink if necessary.","actions.rest.name":"Guard the den","actions.rest.desc":"Stay in the den and keep still to conserve energy.","actions.explore.name":"Leave the den","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your familiar den and look for new rocks and food.","actions.mate.name":"Mate","actions.mate.done":"Your arms become entwined. Afterward you lay {n} eggs.","idle.0":"You stay in the den, your arms swaying gently.","idle.1":"Water flows through the mouth of the den, bringing a little oxygen and sand.","idle.2":"Your skin slowly changes color with the light.","idle.3":"In the distance, crabs crawl over the rocks. Their sound is very faint.","idle.4":"You taste the water with your suckers, judging where the shellfish are.","idle.5":"The daylight is bright, and you pull your body deep into the den.","idle.6":"A small fish swims past the entrance, and you do not move.","idle.7":"On the reef at night you crawl slowly, your color constantly shifting.","actions.forage.text.0":"You pin a crab with your arm and slowly drag it back into the den.","actions.forage.text.1":"You push into a shell and pull out the meat inside.","actions.hide.text.0":"Your skin becomes as rough as the reef rock.","actions.hide.text.1":"You press against the stone and do not move.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stay quietly in the den.","actions.explore.text.0":"You leave the den and crawl across a stretch of sand."},"panda":{"name":"Giant panda","tagline":"A carnivore that lives on bamboo, and spends more than ten hours a day eating it.","survivalNote":"Wild giant panda lifespans are rough estimates drawn from long-term field monitoring and captive records; captive animals usually live longer on average. This curve is an estimate for a mixed population.","epitaph":"You lived through many springs in the bamboo forest. Bamboo flowers, and then it grows back.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Carnivora › Ursidae","size":"About 100 g at birth; 80–120 kg as an adult","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Litter position","birth.rolls.2.label":"Mountain range","birth.rolls.3.label":"Bamboo species","birth.siblingsLabel":"Littermates","birth.intro":"{r0}. You weigh about 100 g, roughly one nine-hundredth of your mother’s weight. You are {sex}. {r1} Your eyes are still closed, and only a sparse coat of white fur covers you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"You were born in an arrow bamboo thicket in the Minshan range of Sichuan, your mother giving birth beneath a fallen tree","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"You were born in a valley in the Qinling mountains of Shaanxi, deep in the bamboo","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"You were born in the nursery of the Chengdu Research Base of Giant Panda Breeding","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"You were born in an incubator at a zoo","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Your mother has only you.","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"You are one of twins, and the other was born first.","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"the damp valleys of the Qinling's south slope, where fog rarely lifts","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"the high bamboo forests of the Minshan, where snow piles deep in winter","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"the Qionglai bamboo forests, not far from the giant panda base in Chengdu","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"the Liangshan bamboo forests, on steep slopes with a road below","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a forest dominated by arrow bamboo, which flowers only once every few decades","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a forest dominated by Bashan wood bamboo, with tender shoots in spring","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a mixed forest of several bamboo species, with plenty of shoots but tough leaves on the old canes","stages.0.name":"Cub (0–1 year)","stages.1.name":"Young adult","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Arrow bamboo flowered and died back, leaving nothing to eat","causes.0.text":"The bamboo in this forest flowered one spring. After it flowered, the stands dried up one patch at a time. You walked the whole valley and could not find a single green cane.","causes.1.short":"Your mother could not keep you alive","causes.1.text":"Your cries grew weaker, and your mother’s milk was not enough. In the nest of grass, your breathing slowed until it stopped.","causes.2.short":"Taken by a leopard","causes.2.text":"A leopard burst out from behind a rock. Your mother charged at it, but was a moment too late.","causes.3.short":"Intestinal parasites or pneumonia","causes.3.text":"Your belly swelled, and your droppings had blood in them. You hid in a corner, refusing food and staying still.","causes.4.short":"A new road split your habitat in two","causes.4.text":"You tried to return to the bamboo forest, but a newly built road cut the valley in two. You walked for three days and never found a place to cross.","causes.5.short":"A poacher’s steel trap","causes.5.text":"A steel trap clamped onto your foreleg. You struggled for a long time, and in the end could not move at all.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"You lay under a clump of bamboo for a long time, breathing more and more slowly. Bamboo leaves settled on your fur, and no one came to disturb you.","events.0.text":"Your mother covers you with her body. Only the patch of skin pressed against her belly is warm.","events.0.fact":"A newborn giant panda cub weighs about 100 g, roughly one nine-hundredth of its mother’s weight (National Geographic, 2020, report on giant panda cubs).","events.1.text":"When you wake, your mother rolls over and pins you under her. You let out a thin, high cry, and she pulls you back into her arms.","events.2.text":"Your eyes open. The black-and-white markings start to show on your body.","events.3.text":"Your mother finishes eating bamboo and leaves a few leaves on her lips. You climb up and lick them clean.","events.4.text":"You leave the den for the first time and tumble into a clump of bamboo.","events.5.text":"You chew bamboo for the first time, working at it for a long while before spitting it out.","events.6.text":"Your mother takes you to a new stand of bamboo. You stay there together for three days.","events.7.text":"Another cub cries beside you. Your mother can care for only one, and the other is pushed aside.","events.8.text":"You climb a tree. A branch snaps, you fall, and you make no sound at all.","events.9.text":"You eat bamboo for more than ten hours a day, moving slowly as you chew and hardly stopping for a step.","events.9.fact":"Giant pandas spend 10–14 hours a day feeding, eating from a dozen to several dozen kilograms of bamboo (Schaller et al. 1985).","events.10.text":"A bone on your forepaw works like a thumb. It is really an enlarged wrist sesamoid.","events.10.fact":"Gould’s 1980 book The Panda’s Thumb argues that the giant panda’s “thumb” is a false thumb formed from an enlarged radial sesamoid.","events.11.text":"You roll over under a tree, bending a clump of bamboo down. When it springs back, a leaf slaps your face.","events.12.text":"You leave your scent on a tree trunk, a message for other pandas.","events.13.text":"Spring comes, and your body changes. The breeding window is short, lasting only a few days.","events.14.text":"On a ridge you hear the call of another male panda. The two of you face each other across two valleys, and neither moves in first.","events.15.text":"At the breeding base you give birth to a cub. A keeper weighs it on a scale, then places it back against you.","events.16.text":"New shoots push out from the bamboo in spring. You eat until you are full.","events.17.text":"Snow covers the valley. You dig out the roots of a bamboo clump and chew them slowly.","events.18.text":"Your teeth have worn down a little, and the bamboo fibers are harder to chew.","events.19.text":"You lie on a rock warmed by the sun, and stay there for the whole afternoon.","events.20.text":"Each day a keeper cuts bamboo into lengths and sets it before you. You eat slowly, then sleep.","events.21.text":"The arrow bamboo on the hillside has just put out new leaves, and you strip off half a clump in one go.","events.22.text":"You chew bamboo at the edge of the forest, and soon your fur is covered in bits of leaf.","events.23.text":"On the spring hillside you find a few tender bamboo shoots. Biting into one fills your mouth with juice.","events.24.text":"Footsteps come down from the ridge. You pull back into the arrow bamboo and stay motionless until the sound fades into the mist.","events.25.text":"You spend the afternoon in a rock cave. Thunder rolls outside, one clap after another, and you do not go out.","events.26.text":"After a light rain you lie on the rocks for the afternoon while your fur slowly dries.","events.27.text":"You cross a ridge and find, in another valley, a large stand of bamboo that has never flowered.","events.28.text":"You walk a long way along a mountain path, and a rusty wire cuts your forepaw. Blood seeps out.","events.29.text":"Spring comes, and you catch the scent of an unfamiliar male. You stand at the mouth of the cave for a long time before deciding to follow it.","events.30.text":"You stay in a hidden rock cave for three days. That winter, something begins to stir in your belly.","events.31.text":"The Qinling valley is damp, and the fog never lifts. Your mother's fur smells of wet earth, and you snuggle against her.","events.32.text":"You climb up the gully. The stream runs clear, and thick moss covers the stones.","events.33.text":"A few farmhouses stand in the gully, and dogs bark up from below at night. You circle around them and do not go near.","events.34.text":"Winter in the Minshan is long, and snow falls for months. Your mother digs a den beneath the snow, and you stay inside for a long time without coming out.","events.35.text":"When the snow stops, the sunlight on the white ground is bright. You rest under a rock for an afternoon, and your fur warms up again.","events.36.text":"The wind on the ridge is cold. You stand there and look at the distant snow line, a place you have never been.","events.37.text":"On a small trail in the Qionglai, a team of researchers passes along the slope above you. They do not come close, only make a note in their notebooks.","events.38.text":"A large patch of new bamboo has come up in the forest, shiny and tender. You eat for a long time, until your hunger is completely gone.","events.39.text":"An infrared camera is mounted at the edge of the bamboo forest, and it flashes every night. You get used to it, and you remember where it is.","events.40.text":"You stop at the edge of a road on the hillside. Trucks pass one after another, and the dust they kick up settles on your fur.","events.41.text":"You go down the slope looking for bamboo, and your foot slips, sending you tumbling down a stretch. You stand up, covered in dirt.","events.42.text":"Below the slope you find a small patch of bamboo, thin and dense. There is the scent of other pandas here.","events.43.text":"You walk a long time through the arrow bamboo. The canes are tall and the leaves dense, so walking is slow, but eating is easy.","events.44.text":"Arrow bamboo shoots are tender in spring. You hold a cane down with one paw and bite among the leaves with your mouth.","events.45.text":"In an arrow bamboo forest you see some canes flowering, their leaves a pale yellow. You go around that stand and look for other bamboo.","events.46.text":"In spring, shoots push up from the ground one by one. You pick the most tender one, and when you bite into it your mouth fills with juice.","events.47.text":"You spend a whole afternoon in one bamboo forest, eating every cane you can reach.","events.48.text":"The bamboo is so dense that you get lost in it. When you finally walk out, your fur is covered in scraps of bamboo leaves.","events.49.text":"A few large trees grow among the bamboo. In autumn you find some fallen fruit among the leaves on the ground.","events.50.text":"In summer there are many shoots, but the leaves on the old canes are tough. You eat slowly, and a sharp edge cuts a small gash at the corner of your mouth.","events.51.text":"In winter, bamboo shoots are almost impossible to find. You dig through tree bark and dead grass.","events.52.text":"The first time you leave the den, you head straight for an unfamiliar tree hollow. Your mother calls low behind you.","events.53.text":"You wait a long time at the den entrance, until the wind has carried away every sound, before you dare to step out.","events.54.text":"You come across a rock you have never seen and sniff it for a long time, and you do not snap out of it until a bird flies past.","events.55.text":"You are the lightest cub in this litter. When your mother holds you, you are always pinned at the bottom, and it is hard to get enough milk.","events.56.text":"Your paws are thicker than those of other cubs your age. In the bamboo forest you push a fallen cane aside and drag it to one side.","decisions.0.title":"The bamboo has flowered","decisions.0.body":"The bamboo in the valley has flowered. Bamboo that flowers slowly dies, and the leaves before flowering have turned bitter. Your body needs another stand of bamboo.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Head for a stand that has not flowered","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑ on the road","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You walk for three days and cross two ridges. In the new stand, the leaves are still green.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay put and wait for new bamboo","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Hunger↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You wait for a long time. The new bamboo does not come up, and your body grows lighter every day.","decisions.1.title":"A gate left open","decisions.1.body":"A keeper takes you to a semi-wild woodland. The gate has been left open. Outside there is plenty of bamboo, but no one is watching you.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Walk out","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Freedom · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"For the first time you walk into bamboo with no fence around it. Wind blows from every side, and each leaf is different.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Return to the enclosure","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Familiar","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You walk back to the enclosure you know. The keeper sets bamboo in front of you, and you lower your head and begin to eat.","decisions.2.title":"Two cubs","decisions.2.body":"Your mother can hold only one of you in her arms. The other cries thinly on the grass, growing colder.","decisions.2.fact":"Wild giant panda twins can usually be raised to independence only one at a time; the mother tends the cub she judges stronger (Schaller et al. 1985).","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Squeeze into your mother’s arms","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety · The other may die","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You push into your mother’s arms. The other cub’s cries grow weaker, and then they stop.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay quietly where you are","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay still, and you stay cold. Slowly your mother’s breath comes closer to you.","decisions.3.title":"A call from below","decisions.3.body":"A strange call rises from the valley below, high and trembling. A female panda is searching for you.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Go down to find her","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Breeding chance · Risk↑ on the road","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You cross a ridge and find her. That season, the two of you stay together in one stand of bamboo.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay in the bamboo","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety · Lose the chance","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay in the bamboo, and her call fades slowly into the wind.","decisions.4.title":"A winter snow","decisions.4.body":"The snow has fallen for two months. The bamboo lies buried, with only a few stalks poking out above it. Your old den is on the sheltered side of the ridge, but the food is running out.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Follow the old trail down to the bamboo below","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You walk through the snow for a long time, and at last you find a stand of bamboo the snow has not crushed.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay in the old den","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hunger↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You spend the whole winter in the den. When the snow stops, you are already very thin.","decisions.5.title":"Your mother heads for the ridge","decisions.5.body":"Your mother walks toward the ridge, her shape coming and going among the bamboo. Your legs are still unsteady, and the wind outside the den is cold.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Follow her up the ridge","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Well fed · Leopards on the route","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You stumble after her and catch up. The wind on the ridge is cold, and the two of you huddle beneath a clump of bamboo.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Wait at the den for her to return","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You wait at the den all afternoon. At last your mother comes back, with bamboo leaves on her lips and bits of grass on her fur.","decisions.6.title":"The edge of the bamboo","decisions.6.body":"Another male panda is making its way along the ridge toward your bamboo. Its call is low, and it has left scratch marks on the tree trunks.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Hold this bamboo","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Risk of injury↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You patrol the boundary back and forth for three days. At last the other panda turns around and leaves, having only left a scratch on a rock.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Give up the bamboo and climb to the ridge","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety · Bamboo is far","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You climb toward the ridge. The bamboo there is tall, but it is far from the rock cave you know.","decisions.7.title":"Old, and the bamboo is below","decisions.7.body":"Your teeth have worn blunt, and chewing bamboo takes more effort each time. Below the slope lies a new stand of tender bamboo, but a newly built road runs between you and it.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Cross the road and go down the mountain","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Vehicle risk","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You cross the road while no vehicles are coming. The bamboo there is tender, and you eat for a long time.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay on the mountain and eat tender shoots","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety · Little food","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You find a few bamboo shoots on the mountain. They are tough and bitter. You chew them slowly while the days grow darker.","decisions.8.title":"The farmhouse in the gully","decisions.8.body":"One household sits in the gully, with dried corn hanging at the door. At night a dog barks in the yard, and you smell the corn. You could go down the hill to eat, or stay in the bamboo forest.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Go down and eat a meal at the edge of the cornfield","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Dog bite risk","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You eat a meal at the edge of the cornfield. A dog charges at you and bites your hind leg, and by the time you run back to the bamboo forest you have several bloody marks.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay in the bamboo forest and eat what is left","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Little food","decisions.8.options.1.result":"The bamboo you find in the forest is thin and tough. You finish one patch and move on to the next.","decisions.9.title":"Arrow bamboo in flower","decisions.9.body":"Some of the arrow bamboo in the valley has begun to flower. The leaves turn yellow, and the canes dry out patch by patch. Your bamboo has not yet flowered, but the neighboring valleys are already drying out.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Look for unwithered bamboo at the edge of the flowering forest","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"At the edge of the flowering forest you find some bamboo that is still green. It is old and bitter, and you chew on it for a long time.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay in the original forest and wait for it to thin out","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hunger↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You eat less and less each day. Bamboo leaves drop one by one in the wind, and you watch them fall, day after day.","opening.title":"How your mother holds you","opening.body":"You are small and light, your eyes still shut, your body covered in thin white fur. You cannot even roll over. In the den your mother holds you against her chest, and her body is your only source of warmth.","opening.options.0.t":"Your mother holds you constantly and rarely leaves the den","opening.options.0.hint":"Warmth · Mother goes hungry","opening.options.0.result":"Your mother clasps you tightly and stays still for a long time. Her belly rumbles with hunger, but you are warm.","opening.options.1.t":"Your mother goes out to eat and leaves you in the den","opening.options.1.hint":"Mother has milk · You get cold","opening.options.1.result":"Each time your mother goes out, she is gone for a long while. When the den grows cold, all you can do is call her back with a thin cry.","actions.forage.name":"Eat bamboo","actions.forage.desc":"Spend more than ten hours a day among the bamboo, stripping leaves one by one, and now and then chewing a shoot.","actions.hide.name":"Hide deep in the bamboo","actions.hide.desc":"Slip into the thickest arrow bamboo or a rock cave, make no sound, and stay still. You eat less, but you are hard to find.","actions.rest.name":"Doze in the sun","actions.rest.desc":"Lie down beside a rock or tree root to bask, or roll over and sleep for a while.","actions.explore.name":"Cross ridges and valleys","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the valley you know for other ridges and stands of bamboo. The road is long, and there is more to encounter.","actions.mate.name":"Seek a mate in spring","actions.mate.desc":"For a few days in spring, females come into estrus. Afterward, she raises her cubs alone for months.","actions.mate.done":"Spring passes, and you are carrying {n} cub(s).","idle.0":"The sweet scent of bamboo drifts in. You sniff, then lower your head again.","idle.1":"A breeze blows in from far away, and the leaves rustle.","idle.2":"The sound of a distant stream never stops.","idle.3":"You yawn, and your body slowly curls up.","idle.4":"A thin rain begins to fall, and droplets roll across the leaves.","idle.5":"You roll over, and something under you makes a soft sound.","idle.6":"Footsteps pass somewhere far off, then quickly fade away.","idle.7":"Night falls, and the birdsong in the mountains gradually softens.","actions.forage.text.0":"You sit back on your haunches, hug a clump of arrow bamboo, and tear off the leaves one by one.","actions.forage.text.1":"You have gnawed the bamboo down to thin stalks, and bits of cane lie scattered on the ground.","actions.forage.text.2":"You eat slowly, and green juice smears your lips.","actions.hide.text.0":"You push into a dense thicket of arrow bamboo, and the outside sounds grow distant.","actions.hide.text.1":"Bamboo leaves brush against your body and rustle.","actions.hide.text.2":"You lie at the mouth of a rock cave, with only your eyes showing.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie on a sun-warmed stone, belly up, and sleep soundly.","actions.rest.text.1":"You roll over, bending a clump of bamboo, and it springs back and hits your face.","actions.rest.text.2":"The wind in the valley is cool, and you bury your head in your forepaws.","actions.explore.text.0":"You cross a ridge, and stones roll away under your feet all the way down.","actions.explore.text.1":"On the far side of the valley, a stream glints through the mist.","actions.explore.text.2":"You stop in a strange stand of bamboo, sniff, and then move on.","actions.mate.text.0":"The spring mist is thick, and you stay in the rock cave all night.","actions.mate.text.1":"A strange call comes from far off, and you stop to listen for a long time.","actions.mate.text.2":"You leave a mark on a tree, so other pandas know you are here."},"penguin":{"name":"Emperor penguin","tagline":"Through the Antarctic winter, your father keeps your egg on his feet for two months.","survivalNote":"Juvenile mortality in the first year is high; once adult, annual survival is about 85–95%. This curve is a rough estimate from mark-recapture studies and census counts, not precise data.","epitaph":"You stood on the ice through many winters. The winters here are long, and you were one of the birds that endured them.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Position on the ice","birth.rolls.1.label":"Father’s condition","birth.rolls.2.label":"Breeding colony","birth.rolls.3.label":"That year's sea ice","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the same breeding colony","birth.intro":"You are an egg, laid {r0}. You are {sex}. Your father covers you with his feet and brood pouch, and {r1}. About {siblings} eggs, including yours, lie in the colony nearby.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"on the sea-ice edge by the open ocean, where food is close but the wind is strong","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"on the inner side of the ice shelf, sheltered from the wind but far from the sea","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"on a flat stretch of sea ice in moderate wind","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"he had eaten well before winter","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"he moulted late and is in poor condition","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a large colony on the Antarctic Peninsula, with tens of thousands of penguins on the same ice","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a small colony on a remote island, with only a few hundred pairs","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a colony on an island near a research station, where people often walk","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"thick sea ice that stretches far out, so penguins must walk a long way to reach open water","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"sea ice that broke up early, with thin ice at the shore and plenty of fish in the water","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"calm sea ice with no strong winds, good for raising chicks","stages.0.name":"Egg (on the father’s feet)","stages.1.name":"Chick (in the creche)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (first time at sea)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Seized by a leopard seal","causes.0.text":"A leopard seal surges up from the water under the ice. It grips your back and drags you under. You see only the shadow on the ice sink away.","causes.1.short":"Run down by orcas","causes.1.text":"A few orcas surface along the edge of a crack in the ice. You swim fast, but one reaches you from behind.","causes.2.short":"Frozen to death in a blizzard","causes.2.text":"The blizzard blows for three days. Your father stands beside you, and the snow piles thicker on his body. His warmth cannot hold back the wind.","causes.3.short":"Parents never return; starved","causes.3.text":"Your parents stay at sea longer than usual to forage. You wait in the creche, but the one who should come back never does.","causes.4.short":"Ice breaks apart and drifts out to sea","causes.4.text":"The slab you are standing on cracks, and the gap beneath your feet suddenly widens. You leap, but you cannot reach the next floe.","causes.5.short":"Crushed in the crowd","causes.5.text":"The storm comes, and every penguin crowds together. You are pressed down underneath and cannot catch your breath.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your feathers grow thinner, and your body heavier. One day you lie down on the ice and do not get up again.","events.0.text":"You are inside the shell, with your father’s belly feathers and the warmth of his brood pouch around you. Outside the wind howls, but in here it is warm.","events.1.text":"Your father has not eaten for two months. He stands still, shifting his feet only now and then to tuck the egg in a little more.","events.2.text":"Your father huddles with the other males. They take turns moving into the middle, and no one stays on the outer ring all the time.","events.3.text":"A gust blows snow into the little patch where you sit. Your father pulls you in tighter.","events.4.text":"You hatch, wet and shivering. Your mother returns from the sea, finds you, and feeds you your first meal.","events.5.text":"You are in a creche with other chicks. When the parents come back, each one must find its own young.","events.6.text":"Your parents have not come back yet. You are hungry, and the chicks around you are calling, but no one comes.","events.7.text":"The blizzard closes in. You huddle with a crowd of chicks, body pressed against body, and the warmth builds up in layers.","events.8.text":"A skua circles over the ice and fixes its eye on a chick that has strayed alone.","events.9.text":"You walk down to the ice edge for the first time and dive into the sea. The water is cold, but your body knows at once how to swim.","events.10.text":"You swim a long way along the ice edge, past a stretch of unfamiliar ice. A leopard seal rests on it, and you swing wide around its shadow.","events.11.text":"You dive down past a hundred meters. It is dark all around, and you feel the movements of the fish shoal with your body alone.","events.12.text":"A seal’s head breaks through a hole in the ice. You tuck in your body and slip into a group of companions, so it cannot pick out a target.","events.13.text":"You and your companions huddle on the ice, and the birds on the outer edge keep rotating inward. You stand in the middle, warm.","events.14.text":"Under the water a school of fish forms a silver wall. You chase it until your body is nearly spent.","events.15.text":"You wander far out along the ice edge. The ice here is thin, and it gives under your feet for a moment before you steady yourself.","events.16.text":"In winter you find a mate. You stand together on the ice, calling softly to each other.","events.17.text":"You lay an egg. You catch it on your feet and tuck it under your brood pouch.","events.18.text":"A strong wind rises over the sea. You wait with the other penguins for the wind to drop.","events.19.text":"A seabird flies overhead. You glance up, then bow your head again.","events.20.text":"During the molt, a large patch of your feathers falls out. You must stay on the ice and not go into the water until new feathers grow in.","events.21.text":"You move more slowly now. You go to sea less often than before, and each time you come back you need a long rest.","events.22.text":"You huddle with a group of old penguins. They barely move, shifting their bodies only now and then.","events.23.text":"A seal slides off the ice. You do not see it, only your companions leaping into the sea all at once.","events.24.text":"Researchers place an instrument on the ice and fix a tag near you.","events.25.text":"Tens of thousands of penguins are packed together, and your father stands in the middle of the crowd, holding you on his feet. Strange feet all around you tread back and forth across the ice.","events.26.text":"The chicks gather into one large crèche of young birds. Adults pick their own chicks out of the chaos.","events.27.text":"You squeeze into the middle of the group, where it is warm and the wind cannot reach. Penguins on the outer edge take turns standing in the wind.","events.28.text":"There are only a few hundred pairs on this island. Your father finds your mother easily, and the two of them pace back and forth on the ice.","events.29.text":"Your parents soon return from the sea and deliver food into your mouth. You do not have to wait long.","events.30.text":"The food on the island is soon eaten up. For the first time you swim a long way out and find another stretch of open water.","events.31.text":"From the distant research station comes the hum of a generator. Beside your nest a wooden stake is driven into the ice, with a sign hanging from it.","events.32.text":"Expedition members walk to the edge of the ice to take photographs. You all huddle together, and nobody comes close to you.","events.33.text":"You swim past the waters where the research station's boats pass. People on board lean out and wave at you.","events.34.text":"The thick ice extends all the way to the shore, and your parents must walk a long way before they reach the water. Your footsteps leave a long trail across the ice.","events.35.text":"You enter the sea for the first time, and you must first cross a long stretch of thick ice to reach open water. You stop to rest several times along the way.","events.36.text":"That year, the sea ice keeps your breeding ground far from the open sea. Every trip to the water costs you a great deal of energy.","events.37.text":"The ice surface starts to soften. Your father stands at the edge, his body swaying with the waves.","events.38.text":"There are plenty of fish in the sea. You swim into a school of silver baitfish and eat quickly.","events.39.text":"Cracks in the ice multiply. You hop between them, and now and then a seal surfaces from below to look at you.","events.40.text":"There are no strong winds this year. You sit in the sun on the ice, and your down grows longer day by day.","events.41.text":"The calm sea makes your first swim less frightening. You learn to glide through the water.","events.42.text":"The sea is calm, and you stand on the ice for a long time without anything disturbing you.","events.43.text":"You are the first to jump into the sea, while your companions are still hesitating at the edge of the ice. You swim fast and soon disappear from sight.","events.44.text":"You stand at the edge of the ice for a long time, watching the water for shadows, before you slowly slip in.","events.45.text":"You follow a seabird for a long way, watching how it dives headfirst into the water and catches a fish.","events.46.text":"You are smaller than the other chicks in your brood. You can never snatch food from the others when your parents bring it, so you always have to wait until last.","events.47.text":"A leopard seal bites your foot from beneath the ice. You fling it off hard, and it lets go.","decisions.0.title":"Your parents have not returned","decisions.0.body":"The edge of the creche is cold. Warm chicks are packed in the middle, and only wind lies outside. You are hungry, and your parents have been gone a long time.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle of the creche","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Warm · Eat less","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You push into the middle of the creche, and your body warms quickly. Your belly is still empty.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Walk to the ice edge to look for your parents","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Might find them · Leopard seals","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You reach the ice edge and see a row of dark dots on the sea, but you cannot tell which one is your parent.","decisions.1.title":"First time in the sea","decisions.1.body":"The young penguins stand on the ice edge, peering into the water one by one. The water is cold, and food and seals both lie beneath the surface.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Jump in with your companions","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Eat well · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You jump in, and the cold nearly stops your breath. Still, you soon find a school of fish.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait on the ice for a few days","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You wait on the ice. When your companions come back, they all smell of fish.","decisions.2.title":"Not enough food","decisions.2.body":"The fish near the colony are growing scarce. Your body is much lighter than last year, and the feathers on your chest are beginning to thin.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Go farther to a new feeding ground","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You walk a long way along the ice edge, and at last you meet a new school of fish.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay near the ice, relying on companions","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Near · Little food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You stay near the ice. When your companions return, you get a share of their fish.","decisions.3.title":"Pair up in winter or not","decisions.3.body":"You are an adult now. On the winter ice, males and females are already pairing up. Your body still has some fat to spare.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Pair up this winter","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ · Stamina↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You stand beside your mate and lay an egg. For the next two months, you eat almost nothing.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Rest and rebuild your body this year","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑ · No egg","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You stay on the ice a while longer to build your strength back. Other penguins begin incubating, and you do not join them.","decisions.4.title":"A leopard seal at the ice edge","decisions.4.body":"A leopard seal lies on the ice edge, eyes half-closed, but its head is turned toward you. The slab you stand on is one step from the water.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Stay still","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Very dangerous","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You stand without moving. The seal eases toward you, unhurried.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Slide off the ice into the water","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You slip into the water and swim the other way. The seal does not give chase.","decisions.5.title":"Time to molt","decisions.5.body":"The molting season is close. You cannot go into the water while molting, and must stay on the ice for more than a month. Over this time your body grows lighter little by little.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Stay on the ice and stay out of the water","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You stay on the ice for more than a month, and your feathers slowly grow back. You are much lighter now.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Go into the water to find the last fish","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ · Wet feathers","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You dive and find a few fish. But your soaked feathers leave you bitterly cold.","decisions.6.title":"A bitter winter","decisions.6.body":"Blizzards have blown for several days straight. Your strength is running out, and the old penguins around you stop moving one by one.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Push into the center of the crowd","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Warm · Crowded","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You squeeze into the center of the crowd. You heat up quickly, but you are shoved back and forth again and again.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Walk alone to the outer ring","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Cold · You can see the sea","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You walk out to the outer ring, and the wind quickly carries your heat away. You can see the sea, but you have no strength left to reach it.","decisions.7.title":"Too crowded","decisions.7.body":"There are more and more chicks, and you have been pushed to the outer ring of the group. Wind blows in from outside in gusts.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle and let other chicks take the wind","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Crush risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You squeeze into the middle, and your body warms up. One after another, chicks from the outer ring shuffle in next to you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay on the outer ring and wait for a gap","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Hungry · Risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stand in the wind for a long time, until a gap opens up, and then you edge into it.","decisions.8.title":"A thick sheet of ice","decisions.8.body":"You have to cross a large expanse of thick ice before you can reach the sea. There are cracks in the ice, and the water in the distance is deep blue.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Take the shortest route","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Ice-crack risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You take the shortest way. The sound of cracking ice rings out under your feet, and you leap over a gap.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Take the long way along the ice edge","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You follow the edge of the ice for a long way before you reach open water.","decisions.9.title":"The edge of the broken ice","decisions.9.body":"The sea has cut the ice into chunks. Between them is a school of fish, but the chunks could tip at any moment.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Jump onto a chunk and go after the fish","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Tipping risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You jump onto a chunk of ice and swim after the school for a while. The chunk tips, and you nearly get thrown into the water.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Wait on the shore until the ice settles","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safe · Hungry","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You wait on the shore for a long time. The chunks slowly drift together, but the school of fish has already moved away.","opening.title":"The egg-laying floe","opening.body":"You are still an egg, not yet laid. Your mother is choosing a spot on the winter sea ice. The ice edge by the sea has food close at hand, but the wind is strong and the ice is thin. The inner side of the ice shelf is sheltered and far from the sea. She must hand you to your father’s feet within minutes.","opening.fact":"A satellite census in 2009 estimated about 238,000 breeding pairs across 46 colonies (Fretwell et al., PLoS ONE, 2012).","opening.options.0.t":"At the sea-facing ice edge","opening.options.0.hint":"Food close · Strong wind, thin ice","opening.options.0.result":"You are laid at the sea-facing ice edge. Your father quickly takes you onto his feet, but the wind blows in from the ocean and it is bitter.","opening.options.1.t":"On the inner side of the ice shelf","opening.options.1.hint":"Little wind · Far from the sea","opening.options.1.result":"You are laid on the inner side of the ice shelf. The wind is light, but your companions must walk a long way to reach the sea.","actions.forage.name":"Forage at sea","actions.forage.desc":"Leave the ice and dive into the sea to catch fish and krill.","actions.hide.name":"Squeeze into the middle of the group","actions.hide.desc":"Hide at the heart of the group, away from the wind and seals.","actions.rest.name":"Huddle for warmth","actions.rest.desc":"Press together with companions in a tight cluster, and let the birds on the outer ring rotate inward.","actions.explore.name":"Long-distance journey","actions.explore.desc":"Walk a long way along the ice edge in search of new feeding grounds.","actions.mate.name":"Pair up and lay eggs","actions.mate.done":"You lay {n} egg(s) on the ice and catch each one on your feet right away.","idle.0":"Wind sweeps across the ice with a thin, moaning sound.","idle.1":"Your shoulder rests against a companion’s. Neither of you moves.","idle.2":"Seawater strikes the ice edge in the distance, a slow, steady thud.","idle.3":"You bow your head, push your beak into your feathers, and shake them out.","idle.4":"The sky stays white, and you cannot tell what hour it is.","idle.5":"A companion beside you calls in a low voice.","idle.6":"Snow lands on your back, melts quickly, and freezes into ice again.","idle.7":"You catch a faint scent of fish drifting up from the sea.","actions.forage.text.0":"You dive into the water, and the cold tightens your body.","actions.forage.text.1":"The scent of the shoal is faint in the water, and you follow it.","actions.hide.text.0":"You push into the middle of your companions, and the wind suddenly drops away.","actions.rest.text.0":"Your shoulder rests against a companion’s. Neither of you moves.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk along the ice edge, and the snow underfoot is deep."},"pig":{"name":"Pig","tagline":"Pigs are clever, yet a typical pig’s life lasts only about six months.","survivalNote":"Fattening pigs are usually sent to market at 5–6 months (around 110–120 kg). Gilts kept for breeding typically farrow 3–4 litters and are culled after about 2–3 years. Risk multipliers distinguish between the different fates.","epitaph":"You lived for roughly six months, and most of that time was spent on concrete.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Artiodactyla › Suidae","size":"About 110–120 kg at market","birth.rolls.0.label":"Type of farm","birth.rolls.1.label":"Future","birth.rolls.2.label":"Breed","birth.rolls.3.label":"The year you were born","birth.siblingsLabel":"Piglets in your litter","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}. You weigh about 1.3 kg and are {sex}. There are {siblings} piglets in your litter. Your future: {r1}.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a large industrial farm, where sows are kept for long periods in gestation crates","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a mid-sized farm, where sows are kept in groups with room to move","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a smallholder’s pigsty, where the sow wanders the yard","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a pig on the fattening line, headed for market","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"one of the rare sows kept back for breeding","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a commercial cross of Large White and Landrace, which grows fast","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a Duroc sire line, with darker meat and slow growth","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a local black pig, with thick skin and plenty of fat, growing slowly","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a year of mild weather and steady feed prices","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a bitterly cold winter, when the farrowing house did not have enough heat lamps","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a scorching summer, when the pig house relied on fans alone to cool down","birth.rolls.3.options.3.0":"a year of African swine fever, when neighboring farms were culling their pigs","stages.0.name":"Birth","stages.1.name":"Suckling","stages.2.name":"Nursery","stages.3.name":"Finishing","stages.4.name":"Breeding","causes.0.short":"Slaughter","causes.0.text":"You are herded up a ramp into a truck and taken to the slaughterhouse. The truck is packed with pigs. A few hours later, it is all over.","causes.1.short":"Crushed by the sow","causes.1.text":"The sow rolls onto her side and pins you beneath her. You cannot wriggle out from under her.","causes.2.short":"Diarrhea and dehydration","causes.2.text":"Bacteria in your gut drain the water from your body. You grow lighter and lighter until you stop moving.","causes.3.short":"Respiratory disease","causes.3.text":"You cough harder and harder, your breathing rasping like a bellows. A few days later you can no longer eat.","causes.4.short":"Bitten by a pen-mate, and the wound became infected","causes.4.text":"A pig in your pen bites your tail, and the wound becomes infected. Your body slowly heats up, and then you no longer stand.","causes.5.short":"Heat stress","causes.5.text":"In summer the barn is not cooled. You pant with your mouth open, and by the afternoon you collapse.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your body grows weaker by the day, and one night you do not wake up.","events.0.text":"You open your eyes in the farrowing crate, still smelling of amniotic fluid. Your mother grunts softly beside you.","events.1.text":"A worker clips your needle teeth short, so that you cannot bite your mother’s teats while nursing.","events.2.text":"A worker castrates you, and you let out a squeal.","events.3.text":"A worker gives you an iron injection. In a closed environment, piglets struggle to get enough iron any other way.","events.4.text":"You push up to your mother and nurse. Your littermates shove against you, and some are forced to the back.","events.5.text":"When your mother lies down, you are caught between her body and the edge of the crate. Your snout presses against the wooden bars.","events.6.text":"The farrowing room is cold. You squeeze in among your littermates, shivering together.","events.7.text":"You taste solid feed for the first time. It is dry and sweet, and you root it all over the pen with your snout.","events.8.text":"You are moved to a new pen. Strange pigs surround you, shoving one another and starting fights.","events.9.text":"A pen-mate bites your tail, leaving a wound.","events.10.text":"The floor of the pen is slatted, and waste drops through the gaps. Your hooves step onto the slats, one careful step at a time.","events.11.text":"A pig at the far end of the pen is coughing. After a while, you start coughing too.","events.12.text":"You eat your feed every day and grow fast. Two weeks later, you are several kilograms heavier than before.","events.13.text":"The pen is hot, and the floor is damp. You lie beside the water trough, where it is a little cooler.","events.14.text":"A person in work clothes opens the door and walks along the aisle, carrying a stick for driving pigs.","events.15.text":"A large pig bumps back and forth at the feed trough. You are pushed into a corner.","events.16.text":"For the first time, you are driven up the truck ramp. Your hooves slip on the steel plates, and the pigs behind shove you forward.","events.17.text":"You farrow your first litter, {n} piglets in all.","events.18.text":"Your pen is only big enough to stand up and lie down in. You cannot turn around.","events.19.text":"You farrow another litter, a few piglets fewer than the first.","events.20.text":"The piglets are taken away and weaned, and sent off to fatten. In the breeding pen, you wait for your next heat.","events.21.text":"A keeper writes a number beside the tag in your ear. It is your litter size.","events.22.text":"You push up to your mother and work your snout between a row of teats.","events.23.text":"The feed trough is narrow and shallow. Your front hooves step into it, and you get a mouthful of dry feed.","events.24.text":"You shrink into the corner, the cold concrete behind you. Footsteps of a large pig stop on the far side of the pen.","events.25.text":"You wriggle to the other side of your mother’s body, away from the littermates shoving you.","events.26.text":"You have eaten your fill, and you fall asleep lying beside your mother.","events.27.text":"You lie on your side on the concrete, your body pressed to the floor, warmth slowly seeping out of you.","events.28.text":"At the edge of the pen you find a loose wooden slat, and you root at it until it creaks.","events.29.text":"You root your way to the far corner of the pen, where an old pile of bedding smells a little damp.","events.30.text":"You are driven into the breeding pen. The boar next door calls for a long time before he is brought over.","events.31.text":"Your pen is only big enough to stand up and lie down in. You can only shuffle a few steps where you are.","events.32.text":"You feed quickly, and fat soon builds up on your body. Your littermates are thinner than you.","events.33.text":"High-energy feed has been added to the ration. You grow fast, and your belly sways as you walk.","events.34.text":"Your weight climbs day by day. The stockman has hung a record sheet on your pen gate with your number on it.","events.35.text":"Your coat is a little darker than your littermates', your body is sturdy, and you suckle with force.","events.36.text":"You grow more slowly than other pigs, but your meat is good. The stockman says you are destined to become a sire for other pigs.","events.37.text":"You are kept back as breeding stock. The breeding technician lingers at your pen for a long time.","events.38.text":"Your skin is thick and your coat is glossy black. When your littermates push and shove, you always find a good spot.","events.39.text":"You grow slowly and carry thick fat. The stockman says this kind of pork tastes good, but it will take another summer to get there.","events.40.text":"At the edge of your pen you root a small hole in the wet soil, and there is an earthworm in it. You eat it carefully.","events.41.text":"The weather this year is neither too cold nor too hot, and the temperature in the farrowing house holds steady. You huddle together and sleep soundly.","events.42.text":"The feed price holds steady, and there is feed in the trough every day. You eat until you are full.","events.43.text":"Every week a disinfection worker comes to the farm and sprays disinfectant along the corridors.","events.44.text":"The farrowing house is bitterly cold, and you shiver from the moment you are born. The heat lamp lights only one side, and the other side does not reach you.","events.45.text":"You and your littermates huddle together for warmth. One piglet cannot warm up no matter what, and its squeals grow weaker and weaker.","events.46.text":"The pig house is damp in winter, and a thin layer of ice has formed on the floor. Every time you step on it, your hooves slip.","events.47.text":"In the summer pig house the fans roar, but all they blow is hot air. You pant with your mouth open.","events.48.text":"The stockman has sprinkled water on the roof of the pig house, and the water leaves streaks across the floor.","events.49.text":"In summer your appetite gets worse by the day. The stockman mixes the feed with water.","events.50.text":"An outbreak has hit a pig farm in the neighboring village, and all the pigs there have been culled. Disinfection trucks come and go, and your pen is sealed off.","events.51.text":"The stockman is allowed in only once. He wears white protective clothing, feeds you, and leaves.","events.52.text":"The pigs on the farm are bled one by one for testing. On the day the results come in, one pig is missing from your pen.","events.53.text":"You are the first to push to the front of the sow and clamp onto a teat. The other piglets squeal behind you, but you do not let go.","events.54.text":"You huddle in the corner of your pen for a long time, until the feed is nearly cold, before you dare to eat.","events.55.text":"You keep pressing against the bars, sniffing around to see what is new in the corridor. When the cart passes, your nose turns with it.","events.56.text":"You are smaller than your littermates and cannot squeeze into the front row of teats. By the time you get there, the milk is running low.","events.57.text":"You are stocky and always shove other pigs aside as you walk. The stockman says you are the sturdiest pig in the pen.","decisions.0.title":"Where the milk is","decisions.0.body":"The sow has several rows of teats, and the front row gives the most milk. Your littermates are all fighting for it. Do you push your way forward?","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Push to the front row","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Crush risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You shove two piglets aside, bite onto a teat, and milk soon starts to flow.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay at the back","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You wait at the back for a long time, and sometimes you get only a little.","decisions.1.title":"The sow lies on her side","decisions.1.body":"The sow lies down on her side, and you crowd around her. When her body sinks, it will press down on the piglets beside her. Burrowing under her belly is warm, but dangerous.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Burrow under her belly","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Crush risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You burrow under her belly and stay warm for a while.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay at the edge of the pen","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Cold","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You curl up at the edge of the pen, shivering with cold.","decisions.2.title":"The mirror on the wall","decisions.2.body":"A mirror hangs on the wall of the pen. A pig in it is watching you. You can walk over to it, or you can go around.","decisions.2.fact":"Broom, Sena and Moynihan (2009): pigs can understand images in mirrors and use them to find hidden food.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Move closer and look","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Exploration","decisions.2.options.0.result":"The pig in the mirror moves when you move. You circle the mirror once, unsure who it is.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Go around it","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Calm","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You walk past the mirror, and the pig inside it walks away too.","decisions.3.title":"The gate opens","decisions.3.body":"The gate swings open. A person stands in the doorway holding a stick. You can hide in a corner, or push forward to the trough and eat. None of these choices will change the day you leave for market.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Hide in the corner","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You shrink into the corner, and no one looks your way.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Go for the feed","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Bumped↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You squeeze into the feed trough and eat a few mouthfuls. A large pig bumps into you.","decisions.4.title":"A warm spot","decisions.4.body":"In the farrowing room, a warm patch shielded by the sow’s body is where the piglets crowd together. The edge of the crate is much colder. These choices will not change the day you leave for market, only how warm you are.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Crowd in among the piglets","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Squeezed↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You push into a heap of piglets and soon warm up. A piglet beside you squeals as it is squeezed.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay at the cold edge","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Warmth↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You curl up at the edge of the crate. Cold air blows in through the gaps.","decisions.5.title":"The pig that bites tails","decisions.5.body":"A pig in your pen starts biting your tail, and its teeth are sharp. You can hide in a corner of the pen, or bite back.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Hide in a corner of the pen","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Wounds↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You shrink into a corner. The other pig circles outside for a while, then walks off.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Bite back","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Injury↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You clamp onto its ear. The wound starts to burn with heat.","decisions.6.title":"A stifling afternoon","decisions.6.body":"The barn is not cooled. There is a little coolness by the water trough, but it is packed with pigs. You pant with your mouth open, your body burning. These choices will not change the day you leave for market, only how miserable you feel now.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Push to the water trough","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Cooler↑ Squeezed↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You push in by the water trough, and cool water splashes on you. The pigs around you shove, and you do not pull away.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Pant where you stand","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Heat stress↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You keep panting, all through the afternoon, and your body grows heavier and heavier.","decisions.7.title":"The pen after weaning","decisions.7.body":"The piglets have just been taken away, and your teats are still swollen. The keeper opens the gate and drives you into another pen. There you can turn around, but the next pen holds a strange sow.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Walk into the other pen","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Space↑ Unfamiliar↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You walk into the new pen, and the sow next door looks at you through the bars.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in your old pen","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Health↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You lie down in your old pen, and your milk slowly dries up.","decisions.8.title":"The stockman's pick","decisions.8.body":"The stockman walks between the pens, choosing the one to keep as a future breeding pig. You can push up beside his feet, or hide at the back.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Push up beside his feet","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Picked↑ Shoved↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"He reaches down and pats your back, then turns to look at the other pigs.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Hide at the back","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safer↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You stay hidden at the back, and no one reaches out to touch you.","decisions.9.title":"Under the heat lamp","decisions.9.body":"The heat lamp lights only one side, and the lit side is crowded with piglets. The other side is colder, but the water trough is closer there.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Squeeze to the lit side","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Body heat↑ Shoved↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You squeeze under the lamp, and your body quickly warms up.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Go to the water trough on the other side","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Water↑ Body heat↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You drink a few mouthfuls, but your body only grows colder.","decisions.10.title":"The day the farm is sealed","decisions.10.body":"One pig on the farm has tested positive, and the farm has been sealed off. A transport truck has stopped at the gate. You can push to the door, or stay in your pen.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Push toward the door","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Loaded onto a truck↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You push to the door, and it opens a crack. Outside, someone is calling out numbers loudly.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay in your pen","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You stay in the pen until the truck leaves. The smell of disinfectant outside is sharp in your nose.","decisions.11.title":"The door left unlatched","decisions.11.body":"The stockman has not closed the door properly, and a line of light seeps through the gap. You can walk to the door to look, or stay in the pen.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Walk to the door and look","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Safety↓","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You push to the door, and people pass along the corridor outside. You back off again.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Stay in the pen","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Safer↑","decisions.11.options.1.result":"The line of light stays on until the stockman comes back and shuts it.","opening.title":"The first milk","opening.body":"You were just born, and you are still wet. Your mother lies on her side and grunts low. There are several rows of teats, and the front row gives the most milk, but your littermates are all pushing toward it. Do you try to get to the very front? In the first few days, the teats close to your mother are the easiest to reach and the first milk, colostrum, is richest there, but you are also the most likely to be crushed.","opening.options.0.t":"Fight for a front-row teat","opening.options.0.hint":"Colostrum↑ Crush risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"You shove the piglet beside you aside and bite onto a teat. The colostrum is thick and yellow, and you feed for a long time.","opening.options.1.t":"Take a rear teat","opening.options.1.hint":"Less colostrum · Safer","opening.options.1.result":"You suckle a few mouthfuls from a rear teat, and there is very little milk. The piglets beside you do not press down on you.","actions.forage.name":"Push to the feed trough","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the smell or the sound, and force your way to the feed trough or the teats.","actions.hide.name":"Withdraw to a corner","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the crowded spots where people and pen-mates gather, and tuck yourself into a corner or against the pen wall.","actions.rest.name":"Lie on your side on the concrete","actions.rest.desc":"Lie on your side with your body pressed to the floor, and move as little as possible.","actions.explore.name":"Root around the pen","actions.explore.desc":"Use your snout to root the floor and the bars, and check every corner of the pen.","actions.mate.name":"Breed","actions.mate.desc":"You are driven into the breeding pen to mate with a boar.","actions.mate.done":"You are pregnant again, with a litter of {n} piglet(s).","idle.0":"The pen floor is partly wet, partly dry.","idle.1":"You root at the floor with your snout, find nothing, and root again.","idle.2":"The pigs around you sleep, their breathing rising and falling together.","idle.3":"A fly lands on your ear, and you shake your head.","idle.4":"The sound of the feed truck drifts in from the far end of the corridor.","idle.5":"Pigs in the next pen squeal, their voices rising and falling.","idle.6":"You shift your body and lie down in a new spot.","idle.7":"Sunlight comes only through a small high window, and soon moves away.","actions.forage.text.0":"You push to the front and stick your snout into the trough.","actions.forage.text.1":"The pigs beside you shove you, and you do not let go.","actions.forage.text.2":"The feed smells sweet, and you eat in a hurry.","actions.hide.text.0":"You shrink into a corner and rub against the damp wall.","actions.hide.text.1":"Pigs call from somewhere distant, and you lower your head a little more.","actions.hide.text.2":"Footsteps sound down the aisle, and you do not raise your head.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie on your side, and your breathing gradually slows.","actions.rest.text.1":"The concrete is cool, and you shift your body.","actions.rest.text.2":"The pigs around you lie down one after another.","actions.explore.text.0":"You root at the floor with your snout and turn up a little damp soil.","actions.explore.text.1":"You walk once around the bars and end up back where you started.","actions.explore.text.2":"There is a gap in the bars, and you grind your snout against it for a long time."},"pigeon":{"name":"Feral pigeon","tagline":"A city bird descended from domesticated rock doves. You will find them almost everywhere: squares, stations and window ledges.","survivalNote":"Lifespans for city pigeons come from banded individuals and urban surveys, and most do not survive their first year. This curve is a rough estimate from band-recovery data, not a precise census.","epitaph":"You were a descendant of domesticated rock doves, flying from one eave to the next. The city fed you, and it also gave you many dangers.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Parents’ food source","birth.rolls.2.label":"City","birth.rolls.3.label":"Weather that year","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in your clutch","birth.intro":"You hatched from an egg {r0}, and you are {sex}. Your parents forage for food: {r1}. Your clutch held {siblings} eggs, including yours.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"in the eaves of an old building beside a square","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"on a steel beam at the railway station","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"on a row of window ledges under a bridge","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"in the bell tower of an old-town church","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"crumbs by a bakery door","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"rotting leaves in a vegetable market","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"bread that visitors toss to them in the park","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a pigeon loft on a rooftop in a Beijing hutong, with a small band on your leg","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a high-rise in Manhattan, its window ledges thick with dust","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"an old market in Mumbai, where someone scatters grain nearby every morning","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"a granary in a small town, with only a few pairs of pigeons and few cats","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a hot summer, when the water on the window ledges dried up quickly","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a cold winter, with few heating vents in the city","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a rainy spring, with plenty of insects in the streets","stages.0.name":"Egg (incubating)","stages.1.name":"Squab (fed crop milk)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (left the nest)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old bird","causes.0.short":"Caught by a peregrine falcon","causes.0.text":"A peregrine dives from the roof, and its talons close on you from behind. You never heard it come.","causes.1.short":"Killed by a car","causes.1.text":"You lower your head to peck at bread crumbs on the road, and a taxi suddenly accelerates beside you.","causes.2.short":"Foot caught in anti-pigeon spikes; starved","causes.2.text":"You land on the anti-pigeon spikes on a window ledge, and your toes get wrapped in fine wire. You struggle for three days and never manage to fly.","causes.3.short":"Frozen in winter","causes.3.text":"Night after night below freezing, you shelter beside an air-conditioning unit, but the wind still gets into your feathers.","causes.4.short":"Trichomoniasis","causes.4.text":"Yellow-white patches grow in your throat, and swallowing becomes harder. More than a week later, you stop eating.","causes.5.short":"Killed by rats in the nest","causes.5.text":"A rat climbs into the nest at night. It bites one of the eggs first, and then it is your turn.","causes.6.short":"Eggs chilled, or squab starved","causes.6.text":"Your parents go out for food and come back too late. You are too hungry in the nest to even call out.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your flight grows slower, and one time you fail to make it up to the roof. You land at the edge of the sidewalk and never rise again.","events.0.text":"Inside the shell you can hear your parents cooing outside. Two pigeons take turns sitting on you, and their bodies are warm.","events.1.text":"Your parents turn the eggs beneath them. This time, they do it with practiced ease.","events.2.text":"You open your beak for the first time, and your father’s throat pushes out a white mass of crop milk. You swallow it, and it is thick.","events.3.text":"A pigeon lands on a branch beside the nest, smelling faintly of damp smoke. Your parents bend low to shield you.","events.4.text":"Feathers are growing on your wings. The nest is cramped, and you can only keep shifting from one spot to another.","events.5.text":"A rat squeaks twice on a rafter beside the nest. Your parents tuck you beneath their wings.","events.6.text":"You follow your mother to the square, and peck up a grain of corn. A child runs past you, clutching a bag of popcorn.","events.7.text":"For the first time you fly with the flock across a street. Cars flash below you, and you nearly crash into an electric wire.","events.8.text":"A handful of crumbs is scattered by the bakery door. The flock drops down, and dozens of heads peck at once.","events.9.text":"A cat crouches by a flowerbed, its eyes fixed on you. You pull into a hollow beneath the eaves and stay there until it walks away.","events.10.text":"You perch on a TV antenna on a roof, tuck one foot into your feathers, and doze half awake.","events.11.text":"A peregrine circles above the city. The flock bursts apart at once, hundreds of birds scattering in different directions.","events.12.text":"Someone scatters a handful of feed in the square. You eat quickly, and the birds beside you eat even faster.","events.13.text":"Your mate turns its neck on the window ledge, cooing. The two of you begin to build the nest again.","events.14.text":"In spring, you lay a clutch of {n} eggs on the window ledge.","events.15.text":"Anti-pigeon spikes have been fitted on the ledges of the square. Some of your companions have their feet caught in them, and you steer clear of that row.","events.16.text":"You fly over a bridge toward another railway station. There are more crumbs there than here, but it is also more crowded.","events.17.text":"The weather turns suddenly cold, below freezing for three days. You crouch beside an air-conditioning unit, and the moisture in your feathers slowly freezes.","events.18.text":"A man swings a broom, sweeping pigeon droppings off the ledge. You fly to another roof.","events.19.text":"Beside a street-corner bin lies a moldy piece of bread. You take a bite, and your stomach feels a little off.","events.20.text":"White patches grow in your throat, and it becomes harder and harder to swallow.","events.21.text":"You fly lower now, and it takes a long time to land on a roof.","events.22.text":"The city lights stay on all night. You doze on an electric wire, waking now and then to see people walking below.","events.23.text":"One of your eyes begins to turn pale, and things look a little blurry.","events.24.text":"Before you have grown feathers, the pigeon keeper holds you in his hands for a look. His hands are warm, and he fits a small band around your leg.","events.25.text":"At dusk the pigeon keeper opens the loft door and lets you all go. You circle the lane three times, then fly back to the loft on the rooftop.","events.26.text":"You are carried far away and released. You remember the direction of home, and in the end you fly back to the loft.","events.27.text":"The window ledges of the tall building are covered in dust. When the wind blows, fine feathers drift into your nest.","events.28.text":"Outside the subway entrance is a hot dog cart. You and a flock of pigeons peck a piece of bread off the ground together.","events.29.text":"An ambulance screams down the street, and the flock bursts into the air all at once, then settles again on the corner.","events.30.text":"Every morning someone scatters grain at the old market. You crowd together with many pigeons around the grain pile and eat.","events.31.text":"On a hot afternoon the concrete on the rooftop is scorching. You hold your beak open and cool your body with water from your throat.","events.32.text":"You rest in the shade of the rooftop awning, listening to the haggling in the market below.","events.33.text":"The granary is dark, and only a beam of light from the doorway falls on the nest. Your whole brood is quiet.","events.34.text":"At the granary doorway you find a few grains of wheat in the pile. Food is scarce, and you eat each grain slowly.","events.35.text":"A cat crouches behind the pile of grain. You press against the ceiling beam and wait for it to walk away.","events.36.text":"The nest was stuffy that summer. Your parents stayed close the whole time, panting with open beaks to cool you.","events.37.text":"The window ledges dried out quickly. You line up at the pool on the rooftop, waiting to drink.","events.38.text":"All afternoon you huddle in the shade of a tree, not moving at all.","events.39.text":"When the weather turns cold, the eggs in the nest cool down too. Your parents take turns holding you and your siblings under their bellies.","events.40.text":"The city has few heating vents. Each evening you must find a sheltered spot and tuck your feet into your feathers.","events.41.text":"Your chest feels cold, and when you take off, your wings need much more effort.","events.42.text":"It rains for several days, and insects crawl out onto the sidewalk. You peck them up one by one.","events.43.text":"When the rain stops, the eaves drip. You stand beneath an eave and smooth your soaked feathers one by one.","events.44.text":"The rain has soaked the nest. Your parents carry dry grass in their beaks and replace it one stem at a time.","events.45.text":"You are the first to fly up onto the rooftop. You land on a power line and watch the whole street below.","events.46.text":"Before each landing, you circle once in the air to make sure there are no cats or children nearby.","events.47.text":"You stare at a plastic bag for a long time, then nudge it open with your beak. Inside is a flattened piece of bread.","events.48.text":"You are smaller than your brothers and sisters. When your parents feed, you are always pushed to the back.","events.49.text":"A flock of pigeons fights over a piece of bread in the square, and you use your wing to shove one of them away.","decisions.0.title":"Your parents go out for food","decisions.0.body":"Your parents have been gone for a while. At the edge of the nest is a thin twig, and you can climb onto it to see the street outside.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Wait in the nest","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You huddle at the bottom of the nest and wait for your parents to return. Your stomach growls for a long time.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Shuffle to the edge to look out","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Wider view · Might fall","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You shuffle toward the edge, and a gust nearly pitches you off the ledge. Just then your parents fly back.","decisions.1.title":"Where to eat","decisions.1.body":"You can fly a short distance on your own now. The square has plenty of crumbs, but many people. The station roof is quiet, but offers less to eat.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Eat in the square","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Crowds","decisions.1.options.0.result":"The square is scattered with crumbs. You peck up a lot, and one pigeon bumps your shoulder.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Go to the station roof","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Less food","decisions.1.options.1.result":"The station roof is quiet, but the crumbs there are only enough for one meal.","decisions.2.title":"Spring again","decisions.2.body":"The rainwater on the window ledge has dried out. Your mate is gathering thin grass again. Raising another brood this season will be harder on you.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Build another nest","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ · Stamina↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"The two of you build a small nest again. Over the next few weeks, your feathers fall out one by one.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Eat for yourself alone","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Stamina↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You do not build another nest. You eat in the square for a long time, and your feathers grow a little brighter.","decisions.3.title":"A dark shape drops from the roof","decisions.3.body":"A shadow moves on the roof, and the flock goes completely silent. A crumb is still in your beak.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Dive under the eaves","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You slip under the eaves. The shadow sweeps past and flies away.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Keep pecking at the crumbs","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Eat well · Very dangerous","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You peck twice more. Only seconds later do you notice the shadow is already above your head.","decisions.4.title":"A winter night","decisions.4.body":"The temperature has dropped below zero. Streetlights glow over the square, and there is some bread people have thrown away on the ground. Near the chimney, a little warm air rises.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Roost in the warm air from the chimney","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Warm · No food","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You sleep all night beside the chimney. By morning, your feet are still cold.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Look for food under the streetlights","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ · Cold","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You peck up a few pieces of bread under the streetlights. By the time you fly back to the roof, you are shivering with cold.","decisions.5.title":"Your eyes are failing","decisions.5.body":"Your eyesight grows worse by the day. People move back and forth across the square, and you cannot tell which one is the person who feeds you.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Keep searching for crumbs in the square","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Danger↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You still go to the square every day. Someone scatters crumbs for you, and someone else waves you away.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stay only on the familiar roof","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Less food","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You stay on that one roof. There is little to eat there, but you know the position of every tile.","decisions.6.title":"Released far away","decisions.6.body":"The pigeon keeper has packed you into a cage and driven far away. When the cage opens, you are standing on unfamiliar streets.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Fly toward the direction you remember","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Home · Stamina↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You fly toward the direction you remember for a whole afternoon before you see a familiar rooftop.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Rest under a nearby eave first","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safe · Risk of getting lost↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You rest under the eave overnight. The next day the wind has shifted, and it takes you a long time to find the direction of the loft.","decisions.7.title":"The rooftop pool","decisions.7.body":"The rooftop pool steams in the sun. A few pigeons crowd around the edge, and you cannot squeeze in.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze in and drink","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Pecking risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You push in and take a few sips. The pigeon beside you pecks at your wing with its beak.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Fly to a gutter farther away to drink","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You fly to the edge of a drainage gutter and take a sip. The water is cool.","decisions.8.title":"The vent at night","decisions.8.body":"This winter is especially cold. At the back of the building there is an exhaust vent, and warm air blows out of it in gusts.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Crowd beside the exhaust vent","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Crowded","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You huddle at the edge of the vent, warm air blowing on your feathers. At dawn a cleaner chases you away.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay under the open eave","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Cold · Risk↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You huddle under the eave while the wind blows in from every side. By dawn your feet have gone stiff with cold.","opening.title":"The mother chooses the nest site","opening.body":"You are still an egg, in an old nest on a window ledge. Your parents have just chosen this spot: on one side a busy square with plenty of crumbs, along with cats and peregrines; on the other, a quiet old building with less food and fewer dangers.","opening.fact":"Pigeons were among the first birds to be domesticated, and feral city pigeons keep the cliff-nesting habits of their wild ancestors (Johnston and Janiga, Feral Pigeons, Oxford University Press, 1995).","opening.options.0.t":"On the window ledge by the square","opening.options.0.hint":"Food-rich · Many threats","opening.options.0.result":"You hatch on a window ledge beside the square. All day you hear the crowds and the traffic.","opening.options.1.t":"In the quiet old building","opening.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Less food","opening.options.1.result":"You hatch on a window ledge of the old building. Nothing much stirs outside, and your parents must fly a long way to find food.","actions.forage.name":"Peck up crumbs","actions.forage.desc":"Look for bread crumbs, popcorn and corn in the square and on the sidewalk.","actions.hide.name":"Hide under the eaves","actions.hide.desc":"Crouch under the eaves or in a hollow of the window sill, out of sight of people and cats.","actions.rest.name":"Rest on a roof","actions.rest.desc":"Doze on a roof or an electric wire, with one foot tucked into your feathers.","actions.explore.name":"Fly to another square","actions.explore.desc":"Follow the flock to farther neighborhoods in search of new feeding spots.","actions.mate.name":"Build a nest and raise young","actions.mate.done":"You and your mate lay {n} more egg(s) on the window ledge.","idle.0":"You coo twice from the roof, and nobody answers.","idle.1":"Footsteps pass in the street, in waves.","idle.2":"You push your beak into your feathers and shake yourself out.","idle.3":"The fountain in the square starts up, and mist settles on your back.","idle.4":"Another pigeon lands on the tiles beside you and then flies off again.","idle.5":"Someone in the building opens a window and pours a basin of water outside the ledge.","idle.6":"You stand on a wire for a long time, and your toes feel a little cold.","idle.7":"Someone whistles in the distance, and the flock rises and settles again.","actions.forage.text.0":"You keep your head down and peck one grain at a time.","actions.forage.text.1":"A child tosses a bag of popcorn to the ground, and you all rush toward it.","actions.hide.text.0":"You crouch under the eaves while footsteps pass outside, in waves.","actions.rest.text.0":"You doze on the roof, with the sound of distant traffic in your ears.","actions.explore.text.0":"You follow the flock over a street, with cars flashing below."},"rat":{"name":"Brown rat","tagline":"You live under the city, and most people only ever glimpse your shadow after dark.","survivalNote":"Wild brown rats have very high juvenile mortality, and most adults die within a year. This curve is a rough estimate based on urban predation, poisoning, disease and deliberate culling.","epitaph":"You lived less than a year. Beneath the city, there are countless shadows like yours, and few people have ever counted them.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Rodentia › Muridae","size":"Body length about 20–25 cm (excluding tail); about 300 g","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"The year you were born","birth.rolls.2.label":"Your mother's history","birth.siblingsLabel":"Littermates","birth.intro":"You were born {r0}. You are {sex}, nearly hairless, with your eyes tightly shut, and you weigh less than 6 g. Your littermates number about {siblings}.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"in a dirt burrow beside a sewer","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"under a heap of construction rubble","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in a gap in the wall of a restaurant kitchen","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"under a wooden crate in a port warehouse","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an ordinary year, with summer rain and a mild winter","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a warm winter, when the city barely saw snow all season","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a severe drought, with the sewer water level falling steadily","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"a rainy season of weeks of downpours, with the sewers flooded year-round","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a mother who knows people's routines and forages at night behind restaurant kitchens","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a young mother, raising her first litter","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a mother who injured a hind leg in a trapping raid and now limps","stages.0.name":"Pup (eyes still closed)","stages.1.name":"Young rat","stages.2.name":"Adult","stages.3.name":"Old rat","causes.0.short":"Accidental death as a pup","causes.0.text":"A corner of the nest is trampled. You could not dig your way out of the soil, and you cried on and on until your voice grew faint.","causes.1.short":"Died of cold or hunger","causes.1.text":"Your mother never returns to the nest. You and a few siblings slowly grow cold in the chilled nest.","causes.2.short":"Caught by a cat","causes.2.text":"A cat drops from a wall and pins you to the ground. You manage only a few steps.","causes.3.short":"Taken by a barn owl","causes.3.text":"A barn owl lands silently on a power line above the roof. You are in the grass and never hear it approach.","causes.4.short":"Ate rat poison","causes.4.text":"You eat a rodenticide pellet. Over the next few days your body slowly fails, and you die beside a sewer.","causes.5.short":"Caught in a trap","causes.5.text":"A wooden trap snaps shut and closes on your neck.","causes.6.short":"Leptospirosis","causes.6.text":"The water you drank was contaminated by another rat’s urine. You run a fever, and your liver and kidneys fail, one after the other.","causes.7.short":"Died of old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth wear down, and your hind legs lose their strength. You stop in a quiet corner and never move again.","events.0.text":"You and your siblings huddle together. The burrow is warm, and footsteps sometimes pass across the floor above.","events.1.text":"Your mother has gone out to forage. You squeak in the nest, waiting for her to return.","events.2.text":"Your eyes open. The world is blurry, with only light and outlines.","events.3.text":"Your teeth begin to come in, and you bite a brother’s ear. He squeals.","events.4.text":"Your mother carries each of you in her mouth to another burrow. You swing back and forth in her jaws.","events.5.text":"You follow your siblings to rummage through a trash bin. A plastic bag is torn open, and the smell of oil and scraps rushes out.","events.6.text":"A large rat pushes you away from the food. You retreat to a corner and nibble on a few bread crumbs on the floor.","events.7.text":"You walk along the sewer, the water beneath your feet dark and slow. In the distance the sound of running water beats steadily.","events.8.text":"For the first time, the shadow of a cat startles you. Your whole body jumps, and you flee into a pipe.","events.9.text":"You and a companion find a heap of rotting cabbage leaves in the sewer. You bite into one, and it is soft and faintly sour.","events.10.text":"A male leaves his scent at the mouth of your burrow. He patrols the edge of your territory several times.","events.11.text":"You give birth to your first litter. A litter usually has six to ten pups, and there may be several litters in a year.","events.12.text":"The weather turns cold, and your fur stiffens. You squeeze into a pile of cardboard boxes.","events.13.text":"Beside a fresh pile of food, you find some grains. They smell unfamiliar, and you sniff them for a long time before eating.","events.14.text":"You rest at the edge of the sewer while water slowly flows past your feet.","events.15.text":"Your companions roll over you and groom each other. Together you give off a high, squeaking chorus that human ears cannot hear.","events.15.fact":"Panksepp and Burgdorf (2003, Physiology & Behavior): tickled rats emit ultrasonic “laughter” at about 50 kHz, which human ears cannot hear.","events.16.text":"You move more slowly now, and your hind legs are a little unreliable when you run.","events.17.text":"A cat passes nearby again. You do not run. You stay pressed in the corner until it goes by.","events.18.text":"You tear open a plastic bag and find half a loaf of bread. You take a bite, and it is a little damp.","events.19.text":"Behind the market, in a heap of rotting leaves, you find a bone and gnaw on it for a long time.","events.20.text":"You slip into the mouth of a sewer pipe, with water covering your paws. Footsteps pass overhead, and light flickers across the wall.","events.21.text":"You hide in a crack between bricks. Outside, someone shines a flashlight your way, and the beam sweeps across the wall.","events.22.text":"You curl up in your burrow, and the sounds outside slowly fade.","events.23.text":"You sleep in the nest with your siblings, your bodies rising and falling together.","events.24.text":"You follow the sewer deep into the distance. The smell of oil never leaves you.","events.25.text":"You climb up a length of pipe and reach a neighborhood you have never visited. A cat is crouched at the far end of the road.","events.26.text":"A female stops at the mouth of the burrow, sniffs at you, and then walks away.","events.27.text":"You huddle together in the nest. In the days that follow, the female’s belly slowly swells.","events.28.text":"The nest holds neither too much warmth nor too little. When your mother goes out, you keep listening at the entrance until she comes back.","events.29.text":"Rainfall is normal this year, and so is the sewer water level. You walk a long way through the pipes and meet nothing unusual.","events.30.text":"The garbage heap in summer smells the same as last year. You have grown used to it.","events.31.text":"It did not snow this winter, and the burrow entrance never froze. You grew up with your siblings in warm soil.","events.32.text":"The garbage heap did not freeze in winter. Rotten vegetables and oil scraps were always there, so you did not have to run far to find them.","events.33.text":"This winter was warm, and your nest raised several more survivors than in other years.","events.34.text":"The sewer water level falls a little more each day, leaving rings of mud on the pipe walls. Your mother has to travel a long way to find a puddle that has not dried up.","events.35.text":"The city has rationed water, and the slop bins behind restaurants are empty. You rummage through a corner of the market half the night and find only a handful of moldy greens.","events.36.text":"After the drought, rat groups have grown smaller. Now and then you meet a companion you have never seen, so thin that only skin is left.","events.37.text":"For several weeks in a row it rains hard, and the sewer water rises to the base of the walls. You soak in the water all night, cold and damp.","events.38.text":"The rain has scattered the garbage heap, and food has washed into the ditch. You can only scoop it up one bite at a time from the water's edge.","events.39.text":"Moisture has grown mold at the foot of the wall. Every burrow you crawl through smells musty.","events.40.text":"Your mother takes you through the streets and alleys, teaching you one by one which hours have fewer people and which bins are worth visiting.","events.41.text":"You remember the route your mother taught you. A bin lid is broken, and you squeeze in and eat half a sausage.","events.42.text":"Someone has set a trap at the foot of the wall. You smell it and go around.","events.43.text":"Your mother is still learning how to raise a litter. She left one young rat outside, and you listened to its cries fade into the distance.","events.44.text":"Your mother does not have enough milk. You and your siblings fight over it, and one of them grows thinner and thinner until it stops moving.","events.45.text":"You gradually learn the things your mother never taught you. You know where to hide the nest, and which road has cats.","events.46.text":"One of your mother's hind legs does not work properly. She has to stop and rest on the way back, and you wait in the nest for a long time.","events.47.text":"Your mother cannot run fast. Once a cat chased her to the burrow entrance, and she pushed you into the bricks one by one, then drew the cat away herself.","events.48.text":"Copying your mother, you walk with a slight limp. Your steps are light and slow, but you can always hear every sound at the base of the wall.","events.49.text":"In the evening you dare to poke your head out of the wall crack to watch passersby. Someone whistles, and you do not hide.","events.50.text":"You linger at the entrance for a long time, listening for footsteps outside. Today you wait longer than usual, and in the end you go out for nothing.","events.51.text":"You crawl into a pipe you have never explored and sniff all the way around. The sound of water comes from far off, and the exit is under another street.","events.52.text":"You are a little smaller than your siblings, and you always lose out when fighting for milk. Your cry is thin and weak, and your mother sometimes picks up other young ones first.","events.53.text":"Your shoulders are broad, and your bite is strong. A strange male pushes into your wall burrow, and you bite his ear and drive him out.","decisions.0.title":"The food in the burrow is running out","decisions.0.body":"You and your siblings have been weaned, and the food left in the burrow is almost gone. Your mother has not come back for a long time. The garbage heap outside smells wonderful, but you know there are cats out there.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Wait in the burrow","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You wait in the burrow for two days. Some of your siblings never come back.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Go out to forage","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Cat risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You find a piece of bread and gnaw at it for a while. A shadow slips across the top of a wall.","decisions.1.title":"A crack in the manhole cover","decisions.1.body":"A sewer manhole cover is open a crack, and rainwater trickles down through it. On the ground above, a bird is pecking at food, and in the distance there is the shadow of a cat.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Slip down the drain","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓ Disease risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You slip into the sewer. The water is dark and the footing is slick, but there are no cats.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay above ground and look for food","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Predation risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You find a piece of bread on the surface. Your ears stay pricked up the whole time.","decisions.2.title":"A new bait","decisions.2.body":"A small pile of pellets lies in the corner, and it smells sweet. Your companions walk around it one by one, and hardly anyone goes near. You could creep up for a taste, or watch from the side.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Eat it","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Poison risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You eat a few pellets. They taste faintly sweet.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Watch from the side","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You watch from a distance for a long time. A companion eats a few bites, and afterward is never seen again. You stay hungry and keep looking for something else.","decisions.3.title":"A strange male","decisions.3.body":"A strange male has wandered into your territory, his teeth bared.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Charge and fight","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Keep territory · Injury↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You bite his ear and hold on until he runs off. One of your legs is bleeding.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Step aside","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Territory↓ Injury↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You give up that corner. He sniffs around it for a while, then leaves.","decisions.4.title":"The weather turns cold","decisions.4.body":"The air is cold, and rain seeps in through the cracks in the wall. Your fur is stiff with the chill. Beside you is a pile of cardboard boxes, warm and dry inside. The garbage heap outside can still yield food at night, but cats come out at night too.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Burrow into the pile of boxes","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Warmth↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You squeeze into the middle of the boxes. The cardboard is dry, and you soon warm up.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Go out to forage","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Cat risk↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You find a piece of bread and gnaw at it for a while. A shadow slips across the top of a wall.","decisions.5.title":"A puddle","decisions.5.body":"A puddle lies on the ground, with oil slicks floating on the surface. Along the base of the wall are several sets of rat footprints, so companions have clearly drunk here. Somewhere far off is a clean tap, but it is a long way.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Drink from the puddle","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Infection risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You drink a few mouthfuls. The water is lukewarm, with an odor you cannot quite place.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Go far off to find clean water","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Long way","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You walk a long way along the base of the wall before you find a single drop of water.","decisions.6.title":"Out at night for food","decisions.6.body":"Your hind legs are not as strong as they used to be, and the food in the burrow is almost gone. Going out at night for food means meeting the risk of cats. You can wait in the burrow, or you can try going out.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Forage at night","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Cat risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You find a small cracker and gnaw on it for a while. Your ears stay pricked the whole time.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the burrow","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Health↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay in the burrow and slowly chew the last little bit you have.","decisions.7.title":"Water rising","decisions.7.body":"The sewer water has risen to the base of the walls, and the water in the pipes is fast and black. You can climb to a high spot on the pipe wall, or search for food before the water recedes.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Climb to a high spot on the pipe wall","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You cling to a piece of rust on the wall and stay there until the water drops.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Search for food before the water recedes","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Disease risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You dig a scrap of meat out of the muddy water, and you drink a few mouthfuls of foul water.","decisions.8.title":"The burrow is drying out","decisions.8.body":"The damp air in the burrow is less each day. There is still a pool of standing water at the far end of the pipe, but you must cross a stretch of open concrete to reach it.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Cross the concrete to find water deeper in","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Risk of being spotted↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You hug the base of the wall and creep across. The concrete glares bright, but you reach the water and drink.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay in the burrow and wait for rain","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Stamina↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You curl up in the burrow and wait day after day. The rain never comes.","decisions.9.title":"Two paths at night","decisions.9.body":"You remember a route your mother taught you. It has many bins, but you must cross a lit intersection. Another route is longer, but it has no lights.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Take the route your mother taught you","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Being seen↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You cross the intersection with the light falling on your back. You do not stop, and you go all the way to the bins.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Take the long way round","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You go a long way round, and your fur is covered in sewer mud. There is nothing there.","decisions.10.title":"Your mother has not come back","decisions.10.body":"Your mother has not returned to the burrow for a long time. A cat is calling outside. You can go out to find her, or stay in the burrow.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Go out and look for her","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Safety↓ Find your mother","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You find her in a corner by the wall. She has a fresh cut across her side.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay in the burrow","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You wait in the burrow all night. After that, you never hear her footsteps again.","decisions.11.title":"The street by day","decisions.11.body":"A bin sits on the far side of the road, and foot traffic is heavy in daylight. You can wait until night, or go over now.","decisions.11.options.0.t":"Cross the road now","decisions.11.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of cat spotting↑","decisions.11.options.0.result":"You hug the edge of the road and dart across, a tire brushing past you. You return to the wall crack with a biscuit in your mouth.","decisions.11.options.1.t":"Wait until night to go","decisions.11.options.1.hint":"Safer↑ Food↓","decisions.11.options.1.result":"You watch people come and go from the wall crack, until only the street lamps remain.","decisions.12.title":"No room in the nest","decisions.12.body":"Space in the nest is limited. You are smaller than your siblings and cannot squeeze into the ring around your mother.","decisions.12.options.0.t":"Wait on the outside","decisions.12.options.0.hint":"Food↓","decisions.12.options.0.result":"You wait on the outside for a long time before your turn for a drop of milk comes.","decisions.12.options.1.t":"Find food on your own through the wall cracks","decisions.12.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.12.options.1.result":"You squeeze out through a crack in the wall and find a small piece of moldy bread.","opening.title":"Your mother chooses the nest","opening.body":"Your eyes are still closed, and you can only tell scent and warmth apart. Your mother carries you all, hesitating between two places: a gap in the wall, dark and warm, far from human footsteps; or a dirt burrow beside a sewer, close to the garbage heap, where food smells drift in, but people and cats often pass by.","opening.options.0.t":"Choose the nest in the wall gap","opening.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","opening.options.0.result":"Your mother carries you into the wall gap one by one. It is quiet there, but she has to travel a long way to find food.","opening.options.1.t":"Choose the dirt burrow beside the garbage heap","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Discovery↑","opening.options.1.result":"Your mother carries you into the dirt burrow. The smell at the entrance is rich and the noise never stops, with footsteps all day long.","actions.forage.name":"Rummage through garbage","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the smell to trash bins, rotting leaves and food scraps.","actions.hide.name":"Squeeze into a wall gap or sewer","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the street and slip into a gap in the wall or the mouth of a sewer pipe.","actions.rest.name":"Sleep in the burrow","actions.rest.desc":"Curl up in a ball to sleep in the burrow.","actions.explore.name":"Follow the sewer","actions.explore.desc":"Follow the sewer or the gutter along the street to a place you have never been.","actions.mate.name":"Mate","actions.mate.desc":"An adult female and male mate in the nest; afterward the female gives birth to a litter of pups.","actions.mate.done":"Your nest gains {n} more pup(s).","idle.0":"You sniff along the base of the wall with your nose.","idle.1":"Echoes in this area linger, each sound stretched out far.","idle.2":"You lick yourself clean, brushing the dust from your fur.","idle.3":"A subway train passes somewhere in the distance, and the ground trembles faintly.","idle.4":"You gnaw on something in a corner for a while, and dust hangs in your mouth.","idle.5":"The sound of a garbage truck drifts up the street from far away.","idle.6":"Your whiskers tremble in the dark.","idle.7":"You squeeze through the wall gap, stop, and sniff at the air.","actions.forage.text.0":"You tear open a plastic bag and find half a loaf of moldy bread.","actions.forage.text.1":"You follow the smell of oil and scraps, searching bit by bit.","actions.forage.text.2":"A large rat shoves you away from the food.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip into the wall gap, where the light from outside cannot reach.","actions.hide.text.1":"Water covers your paws, and you pull your body back into the pipe.","actions.hide.text.2":"Footsteps pass overhead, then slowly fade into the distance.","actions.rest.text.0":"You curl up in a ball in the burrow, and the sounds outside slowly fade.","actions.rest.text.1":"You lie pressed together with your companions, your bodies rising and falling.","actions.rest.text.2":"You wake, sniff the air, and fall asleep again.","actions.explore.text.0":"You follow the sewer deep into the distance, and the smell of oil never leaves you.","actions.explore.text.1":"Water echoes in the pipe, a steady drip after drip.","actions.explore.text.2":"You climb up a length of pipe and reach a neighborhood you have never visited."},"salmon":{"name":"Chum salmon","tagline":"The river you were born in is where you will return to die.","survivalNote":"Rough estimates drawn from age determination of returning fish and catch data. Mortality in the egg and fry stages is very high, and many individuals are still eaten or caught after entering the sea.","epitaph":"You grew for several years in the sea, then returned to the river where you were born. Your body fed the trees along the banks, and your eggs wait beneath the gravel for next spring.","taxon":"Chordata › Actinopterygii › Salmoniformes › Salmonidae","size":"Adults about 60–80 cm long and 3–8 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Hatching method","birth.rolls.2.label":"River conditions that year","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in your spawning nest","birth.intro":"You are still an egg, buried in the gravel of {r0}, breathing the oxygen the current carries. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs share your spawning nest.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"of a small river on the North Pacific coast","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"of a stretch of riverbed where groundwater seeps up","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"of a stretch of riverbed choked with silt","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a wild riverbed, its gravel shaped by the river itself","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a hatchery tank, where you grew until you could be released into a river","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a straightened channel, with concrete banks and a flat bed","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a winter of heavy snow, with a fast spring melt that scoured the riverbed","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a drought year, with shallow, warm water","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a year of normal rain, with cool water and many aquatic insects in the streams","stages.0.name":"Egg (in the riverbed)","stages.1.name":"Fry (in the river)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile at sea","stages.3.name":"Adult at sea","stages.4.name":"Return to the river to spawn","causes.0.short":"Egg buried in silt or washed away","causes.0.text":"The river rises suddenly, and silt presses into the gaps between the gravel. Oxygen-starved eggs turn white one by one, and some are swept out of the riverbed by the flow.","causes.1.short":"Taken by a kingfisher","causes.1.text":"A kingfisher skims over the water, and its bill plucks you from the surface. The moment you leave the gravel, you are carried up into the air.","causes.2.short":"Seized by a seal","causes.2.text":"A seal rushes up from below, and its teeth clamp onto your body. You struggle hard, but it does not let go.","causes.3.short":"Run down by orcas","causes.3.text":"The calls of orcas come up from the seafloor. Your companions turn at once, but in the moment you fall behind, the orca is already close.","causes.4.short":"Caught in a drift net","causes.4.text":"An invisible net hangs across your gills. The more you struggle, the tighter it binds.","causes.5.short":"Caught by a brown bear","causes.5.text":"A brown bear crouches beneath a waterfall. With one swipe of its paw, it flings you from the water onto the bank.","causes.6.short":"Exhausted by the current, died after spawning","causes.6.text":"You swim upstream through the rapids, your energy long since spent. After spawning, your body slowly stops, and drifts downstream with the water.","causes.7.short":"Died after spawning","causes.7.text":"You bury your eggs in the riverbed, and your body slowly comes to rest. Your remains will become nourishment for the river.","events.0.text":"The eggs lie among the gravel. Water slips slowly through the gaps, bringing oxygen. Your embryo is slowly developing.","events.1.text":"A tiny insect crawls past the gravel, and its body brushes the egg membrane. The membrane trembles faintly.","events.2.text":"Once you hatch, your yolk sac still hangs beneath your belly. You hide in a crack in the riverbed and draw nourishment from it.","events.2.fact":"Fry of Pacific salmon feed on their yolk and only begin to forage actively after leaving the gravel (Groot and Margolis, 1991).","events.3.text":"You hide in a crack in the riverbed, perfectly still. The water slides over your body.","events.4.text":"You chase down a water insect larva and swallow it whole.","events.5.text":"The current carries you down to the river mouth. The salt water tightens your body, and you can only adjust slowly.","events.6.text":"A kingfisher skims over the surface, and you shrink back under a stone.","events.7.text":"In the sea you chase a group of small shrimp, which scatter rapidly through the water.","events.8.text":"You swim into a deeper layer of water, which is safer than the surface.","events.9.text":"You drift with the ocean current beyond the continental shelf, where the water temperature stays steady.","events.10.text":"Birds dive over the sea surface, and you sink a few meters deeper.","events.11.text":"You feed greedily in a swarm of krill. Your body grows sturdier by the day, and your color turns silver-white.","events.12.text":"You swim far to the north, where the sea is cold and deep. Here there is more food than in the south.","events.13.text":"You hang in the water with a group of your kind, drifting slowly with the current.","events.14.text":"A deep, urgent call from orcas comes up from the seafloor. All your companions turn away to escape.","events.15.text":"The earth’s magnetic field and the scent of the river seem to guide you. It is time to go home.","events.15.fact":"After years at sea, Pacific salmon find their natal river using the Earth’s magnetic field and the odor of the stream (Groot and Margolis, 1991).","events.16.text":"You return to the river where you were born. The water has turned fresh, and the smell of the riverbed has changed. You recognize it.","events.17.text":"You dig a pit in the riverbed and lay your eggs in it. Beside you, a male releases his milt.","events.18.text":"Your body darkens, and red and black streaks appear along your flanks. Your fins are worn and torn.","events.19.text":"You push upstream against the current. Fish like you are everywhere on the riverbed, all digging pits of their own.","events.20.text":"A brown bear crouches beneath a waterfall, its claws pulling fish from the water again and again.","events.20.fact":"The bodies of dead salmon carry nitrogen from the ocean into riverside forests, where it promotes the growth of spruce and other trees (Helfield and Naiman, 2001, Ecology).","events.21.text":"Your skin begins to flake away, and your fins can no longer keep you balanced. The current carries you to a stone on the bank.","events.22.text":"Water flows slowly between the gravel, carrying oxygen. The eggs around you turn white one by one, and you are still inside your transparent shell.","events.23.text":"You swim out of the gravel for the first time. The riverbed is covered with stones and fallen leaves, and you know every one of them.","events.24.text":"You linger for a long time in the shallows at the river mouth, tasting the water, before slowly swimming out to sea.","events.24.fact":"Juvenile salmon imprint on the smell of their home river and use it to find their way back to it (Hasler & Scholz 1983, Olfactory Imprinting and Homing in Salmon)","events.25.text":"In the tank you share, water trickles in slowly through a pipe. Staff check on you every day and count your numbers.","events.26.text":"You are released into the river for the first time. The current is much faster than in the tank, and it keeps carrying you downstream. You cannot stop.","events.27.text":"You have spent several months at sea, but your adipose fin was clipped. The other fish no longer treat you as one of their own.","events.28.text":"The riverbanks are concrete and the riverbed has been leveled. Eggs can only settle between the pieces of crushed stone.","events.29.text":"You swim along a straight channel with no gaps to hide in. A bird circles overhead, and you press against the bottom and stay perfectly still.","events.30.text":"A sluice gate downstream drops the water level suddenly. You are trapped in a shallow hollow, and it takes a long time to swim out.","events.31.text":"A surge of meltwater rushes in and pushes silt into the gaps between the gravel. Half the eggs around you are washed away.","events.32.text":"The water is fast and murky, and it sweeps you into an unfamiliar bend. You cannot find the gap you were in before.","events.33.text":"There is plenty of food at sea, and you grow quickly and strongly.","events.34.text":"The water keeps getting shallower, and the surface of the eggs begins to touch air. Several of the eggs near you have already dried out.","events.35.text":"The river has shrunk to a thin trickle. You and your companions crowd into a deep pool, and the water around you keeps getting warmer.","events.36.text":"The river mouth is saltier and warmer than in other years. At sea you are hungrier than usual.","events.37.text":"Aquatic insects are abundant in the stream. You eat them one mouthful at a time, and your body grows fast.","events.38.text":"The water is cool, and tiny bubbles of oxygen cling between the stones. You grow slowly inside your shell.","events.39.text":"The seawater at the river mouth grows saltier each day. You follow a school of young fish and slowly swim into deeper water.","events.40.text":"You are the first to leave the gravel and swim into the fast current in the middle of the river. Your companions are still hiding, and you are already far ahead.","events.41.text":"You stay in the gravel for a long time, waiting for the movement in the water to pass, and only then slowly move on.","events.42.text":"You swim among the reefs on the seafloor, watching how each kind of fish feeds.","events.43.text":"You are smaller than your companions and cannot squeeze into the gaps between the stones. You have to wait outside for the current to carry food to you.","events.44.text":"You grow fast at sea. A seal bites your back and leaves only a shallow wound.","decisions.0.title":"When to go to sea","decisions.0.body":"You have hatched from the egg, but your body is still small, and you hide among the stones. The spring flood brings stronger currents, and you can already sense the salt water at the river mouth. You must decide when to drift downstream.","decisions.0.fact":"Pacific salmon fry stay in the river for weeks to months before drifting downstream to the sea (Groot and Margolis, 1991).","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Ride the spring flood to the sea","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You are swept by the spring flood into the river mouth. The salt water is uncomfortable, but the sea lies just ahead.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Wait a few weeks in the shallows of the river mouth","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You wait in the shallows of the river mouth for a few weeks and eat plenty of insects. The river mouth is also a hunting ground for many birds.","decisions.1.title":"Follow the school, or swim alone","decisions.1.body":"A group of your kind is swimming north through the sea. Following them makes you less conspicuous; swimming alone may lead you to more food.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Follow the school north","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You follow the school north. The fish block the predators’ view with their numbers.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Leave the school and forage on your own","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You leave the school and feed for a long time in a new krill patch. Only afterward do you notice that no companions are beside you.","decisions.2.title":"A shadow beneath the surface","decisions.2.body":"The shadow of a large fish rises from below. It is much bigger than you, and your fins have begun to tense.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Dive into deeper water","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You dive into deep water. The shadow pauses for a moment, then slowly fades away.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Keep chasing prey near the surface","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You keep feeding near the surface, and the water behind you suddenly churns.","decisions.3.title":"Time to go home","decisions.3.body":"There is still enough food in the sea. But your body begins to feel a pull you cannot quite name: the magnetic field and the scent of the river seem to be calling you.","decisions.3.fact":"After spending years at sea, Pacific salmon return to their natal river using the Earth’s magnetic field and their sense of smell (Groot and Margolis, 1991).","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Head southwest, following the magnetic field back","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You turn southwest. Your body begins preparing for the journey home, and your skin slowly changes color.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Feed longer in the sea","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Offspring↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You feed for a long time more, and your body grows heavy. The journey home is put off again and again.","decisions.4.title":"Before the waterfall","decisions.4.body":"You have reached the narrowest stretch of the river. Ahead, a waterfall pours down, and the water is fast. Your body is already exhausted.","decisions.4.fact":"Salmon carcasses carry nitrogen from the sea back into riverside forests, promoting the growth of nearby trees (Helfield and Naiman, 2001, Ecology).","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Leap over the waterfall","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You leap and plunge into the curtain of water. A paw reaches out from the bank.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Find a spring-fed spawning site downstream","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety · Long detour","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You circle back downstream and find a stretch of riverbed where groundwater seeps up. The water is cold, and you stop there.","decisions.5.title":"Another male","decisions.5.body":"A larger male blocks the way between you and a female. His jaws are open, as if to warn you off.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Bite his flank and fight for her","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You crash into each other over the riverbed. You win once, but you come away with several new wounds.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Slip around to the side and seek another female","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Offspring↑ Safety","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You circle around to the side. The female does not reject you, and you lay your eggs in a freshly dug pit.","decisions.6.title":"Release day","decisions.6.body":"The staff open the gate of the tank, and you are let out into the river. The current is fast, and kingfishers hover over the water.","decisions.6.fact":"Hatchery-reared salmon usually have lower reproductive success in the wild than wild-born fish, and the gap widens over successive generations of hatchery breeding (Araki, Cooper & Blouin 2007, Science)","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Swim with the current, all the way to the sea","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Fast · Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You ride the current all the way downstream without stopping. A kingfisher passes over your head, and you do not look back.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Hide in a gap in the bank for a few days","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You slip into a gap in the bank and wait for the movement in the water to settle.","decisions.7.title":"The river mouth","decisions.7.body":"You reach the river mouth. The taste of the water has changed: on one side is the familiar river, and on the other is salty sea water.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Keep swimming out to sea","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You enter the seawater. The taste of the river fades slowly behind you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Pause in the shallows and taste the river water","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay in the shallows for a long time, memorizing the taste, before turning to swim out to sea.","decisions.8.title":"An unfamiliar bend","decisions.8.body":"The river carries you into a bend. A whirlpool turns on one side, and on the other is a wide bed of aquatic plants.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Slip into a gap in the stones at the edge of the whirlpool","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You slip into the gap along the stones. The whirlpool spins outside for a few turns, then slowly calms.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Follow the current toward the aquatic plants","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You cross the current and slip into the plants. They grow thick, and you find some insects to eat among them.","decisions.9.title":"Only one deep pool left","decisions.9.body":"The river has shrunk to a thin stream, and the deep pools you can stay in are getting fewer. Downstream there is a bed of aquatic plants, but you would have to cross a shallow stretch to reach it.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Cross the shallows to reach the plants","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Bird-snatch risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You skim across the shallows along the surface. Your back is exposed, and a kingfisher's shadow flickers over you as it passes.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay in the deep pool and wait for the water to return","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You wait in the deep pool. The water returns slowly, and you go hungry for a long time.","opening.title":"The spawning riverbed","opening.body":"You have not been born yet. Your mother is digging a pit in the riverbed and deciding where to bury her eggs. Upstream, the gravel is clean, the current is swift, and oxygen is plentiful, but the flow may wash the eggs away. Downstream, the water moves slowly and carries more silt, so the eggs can easily suffocate.","opening.options.0.t":"Bury them under gravel in the swift upstream current","opening.options.0.hint":"Oxygen↑ Washout risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs are buried deep in the gravel. Water passes through the gaps, bringing oxygen, along with many things drifting in it.","opening.options.1.t":"Bury them in the sandy bed of the slow downstream reach","opening.options.1.hint":"Silt↑ Oxygen↓","opening.options.1.result":"The eggs are buried in the sand downstream. The water is slow, and silt settles over them layer by layer.","actions.forage.name":"Forage","actions.forage.desc":"Eat aquatic insects in the river, and chase small shrimp and krill at sea.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in deep water or among stones","actions.hide.desc":"Retreat into a crevice between stones or a deeper layer of water, to be seen less.","actions.rest.name":"Drift with the current","actions.rest.desc":"Stop swimming on your own and drift slowly with the ocean current to conserve energy.","actions.explore.name":"Swim toward distant waters","actions.explore.desc":"Leave familiar waters for a farther sea or river mouth.","actions.mate.name":"Spawn","actions.mate.done":"You lay {n} eggs on the riverbed, and they are covered by the pit you dug.","idle.0":"You swim slowly through the water, your gill covers opening and closing.","idle.1":"The river’s temperature changes slowly with the seasons.","idle.2":"A bird flies past in the distance, and its shadow falls across the water.","idle.3":"In the sea your body gradually takes on a shine, its color shifting with the depth.","idle.4":"The ocean current pushes you north, and you swim along with it.","idle.5":"You smell salt, along with the mud of the river and the scent of fallen leaves.","idle.6":"Among the gravel on the riverbed, some eggs wait quietly.","idle.7":"A waterbird perches on the bank, and its eye flashes in the sunlight.","actions.forage.text.0":"You chase a group of small shrimp, which scatter rapidly through the water.","actions.forage.text.1":"You feed greedily in a swarm of krill.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip into a crevice between the stones and hold perfectly still.","actions.hide.text.1":"You swim into a deeper layer of water, which is safer than the surface.","actions.rest.text.0":"You drift slowly with the current.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim toward distant waters with the current, and the water grows colder."},"sardine":{"name":"Sardine","tagline":"A single sardine survives mostly by luck, but a shoal of them is a staple for seabirds, dolphins and large fish","survivalNote":"Early mortality in sardines is extremely high, and only a very small share of eggs reach the adult stock. Lifespans can exceed ten years. This curve is a rough estimate based on early life-history studies and fisheries statistics","epitaph":"A single batch may hold tens of thousands of eggs, and only a handful of them reach adulthood. That is the sardine's arithmetic, and the sea's.","taxon":"Chordata › Actinopterygii › Clupeiformes › Clupeidae","size":"Adults about 15–25 cm long","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth area","birth.rolls.1.label":"Sea conditions that year","birth.rolls.2.label":"Hatching season","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs spawned together in the same batch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, drifting in {r0}, about one millimeter across. You are {sex}. Your batch holds roughly {siblings} eggs, but only a small fraction will grow up.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a coastal upwelling zone, a shallow sea rich in plankton","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the western edge of a warm ocean current","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a windy, murky stretch of inshore water","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"open seawater far from the coast","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an ordinary year, with water temperature and currents in their usual range","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"an El Niño year, with warm water pushing east and weaker upwelling","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a La Niña year, with strong cold upwelling and plenty of plankton","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"late-spring hatching, in time for the spring plankton peak","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"midsummer hatching, with warm water, many eggs, and high early mortality","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"late-autumn hatching, as the plankton is already thinning out","stages.0.name":"Egg (drifting)","stages.1.name":"Larva","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (in the shoal)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old fish","causes.0.short":"Caught in a purse seine","causes.0.text":"The net ring tightens slowly along the side of the boat, and the shoal is pressed together. You thrash in the net for several minutes before being scooped into the hold.","causes.1.short":"Herded into a bait ball by dolphins","causes.1.text":"Dolphins pen the shoal into a tight ball and take turns darting in to feed. You were on the outer layer, and you did not survive the third rush.","causes.2.short":"Snatched by a gannet's dive","causes.2.text":"A gannet folds its wings and drops from a dozen meters up, straight into the water. Its bill pinches you out of the shoal.","causes.3.short":"Scattered by tuna and eaten","causes.3.text":"A school of tuna rises from below and the shoal bursts apart. You were not fast enough.","causes.4.short":"Eaten early in life","causes.4.text":"You were still tiny. A copepod, a jellyfish or a larger fish ate you, along with the companions around you.","causes.5.short":"Starved after the plankton peak passed","causes.5.text":"Plankton suddenly thinned out over several days, and your gut ran empty. Every stretch of sea you swam through was bare.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your scales lost their sheen and you swam more and more slowly. In the end you drifted to the edge of the shoal and sank.","events.0.text":"The eggs drift at the surface, thousands of them crowded together, just like you.","events.1.text":"You hatch into a larva only a few millimeters long. Your mouth is tiny, and you can eat only the smallest plankton.","events.2.text":"Among the plankton you find a small copepod nauplius and eat it.","events.3.text":"A jellyfish's tentacle sweeps past, and you barely avoid it.","events.4.text":"A breeze pushes the surface water toward the shore, and you are carried into shallower, warmer water.","events.5.text":"You find no food all day. Your body turns transparent and slowly narrows.","events.6.text":"You gather with fish of your own age. When the group turns, every fish seems to receive the signal at once.","events.7.text":"At dusk the shoal swims close to the surface, and you eat a great deal of zooplankton.","events.8.text":"A dolphin charges in from beneath the shoal, and you all wheel into a ring at once.","events.9.text":"Summer seawater is warm. Your shoal swims slowly and feeds only lightly.","events.10.text":"You spawn for the first time, releasing eggs and sperm into the water, where they meet.","events.11.text":"A flock of boobies circles above the shoal. Their shadows fall on the surface, and the shoal sinks deep at once.","events.12.text":"The shoal bunches into a ball, and the fish on the outside keep getting bitten off. You are in the middle and pick up a few scraps.","events.13.text":"A fishing boat works at night, its lights come on, and the shoal is drawn toward the glow.","events.14.text":"In the distance a purse seine is closing, and the shoal is being encircled bit by bit.","events.15.text":"Upwelling brings deep nutrients to the surface, and plankton suddenly multiplies.","events.16.text":"The sea warms and plankton numbers fall. You eat a little less.","events.17.text":"You are full, your body thick and solid, and you hold steady within the shoal.","events.18.text":"A tuna passes beside the shoal. The shoal splits and closes again.","events.19.text":"You slow down and can only follow along the edge of the shoal.","events.20.text":"Your scales have lost their sheen, and you bear several old scars.","events.21.text":"You have not spawned for a long time, and your gonads have shrunk.","events.22.text":"A group of young sardines passes you, and you keep out of each other's way.","events.23.text":"In the surface water you find a copepod nauplius and eat it.","events.24.text":"At dusk the shoal feeds on zooplankton near the surface. The sunset turns the water gold, and you eat well.","events.25.text":"A flock of boobies circles above the shoal. You dive, take one mouthful of zooplankton, and are driven back up.","events.26.text":"A cold current wells up suddenly, and the surface plankton sinks at once.","events.27.text":"You squeeze into the center of the bait ball. Outside you hear dolphins slamming into the shoal, but the center is quiet.","events.28.text":"You hide close to the seabed, above a layer of fine sand. Bird shadows sweep back and forth across the surface far above.","events.29.text":"You drift with the current while the shoal around you slowly turns. Your body recovers a little.","events.30.text":"A shark glides slowly past from below. It does not come close, but the water pressure dips.","events.31.text":"You swim into an upwelling where the water is cool and plankton is dense, and you feed well.","events.32.text":"You leave the shoal and lose your way in shallow inshore water. There are many rocks, and you circle several times before you find the shoal again.","events.33.text":"Beside a seagrass bed in shallow water, a few small fish stay with you for a while. They are not your shoal, and they soon swim off.","events.34.text":"Near the surface, you and your companions release eggs and sperm at the same time. In the seawater they find each other.","events.35.text":"The plankton peak arrives on time this year. You feed near the surface for several days and never go hungry.","events.36.text":"The shoal migrates with the seasons. You swim along a familiar route, and nothing goes wrong on the way.","events.37.text":"The surface water stays warm, and copepod larvae are scattered widely. For several days you cannot find a dense patch of plankton.","events.38.text":"A warm current pushes up from the south, and the shoal sinks into deeper water, leaving its usual feeding grounds.","events.39.text":"Cold water wells up from the depths, and the surface is rich with nutrients. You eat your fill in a single day.","events.40.text":"The cold water stretches your feeding range. You swim along the edge of the upwelling for a long time, eating well.","events.41.text":"The spring water warms day by day. The eggs develop smoothly, and within a week you have grown eyespots.","events.42.text":"You open your mouth for the first time and run straight into a dense patch of copepod larvae. You feed quickly.","events.43.text":"The summer water is warm, so the eggs develop fast. You use up your yolk earlier than most.","events.44.text":"The water is warm and your metabolism runs fast. You have not found enough food yet, and your body is already getting thin.","events.45.text":"Plankton grows scarcer through autumn. You search for food every day, and you find less each time.","events.46.text":"You grow up a season later than fish of the same age. When the shoal turns, you always lag behind and get pushed to the edge.","events.47.text":"A pod of dolphins charges in from far off. You are the first to turn, and you lead the shoal to the other side.","events.48.text":"You are smaller than other juveniles your age. You cannot compete for plankton, and you can only pick up the scraps the others leave behind.","events.49.text":"You stay at the edge of the shoal for a long time, until you are sure the water ahead is quiet, and then you follow the group forward.","events.50.text":"Your body is sturdy. You swim into the middle of the shoal without being hurt, and the wake of the school pushes you to the front.","decisions.0.title":"Spawning season","decisions.0.body":"The seawater has warmed to a range suited to spawning, and your body urges you on. When you spawn, you gather close to the surface.","decisions.0.fact":"Sardines release eggs and sperm directly into the sea, and early mortality among eggs and larvae is very high (Hjort 1914, the classic study of early fish mortality)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Spawn heavily at the surface","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You release your eggs near the surface. They drift apart like a thin layer of snow.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Linger a few more days in deep water","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Offspring↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stay in deeper water. The eggs are still inside you, and there is no hurry.","decisions.1.title":"Bait ball","decisions.1.body":"A dolphin charges up from below, and the shoal gathers into a tight ball. The fish on the outside are taken one by one, while those in the middle can still move.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Slip into the middle of the ball","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You squeeze into the middle of the ball. The muffled sound of fish being bitten on the outside reaches you.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Break away from the edge","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Danger↑↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You swim out from the edge. The dolphin's beak snaps shut behind you, just a little too late.","decisions.2.title":"The net ring in the distance","decisions.2.body":"A fishing boat far off has lowered its net. The sound of the ring presses heavily toward you, and the shoal is slowly closing in.","decisions.2.fact":"Purse seining is one of the most common ways of catching sardines, and the catch is widely used for fishmeal and fish oil (FAO, The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture, 2022)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Dive and keep to the seabed","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety · Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You dive deep and keep close to the seabed. Above you the net ring closes with a loud roar.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Follow the shoal toward the net mouth","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Danger↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You follow the shoal toward the mouth of the net. The ring tightens behind you.","decisions.3.title":"The plankton peak is over","decisions.3.body":"Plankton has suddenly declined over the past few days, and you cannot find food. Shallow water near the coast may have some, but it is more dangerous.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Head for shallow water near the coast","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You swim into the shallows near the coast. There is plenty to eat there, along with many larger fish.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay offshore and keep searching","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You keep swimming offshore, hoping the next patch of water has food.","decisions.4.title":"A warm current","decisions.4.body":"A warm current is flowing in from the coast and has raised the water temperature by several degrees. Plankton grows fast in it, but the big fish that eat plankton like it too.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Follow the warm water toward the shore","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You swim into the warm water. There is plenty to eat here, and many fish much bigger than you.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay in the cold water","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You stay in the cold water and wait for the current to bring food to you.","decisions.5.title":"Big shoal or small shoal","decisions.5.body":"You can now swim in a group. A large shoal protects you, but it has many mouths to feed. A smaller group is easier to find food for, but less safe.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Stay in the big shoal","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Less food","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You stay in the big shoal. When it turns, you turn with it.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Look for a smaller group","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You find a small group of fish where the plankton has not yet been eaten away.","decisions.6.title":"The edge of the shoal","decisions.6.body":"Your scales have lost their sheen. The young fish gather at the center of the shoal, and you are slowly drifting toward the edge. That is where you are most easily seen, and where you are eaten first.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Return to the center, pressed among the young fish","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You slowly swim back into the middle of the shoal. The young fish crowd you, but at last you have cover again.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay at the edge and keep feeding","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay at the edge and eat your fill. In the distance the net ring is tightening, and you slip out through a gap in it just in time.","decisions.7.title":"The last migration","decisions.7.body":"The seasons turn, year after year. Your body is much lighter than before, and the shoal is getting ready to migrate to warmer waters. You can follow the group, or leave and wait in the shallows.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Migrate with the shoal","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You swim slowly with the shoal. The water grows warmer day by day, and there is a little more food than before.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Leave the shoal and go alone to the shallows","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You leave the shoal for the shallows. It is quiet there and food is scarce, but no large fish cast their shadow over you.","decisions.8.title":"Warm water pushes in","decisions.8.body":"Warm water from the south scatters the plankton near the surface. The cold water is still in the depths, but it is far from you.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Dive into the cold water below","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You swim deeper. The water grows colder, and the warm layer spreads slowly over your head.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay in the warm water and wait for plankton","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food · Risk↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You stay in the warm water. There is little plankton here, but you still catch a few mouthfuls.","decisions.9.title":"The edge of the upwelling","decisions.9.body":"Cold water rises from the depths and forms a clear boundary just below the surface. On one side the plankton is dense enough to look white, and on the other side there is almost nothing.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the upwelling edge and eat your fill","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You push into the boundary. There is plenty to eat, and tuna are waiting in the water below.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay on the outside of the shoal and eat slowly","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay on the outer edge of the shoal and eat less, but every mouthful is safe.","decisions.10.title":"A strange smell","decisions.10.body":"A strange smell drifts in from the edge of the shoal, seemingly from a patch of seaweed near the shore. It is unlike anything you have smelled before.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Follow the smell to see what it is","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You leave the shoal and swim toward where the smell comes from. The seaweed is full of small creatures, and there are strange fish too.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay in the shoal and do not move","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You do not go to look. The shoal moves on, and the smell soon fades.","opening.title":"When and where to spawn","opening.body":"You are still an egg. The shoal your mother belongs to is gathering near the surface, the water temperature is right, and spawning is about to begin. Spawning by day at the surface yields more eggs, but seabirds and fish can see them. Spawning at night in somewhat deeper water is far quieter.","opening.options.0.t":"Spawn together at the surface by day","opening.options.0.hint":"Eggs↑ Seabirds↑","opening.options.0.result":"The eggs scatter near the sunlit surface like a thin layer of snow. There are a great many of them, but the seabirds can see them too.","opening.options.1.t":"Spawn at night in deeper water","opening.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","opening.options.1.result":"The eggs sink into the water under cover of darkness. Plankton is scarcer here, and you and your companions must live off the yolk for the first few days.","actions.forage.name":"Filter-feed at the surface","actions.forage.desc":"Graze zooplankton near the sea surface. The shoal rises together and sinks together.","actions.hide.name":"Slip into the bait ball's center","actions.hide.desc":"Squeeze into the center of the shoal, or keep to the seabed to avoid seabirds and sharks.","actions.rest.name":"Drift with the current","actions.rest.desc":"Drift slowly in the current, barely using any effort.","actions.explore.name":"Leave the shoal for the shore","actions.explore.desc":"Break away from the shoal and swim toward the upwelling or the shallows near the coast.","actions.mate.name":"Spawn near the surface","actions.mate.desc":"The mature shoal gathers near the surface and releases eggs and sperm into the water together.","actions.mate.done":"You and your companions released {n} eggs together, and they soon drifted apart.","idle.0":"Sunlight on the surface is warm and bright, and the water slowly deepens from pale blue.","idle.1":"Somewhere in the distance a boat is passing. Its engine sound reaches you through the water, muffled.","idle.2":"The waves rise and fall, and you drift gently with the current.","idle.3":"The water temperature around you shifts slowly, sometimes warmer, sometimes cooler.","idle.4":"The shadow of a seabird crosses the surface and is gone in a moment.","idle.5":"Tiny creatures drift through the water, carried along with no direction of their own.","idle.6":"The night sea is dark, and every so often a faint glow flickers past.","idle.7":"Sometimes you can feel the water stirring gently nearby.","actions.forage.text.0":"You open your mouth, and seawater and zooplankton rush in together.","actions.forage.text.1":"The shoal circles near the surface, then slowly sinks again.","actions.forage.text.2":"Tiny copepods drift in the water, and you swallow them one by one.","actions.hide.text.0":"Fish are all around you, and you can barely see outside.","actions.hide.text.1":"The shoal tightens, and every fish turns at the same moment.","actions.hide.text.2":"A bird's shadow passes over the surface in the distance, and you sink with the shoal.","actions.rest.text.0":"You drift with the current, your fins perfectly still.","actions.rest.text.1":"The waves rise and fall, gently carrying you along.","actions.rest.text.2":"The shoal beside you slowly turns, and you turn a little too.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim into cooler water, where plankton is so thick it looks green.","actions.explore.text.1":"The seabed of the shore appears below you, and the light grows brighter.","actions.explore.text.2":"In the shallows you meet a few unfamiliar small fish, which soon scatter.","actions.mate.text.0":"The shoal gathers near the surface, and the water temperature is just right.","actions.mate.text.1":"You and your companions release eggs and sperm at the same time.","actions.mate.text.2":"They drift apart slowly in the seawater and soon are out of sight."},"sea_turtle":{"name":"Green sea turtle","tagline":"A clutch of about a hundred eggs, most of which never reach the sea. Fewer than one in a thousand survive to adulthood","survivalNote":"Hatchling mortality is the highest of any stage. Survival from beach to adulthood is often estimated at about one in a thousand. The curve after adulthood is a rough estimate, and very long-lived individuals are rare","epitaph":"A clutch of roughly a hundred eggs yields only a few survivors to adulthood. Your life was a small part of this sea.","taxon":"Chordata › Reptilia › Testudines › Cheloniidae","size":"About 5 cm at hatching; adult carapace about 1 m long","birth.rolls.0.label":"Nest location","birth.rolls.1.label":"Ocean basin","birth.rolls.2.label":"Guardianship of the beach","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid in the same clutch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, buried in {r0}. The temperature of the sand decides your sex, and this time you are {sex}. There are {siblings} eggs in your clutch. Your mother dug the hole alone in the night, laid her eggs, and crawled back into the sea.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a sun-baked sand pit high on the beach","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a shadier sand pit near the waterline","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a sand pit in the shade of a coconut palm","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a beach on the western Pacific, where currents carry hatchlings east","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a beach on the western Atlantic, with seagrass beds close offshore","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"an Indian Ocean island, rich in seagrass but also full of fishing nets","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a protected beach, patrolled at night by people with flashlights","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"an unguarded beach, with vehicles driving across the sand at night","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a brightly lit beach, lit all night by a seaside hotel","stages.0.name":"Egg (in the sand)","stages.1.name":"Hatchling (from beach to sea)","stages.2.name":"Drift years (the \"lost years\")","stages.3.name":"Juvenile near shore (seagrass and reef)","stages.4.name":"Adult sea turtle","stages.5.name":"Old turtle","causes.0.short":"Taken by crabs or gulls","causes.0.text":"Crabs and gulls found the newly hatched turtles first. You crawled only a few meters before you stopped moving.","causes.1.short":"Seized by a shark","causes.1.text":"A shark came up from below and clamped onto your hind leg. Your shell cracked between its teeth.","causes.2.short":"Drowned on a longline hook","causes.2.text":"The hook caught your foreflipper. You were hauled up onto the boat and out of the water too long, and you could not breathe again.","causes.3.short":"Drowned in a gillnet","causes.3.text":"The gillnet tangled around your neck. You could not swim up to breathe, and you sank in the net.","causes.4.short":"Swallowed plastic blocked the gut","causes.4.text":"You mistook a translucent plastic bag for a jellyfish and swallowed it. It lodged in your digestive tract, and slowly you stopped eating.","causes.5.short":"Struck by a boat propeller","causes.5.text":"A speedboat passed over the surface. Its propeller cut a deep gash across your carapace.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"The gaps between your breaths grew longer and longer. In the end you settled to sleep in a seagrass bed and did not wake.","events.0.text":"The sand pit stays at a steady temperature. The eggs grow slowly in the middle of the sand.","events.1.text":"During incubation, the temperature of the sand is deciding your sex. Warmer incubation usually produces females.","events.1.fact":"The sex of green sea turtles is set by incubation temperature, with higher temperatures favoring females (Mrosovsky & Yntema 1980)","events.2.text":"You wriggle out of the eggshell and crawl toward the sea with dozens of siblings.","events.3.text":"You drift with the ocean current on a large raft of sargassum. It offers shelter, and small shrimp and fish live among its fronds.","events.3.fact":"Carr (1987) called the stage when hatchlings drift in the open-ocean surface the \"lost years.\" It remains one of the least studied periods in the turtle's life","events.4.text":"Another raft of sargassum drifts by. You crawl in and bite a small shrimp from among the strands.","events.5.text":"A storm at sea tumbles you over, and you stay underwater a long time before you surface again.","events.6.text":"A seabird lands on the water and circles low. You hide tightly among the weed.","events.7.text":"A translucent plastic bag drifts past, looking like a jellyfish. You swim over and swallow a mouthful.","events.8.text":"At night, phosphorescence lights up around you, and you seem to swim through a field of stars.","events.9.text":"You reach a seagrass bed, and the blades sway gently in the water.","events.10.text":"A school of small fish passes through the seagrass, and you eat several of those nearby.","events.11.text":"Barnacles have settled on your carapace, slowing you down a little.","events.12.text":"For the first time you see a large sea turtle slowly grazing on seagrass at the edge of a coral reef.","events.13.text":"The engine of a fishing boat passes overhead, and the water shakes.","events.14.text":"You graze on the seagrass bed, and every bite cuts off several blades.","events.15.text":"You swallow a piece of plastic, and it stays in your stomach for a long time.","events.16.text":"In the distance, the float of a longline drifts on the surface. You swim around it.","events.17.text":"The shadow of a tiger shark flashes past in the distance.","events.18.text":"You crawl onto the beach for the first time, dig a pit, and lay {n} eggs in the night.","events.19.text":"At night you swim over a coral reef with the current, and the shadows of the rocks slide beneath you.","events.20.text":"You swim more slowly now, and the pauses between your breaths grow longer.","events.21.text":"Algae and barnacles have grown on your shell.","events.22.text":"The gaps between your nesting seasons grow longer, and you lay less often.","events.23.text":"You bite a small shrimp in the sargassum. It curls up and retreats deeper into the weed, and you do not follow.","events.24.text":"You bite through several seagrass blades, and as you chew, small fish scatter from the leaves.","events.25.text":"You graze on the seagrass bed one mouthful at a time. The grass beneath you is stripped bare.","events.26.text":"Your teeth have worn down, and you can only crush the seagrass slowly.","events.27.text":"You slip into a clump of sargassum, and the weed quickly covers you. It is dark and busy inside, full of small creatures.","events.28.text":"You tuck into a crack in the reef, with only half your head showing. A shark's shadow glides slowly past the rocks outside.","events.29.text":"You sink in the water, and each breath is held a long time. The water around you is still.","events.30.text":"You doze beneath the sargassum as it sinks. When you wake, the raft has drifted a little farther away.","events.31.text":"You swim into an unfamiliar reef, where the coral is a riot of colors and fish are too many to count.","events.32.text":"You leave your familiar seagrass bed and swim toward the open sea for several days. You see only blue water on the way, and no food at all.","events.33.text":"You crawl onto the beach and dig a pit to lay your eggs. The eggs drop one by one, and you keep at it through the night.","events.34.text":"On the beach you see a flashlight blink in the distance. You stop digging, then slowly resume.","events.35.text":"You and your littermates charge into the sea together, and the currents quickly carry you toward the deep water to the east.","events.36.text":"On the drift, you rest on a large mat of sargassum that rocks slowly with the current. In the weed you find a small group of companions.","events.37.text":"You grow up on seagrass beds, and more and more algae settle on your shell.","events.38.text":"You crawl across the mudflat toward the glint of light on the water. On the shore, a crab pokes its head out of the sand, then pulls it back in.","events.39.text":"The Atlantic current is strong. It carries you to a floating mat of seagrass, and there you stop.","events.40.text":"You swim to a seagrass bed, bite off a blade, and chew it slowly.","events.41.text":"The shore is hung with many fishing nets. You swim through shallow water and almost get tangled in a net line.","events.42.text":"The reef has many crevices. You slip into one and escape a school of fish chasing you.","events.43.text":"You swim past one seagrass bed after another. The island has shrunk to a line on the distant horizon.","events.44.text":"At night, a man in a reflective vest watches over the sand pit. He crouches beside it and shines a flashlight carefully over the nest.","events.45.text":"The patrol builds a path out of wooden boards to help you crawl toward the sea. You crawl slowly, but you do not stray.","events.46.text":"You return to the protected beach, and a small marker pole stands where your nest was.","events.47.text":"Late at night a vehicle drives across the beach. Where its tires press down, the sand cracks open.","events.48.text":"Halfway to the water, the headlights of a vehicle on the beach sweep over you. In the glare you scramble in circles and lose your bearings.","events.49.text":"You climb onto the beach, ready to dig a hole, when a crowd gathers around you holding up their phones. You stop and wait for them to leave.","events.50.text":"The lights of the seaside hotel stay on all night. The hatchlings crawl toward the glow, reach the grass, and are baked by the sun when morning comes.","events.50.fact":"Hatchling sea turtles orient toward the brightest horizon, and artificial lights can lead them away from the sea (Witherington & Martin 1996, a report on lighting and sea turtle conservation in Florida)","events.51.text":"The light above the sand pit flickers on and off. You are still inside the shell and do not know where the light comes from.","events.52.text":"You climb onto the beach, and the light falls on the sand behind you. You press the sand down hard, trying to keep the light off the eggs.","events.53.text":"You are the first to charge into the surf, while the other hatchlings are still struggling on the sand. You do not look back.","events.54.text":"You pause at the water's edge and wait for one wave to recede, then dash into the sea on the next.","events.55.text":"You turn over the drifting sargassum and see a small crab trapped under one of the leaves.","events.56.text":"You are the smallest and crawl slowly up the beach. A wave pushes you back to shore, and you have to start again.","events.57.text":"A shark clamps onto your hind leg, and you kick hard until it lets go. A scar is left across your shell.","decisions.0.title":"The beach at night","decisions.0.body":"You climb out of the sand pit. Toward the sea there is a glow, the moon reflecting on the water. On the other side are dunes, their shadows deep.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Crawl at full speed toward the bright sea","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Crabs and gulls↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You crawl for several minutes without stopping. A few crabs chase you for a while, but none catch up.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Crawl into the shadow of the dunes","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Danger↑↑ Dehydration↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You crawl into the dune's shadow. There is no sea there, so you wait for daylight and then bake under the sun for a whole day.","decisions.1.title":"Sargassum","decisions.1.body":"A large raft of sargassum drifts in the distance. Inside it you can hide from predators, and it holds many small creatures. Open ocean is deeper, and there is no cover there.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Move close to the sargassum and hide inside","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Safety↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You slip into the gaps of the sargassum. It is dark and busy inside, and small shrimp crawl around you.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Swim toward the deep open sea","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↓ · Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You leave the sargassum and swim into the depths of the sea. Only blue water surrounds you.","decisions.2.title":"Seagrass bed or reef","decisions.2.body":"The seagrass beds near shore have plenty of food, and fish gather at the edges of the coral reef. But the reef edge is full of sharks, and you must choose a place to live for a long time.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Stay in the seagrass bed","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Steady risk","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You stay on the seagrass bed, eating some leaves each day, and slowly growing.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Go to the edge of the reef","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Opportunity↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You swim to the edge of the reef. Fish are plentiful, but a large shark circles slowly beneath the reef wall.","decisions.3.title":"Return to the beach where you were born","decisions.3.body":"Something you cannot quite name pulls at your body, pointing toward a distant coast. That may be the beach where you hatched, and the route crosses a fishing ground.","decisions.3.fact":"Sea turtles use the strength and inclination of the Earth's magnetic field to determine their position, and can return to the vicinity of their natal waters (Lohmann & Lohmann 1996)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Migrate over long distances to nest","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You swim hundreds of kilometers. Passing through the fishing ground, you see the floats but do not steer around them.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Stay in your familiar seagrass bed","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Offspring↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You do not go to that distant beach. You keep grazing on the seagrass bed you know.","decisions.4.title":"A longline","decisions.4.body":"Ahead lies a longline, its floats strung out across the surface and bait hanging from the hooks. You can smell fish.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Dive deeper to go around it","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You dive into deeper water and go around the line. A thin cord brushes across your back.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Eat the bait","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You bite into the bait, and the hook sinks into the corner of your mouth.","decisions.5.title":"Cold water mass","decisions.5.body":"The current has carried you into colder water, and the temperature falls by the day. Ahead is a warm current with more food, but the current pushes you outward, and you must decide which way to swim.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Follow the warm current","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You swim with the warm current. Algae and small creatures grow more abundant here. You tire, but you eat well.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Stop in the cold water and drift","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Energy↓ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You stop in the cold water and drift slowly with the current. The water is clear around you, with no food and no predators.","decisions.6.title":"Sunlight on the surface","decisions.6.body":"The surface water is warm, and you could float up to bask and warm your body. But seabirds fly over the surface, circling low.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Float up and bask in the sun","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Energy↑ Seabirds↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You rise to the surface and bask for several hours. A seabird flies past you, and you dive again.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay underwater","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Energy↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay underwater, and your body stays cool.","decisions.7.title":"The seagrass beds turn gray","decisions.7.body":"Over the years, your familiar seagrass beds have shrunk. Patch by patch the grass has turned into gray mud. In the distance a new seagrass bed has grown up, but reaching it means crossing a fishing ground, where the net floats are lined up in a row.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Cross the fishing ground to the new seagrass bed","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Danger↑↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You cross the fishing ground carefully, with the net lines swaying around you. You reach the new seagrass bed with a long thin scrape left on your shell.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay in the remaining seagrass and eat less","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You stay in the seagrass that is left, eating only a little each day. The grass grows slowly, but your body can still hold out.","decisions.8.title":"A fork in the drift","decisions.8.body":"The current splits beneath you into two streams. One is warm and full of algae; the other is colder and runs farther from shore.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Follow the warm water","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You drift with the warm current for a long time, and more and more algae cling to you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Head toward the colder water","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You swim slowly toward the cold water. It grows cooler, and the algae around you becomes sparse.","decisions.9.title":"The bright beach","decisions.9.body":"You have just climbed out of the sand pit. Ahead there are two ways: one toward the darker part of the sea, and one toward the lights of the seaside hotel.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Crawl toward the darkness","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You crawl toward the darker part of the sea. The sand is hot, but the way is right.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Crawl toward the lights","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You crawl toward the lights. Crabs and gulls on the beach are heading the same way.","decisions.10.title":"Tire tracks","decisions.10.body":"A vehicle at night has pressed a deep rut into the sand. The sand inside the rut is soft, and you must crawl through it to reach the sea.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Wait until the vehicle leaves, then crawl slowly","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Safety · Slow","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You wait on the sand while the headlights stay on, until the vehicle drives far enough away.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Crawl straight through the rut","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Fast · Risk↑↑","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You tumble over the rut, your body coated in soft sand. Gulls wheel above your head.","opening.title":"When to climb out of the sand","opening.body":"You have hatched and are huddled at the bottom of the sand pit with your clutchmates. Outside the mouth of the pit the sky is still a gray light, and some companions have already begun to dig upward. If you wait for full dark, fewer birds will be about, but you will be far behind the others.","opening.options.0.t":"Follow your clutchmates and climb as dusk falls","opening.options.0.hint":"More company · Birds can still see","opening.options.0.result":"You climb out of the sand pit with your clutchmates. Tiny footprints quickly mark the sand's surface.","opening.options.1.t":"Wait for full dark and climb alone","opening.options.1.hint":"Birds cannot see you · Fall behind","opening.options.1.result":"You wait until the night is fully dark before moving. The mouth of the pit is quiet, with only the distant sound of waves. By the time you reach the sea, most of your clutch has already moved on.","actions.forage.name":"Eat seagrass and small shrimp","actions.forage.desc":"Bite off blades on the seagrass beds, or find small shrimp and fish in the sargassum.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in weed or reef crevices","actions.hide.desc":"Slip into the sargassum or the gaps in the coral reef, with only half your head showing, so predators cannot find you.","actions.rest.name":"Sleep submerged","actions.rest.desc":"Stop in the water and let your body sink to rest. Each breath is held for a long time.","actions.explore.name":"Swim toward the open sea","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the familiar seagrass bed and swim in an unknown direction.","actions.mate.name":"Climb onto the beach to lay eggs","actions.mate.desc":"Adult females return to familiar beaches, crawl ashore at night, and dig pits to lay their eggs.","actions.mate.done":"You dug a pit in the night, laid {n} eggs, and then crawled back into the sea.","idle.0":"The temperature around you changes slowly, warm by day and cool at night.","idle.1":"A breeze passes, and the sand on the surface or the water ripples gently.","idle.2":"Light comes from far away, its brightness changing with the hours.","idle.3":"You do not know where you are, only that everything around you is slowly changing.","idle.4":"Sometimes creatures pass near you, and you do not know what they are.","idle.5":"Your body grows a little each day, and each day differs a little from the last.","idle.6":"It is quiet around you, with only faint sounds from far away.","idle.7":"Everything here is slow, so slow that you barely feel time passing.","actions.forage.text.0":"You bite off a seagrass blade, and juice runs out as you chew.","actions.forage.text.1":"A small shrimp curls up and backs deeper into the weed, and you do not chase it.","actions.forage.text.2":"Small fish in the seagrass scatter at once, then slowly regroup.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip into a clump of sargassum, and it quickly covers you.","actions.hide.text.1":"The water outside the reef is bright, and you stay still in the crevice.","actions.hide.text.2":"A seabird circles over the water, and you keep your head hidden.","actions.rest.text.0":"You sink in the water, and each breath is held for a long time.","actions.rest.text.1":"When you wake, the weed has drifted a little farther away.","actions.rest.text.2":"The current carries you slowly along, and you do not stir.","actions.explore.text.0":"You cross a stretch of water with no seagrass, and see nothing but blue.","actions.explore.text.1":"A strange reef appears in the distance, its coral in a jumble of colors.","actions.explore.text.2":"After a long swim, you find the next seagrass bed where you can settle.","actions.mate.text.0":"You crawl onto the familiar beach, and the sand still holds the day's warmth.","actions.mate.text.1":"You dig a deep pit, and the sand is pushed aside bit by bit by your hind flippers.","actions.mate.text.2":"The eggs drop into the pit one by one, and you slowly cover them with sand."},"sheep":{"name":"Sheep","tagline":"One of the first animals humans domesticated. Sheep can remember dozens of different faces","survivalNote":"Meat lambs go to market at 6–9 months (the cliff near 240–270 days on the curve). Ewes kept for breeding can live 5–10 years. Risk multipliers stand in for differences in purpose and origin, and are a rough approximation","epitaph":"You lived about eight months, or five years. No one in the flock remembers your name, but they know your face.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Artiodactyla › Bovidae","size":"Adults about 45–100 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Origin","birth.rolls.1.label":"Intended use","birth.rolls.2.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.3.label":"Who looks after you","birth.siblingsLabel":"Lambs born the same year in the same flock","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}. Your wet fleece still clings to you, and you are {sex}. About {siblings} lambs were born in your flock the same year. Your future: {r1}.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a merino station on the dry grasslands of Australia","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a hill farm in Britain, where sheep graze among heather and on the slopes","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a Mongolian flock on the grasslands of Inner Mongolia","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a farm on the green hillsides of New Zealand","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a meat lamb, sent to market at about 8 to 9 months","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"kept as a ewe for breeding and wool","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a spring lamb, born as the grass turns green and the nights are still cold","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a summer lamb, born when the pasture grass is at its thickest","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a winter lamb, born in the pen while the snow outside has not yet melted","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a shepherd who counts the flock every morning and gives special care to any sheep that seems unwell","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a shepherd who visits only every few days, so the flock finds its own grass and water","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a twelve-year-old boy who has to look after several hundred sheep at once","stages.0.name":"Newborn","stages.1.name":"Lamb","stages.2.name":"Young adult (fattening)","stages.3.name":"Ewe","causes.0.short":"Slaughter","causes.0.text":"You are loaded onto a truck and taken to the abattoir. What follows is quick, and you have almost no time to think about anything.","causes.1.short":"Killed by a fox or stray dog","causes.1.text":"At night a fox or a stray dog slips through a gap by the pen. The flock scatters, and you cannot get far.","causes.2.short":"Carried off by a raptor","causes.2.text":"A raptor dives from the hillside and carries off a lamb that had fallen behind. That lamb was you.","causes.3.short":"Frozen or starved","causes.3.text":"In spring, a sleet storm comes, and you and your mother cannot find shelter from the wind.","causes.4.short":"Pneumonia","causes.4.text":"You cough, mucus runs from your nose, and your breathing grows slower and slower.","causes.5.short":"Foot rot","causes.5.text":"The muddy ground of the rainy season begins to rot your hooves. You cannot walk any farther, and you lie down in the mud.","causes.6.short":"Drought, not enough grass","causes.6.text":"The pasture dries up, and the flock eats every edible plant nearby. You grow so thin that only your frame remains.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth have worn down, and you graze more and more slowly. You lie in the grass and do not get up again.","events.0.text":"You are licked dry by your mother as soon as you hit the ground. You raise your head for the first time and hear her calling you.","events.1.text":"You taste colostrum for the first time. It is thick and yellow, and you drink it greedily.","events.2.text":"The shepherd performs a small operation on the skin of your hindquarters (mulesing), to keep it from collecting dung and to prevent fly-strike maggots.","events.3.text":"You follow your mother with the flock, every sheep taking much the same path.","events.3.fact":"Kendrick et al. (2001, Nature): sheep can recognize about 50 different sheep faces and remember them for at least two years","events.4.text":"After a rain, new grass sprouts from the ground. You graze with your heads down, and swallow more than a little mud.","events.5.text":"A fox walks along the hillside. The flock lifts its heads all at once, then stops still.","events.6.text":"The shepherd puts an ear tag in your ear and records your number.","events.7.text":"The sheepfold is crowded at night, and the flock's body heat rises together. Your wool is soaked with sweat, then slowly dries.","events.8.text":"The grass on the pasture grows tall. You have eaten your fill and lie in the sun, chewing your cud.","events.9.text":"You are driven into a pen and shorn of a layer of wool. The shearer works quickly, and fleece falls to the ground in pieces.","events.10.text":"The flock moves along the hillside. One sheep suddenly leaps, and all the others run a few steps after it.","events.11.text":"Dogs bark on the far side of the hill. The flock huddles together.","events.12.text":"You are herded into a pen, and the sheep around you are all calling out. A truck has already parked at the gate.","events.13.text":"A strong wind blows across the grassland, and sand hits your face. The flock stands with its back to the wind.","events.14.text":"You give birth for the first time. The lamb stands as soon as it lands, and you lick its face.","events.15.text":"Year after year, you bear one or two lambs, and your body grows thinner each year.","events.16.text":"One of your teeth loosens. When you graze, you can chew only on the other side.","events.17.text":"The rainy season comes, and your hooves hurt again. Each day you limp to the water.","events.18.text":"The shepherd counts the flock, pauses when he reaches you, and then goes on counting.","events.19.text":"You gnaw at grass roots, and swallow more than a little mud.","events.20.text":"New grass springs up at the edge of the pasture, and the flock crowds together to get at it.","events.21.text":"You squeeze in behind a ewe, and the bodies of the flock block the wind.","events.22.text":"You huddle in the middle of the flock. The sheep on the outside are bleating, and you hear only their voices.","events.23.text":"You lie beside a ewe, your ears twitch, and you soon fall asleep.","events.24.text":"You lie in the grass chewing your cud, and your body slowly warms.","events.25.text":"You leave the flock and walk a few steps up the hillside. In the distance there is the shadow of a fox.","events.26.text":"You follow a stone wall and find a patch of turf no one has trampled, and you eat with relish.","events.27.text":"A ram is driven into the flock and chases you around the stone wall. You dodge a few times, but in the end you cannot escape.","events.28.text":"The spring wind is still cold, and your wet fleece chills quickly as soon as you land. Your mother licks you hard until you give a small bleat.","events.29.text":"The grass has just turned green. The first mouthful you eat is tender and sweet, with a taste of water.","events.30.text":"Spring rain falls for several days, and the pen is full of mud. Your hoof sinks into it.","events.31.text":"The summer pasture stretches wide. You have only just been born, and you already smell green grass mixed with hot wind.","events.32.text":"The grass is tall and thick. You bounce through it, and seeds quickly stick to your coat.","events.33.text":"Flies are thick in summer, landing on your ears and the corners of your eyes. You shake your head again and again.","events.34.text":"The pen still holds the chill of snow. You press in under your mother's belly, and your wet fleece slowly dries and warms.","events.35.text":"The snow has not melted, and the grass is buried beneath it. You can only chew on the dry hay in the pen, which is hard and harsh.","events.36.text":"The shepherd lights a lamp in the shed and fastens ear tags on you newborn lambs.","events.37.text":"The shepherd counts the flock every morning. If someone looks wrong, he picks that one out and feeds it a little extra by itself.","events.38.text":"The shepherd divides the pasture into several plots and moves the flock from one to the next. When you finish one plot, you move to the next, and there is always grass.","events.39.text":"The shepherd treats your hoof with medicine, very gently. You stand in his hands for a long time and do not pull away.","events.40.text":"The shepherd visits only every few days. The flock finds its own grass and water, and you learn to follow the old sheep.","events.41.text":"The trough at the edge of the pasture leaks, and no one has fixed it for days. You and the flock have to go to a muddy pool far away to drink.","events.42.text":"One sheep's leg gets caught in a gap between stones. No one comes, and it cries for a long time before it finally wrenches itself free.","events.43.text":"The shepherd is a twelve-year-old boy. He drives the flock up the hill, and sometimes he runs along with the sheep for a while himself.","events.44.text":"The boy walks behind the flock with a long stick. He gives a whistle, and the flock surges immediately in one direction.","events.45.text":"The boy falls asleep on the grassy slope, and the flock scatters across a wide area. When he wakes, he drives the sheep back one by one.","events.46.text":"You are the first to run from your mother's side, leap over a small ditch, and get out in front of the flock.","events.47.text":"You always stay close to your mother, and any little sound makes you stop. The flock moves slowly, and so do you.","events.48.text":"You reach a gap in the stone wall and stick your head out to look at the pasture on the other side. A strange sheep over there is looking back at you.","events.49.text":"You are smaller than the other lambs from your birth, and your mother licks them first. By the time you stand up, you are a beat behind.","events.50.text":"You have a large frame and a thick fleece. In winter you always stand on the outer edge of the flock, shielding the others from the wind.","decisions.0.title":"A shadow on the hillside","decisions.0.body":"The shadow of a raptor passes over you. The flock is in chaos. You can squeeze into the middle of the flock, or stay on the outside and keep grazing.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle of the flock","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You push into the middle of the flock. The shadow passes overhead and flies away.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay on the outside","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You keep your head down, grazing, and do not look at the sky.","decisions.1.title":"Separated","decisions.1.body":"The flock has moved to another pasture. A fence stands between you and your mother, and she is calling you from the other side. You can squeeze under the fence, or wait where you are.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Crawl under the fence","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Injury risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You squeeze through the fence, leaving a tuft of wool behind, and at last you find your mother.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait where you are","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety↓ Food↓","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stand on this side of the fence for a long time. Your mother does not come back, and you call out several more times.","decisions.2.title":"The pasture is eaten bare","decisions.2.body":"The grass on the pasture has all been eaten. In the river valley far off there is still a patch of green, and the herder is driving the flock toward it. You can follow, or stay where you are.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Follow the flock","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk on the road↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You walk for several days, shivering with cold at night. At the river valley there is less grass than you hoped, but at least you can eat your fill.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Stay where you are","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You nibble at dry hay, and you grow thinner by the day.","decisions.3.title":"Shearing day","decisions.3.body":"The herder is about to shear the flock. You can wait pressed together with the others, or hide in a corner of the pen. Whatever you choose, the shearing will happen.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Wait pressed together","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You wait with the flock, and one by one it is your turn.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Hide in a corner","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety↓ Stress↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You are huddled in the corner when the herder drags you out from behind.","decisions.4.title":"Where to lamb","decisions.4.body":"Lambing season is near. The shed is dry and sheltered, but crowded with ewes. On the hillside there is a spot out of the wind, quiet, but not far from a fox's den.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Lamb in the shed","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You walk into the shed and give birth to your lamb on the hay. The flock calls outside all night.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Lamb on the hillside, out of the wind","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Higher predation risk","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You give birth on the sheltered side of the hill. Through the night a few dogs bark, and you hold your lamb close.","decisions.5.title":"Rain again","decisions.5.body":"The rainy season has returned, and the ground is deep in muddy water. The hay in the shed is dry, but you must soak in the mud for a long time to reach grass.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Stand on the dry hay in the shed","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Health↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You stand on the hay, and your hooves soon dry out.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Graze in the muddy ground","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Hoof disease risk↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You wade through mud all day and manage a few mouthfuls of tender grass. Over the next few days, your hooves begin to ache.","decisions.6.title":"Teeth loosening","decisions.6.body":"Your teeth are getting looser, and when you chew grass, bits of it keep falling from your mouth. At the edge of the pasture there is feed brought by people, but the grass on the pasture is more abundant.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Graze on the pasture","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Energy↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You graze with your head down all day, and more grass falls from your mouth than you manage to swallow.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Wait by the shed for the feed delivery","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↓ Safety↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You wait by the shed, and when the feed arrives you eat slowly.","decisions.7.title":"Heavy snow overnight","decisions.7.body":"Heavy snow fell overnight, and the wind has pushed the shed door open a crack. You can squeeze into the middle of the flock, or go to the dry hay pile by the door.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the middle of the flock","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Warm↑ Safety↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You push into the middle of the flock, and you soon warm up. Outside, the wind gusts in waves.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay on the dry hay pile by the door","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Eat more · Cold wind risk↑","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You eat a lot of hay by the door, but the cold wind blows steadily against your back.","decisions.8.title":"The leaking trough","decisions.8.body":"The trough at the edge of the pasture has leaked for several days, and the shepherd has not come to fix it. There is a muddy pool farther off, but the walk is long.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Walk to the distant pool to drink","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Water↑ Stamina↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You walk a long way. The muddy water smells sour, but you drink a few mouthfuls anyway.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Wait for the shepherd by the trough","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Water↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You wait by the trough for three days before the shepherd finally comes.","decisions.9.title":"A gap in the stone wall","decisions.9.body":"There is a gap in the stone wall, and the pasture on the other side looks different. The flock is still waiting for the shepherd's whistle.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Jump through the gap","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Predator risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You leap through the gap and take a big mouthful of grass outside. In the distance, a dog barks twice.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay with the flock","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You keep pace with the flock, moving slowly, and do not look over the wall.","decisions.10.title":"The shepherd's footsteps","decisions.10.body":"The shepherd is coming down the slope with a stick in his hand, and the flock has all lifted its head. You can hide behind your mother, or walk to the front to look at him.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Hide behind your mother","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.10.options.0.result":"You hide behind your mother, with only one ear showing.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Walk to the front to look","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Safety↓","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You walk to the front. The shepherd pats your head, then goes back to counting the others.","opening.title":"Follow the ewe, or stay in the corner of the shed","opening.body":"You have just landed, and your wet fleece still clings to you. Your mother gets up and is about to lead the flock toward the pasture. You can stand and follow her at once, or wait in the sheltered corner of the shed.","opening.options.0.t":"Follow closely behind your mother","opening.options.0.hint":"Milk↑ Cold risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"You stumble along behind your mother, nearly left behind several times. You kept up each time, and you got your milk.","opening.options.1.t":"Stay in the corner of the shed","opening.options.1.hint":"Shelter · Less milk","opening.options.1.result":"The shed blocks the wind. Your mother walks far off, and you wait in the corner for her to come back. You wait a very long time.","actions.forage.name":"Graze with head down","actions.forage.desc":"Crop your way along the pasture, taking an extra mouthful whenever you find tender grass.","actions.hide.name":"Squeeze into the middle of the flock","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the outer edge and push into the middle of the flock, shielded by the other sheep.","actions.rest.name":"Lie down and chew cud","actions.rest.desc":"Lie on the grass, chewing your cud slowly and letting your body relax.","actions.explore.name":"Leave the flock for distant ground","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the flock and walk far off along the hillside or a stone wall.","actions.mate.name":"Mating","actions.mate.desc":"A ram is driven into the flock and chases ewes among them.","actions.mate.done":"You became pregnant, and later bore {n} lambs.","idle.0":"The wind passes over the pasture, and the grass tips all bend one way.","idle.1":"You bleat once, get no answer, and bleat again.","idle.2":"In the flock, every sheep's face looks a little different.","idle.3":"You lower your head to nibble at grass roots on the ground.","idle.4":"Beneath a tree at the edge of the pasture, the flock is dozing.","idle.5":"A dog barks in the distance, then soon stops.","idle.6":"A sheep lies down beside you, chews its cud, and slowly closes its eyes.","idle.7":"The rain has stopped, and the ground smells of earth.","actions.forage.text.0":"You gnaw at grass roots, and a mouthful of mud gets in.","actions.forage.text.1":"You have eaten your fill, and you chew slowly.","actions.forage.text.2":"The sheep around you are eating too, and the sound of tearing grass rises and falls.","actions.hide.text.0":"You push into the middle of the flock, surrounded by the warmth of other sheep.","actions.hide.text.1":"One sheep's hoof steps on your ear, and you shake your head.","actions.hide.text.2":"The wind outside can no longer reach you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie on the grass, chewing your cud slowly.","actions.rest.text.1":"Your ears twitch, then droop again soon after.","actions.rest.text.2":"The sun warms your back, and your body feels cozy.","actions.explore.text.0":"You leave the flock and walk a few steps up the hillside.","actions.explore.text.1":"You follow a stone wall and find a patch of turf no one has trampled.","actions.explore.text.2":"In the distance a sheep calls out, and you look back."},"sparrow":{"name":"House sparrow","tagline":"Ever since cities were built, it has followed people, nesting under eaves and in roadside shrubs","survivalNote":"Most of what we know about city sparrow lifespans comes from banding and field observation (see Anderson 2006 for a review). Most die in their first year, and the first winter is the most dangerous. This curve is a rough estimate. Recorded lifespans are far longer than this range","epitaph":"You were one of the most common birds in the city. This year, there is one less.","taxon":"Chordata › Aves › Passeriformes › Passeridae","size":"About 16 cm long, about 30 g","birth.rolls.0.label":"Nest site","birth.rolls.1.label":"Food in the nest","birth.rolls.2.label":"The surroundings","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the same clutch","birth.intro":"You are an egg, {r0}. Your mother lays one a day, and this clutch holds {siblings} in all. You are {sex}, though it cannot be told yet.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"in a crack in the brickwork under the eaves of an old apartment block","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"in a hollow of a street tree","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in the gap between an air-conditioner unit and a wall","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"in a seam of sheet metal behind a billboard","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"grain spilled around a food market","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"insects and grass seed from a green strip","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"scraps of food from a restaurant's back door","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"the streets of an old district, with many buildings and many people","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"farmland on the edge of town, with heaps of wheat and rice straw","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"the edge of a large park, where shrubs and grass grow together","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"beside warehouses in an industrial zone, where the air is poor but grain often spills at the doors","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Nestling (in the nest)","stages.2.name":"Fledgling (out of the nest)","stages.3.name":"Adult bird","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Pounced on by a cat","causes.0.text":"A gray cat darts out from behind a holly bush. You have time only to flutter your wings once.","causes.1.short":"Taken by a sparrowhawk","causes.1.text":"A sparrowhawk dives low along the rooftop. You hear the rush of wind, and then you hear nothing at all.","causes.2.short":"Starved in winter","causes.2.text":"A cold snap has lasted four days. The insects in the trees have vanished, and the grain on the ground has frozen into ice. Your fat runs out on the third night.","causes.3.short":"Nest raided by a magpie","causes.3.text":"A magpie pushes its beak into the nest hole. Your parents have just flown off to look for food, and the magpie carries off the whole brood.","causes.4.short":"Parent did not return, egg went cold","causes.4.text":"Your mother flew off to forage and did not come back all afternoon. The temperature of your egg slowly fell, and you never woke.","causes.5.short":"Struck a window","causes.5.text":"You did not see the glass. It reflected a row of trees. When you hit it, everything before your eyes went white.","causes.6.short":"Parasites wore down the body","causes.6.text":"Fleas and mites in the nest multiply. You grow thinner each day, and in the end you can no longer fly.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your feathers grow ragged, and your wings no longer lift high. On a quiet morning, you do not wake again.","events.0.text":"Your mother crouches in the nest, her body rising and falling with heat. Every so often she gets up and turns you over.","events.1.text":"Inside your shell, cells divide bit by bit. The outside world is still far away.","events.2.text":"You break out of the shell with no feathers and your beak wide open.","events.2.fact":"The modal incubation period of house sparrows is about 12 days (Anderson 1994, a Michigan study)","events.3.text":"Your parents fly back with a caterpillar in their beaks, and you open your mouth to swallow it. Nestlings need a great deal of protein, and insects are their main food.","events.3.fact":"House sparrow nestlings are fed mainly on insects, while adults eat mostly grain and grass seed (Brzęk et al. 2009, Journal of Experimental Biology)","events.4.text":"Your parents fly off together, leaving you huddled in a heap. A loud cry comes from a neighboring nest.","events.5.text":"A heavy rainstorm drives into the nest hole, and everyone is soaked. Your mother presses her body over you until the rain stops.","events.6.text":"Black feather sheaths push out through your skin, and you begin to itch all over.","events.7.text":"You leave the nest for the first time, glide a few meters, and land on a drainpipe under the eaves.","events.8.text":"You follow your parents across the grass to look for food. They show you how, and you copy them.","events.9.text":"You roll in the dust, and the oil and bugs in your feathers are shaken loose.","events.10.text":"Before dark, you and your companions squeeze into a holly bush and roost together like a gray ball.","events.10.fact":"In winter, house sparrows often roost communally in dense shrubs and ivy to reduce the risk of predation (BTO species account for the house sparrow)","events.11.text":"A black cat lies on a garden wall, basking in the sun, its tail flicking slowly. All of you freeze.","events.12.text":"A cold snap arrives all at once, and the ground is frozen hard. You shelter under the eaves all day.","events.13.text":"An old woman scatters a handful of millet in the park. You all swoop down together and fight hard for it.","events.14.text":"You bathe in a shallow puddle, shake your feathers into a mess, and then preen each one back into place with your beak.","events.15.text":"Black patches grow on your chest. The size of this \"badge\" affects how your companions treat you.","events.15.fact":"The size of the black chest patch in male house sparrows is linked to their status within the group (Møller 1987)","events.16.text":"Spring arrives, and you find a hole under the eaves and begin to build a nest, carrying grass and string. This is your first clutch.","events.16.fact":"House sparrows in Michigan usually raise two to three broods a year (Anderson 1994)","events.17.text":"You lay another clutch, {n} eggs in all.","events.18.text":"A male perches beside you on the windowsill and sunbathes, his wing lightly brushing yours.","events.19.text":"A sparrowhawk glides across the city sky, and the sparrows in the whole stand of trees fall silent at once. You duck deep into the shrubs.","events.20.text":"A heavy rain falls all night, and the food beside the trash bins gets soaked.","events.21.text":"Someone has just tossed a loaf of bread beside the trash bins. You and your companions rush at it and peck off a piece of the hard crust.","events.22.text":"When you take off, your wings begin to tremble, and you fly lower than before.","events.23.text":"Your young are calling from another tree. You hear them and do not fly over.","events.24.text":"You peck at a scatter of vegetable leaves by the food market while the vendor next to you is packing up.","events.25.text":"A caterpillar drops from a blade of grass, and you snatch it before your companions can.","events.26.text":"Beside a knocked-over trash bag, you peck out a piece of moldy bread, its crust furred with green.","events.27.text":"Footsteps pass by in waves outside. You stay deep in the holly bush until dusk, your stomach rumbling.","events.28.text":"A thunderstorm rolls through and lasts all afternoon. You huddle in the shrubs, your feathers soaked through.","events.29.text":"You tuck your head into your wings and fall asleep in a warm spot under the eaves.","events.30.text":"You fly over a river. On the far bank, an old tree is loaded with red berries, and several birds are already pecking beneath it.","events.31.text":"You fly into a wheat field on the outskirts of town, where the grass seed is plentiful and clean.","events.32.text":"You fly into an unfamiliar yard, and a dog bursts out of the door. You flap hard and barely manage to reach the top of the wall.","events.33.text":"In a crack under the eaves you find a discarded length of old electrical cable sheath, and you carry it back to your nest.","events.34.text":"At the mouth of the nest you choose for a long time, and finally pick the softest blade of grass.","events.35.text":"Cars pass along the street one after another. At the curb you peck up a small crumb of cracker.","events.36.text":"The streetlights are bright at night. You sleep under the eaves in their glow, listening to the distant traffic.","events.37.text":"Grain is scattered across the wheat field after the harvest. You peck along the field's edge with a flock of sparrows.","events.38.text":"A tractor suddenly drives out of the field, and the whole flock of sparrows bursts into the air at once. You are not caught up in it.","events.39.text":"Insects hide in the shrubs. You dig through them for a while, and your feathers pick up plenty of grass seeds.","events.40.text":"People out exercising in the morning walk past. You do not fly. Their footsteps pass right by you.","events.41.text":"The warehouse door opens, and a puff of dust drifts out. Your throat hurts a little, but there are a few grains of millet scattered at the door.","events.42.text":"Smoke rises from the factory chimney, and a film of grey soot settles on your feathers.","events.43.text":"You are a little smaller than your siblings in the nest. When your parents bring food, you are always pushed to the very back.","events.44.text":"You are sturdier than the other nestlings. The first time you flap your wings, you fly from the nest hole up to the eaves.","events.45.text":"You are the first to hop down to the roadside to snatch a biscuit a child has dropped.","events.46.text":"You see a cat crouching at the base of a wall, so you take a long detour before landing on the ground.","events.47.text":"You hop around a cart and find a grain of rice that has fallen into a gap in it.","decisions.0.title":"The moment of leaving the nest","decisions.0.body":"The nest is too crowded now. Your parents call from outside the nest hole, their voices growing more urgent. Your wings are long enough, but you have never really tried them.","decisions.0.fact":"House sparrow nestlings usually leave the nest after a little over two weeks, and their parents continue to feed them for a while afterward (Audubon, house sparrow account)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Jump out now","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Early flight · Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You jump out and land in the grass. You flap for a long while before you finally get into the air.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Wait two more days","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You wait two more days. Your parents bring fewer insects now, but the nest is still warm.","decisions.1.title":"Autumn arrives","decisions.1.body":"The leaves are turning yellow, and insects are growing scarce. In the city there are two places to go: the back door of a restaurant is busy and has food scraps, but there are many cats; the reed beds by the river are quiet, but food is scarce.","decisions.1.fact":"Urban house sparrows depend more on food that people discard or provide, while rural birds rely more on grass seed and insects (Audubon, house sparrow account)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go to the restaurant's back door","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk of being spotted by cats↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You can eat scraps every day, but every day you have to watch the wires overhead.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Go to the reed beds by the river","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You follow a flock of sparrows down into the reeds. At night they huddle together, making tiny sounds.","decisions.2.title":"A cat on the windowsill","decisions.2.body":"A cat crouches on a second-floor windowsill, perfectly still. Beneath the sill lies a small heap of grain.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Hide in the holly bush","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You stay in the holly bush all afternoon. The cat leaves, and the grain is scattered by the wind.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Keep foraging as usual","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You peck at a few grains with your head down, then hear the faintest scratch of claws.","decisions.3.title":"Two days of snow","decisions.3.body":"All the food on the ground is buried in snow. The exhaust vent under the eaves keeps steaming, and several sparrows huddle around it. A granary far off may still have grain, but it is a long flight away.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Squeeze into the warm air from the vent","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You crowd in among your companions as warm air gusts over you. At last your feathers stop shivering.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Fly to the granary far off","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You fly over three streets and find a scattering of broken grain through a gap in the granary door. On the way back, your wings stiffen with cold.","decisions.4.title":"Choosing a nest site","decisions.4.body":"A male is calling beside a transformer box on a utility pole. It is warm there, and the gaps are small, but it is close to cats. You still want to go back to last year's eave hole.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Follow him to the transformer box","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety · Fewer offspring","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You build a nest in a gap in the metal box. Each time you fly in, you look around first.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Go back to last year's eave hole","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You return to that hole and find it a little smaller than last year, but still usable.","decisions.5.title":"First landing on the road","decisions.5.body":"You flew too low and dropped into the middle of the road. A bicycle swings out of the lane, its bell ringing sharply. Your parents call from the rooftop, one cry after another.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Flap back to the roadside","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Fast · Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You flap hard and dart in front of the wheels into the grass at the roadside. Your heart is still pounding.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Crouch where you are and wait for your parents","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Slow · Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You crouch in the middle of the road until your parents land beside you and pick you up in their beaks.","decisions.6.title":"The old hole has narrowed","decisions.6.body":"Your wings are growing slower, and the hole under the eaves where you lived last year seems narrower. A young pair of sparrows has just moved in next door and is eyeing that very hole.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Fight the young sparrows for the hole","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Good nest site · Injury risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You peck at each other at the entrance for a long time, and in the end you slip inside. It is warm in there, but it smells strange.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Spend the night in an old wooden crate by the wall","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety · Low energy","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You spend the night in an abandoned wooden crate. It lets the wind through, but no one comes to fight you for it.","decisions.7.title":"The old woman's millet","decisions.7.body":"In the park, an old woman scatters a handful of millet every day. A large flock of you crowds around her feet, often trampled. Your legs are not as strong as they were, and you cannot beat the young sparrows to the grain.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Push to the front and fight for it","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Injury risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You shoulder to the front and peck several grains. A young sparrow slams into your wing, and you almost tumble over.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Back off to a branch and wait","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You wait on a branch. The people leave, and only a few crushed grains of millet are left on the ground.","decisions.8.title":"A night in the straw stack","decisions.8.body":"The harvest season is over, and the straw stacks at the edge of the fields are warm. A few sparrows are roosting under the eaves at the edge of the village.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Burrow into the straw stack for the night","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Warm · Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"The straw is warm. A cat's footsteps pass in the night, and you stay awake the whole time.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Go back under the eaves, close to where people live","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You spend the night under the eaves, and by morning your body is stiff with cold.","opening.title":"The parents' division of labor","opening.body":"You are still an egg, and the nest is already built. The egg needs an adult sitting on it to keep it warm, but the longer one parent sits, the less anyone is out finding insects and grass seed.","opening.options.0.t":"Mother sits on the nest and goes out to feed less","opening.options.0.hint":"Egg stays warmer · Hungry sooner","opening.options.0.result":"Your mother sits on the nest for most of the day, leaving only for a short while in the evening. You are warm beneath her, and it is very quiet.","opening.options.1.t":"Mother goes out often to find food","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ · Egg may grow cold","opening.options.1.result":"Your mother flies off every so often, and the nest sometimes grows cold. When you wake, the nest is often empty.","actions.forage.name":"Peck for food","actions.forage.desc":"Search the ground along roadsides, lawns and food markets with your head down, eating grain, grass seed and insects.","actions.hide.name":"Slip into a shrub","actions.hide.desc":"Hide deep in holly or Virginia creeper, silent and still. Little food is to be found there, but you are hard to spot.","actions.rest.name":"Doze hunched up","actions.rest.desc":"Tuck your head into your wings and fluff your feathers for warmth while your energy slowly returns.","actions.explore.name":"Fly far away","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your familiar neighborhood for a more distant park, riverbank or the countryside. You see much more, and there is more danger too.","actions.mate.name":"Build a nest and lay eggs","actions.mate.desc":"Find a hole under the eaves and line the nest with grass and string. Only the female lays eggs, and she may raise several clutches in a year.","actions.mate.done":"You laid another clutch, {n} eggs in all.","idle.0":"The light fades, and the sounds around you slowly quiet down.","idle.1":"A burst of birdsong comes from somewhere, then stops again soon.","idle.2":"A bicycle rolls slowly out of the lane, its bell ringing sharply.","idle.3":"The old house next door is being demolished, and wall dust drifts over.","idle.4":"A pigeon coos nearby, its voice sometimes close, sometimes far.","idle.5":"The streetlights come on at night, and everything around you slowly falls quiet.","idle.6":"After the rain the air is cool, and the ground smells damp.","idle.7":"Sunlight comes through a gap and leaves a long bright streak on the wall.","actions.forage.text.0":"You bend your head and pick a grain from the ground.","actions.forage.text.1":"A bug wriggles out from the grass roots, and you snap it up.","actions.forage.text.2":"You and your companions crowd onto a patch of ground scattered with vegetable leaves, pecking against each other.","actions.hide.text.0":"You are tucked into the deepest part of the holly bush. Footsteps pass by outside in waves.","actions.hide.text.1":"You and your companions huddle together, and none of you makes a sound.","actions.hide.text.2":"Rain drips down the leaves, and you shake out your feathers.","actions.rest.text.0":"You tuck your head into your wings and doze in a crack in the wall.","actions.rest.text.1":"The sun warms your back, and your feathers puff up and settle.","actions.rest.text.2":"You sleep lightly, and at the slightest noise you open one eye.","actions.explore.text.0":"You fly over two streets and land on a lawn you have never seen before.","actions.explore.text.1":"In the reeds by the river, a flock of sparrows chatters without stopping.","actions.explore.text.2":"You dart back and forth between the wires, and each one sways in turn.","actions.mate.text.0":"You carry a dry blade of grass in your beak and fly into a hole under the eaves.","actions.mate.text.1":"The male hops around beside you, spreading his wings to show you.","actions.mate.text.2":"You spread fine grass in the hole, crouch down, and wait for the first egg."},"spider":{"name":"Orb-weaver spider","tagline":"In the grass along field edges, a large round web often hangs, with a plump female sitting at its center","survivalNote":"Field data on garden orb weavers are scarce. Mortality in the egg sac over winter, dispersal mortality in young spiders and predation pressure on adults are all high. This curve is a rough estimate","epitaph":"You were a web in the grass, and a spider too. Every thread you spun slowly disappeared into the wind.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"The year's conditions","birth.rolls.2.label":"The mother's condition","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the same egg sac","birth.intro":"You spent the winter in an egg sac {r0}. You are {sex}. There are {siblings} eggs in the same sac.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"under the tip of a clump of reed grass","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"among the branches of a pear tree in an orchard","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"on a length of bamboo pole at the edge of a field","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a wet, rainy year, with many mosquitoes in summer","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a dry year, with the grass turning yellow early","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a mild winter, with the egg sac barely touched by frost","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a well-fed mother, with a large, plump egg sac","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a mother who went hungry for a long time, with a small, shrunken egg sac","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a mother who spun the egg sac just before she died, after parasitic wasp larvae had fed inside her body","stages.0.name":"Egg sac","stages.1.name":"Spiderling","stages.2.name":"Adult spider","stages.3.name":"Old spider","causes.0.short":"Paralyzed by a hunting wasp and dragged away","causes.0.text":"A black hunting wasp rushed up from beneath the web. Its venom stung your ganglia, and your legs slowly lost their strength. It dragged you into a burrow to feed its larvae.","causes.1.short":"Picked off by a bird","causes.1.text":"A white-cheeked bulbul perched at the edge of the web. Its bill snapped the threads beside you. Before you could let go, you were carried away.","causes.2.short":"Eaten by a mate after mating","causes.2.text":"Mating had just ended when the female's chelicerae closed on you. You tried to pull away, but your legs were still tangled in her silk.","causes.3.short":"Starved","causes.3.text":"For several weeks no flying insects had landed in the web. Your abdomen shrank a little more each day.","causes.4.short":"Egg sac froze in a cold snap","causes.4.text":"The egg sac hung outside, and a cold snap in winter froze the liquid inside it. You did not live to see spring.","causes.5.short":"Infected by white muscardine fungus","causes.5.text":"White fungal threads grew from your joints. Within a few days they had pushed into your organs.","causes.6.short":"Old age","causes.6.text":"Your webs grew smaller and sparser each night. On the last autumn, you settled in the middle of your web and did not move again.","events.0.text":"Inside the egg sac, the eggs divide one by one. Outside, winter is cold, but the silk of the sac is warm.","events.1.text":"The egg sac hangs from a branch, and the wind rocks it gently, time after time.","events.2.text":"In spring you climb out of the egg sac. Your abdomen is no bigger than a grain of rice, and a fine layer of silk clings to you.","events.3.text":"You climb to the tip of a grass blade and lift your abdomen to release a thread of silk. The wind catches it, and you lift off the ground.","events.4.text":"A gust carries you into an unfamiliar patch of grass. You hang from the thread and spin around several times in the air.","events.5.text":"You crawl between the grass stems, using the silk from your abdomen to climb a little higher each time.","events.6.text":"A tiny aphid lands in your web. You wrap it up and eat it.","events.7.text":"You wait at the center of the web. A mosquito crashes in, the web shudders hard, and you rush out to it at once.","events.8.text":"A small flying insect sticks to your silk. You hold it in place with your chelicerae and inject digestive fluid.","events.9.text":"You hide on the underside of a leaf and press your body flat.","events.10.text":"You slip into a curled leaf. The wind outside grows quieter.","events.11.text":"You stay still at the center of the web, letting the threads sway gently in the breeze.","events.12.text":"You perch at the edge of the web and do not move all day.","events.13.text":"You approach the other spider carefully, tapping out a rhythmic vibration on the web with your front legs.","events.14.text":"A white-cheeked bulbul flies over from the tree, and its shadow flickers across your web.","events.15.text":"A hunting wasp buzzes through the grass. It circles your web several times without landing.","events.16.text":"It starts to rain. Raindrops weigh the silk down until it looks white. You roll up part of the web and wait for the rain to stop.","events.17.text":"The weather turns cool. Dew settles on the web, and the threads grow stickier.","events.18.text":"You begin to make an egg sac, a yellow mass of silk, layer by layer wrapping your eggs inside. There are {n} eggs in it.","events.19.text":"The spiral threads of this web are sticky, while the straight radial threads are not. You can walk across the non-sticky threads without getting caught in your own web.","events.19.fact":"The spiral capture threads of orb weavers are sticky, while the radial frame threads are not (Foelix 2011, Biology of Spiders)","events.20.text":"Your web keeps shrinking. Each day you catch fewer prey.","events.21.text":"At night you spin no new web. You sit for a long time at the edge of the old one.","events.22.text":"A female eats a male near you. Among orb weavers this is not unusual.","events.22.fact":"In many spider species the female eats the male during or after mating, a behavior known as sexual cannibalism (Elgar & Crespi 1992, Cannibalism: Ecology and Evolution among Diverse Taxa)","events.23.text":"Rain soaks the grass until it goes soft. There are more ants and tiny spiders on the ground, and small insects often crawl along the edge of your web.","events.24.text":"It rains without stopping through the summer, and more than a dozen mosquitoes fly into your web in a single day. Your abdomen swells quickly.","events.25.text":"The spring is damp, and aphids and tiny flying insects are everywhere on the grass stems. You crawl out of the egg sac and find food right away at the tip of a grass blade.","events.26.text":"No proper rain falls all summer. The grass leaves turn yellow and curl up, and few flying insects pass your web.","events.27.text":"The air is dry. You stay still in the center of your web, and the water in your body slowly dwindles.","events.28.text":"A long-awaited rain falls. You climb out from under a leaf and rebuild a small web between the grass stems.","events.29.text":"The winter is mild, and the eggs in the egg sac are not frozen. They divide one by one, a little faster than in other years.","events.30.text":"Spring comes early. When you crawl out of the egg sac, the grass is already full of small insects.","events.31.text":"Autumn comes late, and your web hangs on until the weather turns cold before you take it down.","events.32.text":"The egg sac is thick and plump. The eggs are crowded together inside, and each one has plenty of yolk.","events.33.text":"You climb out of the egg sac a little heavier than the other spiderlings from your batch. You climb among the grass stems, and the wind has a hard time blowing you off.","events.34.text":"Your web is denser than the others'. When the first flying insect strikes it, the threads do not break.","events.35.text":"The egg sac is small and shrunken. The eggs inside divide slowly, and you start to develop a few days after the others in your batch.","events.36.text":"You are smaller than the other young spiders, and you climb among the grass stems for a long time before you find a place to anchor your silk.","events.37.text":"Your first web is sparse, and in a day you catch only one small flying insect.","events.38.text":"Your mother's web still hangs on the grass stem, but it has dried out. The silk of the egg sac has a faint, hard-to-describe smell.","events.39.text":"On the day you climb out of the egg sac, a parasitic wasp larva wriggles under the grass stem. It does not touch you, but it lingers nearby for a long time.","events.40.text":"You hide in a curled leaf and press your body flat. The parasitic wasp flies away.","events.41.text":"You are the first to release a thread and drift out of the grass. The other young spiders are still hesitating at the tips of the grass, while you have already floated to the far side of the field ridge.","events.42.text":"You hide on the underside of a leaf for a whole day, and only when you are sure no bird's shadow is above do you crawl out.","events.43.text":"You climb down the grass stem to the outside of the web and feel your way across an unfamiliar leaf. A string of dewdrops hangs at the leaf's edge, and you stay there for a long time.","events.44.text":"You are a size smaller than other spiders. They can spin wide webs, but you can only hang yours between a small patch of grass stems.","events.45.text":"Your legs are longer than those of spiders your age, so you can stretch your web wide. A mosquito flies in, and you pin it down with just one leg.","decisions.0.title":"A single thread","decisions.0.body":"You climb to the tip of a grass blade, and the thread behind your abdomen has pulled out into a single line. A wind blows from the east, and the grass bends away before it.","decisions.0.fact":"Many young spiders disperse by releasing silk and drifting through the air, a behavior known as ballooning (Bell et al. 2005)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Release silk and drift away on the wind","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Dispersal↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You drift several dozen meters and land on a patch of wild grass. The stalks are tall, and there is no place for your web yet.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay put and wait for a better wind","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You reel in the thread and climb back among the grass stems. The wind keeps blowing, but you stay.","decisions.1.title":"A hunting wasp nearby","decisions.1.body":"A hunting wasp is darting about in the grass nearby. Its wings buzz.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Climb under a leaf to hide","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You stay on the underside of a leaf for a long time. The hunting wasp circles your web a few times, then flies away.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Keep waiting for prey on the web","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stay on the web. A flying insect crashes in, but the web shakes violently because of it.","decisions.2.title":"A new web site","decisions.2.body":"You need a new spot for a web. Between two blades of grass there is a good gap, but it sits close to a path, where people pass by often.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Spin the web by the path","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You spin a web beside the path. Flying insects pass in front of you every day, and footsteps brush past underneath.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Spin the web deep in the grass","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You spin a small web deep in the grass. Few insects come by, but it is quiet.","decisions.3.title":"A female's web","decisions.3.body":"You climb to the edge of a large web. At its center a female is waiting. Her chelicerae are much larger than yours.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Tap gently on the web and approach to mate","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Lethal risk↑↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You mate. After mating, her chelicerae bite down on you before you can get away.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Leave and look for another web","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You climb down from the web and head for another plant.","decisions.4.title":"A male approaches","decisions.4.body":"A male taps at the edge of your web several times. He is much smaller than you, and his feet leave a trail of tiny vibrations across the silk.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Accept him and let him mate","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"He deposits sperm on you. A few days later, you begin to spin an egg sac.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Drive him away","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You wrap his legs in silk and let him go. He crawls away.","decisions.5.title":"The last web","decisions.5.body":"The weather grows colder, and the silk of the web starts to stiffen. You can wait on the web for one more meal, or hide under a leaf until winter has passed.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Spin one last web and wait for prey","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You spin your last web. Overnight, frost settles on the silk, but prey still comes.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Hide under a leaf until winter passes","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You hide under a leaf and curl your body up tight. Outside, the wind blows across the leaf and passes on.","decisions.6.title":"The yellowing grass","decisions.6.body":"The grass has turned yellow, and few flying insects have passed your web for a long time. You could take the web down and go to a low hollow in the grass where dew collects, or stay where you are and save your strength.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Go to a low hollow to look for dew","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"There is dew in the hollow, and the grass is greener than elsewhere. You spin a new web on a short blade.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay at your current web","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Saves Stamina · Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You sit perfectly still, and now and then a small insect blunders into the web.","decisions.7.title":"A parasitic wasp under the grass","decisions.7.body":"A parasitic wasp larva is climbing up the grass stem. It moves slowly, pausing at every step.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Climb to a higher leaf tip","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You climb to the leaf tip. The larva stays down at the base of the stem and does not climb any higher.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Press against the underside of a leaf and hide","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You flatten yourself on the underside of the leaf and stay there until its shadow leaves the leaf.","decisions.8.title":"The hunting wasp's shadow","decisions.8.body":"A hunting wasp flies over your web, and its shadow flickers across the threads. It does not look at you, but its flight path is aimed directly at you.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Dive down and grab its wings","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Lethal risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You catch hold of its wings. Its venom stings your leg, and you cannot let go.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Stay still and let it fly away","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.8.options.1.result":"It circles once and flies into the depths of the grass.","decisions.9.title":"No large web yet","decisions.9.body":"The other spiders have already started spinning large webs, but yours is still only a small circle. The weather is turning cool, and flying insects are growing scarce.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Spin a large web now","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You spin a very large web. At night, dew falls and bends a few threads down.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Keep this small web and save your silk","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Steady · Little food","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You do not enlarge the web. Instead you mend the old one and keep waiting.","opening.title":"The mother spins an egg sac in autumn","opening.body":"You have not been born yet. The mother spins an egg sac on a grass stem or a tree branch, and the eggs inside must overwinter until spring. The mother decides where the sac hangs.","opening.options.0.t":"Hang it under a branch near a clump of grass","opening.options.0.hint":"Shelter↑ Less food","opening.options.0.result":"The sac hangs beneath a branch beside a clump of grass. The winter wind is blocked somewhat.","opening.options.1.t":"Hang it from an exposed leaf tip","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"The sac hangs from a leaf tip. When the wind blows, it sways back and forth at the end of the branch.","actions.forage.name":"Wait at the web for prey","actions.forage.desc":"Stay at the center of the web and wait for flying insects to crash into it.","actions.hide.name":"Hide on the underside of a leaf","actions.hide.desc":"Leave the web and hide beneath a leaf, pressing yourself flat.","actions.rest.name":"Stay still","actions.rest.desc":"Remain on the web without moving, letting the threads sway gently in the wind.","actions.explore.name":"Release silk and drift away","actions.explore.desc":"Lift your abdomen, release a thread of silk, and let the wind carry you out of the grass.","actions.mate.name":"Court","actions.mate.done":"You mated. The sperm was stored in the female's storage organ, and she will use it to fertilize her eggs later.","idle.0":"You sit at the center of the web, feeling each vibration in the threads, one by one.","idle.1":"The weather is fine. Sunlight falls on the web between the blades of grass and glints.","idle.2":"A fallen leaf has stuck to the web. You pull it apart and spin a small patch again.","idle.3":"Someone walks along the field path in the distance, and the web sways slightly.","idle.4":"You have eight eyes, but most of them can only tell light from dark. Vibrations in the web are what you care about most.","idle.5":"The temperature drops at night, and dew settles on the threads.","idle.6":"Your abdomen swells and shrinks, holding silk glands and digestive fluid.","idle.7":"Wind passes through the grass, and the whole web sways with it.","actions.forage.text.0":"You wait at the center of the web.","actions.forage.text.1":"The threads of the web are stretched taut, one by one.","actions.hide.text.0":"You slip to the underside of a leaf.","actions.hide.text.1":"The wind blows over the leaf above you.","actions.rest.text.0":"You stay motionless on the web.","actions.explore.text.0":"You lift your abdomen and release a thin thread of silk.","actions.explore.text.1":"The wind carries you away from the grass."},"springtail":{"name":"Springtail","tagline":"In the leaf litter of the forest floor, springtails are often among the most numerous six-legged animals","survivalNote":"Folsomia candida, a standard lab springtail, can live over a hundred days under standard laboratory conditions. Survival in the wild is much lower. This curve is a rough estimate based on soil predation and drought stress","epitaph":"You molted many times in your life. After the last molt, your body slowly merged into the soil.","taxon":"Arthropoda › Hexapoda › Collembola","size":"About 1–3 mm long","note":"Springtails have six legs, but they are not generally classed as insects today. A forked jumping organ (the furcula) is folded beneath the abdomen, and they leap with it.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Season of birth","birth.rolls.2.label":"Condition of the leaf litter","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs laid in the same clutch","birth.intro":"You are in {r0}, a milky-white egg that has not yet hatched. You are {sex}. About {siblings} eggs came from the same clutch as you.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"humus beneath the leaf litter of a woodland","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"beneath a patch of damp moss","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in the topsoil of a farm field","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"in the dry topsoil of sand","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"spring, when the soil is still damp from the snowmelt","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"after a heavy rain at the end of summer","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"autumn, when the most leaves are falling","birth.rolls.1.options.3.0":"a winter afternoon, when the ground has not frozen through","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a thick layer of decaying leaves","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a patch of grass roots just washed by rain","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"dark soil turned over by earthworms","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Juvenile","stages.2.name":"Subadult","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Egg eaten by a predatory mite","causes.0.text":"A predatory mite slipped into the crack of your eggshell. You had not yet hatched, and that was the end.","causes.1.short":"Pierced by a predatory mite","causes.1.text":"A predatory mite seized one of your legs. Its chelicerae pierced your body wall, and your fluids were drawn out bit by bit.","causes.2.short":"Carried off by an ant","causes.2.text":"An ant clamped you in its mandibles and dragged you back to its nest. Your body never came out again.","causes.3.short":"Chased down and eaten by a ground beetle","causes.3.text":"A ground beetle charged down from the soil surface and bit you in two. Your furcula had no time to unfold.","causes.4.short":"Caught by a spider or centipede","causes.4.text":"The legs of a centipede pressed down from above the leaf litter. You had not yet jumped when its jaws seized you.","causes.5.short":"Infected by an entomopathogenic fungus","causes.5.text":"Fungal spores stuck to your body surface. They germinated during several damp days, and threads slowly grew out from inside you.","causes.6.short":"Desiccated as the soil dried out","causes.6.text":"No rain fell for several days, and the topsoil dried out. Water kept seeping from your body surface, and you could not crawl into the damp pores.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"You molted more and more slowly. After your last molt, you stopped moving. The soil slowly broke you down.","events.0.text":"The embryo inside the egg grows. The egg takes up water from the soil and swells a little more each day.","events.1.text":"You have just emerged from the egg. Your body is still white, and the furcula at the tip of your abdomen is folded under you, not yet unfolded.","events.2.text":"You eat a thin piece of fungal thread, scraping it off bit by bit with your mouthparts.","events.3.text":"You molt once. The old skin stays behind you like an empty coat.","events.3.fact":"Springtails molt throughout their lives, including after they become adults (Hopkin 1997, Biology of the Springtails)","events.4.text":"Your furcula has not fully grown, and when you jump you launch only a short distance. You land on the ground and curl into a ball.","events.5.text":"You lose your way among dry grains of sand. Water is drawn out of your skin bit by bit, and you stop only when you reach a damp stone.","events.6.text":"An ant passes along the edge of the soil crack, its antenna brushing across your back. It does not stop.","events.7.text":"You find a thin film of mold in the humus and eat slowly, with great satisfaction.","events.8.text":"Beneath a damp dead leaf, bacteria and fungi grow thick, and the smell is strong. You stay there for several days.","events.9.text":"You curl up in a crack in the soil, your furcula folded beneath you, hardly moving. Tremors reach you from outside, one after another.","events.10.text":"You begin laying eggs. You place them among damp soil particles, a few to several dozen at a time.","events.10.fact":"Folsomia candida is a standard test springtail used in soil ecology and toxicology (ISO 11267; OECD 232)","events.11.text":"You lay another clutch, {n} eggs in all.","events.12.text":"The surface is drying. You slip under a fallen leaf into the moist air and stay there until evening.","events.13.text":"Several days of clear sky leave the topsoil nearly dry. Your body loses water, and your movements slow.","events.14.text":"A predatory mite circles you through the soil crack. It does not attack.","events.15.text":"A heavy rain floods the soil cracks. You and a few companions huddle on a small wet clod, waiting for the water to drain.","events.16.text":"An irregular split opens across your abdomen. You slowly pull your body out of the old skin, and this molt takes much longer than usual.","events.17.text":"A small group of companions gathers around the same patch of mold. No one fights over it. You all simply eat together.","events.18.text":"Your movements slow down. After each molt it takes you longer to recover.","events.19.text":"Your body color has dulled, several wrinkles have appeared on you, and your furcula is slower to retract.","events.20.text":"You scrape back and forth over a damp patch of mold, and your mouthparts fill with fine threads.","events.21.text":"You chew at the underside of a rotting leaf. Bacteria have broken down most of the sugars in the leaf, so you eat what is left.","events.22.text":"You squeeze into a gap between two soil grains, your furcula folded beneath you. Tremors pass through in waves, and you do nothing at all.","events.23.text":"You slip under a fallen leaf, where the humidity is high, and you stay there for several days.","events.24.text":"You fold your furcula against your belly and lie on a layer of damp soil while the water slowly returns to your body wall.","events.25.text":"You curl up between soil grains, moving so slowly you can barely be seen.","events.26.text":"You leave your familiar soil crack, crawl across a patch of coarse sand, and find another damp clod.","events.27.text":"You climb up a dry grass stalk. The view opens out, and in the distance you see an ant on patrol.","events.28.text":"You bury a clutch of {n} eggs among damp soil particles.","events.29.text":"You choose a clod with rainwater on it and lay your eggs in the water film.","events.30.text":"Snowmelt water seeps into the soil, and you grow quickly among the damp soil particles.","events.31.text":"Spring rain falls shower after shower. You find clean fungal threads in the gaps of the soil and fill up earlier than others of your age.","events.32.text":"A downpour scatters the leaves on the surface. You are washed out of a heap of soaked fragments and into another corner.","events.33.text":"After the rain, the soil is full of fungal spores. You eat quickly, and your body soon grows heavy.","events.34.text":"Fallen leaves press down on you one layer after another. You burrow beneath them, eating as you go, until you can barely straighten up.","events.35.text":"The autumn leaf litter is thick, and you find a hollow inside it where you can spend the winter.","events.36.text":"The soil has frozen, and you can barely move. The fat in your body is burned away bit by bit.","events.37.text":"The ice on the soil surface does not melt. You survive until spring, but you are a size smaller than others your age.","events.38.text":"You crawl through the leaf litter, and every leaf is breaking down. Bacteria and fungi are everywhere around you.","events.39.text":"You find a moldy leaf. There is more mold there than usual, and you eat for a long time.","events.40.text":"Rain washes the grass roots bare. You crawl along the gaps between the roots, and your body is covered in mud.","events.41.text":"The sap in the grass roots has dried up, and you can only crowd into a small patch of damp soil.","events.42.text":"An earthworm wriggles past you, overturning the soil and exposing the fungal threads inside.","events.43.text":"The earthworm's castings are soft lumps of dark soil. You crawl over them and feel safe.","events.44.text":"You climb out of the wet soil and walk along a dead twig onto an unfamiliar leaf. There is no smell here that you know.","events.45.text":"The ground trembles. You curl up into a ball and stay that way until the tremor stops.","events.46.text":"A shimmering patch of fungal threads holds you in place for a long time. It is not dangerous, but you do not eat it.","events.47.text":"You are smaller than the other springtails from your batch, and you are often pushed to the outer edge of the soil gaps. Each of your jumps covers only a short distance.","decisions.0.title":"Tremor overhead","decisions.0.body":"A ground beetle rushes over the leaf litter, and the soil grains shake. You can spring away at once, or stay hidden where you are, motionless.","decisions.0.fact":"The furcula of springtails is a pair of ventral appendages on the abdomen. When released, it can throw the body many times its own length (Hopkin 1997)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Spring away with the furcula","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ May land in danger","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You launch yourself and land on an unfamiliar patch of soil. The beetle's footsteps recede behind you.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Play dead and stay still","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Hiding · Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You fold your furcula away, pressed among the soil grains, without moving. Only after the tremor passes do you slowly crawl out.","decisions.1.title":"Dry surface or damp depths","decisions.1.body":"There is plenty of food on the surface, but the soil there is starting to turn pale. Going deeper leads to damp pores, but there is far less to eat there.","decisions.1.fact":"Springtails lose water easily through their permeable body wall, so most species depend on moist microhabitats (Hopkin 1997)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Burrow into the damp depths","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You crawl down along the water films between the soil grains. It is cool there, and your body slowly swells again.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay on the surface and forage","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Desiccation risk↑","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You graze on the surface for a long time. As evening wind blows, your skin tightens, and you can only keep eating.","decisions.2.title":"A capsule on the ground","decisions.2.body":"A tiny transparent capsule lies on the soil surface, left behind by a male moments ago. It holds sperm. You can pick it up into your body, or go around it.","decisions.2.fact":"Male springtails do not mate directly. Instead, they deposit a spermatophore (sperm capsule) on the soil surface, and the female picks it up herself (Hopkin 1997)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Pick it up","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Slight risk↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You pick it up. Over the next few days, your eggs take shape one by one inside your body.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Go around it","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"No risk","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You slip past the capsule. It stays on the soil surface and slowly dries out.","decisions.3.title":"A white patch of mold","decisions.3.body":"A thick white mold patch has grown on the underside of a leaf, and your mouthparts can sense its strong taste. You cannot tell whether harmful fungi lie within the mold.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Eat it","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Fungal infection risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You eat a great deal, and your belly fills. Over the next few days your body color darkens somewhat.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Go around it","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You crawl to the other side of the leaf, where there are fewer bacteria. You have at least taken no risk.","decisions.4.title":"Lay eggs or conserve strength","decisions.4.body":"Your body could still lay more eggs, but food is no longer abundant. Every clutch costs you a great deal.","decisions.4.fact":"In the OECD 232 standard soil reproduction test, the number of springtail offspring is used to assess the effects of soil pollutants (OECD 232, 2016)","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Keep laying many eggs","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Lifespan↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You lay many more eggs. Your body is lighter than before, and your molts come more slowly.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Lay fewer, and save your strength","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Steady","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You lay only a few eggs, then spend what strength you have left on molting and finding food.","decisions.5.title":"The water film in the soil cracks","decisions.5.body":"A breeze blows, and the water film between the soil grains breaks into pieces. Ahead lies a small damp patch of soil, but a stretch of dry sand lies between you and it.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Cross the sand to the damp soil","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Desiccation risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You crawl across the sand. The edge of the damp soil feels cool, and you rest there for a long time.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Wait in the original soil crack","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · The film keeps thinning","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You stay where you are. The water film grows thinner, and your skin begins to dry.","decisions.6.title":"Molting more slowly","decisions.6.body":"The gaps between your molts keep growing, and each one takes a long time. The water in your body is slowly dwindling, and the mold patch on the surface is shrinking too.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Molt deep in the damp humus","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Stable · Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You burrow deep into the humus and slowly shed your old skin there.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Climb onto the mold patch and feed","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Energy↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You feed on the mold patch for a long time, and the molting has to be put off.","decisions.7.title":"A centipede passes overhead","decisions.7.body":"A rustling comes from a gap in the leaf litter, and a centipede's legs press down from above. You can spring away at once, or stay pressed against the soil grains without moving.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Spring away with the furcula","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ May land in danger","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You land a few centimeters away on bare ground, and the centipede's legs sweep past behind you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Stay pressed against the soil grains","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Hiding · Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You fold your furcula away and stay perfectly still. The centipede pauses, then crawls on.","decisions.8.title":"A warm spot in the frozen soil","decisions.8.body":"The soil above has frozen, but there is a small warm, damp patch under the surface. You could stay in a crack in the frozen soil, or climb to a shallower layer to look for food that has not yet frozen.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Wedge yourself into a crack in the frozen soil","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Stamina↑ Food↓","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You curl up in a crack in the frozen soil and do not move, waiting for the soil to loosen.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Climb to a shallow layer to look for food","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You find a small clump of fungus in the shallow layer. It is too cold outside, and you soon crawl back into the soil crack.","decisions.9.title":"A tunnel left by an earthworm","decisions.9.body":"An earthworm has dug a straight tunnel through the soil. It holds moisture and some loosened fungal threads, but it is narrow, and vibrations from outside travel straight down it.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Follow the tunnel","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Safety↓","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You crawl along the tunnel and reach many loosened fungal threads to eat. The vibrations from outside keep you tense the whole time.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay where you are and do not move","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You do not enter the tunnel. You stay with your own small patch of dark soil.","opening.title":"The mother lays her eggs in a soil crack","opening.body":"You are still a milky-white egg, buried among the soil particles. Your mother must decide where to lay you. The deep, damp humus does not lose water easily, but food is scarce there. Near the mold patches there is plenty to eat, but there are also more fungal spores.","opening.options.0.t":"Bury in the damp depths of the humus","opening.options.0.hint":"Steady humidity · Food↓","opening.options.0.result":"You are buried in a layer of damp humus. The water films between the soil grains never break, and you slowly swell.","opening.options.1.t":"Lay beside a mold patch","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Fungal risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"Your egg lies next to the mold. Once you hatch, you can eat quickly, but the spores are also very close.","actions.forage.name":"Graze mold and humus","actions.forage.desc":"Eat slowly on damp mold and humus, scraping off the threads bit by bit with your mouthparts.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in a soil crack","actions.hide.desc":"Squeeze into a gap between two soil grains, with your furcula folded beneath you, out of reach of tremors and predators.","actions.rest.name":"Rest curled up","actions.rest.desc":"Fold your furcula away and stop, waiting for the water in your body to return to the body wall.","actions.explore.name":"Climb out of the soil crack to find mold","actions.explore.desc":"Leave your familiar soil crack in search of new mold and wetter soil.","actions.mate.name":"Lay a clutch of eggs","actions.mate.desc":"The female buries her eggs among damp soil particles. The male leaves only a sperm capsule, which the female picks up herself.","actions.mate.done":"You laid a clutch of {n} eggs.","idle.0":"It is dark and damp between the soil grains.","idle.1":"The gaps in the leaf litter are quiet, with only an occasional tremor coming down from the surface.","idle.2":"There are many tiny sounds in the soil, mostly other creatures moving about.","idle.3":"You have no eyes. You sense what is around you through smell and vibration.","idle.4":"Moisture at the surface evaporates bit by bit, and the soil slowly turns pale.","idle.5":"In the distance, you hear rain dripping onto fallen leaves.","idle.6":"The smell of mold drifts over slowly, not very strong.","idle.7":"In this small patch of soil, your companions crowd together, separated only by a few grains.","actions.forage.text.0":"You scrape back and forth over a damp mold patch, and your mouthparts fill with fine threads.","actions.forage.text.1":"The smell of humus is strong, and you eat slowly.","actions.forage.text.2":"Bacteria grow on the underside of a rotting leaf, and you stay there a long time.","actions.hide.text.0":"You squeeze between two soil grains, your furcula folded beneath you.","actions.hide.text.1":"Tremors pass down from outside in waves, and you do not move.","actions.hide.text.2":"The water film here has not broken yet, and your skin slowly moistens.","actions.rest.text.0":"You curl up on damp soil, your furcula folded against your belly, not moving at all.","actions.rest.text.1":"The water in your body slowly returns to the body wall, and your skin takes on a sheen.","actions.rest.text.2":"It is dark between the soil grains, and nothing happens.","actions.explore.text.0":"You crawl across a rough patch of sand, and your body quickly dries.","actions.explore.text.1":"You smell a small patch of mold and crawl toward it.","actions.explore.text.2":"A solitary ant patrols in the distance, and you go around it."},"squid":{"name":"Japanese flying squid","tagline":"One year, one life. You spawn, and then you die.","survivalNote":"Estimated from the age composition of catches and statolith ageing. Mortality during the planktonic stage is very high, and lifespan is about 1 year (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005)","epitaph":"You finished your whole life in one year. Your eggs will drift apart in the warm current, and one day new squid will swim out from among them.","taxon":"Mollusca › Cephalopoda › Teuthida › Ommastrephidae","size":"Mantle length about 20–35 cm (excluding arms)","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Sea conditions that year","birth.rolls.2.label":"Distribution of fishing boats","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the same mass","birth.intro":"You hatch in {r0}, no bigger than a grain of rice and unable to swim yet. You are {sex}. There are {siblings} eggs in the same mass.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a gelatinous egg mass drifting just below the surface at the edge of the Kuroshio","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"an egg mass tangled in drifting seaweed","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a shallow egg cluster at the edge of a cold-water mass","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"a year with a strong warm current and plenty of plankton","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a year with warmer seas and cold water too weak to bring up nutrients","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a year of strong cold upwelling, with plenty of plankton but heavy wind and waves","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"waters with almost no fishing boats, where the lights at night are sparse","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"inshore waters crowded with fishing boats, whose lights join into one glow at night","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"waters inside the summer fishing ban, where boats are not allowed to enter","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Paralarva (planktonic)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Spawning stage","causes.0.short":"Hooked on a jigging lure","causes.0.text":"The boat lights turn the surface white. You swim toward the glittering bait, and a hook pierces one of your arms.","causes.1.short":"Chased down by dolphins","causes.1.text":"A pod of dolphins charges up from below. You jet away in a burst, but the slowest one in the group cannot escape.","causes.2.short":"Swallowed by a tuna","causes.2.text":"A tuna accelerates behind you. Its mouth is huge, and you do not get the chance to squirt ink.","causes.3.short":"Eaten by a larger squid","causes.3.text":"A larger squid approaches from behind. Its arms wrap around you, and then your body begins to be swallowed.","causes.4.short":"Starved (not enough plankton)","causes.4.text":"There are far fewer copepods and small fish in this sea than last year. Your body grows lighter bit by bit, until you stop moving.","causes.5.short":"Egg mass eaten by shrimp and fish","causes.5.text":"A small shrimp slips into a gap in the egg mass and eats you along with the eggs nearby.","causes.6.short":"Died of exhaustion after spawning","causes.6.text":"Your last eggs are already in the sea. Your body no longer needs you, and you slowly sink toward the seabed.","events.0.text":"Inside the egg mass, the embryo slowly turns. The water temperature changes every day, and your heartbeat follows it.","events.1.text":"A small fish swims past the egg mass. Its mouth brushes the membrane, but it does not bite you.","events.2.text":"You spread your arms and grab a copepod. It is slightly bigger than your own body.","events.3.text":"An upwelling carries you into a warm layer of water. You drift for a long time and leave your old waters behind.","events.4.text":"At night you chase a school of small fish under the lights. They scatter like a silver mist.","events.5.text":"You hang still in the dark water and adjust your direction with small movements of your fins, keeping your body temperature as low as it will go.","events.6.text":"You swim past the edge of the continental shelf. The sea turns deep blue. Food is scarcer, but almost nothing chases you.","events.7.text":"By day you sink into deep water. It is cold and dark there, and your colour darkens to match.","events.8.text":"A shark's shadow passes overhead. You press against the seabed mud and stay perfectly still until it has gone.","events.9.text":"The water warms, and the currents push you north. Your body feels lighter and quicker than before.","events.10.text":"In the distance, a row of brightly lit boats sits in the night. You see some of your kind hauled up onto the decks.","events.11.text":"You move toward the light. Several of your kind are circling beside you. You reach out an arm and pull in the nearest one.","events.12.text":"You swim into a warm sea you have never visited, where plankton is dense.","events.13.text":"A much larger squid swims over. You eat a piece of arm it drops, then leave quickly.","events.14.text":"You find a suitable layer of water at the edge of the warm current. You lay your first egg mass, and it slowly swells in the water.","events.14.fact":"Japanese flying squid breed only once in their lives and die after spawning (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005)","events.15.text":"Your digestive gland begins to shrink, and your body's energy is diverted to spawning. You eat less and less.","events.16.text":"Your mantle thins, and the suckers on your arms lose their sheen.","events.17.text":"You release your last egg mass into the sea. The currents will scatter it, and your body starts to feel very light.","events.18.text":"A pelican's shadow passes over the surface. You sink a few metres, and sunlight turns your body pale.","events.19.text":"Light from the surface filters down. Your skin changes colour in waves, from red to grey and back to white.","events.20.text":"The warm current carries plankton here, and your body grows quickly.","events.21.text":"The seawater warms, and you start swimming north earlier than in other years.","events.22.text":"The sea has too few nutrients, so there are fewer copepods than usual. You search the plankton layer for a long time before you catch one.","events.23.text":"In this same stretch of ocean, there are fewer small fish than last year. Your body grows lighter by the day.","events.24.text":"Cold water wells up from the seafloor, carrying a large amount of plankton. You eat your fill, but the wind and waves are strong, and your body is pushed back and forth.","events.25.text":"The upwelling sweeps you into a murky patch of water, and you are spun around several times.","events.26.text":"The lights of distant fishing boats have joined into a single glow, and everything you see in the water is light.","events.27.text":"The sound of the fishing boats comes down through the water, and your companions sink into deeper water in waves.","events.28.text":"There are few fishing boats in this area. You swim slowly through the night and almost never encounter lights.","events.29.text":"The sea surface is quiet, with only you and a few small fish.","events.30.text":"These are waters under a summer fishing ban, and the boats in the distance have all turned around and left.","events.31.text":"Plankton is denser here than elsewhere, but you do not dare linger in the shallows for long.","events.32.text":"You do not wait for your companions. You chase a small fish alone and swim into the light.","events.33.text":"You pause for a long time at the edge of the deep water. The others of your kind have already moved toward the shallows, but you are still waiting.","events.34.text":"You chase a glowing patch of plankton a long way and forget to look back.","events.35.text":"You are a size smaller than the juveniles from your batch, and in the plankton layer the others crowd you to the edge.","events.36.text":"A fellow squid bites onto one of your arms. You twist free and shake it off, leaving only a pale mark behind.","decisions.0.title":"Drift north or stay","decisions.0.body":"You are still a tiny paralarva, transparent in body. The current keeps pushing north, but the water temperature there changes a great deal, while the plankton to the south is steady.","decisions.0.fact":"Paralarvae of the Japanese flying squid are carried north by the Kuroshio and warm currents, and their spread depends strongly on water temperature (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Drift north with the warm current","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You ride the current north. The sea colour changes day by day, and your body grows along the way.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay at the edge of the cold water","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Steady · Food↓","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You stop at the edge of the cold water. There is less plankton than you expected, but the surroundings are quiet.","decisions.1.title":"Prey is getting scarce","decisions.1.body":"There is nothing to eat in the deep water by day, but at night a few small fish gather under the lights near the surface. You must decide when to feed.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Rise to the surface at night and chase the lights","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You rise under the lights and eat your fill. But the lights also make you very easy to see.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Sink to deep water by day","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You sink into deep water. It is cold there, and your body slows bit by bit.","decisions.2.title":"A larger squid approaches","decisions.2.body":"A squid much bigger than you swims slowly beside you. Its arms flick through the water, as if it is testing you.","decisions.2.fact":"Squid and other cephalopods do eat their own kind, especially when food is scarce or when individuals differ greatly in size (Boyle & Rodhouse 2005)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Bite one of its arms and steal a meal","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk of death↑","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You grab a piece of its arm, but its counterattack comes quickly.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Swim away","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Safety","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You turn and swim off. The larger squid does not follow.","decisions.3.title":"Time to spawn","decisions.3.body":"The water at the edge of the warm current is now right. Your gonads are mature, and many males are swimming around you, their arms twining together.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Head for the spawning ground now","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You swim toward the spawning ground. The water there is warm, and you release your first egg cluster.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Feed for a few more days","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Offspring↓","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You feed for a few more days. By the time you are ready, the spawning grounds nearby are crowded with others.","decisions.4.title":"Lights on the boats","decisions.4.body":"At night a row of bright lights shows in the distance. Small fish drifting in the water are all swimming toward it, and some of your kind are there too.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Approach the light and eat the small fish","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You eat a big mouthful of small fish, then see a line of glittering hooks in the water below.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Sink to deep water and avoid the lights","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You keep away from the lights. The sea is dark, and your body slowly sinks.","decisions.5.title":"The last egg mass","decisions.5.body":"Your stamina is falling, and the suckers on your arms have grown dull. You still have one egg mass left to release.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Spawn in shallow water a little longer","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"In shallow water you release your last egg mass, and the eggs slowly spread out in the sea.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Sink to deep water and rest","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Offspring↓","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You sink into deep water, and your body slowly goes still.","decisions.6.title":"Less food near the surface","decisions.6.body":"There is less plankton near the surface than in other years. Your body needs more food, and you have to decide where to look.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Search the cold water in the depths","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Steady","decisions.6.options.0.result":"Food is thinner in the cold depths than at the surface, but fewer things hunt you there.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Follow the warm current north to richer waters","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You follow the warm current north. The journey is long and hard.","decisions.7.title":"A line of fishing lights","decisions.7.body":"At night, a row of fishing boat lights glows on the sea surface. The squid around you are swimming toward the light.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Swim toward the lights and grab the small fish","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You grab a big mouthful of small fish, and then you see a hook swinging through the water.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Circle around to the dark side of the boat's stern","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You circle to the stern, where the light cannot reach, and the water darkens around you.","opening.title":"Mother looks for a place to spawn","opening.body":"You are not yet born. Your mother swims at the edge of the Kuroshio and must decide where to release the egg mass. The warm current gives good hatching water, but there the plankton also attracts predators.","opening.options.0.t":"Release the eggs in warm water on the inner side of the current","opening.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","opening.options.0.result":"The current carries the egg mass away. You hatch in warm water, with plenty of plankton around you.","opening.options.1.t":"Release the eggs at the edge of the cold water","opening.options.1.hint":"Safer · Food↓","opening.options.1.result":"The egg mass sinks at the edge of the cold water. When you hatch, there is only thin plankton around you.","actions.forage.name":"Forage","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the scent of light and plankton to find something to eat.","actions.hide.name":"Sink to deep water","actions.hide.desc":"Retreat to a deeper, darker layer to reduce the chance of being seen.","actions.rest.name":"Hover","actions.rest.desc":"Stop moving, hold your position with your fins, and save energy.","actions.explore.name":"Head for open ocean","actions.explore.desc":"Leave familiar waters in search of new food and new temperatures.","actions.mate.name":"Spawn","actions.mate.desc":"Release egg masses at the edge of the warm current. After spawning, your body will no longer need you.","actions.mate.done":"You laid {n} eggs.","idle.0":"You drift slowly through the water, your arms swaying gently.","idle.1":"The sea temperature changed several times over the day.","idle.2":"In the distance, a whale calls, low and slow.","idle.3":"Your body is transparent, and its colour shifts with the light.","idle.4":"A small fish passes you without looking.","idle.5":"The current pushes you north, and you swim with it.","idle.6":"You smell salt, and the faint scent of plankton from far away.","idle.7":"At night the lights flicker on the surface. You keep your distance.","actions.forage.text.0":"You spread your arms and grab a clump of krill.","actions.forage.text.1":"In the dark you chase a school of small fish.","actions.hide.text.0":"You press against the sediment on the seabed, and your colour turns as dull as it.","actions.hide.text.1":"Daylight filters down from above. You stay perfectly still.","actions.rest.text.0":"You hang in the water and drift slowly with the current.","actions.explore.text.0":"You swim past the continental shelf, and the water grows bluer."},"swallow":{"name":"Barn swallow","tagline":"It flies across half the world in a single season, then returns to the same eaves","survivalNote":"A rough estimate at the order of magnitude suggested by European barn swallow ringing studies; it does not represent any single population. First-year mortality is highest, and migration is a second peak","epitaph":"Every spring some swallows return to the eaves where they were born. You are one of them, or you are not.","taxon":"Chordata › Aves › Passeriformes › Hirundinidae","size":"Body length about 17–19 cm, weight about 20 g","birth.rolls.0.label":"Nest site","birth.rolls.1.label":"Parents","birth.rolls.2.label":"Migration route","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the clutch","birth.intro":"You were an egg, {r0}. Your shell is thin and faintly pinkish white. Your clutch has {siblings} eggs, and {r1}. You are {sex}, though you cannot tell yet.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"in a mud-cup nest under the eaves of an old farmhouse","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"in a mud nest above a barn door","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"in a mud nest on a beam under a bridge","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"your parents are an experienced pair of older swallows","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"your parents are two young birds nesting for the first time","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"the western route, flying west to Western Europe, across the Mediterranean, and along the edge of the Sahara","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"the eastern route, south through Balkan and Middle Eastern valleys, past the edge of a desert","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"the East Asian route, south along rivers through eastern China and Southeast Asia","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Nestling (in the nest)","stages.2.name":"Fledgling (out of the nest)","stages.3.name":"Adult","stages.4.name":"Old age","causes.0.short":"Caught by a hobby","causes.0.text":"A hobby dives from high above. You see its shadow press down, and then it is over.","causes.1.short":"Pounced on by a cat","causes.1.text":"A cat crouches at the barn door. You fly just over its head, and it rises to its feet.","causes.2.short":"A snake climbed into the nest","causes.2.text":"A snake crawls up the eaves and pushes its head into the nest. Your parents cry outside without stopping, and there is nowhere to hide.","causes.3.short":"Starved in cold rain","causes.3.text":"The cold rain has lasted 3 days, and the insects have all gone into hiding. Your belly has been empty for a long time, and on your last flight your wings have no strength left.","causes.4.short":"Knocked from the air by a storm","causes.4.text":"A violent storm sweeps down from the north, and the sky turns grey. The wind tears at your wings and finally throws you into an unseen darkness.","causes.5.short":"Exhausted during migration","causes.5.text":"You cross a strait with no land to rest on. Your fat reserves run out, and on the last stretch you can only glide forward on momentum.","causes.6.short":"Mud nest collapsed in a downpour","causes.6.text":"Days of heavy rain have softened the mud nest. One afternoon it collapses entirely, taking everything inside with it.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your tail feathers have grown short, and you wobble a little in flight. On a warm summer night, you cannot fly back to the eaves.","events.0.text":"Your parents carry wet mud from the roadside and press it onto the eaves, one pellet at a time. Once dry, the mud becomes a bowl.","events.1.text":"Your mother lies in the nest and covers you with her belly. The wind is strong outside, but the nest stays warm.","events.2.text":"Your parents return with a dragonfly, and your mouth opens wide, a red hole.","events.3.text":"A snake crawls up through a crack in the eaves. Panic spreads through the nest, and you can only curl up small.","events.4.text":"You open your eyes and see a small patch of blue sky.","events.5.text":"Rain falls for days. Water drips from the eaves, and the mud outside the nest softens. Your parents keep patching the walls.","events.6.text":"You fly out of the nest for the first time. You land at the barn door, and your wings feel borrowed.","events.6.fact":"Barn swallow nestlings typically leave the nest at about 3 weeks old (BTO species account for the barn swallow)","events.7.text":"Your parents teach you to catch insects in the air. Your first bite takes in a mosquito, and you choke and spit it out.","events.8.text":"The weather turns cool, and the grass in the fields begins to yellow. Many swallows line up on the power lines, waiting for something.","events.9.text":"A cold rain comes down, and the insects in the fields stop flying. You sit on the power line all day, your belly rumbling.","events.10.text":"Over the river you chase a dragonfly, catch it in one bite, and swallow it down.","events.11.text":"A strong wind blows across the grassland, and the insects are all blown away. You can only search at the edge of the wind.","events.12.text":"Fog rises over the fields. Your wings are coated with a thin film of water, and you fly low.","events.13.text":"Your tail feathers are long and symmetrical. A female lands beside you and tilts her head to look at you for a long time.","events.13.fact":"Møller (1992) found that females prefer males with symmetrical tail feathers (Nature)","events.14.text":"You lay another clutch of eggs, {n} in all.","events.15.text":"You return to last year's mud nest. It is still under the eaves, but a crack has opened in the wall at the left corner.","events.16.text":"You circle over a rice field and notice a swarm of mosquitoes gathering low down.","events.17.text":"A cat sits in the sun at the barn door. You fly over its head, and it looks up at you.","events.18.text":"You perch on the power line, one swallow on the left, another on the right, your companions lined up in a row.","events.19.text":"Your tail has grown shorter again, and the curve of your flight is no longer as graceful as before.","events.20.text":"You spend another summer under the old eaves. There are more insects this year than last, and you feel you can still fly.","events.21.text":"You skim just above the wheat, brushing the grass tips, and swallow three aphids in a single pass.","events.22.text":"A storm is coming, and the insects are flying low. You dive across the reeds by the river and take in many at once.","events.23.text":"You chase a dragonfly along the river surface. It suddenly turns, and you nearly crash into the water.","events.24.text":"You spend the whole day in the mud nest and fly out only briefly at dusk. Your belly rumbles.","events.25.text":"A hobby's shadow passes over the roof. You tuck yourself under a barn beam until it has flown off.","events.26.text":"You sit on the power line through the afternoon, wings folded tight, perfectly still.","events.27.text":"You fly into a valley you have never visited. At its bottom runs a clear river, and the insects along its banks are too many to count.","events.28.text":"You fly over a large city. There are no power lines between the towers to land on, and after a long search you are worn out.","events.29.text":"You find an old patch of mud above a barn door, softened by rain, and carry it back in mouthfuls, pressing it onto the wall again.","events.30.text":"The eggs you have just laid are soaked by a cold rain. You press them beneath your body and warm them dry with your own heat.","events.31.text":"You fly over the Mediterranean all day. Below, the water is blue, and no land is in sight.","events.32.text":"You stop at a wetland in Spain and eat your fill of flying insects.","events.33.text":"You cross a mountain pass, where the wind is strong. You rest for a day at the edge of a pasture.","events.34.text":"You pass the edge of a desert, where the air is hot and dry, and your feathers are full of sand.","events.35.text":"You fly south along a river. The rice fields by the riverbank are full of flying insects, and you feed for a long time.","events.36.text":"The southern rainy season lasts a long time. You fly through the damp forest for days, and your feathers stay wet.","events.37.text":"You are a size smaller than your siblings in the nest. When your parents bring food, you are always pushed to the very edge of the nest.","events.38.text":"You leave the nest for the first time, fly farther than the other young swallows, and land at the far end of the eaves.","events.39.text":"You are the first to fly into a swarm of mosquitoes. The other swallows watch you, but you do not stop.","events.40.text":"You sit on the power line for a long time, waiting for your companions to fly away, and only then slowly follow after them.","events.41.text":"You fly over a rice field and see a frog leap out of the water. You circle near it and catch a flying insect.","decisions.0.title":"Autumn is leaving","decisions.0.body":"The nights are turning cool. There are fewer insects in the fields, and a restlessness you cannot name has taken hold of your body. Your companions gather on the power lines in greater numbers.","decisions.0.fact":"Barn swallows gather in flocks in autumn, waiting for the right wind and weather before setting out (Sussex Wildlife Trust, article on swallow migration)","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave right away while the weather is still good","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Good weather · Weight is low","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You leave the eaves with a flock of swallows. The sky is high, and the ground slowly breaks into patches of colour below.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Feed another week to build up weight","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Stamina high · Weather may turn bad","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You feed for another week, and you grow heavier. On the seventh morning, the weather begins to turn.","decisions.1.title":"Two routes","decisions.1.body":"There are two ways south. One crosses the Mediterranean in the air, with no place to land in between, but the route is short. The other follows the land, stopping at every stretch to find food and to avoid people on the ground.","decisions.1.fact":"The choice of migration route affects energy use and the risk of death on the way (Movement Ecology 2022, a study of barn swallow migration in central Europe)","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Cross the sea","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Short route · No stops","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You fly all day, with only wind and waves beneath you. Your fat runs out just before you reach the shore.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Follow the land slowly","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Long route · Many stops","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stop wherever there are grasslands and lakes, eat your fill, then fly on. Along the way someone raises a gun, and you skim over their heads.","decisions.2.title":"A storm from the north","decisions.2.body":"A yellow-grey cloud bank presses down from the horizon, and the wind whips sand into columns. Your companions circle low, unsure where to go.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Shelter in the trees for the night","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety · Stamina cost","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You spend the night in a stand of trees. The next day the wind has eased, but your feathers are full of sand.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Keep flying before the wind arrives","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Distance gained · Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You fly as hard as you can. The wind presses on your back like a wall, and then you can see nothing at all.","decisions.3.title":"Where to go back to","decisions.3.body":"In your second spring you fly back north from the south. Last year's mud nest is still there, but the wall at its left corner has cracked. On the barn beam there is a new spot, close to the road.","decisions.3.fact":"Most adult barn swallows return to the nest site they used the previous year, while only about 5–10% of young birds return to their natal colony (Saino et al. 2013)","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Return to last year's mud nest","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Familiar · Cracked corner","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You patch the crack and add new mud to the edge of the old nest. It is a little smaller than you remembered.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Find a new beam in the barn","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Offspring↑ Risk↑","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You build a new nest on the beam. Each time you fly in, you have to dodge a cat's paw reaching up.","decisions.4.title":"New swallows under the old eaves","decisions.4.body":"You return to the old eaves. A pair of young swallows has already taken your mud nest and is peering out at you. Your wings are a little shorter than last year, and your mud-carrying is slower.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Drive them off and take back the old nest","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Nest secured · Injury risk↑","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You argue under the eaves for a long time, and in the end they fly away. The nest smells like yours again.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Build a nest under a bridge by the river","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Offspring↓ Safety","decisions.4.options.1.result":"It is dark under the bridge, and the traffic noise shakes loose bits of mud. You gather them one by one and slowly build a bowl.","decisions.5.title":"A cold spring","decisions.5.body":"The April rain has fallen for a full 10 days, and the insects are hiding. Your belly has been empty the whole time, and the eggs in the nest have already gone cold twice.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Try the vent of a greenhouse","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"A few flying insects drift out of the greenhouse vent. You dart in, eat a meal, and fly back out.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Wait for the rain to stop, staying in the mud nest","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You wait in the mud nest for 3 days. When the rain stops, you have lost a good deal of weight.","decisions.6.title":"Time to fly south","decisions.6.body":"On a cool night, you hear distant calls from your companions while you sit under the eaves. You have already flown through many autumns, but your body carries less fat each year.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Fly off with the flock","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Joins migration · Risk on the way↑","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You cross that sea once more. This time your wings ache more than they did last year.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay for the winter","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"No migration · Hungry in winter","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay under the eaves and wait for the first warm wind. It comes slowly.","decisions.7.title":"Hot wind from the desert edge","decisions.7.body":"A yellow haze hangs in the sky ahead, and hot wind is blowing in from the desert. You could fly into the wind, or rest in a stand of trees by the road.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Fly on into the wind","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"On the move · Stamina↓","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You fly into the wind until nightfall. At the edge of the desert, you find a river with water in it.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Rest in the trees for a day","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Steady · Saves Stamina","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You rest in the trees for a day, drink some water, and then slowly take to the air again.","opening.title":"How the parents share the work","opening.body":"You are still an egg. The mud nest is ready, and your mother must sit on it to incubate, but the insects outside will not wait. The longer she is away, the colder the eggs grow; the longer she stays, the less food the household has.","opening.options.0.t":"Mother sits on the nest all day","opening.options.0.hint":"Eggs warmer · Partner works harder","opening.options.0.result":"She lies still and leaves only briefly at dusk. You are wrapped in her warmth, and you stay safe and steady.","opening.options.1.t":"Send Mother out to hunt insects","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ · Nest gets cold","opening.options.1.result":"She flies out every so often and returns with insects in her beak. The nest temperature rises and falls.","actions.forage.name":"Catch insects in the air","actions.forage.desc":"Fly low over water and fields, mouth open, catching mosquitoes, dragonflies and other flying insects. You eat well, but you are also easier to spot.","actions.hide.name":"Hide in the mud nest","actions.hide.desc":"Stay in the mud nest under the eaves and do not come out. You eat little, but you are safe.","actions.rest.name":"Rest on the power line","actions.rest.desc":"Perch on a wire or eave and do nothing, letting your stamina slowly return.","actions.explore.name":"Fly over fields and rivers","actions.explore.desc":"Leave the village for farther fields and river surfaces. There is more to see and perhaps more insects, but more danger along the way too.","actions.mate.name":"Carry mud to build the nest","actions.mate.desc":"Carry mud to the eaves and press it on pellet by pellet. Once dry, it becomes a bowl. The female lays the eggs, and the male guards the nest entrance.","actions.mate.done":"You laid another clutch of eggs, {n} in all.","idle.0":"A breeze comes from over the fields, carrying a little smell of green grass.","idle.1":"The mud bowl under the eaves is a little damp, and wind slips in through the gaps.","idle.2":"A dragonfly passes close by and quickly vanishes into the light far away.","idle.3":"The air after rain is cool, and the insects in the grass come out again.","idle.4":"Dust hangs in the barn, and light slants through the gap in the door.","idle.5":"A low cloud bank sits on the far horizon. You wonder whether it will come this way.","idle.6":"A flock of swallows circles very high, their calls rising and falling.","idle.7":"Dusk is falling, and the sounds under the eaves slowly quiet down.","actions.forage.text.0":"You circle over the river and swallow mosquitoes one bite at a time.","actions.forage.text.1":"A swarm of midges gathers low, and you dive down, then climb away again.","actions.forage.text.2":"The wind over the fields is warm, and clouds of insects drift upward in clumps.","actions.hide.text.0":"You shrink into the mud nest while the wind gusts past outside.","actions.hide.text.1":"Inside the nest it is dim. You crowd together with your companions, and no one moves.","actions.hide.text.2":"A cat walks past the wall outside, and you hold your breath.","actions.rest.text.0":"You perch on a wire with your eyes closed, your body rocking gently in the wind.","actions.rest.text.1":"Rain drips from the eaves one drop at a time. You do not move out of its way.","actions.rest.text.2":"Your companions sit in a row on the wire, and none of them speaks.","actions.explore.text.0":"You cross a field just mown, the ground covered in grasshoppers and insects.","actions.explore.text.1":"On the far bank there is a new row of power lines. You and a few other swallows perch on them in the sun.","actions.explore.text.2":"You fly under a bridge, where a nest of swallows is busy beneath the arch.","actions.mate.text.0":"You carry wet mud and press it into the corner of the wall, one mouthful at a time.","actions.mate.text.1":"The male flits back and forth under the eaves, bringing you the mud you need.","actions.mate.text.2":"The mud bowl slowly dries. You settle into it and press your belly against the rim."},"termite":{"name":"Termite","tagline":"Inside the timber of an old house, a colony may hold several million sterile workers","survivalNote":"Lifespan data for wild worker termites are scarce; in the lab, workers usually survive for months to a year or two. This curve is a rough estimate based on lifespan inside the colony and predation pressure","epitaph":"You were a sterile worker, and your whole life you served the breeders in the nest. In the end, your body became part of the nest too.","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birth colony","birth.rolls.1.label":"Age of the colony","birth.rolls.2.label":"Wood","birth.siblingsLabel":"Eggs in the batch","birth.intro":"You are a worker termite, hatched from an egg laid in {r0}. You are about 4 mm long, white, and have no eyes. You will never reproduce. Your batch has {siblings} eggs.","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"an old pine log hollowed out by termites","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"the space behind a load-bearing beam in an old house","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a nest among the roots of an old banyan tree","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"an old colony more than twenty years old, where the queen is still laying eggs","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"a young colony that split off not long ago","birth.rolls.1.options.2.0":"a colony whose queen has just died and is raising a replacement","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"an old sleeper buried underground and soaked by water","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a sodden old pine log","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"a door frame, its wood thoroughly dry","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"an old board pressed beneath concrete","stages.0.name":"Egg","stages.1.name":"Larva (young worker)","stages.2.name":"Adult worker","stages.3.name":"Old worker","causes.0.short":"Dragged out of the nest by ants","causes.0.text":"A column of ants rushes in through the galleries. Before you can turn around, your leg is seized and you are dragged toward the opening.","causes.1.short":"Infected by green muscardine fungus","causes.1.text":"A hypha pushes through a gap in your joints. Within days, your body is covered in white spore stalks.","causes.2.short":"Ate bait laced with poison","causes.2.text":"The wood smells like food. You bite into it, carry it back to the nest, and share it with your nestmates. After a few moults, your new skin never hardens.","causes.3.short":"Gallery opened, dry air rushed in","causes.3.text":"Someone has dug open a gallery in the wall. The outside air is dry, and within minutes you lose most of your water.","causes.4.short":"Smothered by mould","causes.4.text":"A small patch of white mould grows in the damp nest. It wraps the eggs beside you first, and then you.","causes.5.short":"Old age","causes.5.text":"The symbiotic flagellates in your gut dwindle, and your digestion of cellulose slows. On your last day, you stop on a pile of wood shavings and do not move again.","events.0.text":"The eggs are in the nursery. Workers lick them every so often, removing mould from their surfaces.","events.1.text":"You struggle out of your eggshell. Nestmates are touching you with their antennae, one tap at a time.","events.2.text":"Workers mix chewed wood fibre with digestive fluid and feed it to you. This is the colony's regurgitated food.","events.3.text":"You moult for the first time. Your new skin is still soft, and paler than the others'.","events.4.text":"You are assigned to haul wood shavings. Your job is to carry the nest's waste out to the graveyard.","events.5.text":"A cockroach crawls past a gallery, and the tremor shakes a small piece of mud from the nest wall.","events.6.text":"You leave the nest and climb a mud tube toward a wooden stake buried underground. Outside the tube it is dark and dry.","events.7.text":"You bite off a piece of soft wood and carry its cellulose back to the nest.","events.8.text":"At the end of a new gallery you find a piece of damp wood with a strange sweet smell.","events.9.text":"A plastic pipe blocks your way. You and your nestmates have to turn back and rebuild the mud tube along the old route.","events.10.text":"You retreat to the depths of the nest. Ants are still biting outside, and the humidity inside is high. It is so quiet that you hear only the rub of your nestmates' bodies.","events.11.text":"You lean against the nursery wall with your nestmates, touching heads gently.","events.12.text":"A line of ants scouts along the edge of the gallery, tapping at the mud walls with their antennae.","events.13.text":"A heavy rain falls. The groundwater rises, and humidity in the nest reaches its peak.","events.14.text":"A dry week passes. The nest walls harden, and you add another layer to the mud tubes.","events.15.text":"A group of winged reproductives flies out from a neighbouring nest. They will never come back here, and you will never see them again.","events.16.text":"A soldier plugs a bitten opening with its mandibles. Its head is large, and a white secretion beads on its forehead.","events.17.text":"The flagellates and bacteria in your gut break down wood cellulose into sugar, which is the only way you can absorb it.","events.17.fact":"Termites digest wood cellulose with the help of symbiotic protists and bacteria in their guts (Cleveland 1926)","events.18.text":"An ant has invaded the nest entrance. You seal the opening with mud, and wet earth covers your legs as you work.","events.19.text":"Your movements have slowed by a beat. You no longer go outside, and stay in a corner of the nest looking after the young.","events.20.text":"An old nestmate dies in the nest. Workers carry its body away bit by bit and bury it in the graveyard soil.","events.21.text":"Through a wooden crack softened by rain, a fine piece of root bark shows. You gnaw through it layer by layer.","events.22.text":"You crawl into an old gallery leading to a neighbouring stake. There are no mud tubes here, and light comes through a crack.","events.23.text":"You hide beneath a pile of wood shavings. The tremor outside grows closer, then slowly fades away.","events.24.text":"After feeding, you wait in a very narrow gallery, perfectly still.","events.25.text":"You move toward the heart of the nest. There is a queen, her abdomen so swollen she can barely move. Workers feed her every day.","events.26.text":"The galleries of the nest keep expanding. Your work is heavy, and every day you repair a new tunnel.","events.27.text":"The nest is still small, and there are not many workers. You do not have the strength to plug every gap in the wood.","events.28.text":"The nest was built not long ago, and the wood dust is still fresh. You eat quickly, and the scent of the wood is strong.","events.29.text":"The smell in the nest has changed. Workers wander back and forth, and no one tends the larvae. In the confusion, you are pushed into another tunnel.","events.30.text":"A replacement queen is being looked after in the nest. You are all waiting for her to grow up.","events.31.text":"You dig tunnels through the wet wood, and each bite carries a cool trace of water. The wood dust is soft and is carried away quickly.","events.32.text":"The wood has grown moldy, and the smell is heavy. The protozoa living in your gut break down the fiber, and your body feels heavy.","events.33.text":"This sleeper lies buried, and the soil around it is damp and cold. You dig from the ground down into the wood, and the tunnels fill with mud.","events.34.text":"Muddy water from the wood seeps into the tunnels. You are forced to plug one passage and turn toward another.","events.35.text":"The wood is dry, and you can only dig slowly. Each day you eat very little.","events.36.text":"The paint on the door frame has a sharp smell. You avoid the paint and slip into the wood through a crack beside it.","events.37.text":"You dig a tunnel under the concrete. The board presses down above you, no light gets in, and the quiet is almost unnatural.","events.38.text":"Water drips from a pipe above, and a layer of damp mud forms in the tunnel. Together you plug it up.","events.39.text":"You leave the familiar tunnels and head toward a stretch of wood that has not been bored yet. The wood dust on the way is hard, and your mandibles ache from grinding it.","events.40.text":"An ant pokes into the tunnel entrance from outside. You immediately block the opening and hold it until the ant retreats.","events.41.text":"You are smaller than the other young workers your age, so you can only work at the edge of the tunnels. The big workers carry off the wood dust, and you pick up very little.","decisions.0.title":"A mud tube has been trampled","decisions.0.body":"A section of mud tube outside the nest has been crushed by someone's foot, and outside air is slowly seeping in. You and your nestmates must decide what to do.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Repair the tube at once","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You smear saliva and mud together and slowly seal the crack.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Take the detour through another tube","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Risk↑","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You crawl along a longer mud tube that winds around. The way is long, and it is dark outside.","decisions.1.title":"A damp piece of wood","decisions.1.body":"A piece of wet wood buried in the ground outside the nest holds a lot of cellulose. Getting there means crossing a stretch of ground with no mud tube to protect you.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Go out and forage","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk↑","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You gnaw on the wood all day. On the way back, you smell ants.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Stay in the nest","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You stay in the nest, chewing the shavings your nestmates bring back and eating them slowly.","decisions.2.title":"Ants gather at the entrance","decisions.2.body":"A line of ants is gathering near the nest entrance. Their mandibles are long, and you have only a thin layer of secretion on your forehead.","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Seal the entrance with earth","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Safety↑↑ Food↓","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You pile the mud up layer by layer. Once the entrance is blocked, the noise outside suddenly fades.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Send workers out to block them","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Risk↑↑","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You rush out. The ants bite your legs first, then your antennae.","decisions.3.title":"A board smeared with something sweet","decisions.3.body":"A board lies in a corner, coated with something sweet. Several workers have already passed it, and the wood shavings they bit off are being carried back to the nest.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Bite off a piece and carry it back to the nest","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Lethal risk↑","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You carry it back and share it with your nestmates. The taste was good, but within a few days your skin begins to soften.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Go around it","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"A bit hungry","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You go around the board. But several nestmates have eaten from it, and soon they stop moving.","decisions.4.title":"The nest shell has cracked","decisions.4.body":"A long crack has split the outer mud shell, dried out by the sun. Cold air pours in through it, and the humidity inside begins to drop.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Retreat to the depths along the damp underground passage","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You climb down the deeper passage, and the crack is left behind.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay by the crack and repair it","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Risk↑ Food↑","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You fill the gap with saliva and mud, and the dry air is kept out.","decisions.5.title":"Part of the old nest wall has collapsed","decisions.5.body":"Rain has washed away a corner of the old nest wall. Wood without a mud-tube cover is now exposed to the outside.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Go out and repair the wall","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You climb onto the wall and fill the gap with mud, bit by bit. Wind blows past you.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Retreat to the innermost chamber","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety↑","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You withdraw to the innermost chamber. The sounds outside fade away, bit by bit.","decisions.6.title":"Winged reproductives","decisions.6.body":"The rainy season is near, and the winged reproductives in the nest have gathered together, waiting to fly out. The workers must decide whether to escort them through the wood dust or stay behind and reinforce the tunnels.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Escort the reproductives out of the nest","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Safety↓ Food↓","decisions.6.options.0.result":"You and your companions escort a group of winged reproductives through the wood dust to the surface. Many are eaten by birds before they can take off.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Stay in the nest and reinforce the tunnels","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You stay in the nest and, with your companions, plug every crack in the tunnels one by one.","decisions.7.title":"The waterline under the ground","decisions.7.body":"Water is wicking up through the wet wood, and beneath the tunnels you can already feel a layer of cool, damp mud. You can keep digging into the wet wood, or climb back up to the dry heartwood.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Keep digging into the wet wood","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You dig deep into the wet wood. It is soft, and you eat quickly, but the tunnels could collapse at any moment.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Climb back up to the dry heartwood","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You climb a short distance and return to the dry heartwood. There is less to eat there.","decisions.8.title":"A beam of light through a crack","decisions.8.body":"A thin beam of light leaks in through a crack in the wood. It is bright, and the outside air rushes into the tunnel along with it.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Dig toward the light","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"Your tunnel suddenly reaches the crack. The dry outside air rushes in.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Turn the tunnel toward somewhere else","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safe","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You turn your tunnel elsewhere, and the beam of light soon vanishes behind the wood.","opening.title":"The queen lays a batch of eggs at the heart of the nest","opening.body":"You have not hatched yet. The queen has laid her eggs in the centre of a mature termite nest, and workers will carry them one by one into different nurseries. The heart of the nest is warm and humid, while the outer layers near the wood have food but are closer to ants and dry air.","opening.options.0.t":"Stay in a nursery at the heart of the nest","opening.options.0.hint":"Safety↑ Food↓","opening.options.0.result":"You hatch in a humid nursery, surrounded by hundreds of eggs the same size as yours.","opening.options.1.t":"Be carried to a gallery in the outer layer near the wood","opening.options.1.hint":"Food↑ Risk↑","opening.options.1.result":"You hatch in a gallery near the wood. There is a lot of wood shaving here, but the entrance is close.","actions.forage.name":"Forage outside","actions.forage.desc":"Crawl along the mud tubes to the wood buried underground, bite off cellulose, and carry it back to the nest.","actions.hide.name":"Retreat to the depths","actions.hide.desc":"Hide in the innermost nursery and wait for things outside to quiet down.","actions.rest.name":"Keep still","actions.rest.desc":"Stay still in a damp gallery to conserve energy.","actions.explore.name":"Explore old galleries","actions.explore.desc":"Feel your way out along old routes that have no mud-tube cover, searching for new wood.","idle.0":"In the dark gallery, you find your way by the smell on your antennae.","idle.1":"The nest is damp, and a thin film of moisture lies on the walls.","idle.2":"You have no eyes, and light is dangerous to you.","idle.3":"Your nestmates' antennae stroke over you, one touch at a time.","idle.4":"A faint tremor runs through the floor. All of you stop and wait, then go back to work.","idle.5":"Wood is chewed bit by bit, and the crunching sound is very soft.","idle.6":"Your digestive fluid mixes with wood shavings and becomes food you can pass to others.","idle.7":"Light has come through a thin crack outside, and you soon seal it shut.","actions.forage.text.0":"You crawl outward along the mud tube.","actions.forage.text.1":"You bite off a small piece of wood shaving, slowly.","actions.forage.text.2":"You chop the wood fibres up and carry them back to the nest.","actions.hide.text.0":"You retreat deep into the nest.","actions.hide.text.1":"The sounds outside are blocked by the mud wall.","actions.rest.text.0":"You lie against the damp mud wall, perfectly still.","actions.explore.text.0":"You squeeze out through a crack in an old gallery."},"wolf":{"name":"Gray wolf","tagline":"Your ancestors and the ancestors of dogs were once the same animals. More than 10,000 years ago, they took different paths.","survivalNote":"Survival data for wild gray wolves come mainly from tracking studies in North America and Europe. First-year mortality is very high; adults usually live 4–6 years. This curve is a rough estimate","epitaph":"You left many footprints in the snow. When spring came, they melted away.","taxon":"Chordata › Mammalia › Carnivora › Canidae","size":"Body length about 1.3–1.8 m, weight 25–55 kg","birth.rolls.0.label":"Birthplace","birth.rolls.1.label":"Pack status","birth.rolls.2.label":"Wolf population","birth.rolls.3.label":"Pack size","birth.siblingsLabel":"Pups in the litter","birth.intro":"You were born in {r0}, with your eyes still closed and soft grey fur on your body. {r1}. You are {sex}, and there are about {siblings} pups in the litter.","birth.sex.0.0":"female","birth.sex.1.0":"male","birth.rolls.0.options.0.0":"a rock den in the northern conifer forest","birth.rolls.0.options.1.0":"a cedar grove in a mountain valley","birth.rolls.0.options.2.0":"a thicket at the edge of a pasture","birth.rolls.0.options.3.0":"a den on a hillside near a highway","birth.rolls.1.options.0.0":"Your pack is stable, and both of its dominant wolves are still alive","birth.rolls.1.options.1.0":"Your father is dead, and your mother is raising the pups alone","birth.rolls.2.options.0.0":"a wolf in Yellowstone, descended from wolves brought in from Canada in 1995","birth.rolls.2.options.1.0":"a wolf in Europe, in a patchwork of farms and woodland, with its pack territory pressing against the pastures","birth.rolls.2.options.2.0":"an Arctic tundra wolf, eating lemmings in summer and following reindeer herds in winter","birth.rolls.2.options.3.0":"a wolf on the Mongolian steppe, chasing gazelles and red deer","birth.rolls.3.options.0.0":"a large pack of more than a dozen wolves, with several breeding pairs","birth.rolls.3.options.1.0":"a small pack of five or six wolves, with only one breeding pair","birth.rolls.3.options.2.0":"a pair of adult wolves that have just claimed a territory and do not yet have a pack","stages.0.name":"Pup (in the den)","stages.1.name":"Pup (following the pack)","stages.2.name":"Juvenile (preparing to leave)","stages.3.name":"Adult wolf","stages.4.name":"Old wolf","causes.0.short":"Shot by a person","causes.0.text":"A man raises a gun across the snow. You hear the shot, but the bullet is already on its way.","causes.1.short":"Killed by a car","causes.1.text":"A truck on the night highway does not slow down. Its headlights flash, and then comes a dull thud.","causes.2.short":"Killed by another pack","causes.2.text":"Adult wolves from another pack corner you at the mouth of the valley. You try to run, but they are too close, and their teeth close on the back of your neck.","causes.3.short":"Infected after a moose's hind kick","causes.3.text":"You bite a moose's leg, and it kicks you with a hind hoof. The wound festers over several days, and you can no longer stand.","causes.4.short":"Starved","causes.4.text":"For several weeks you cannot catch any prey. Your ribs stand out more and more, and finally, on a snowy night, you collapse and do not get up again.","causes.5.short":"Canine distemper","causes.5.text":"Canine distemper spreads through the pack. You run a fever and drool, your walk grows more unsteady, and in the den you cough until you have no strength left.","causes.6.short":"Ate poisoned bait","causes.6.text":"A sheep has been left at the edge of the pasture. You swallow it, and soon you feel thirsty and your muscles begin to twitch.","causes.7.short":"Old age","causes.7.text":"Your teeth are worn flat, and your joints have stiffened. The pack has left you a place near the den entrance, and that is where you fall asleep.","events.0.text":"Your mother has gone out hunting. Only a few pups are left in the den, nipping at each other's ears.","events.1.text":"Your mother returns with meat she has partly digested. You fight over it, and she does not back away.","events.2.text":"You step out of the den for the first time. The light glares off the snow, and a crow startles you back inside.","events.3.text":"A fox sniffs at the snow near the den mouth. An adult wolf charges over from the pack and chases it off.","events.4.text":"The pack sets out after a herd of reindeer. You follow behind, learning to run and to bite.","events.5.text":"You follow the pack a long way, to a valley you have never seen. The snow here is deeper.","events.6.text":"A howl rings out in the distance. Members of the pack answer, and you howl too.","events.6.fact":"Wolf howls carry a long way, and packs use them to advertise their territory to other packs (Harrington & Mech, 1983)","events.7.text":"A moose stands in the willows by the river. You look at each other for a long time, and neither of you moves.","events.8.text":"You lie under a cedar, waiting for the pack to get around the flank of the reindeer herd. Snow settles on your back, and you do not shiver.","events.9.text":"You chase down a hare alone for the first time. It leaves a tangle of tracks in the snow, and you eat it.","events.10.text":"The midday sun is warm. You and your companions sleep pressed against the wind-sheltered side of a rock, and when anyone's ear twitches, you all wake.","events.11.text":"Two adult wolves in the pack snap at each other over a piece of venison. You slip to one side and do not dare to approach.","events.12.text":"A road cuts across the valley. You wait until the headlights have passed, then run across.","events.13.text":"On a winter night, you run down a moose. Its hind hoof catches your shoulder, and a cut opens in your skin.","events.14.text":"In the winter den, you huddle with the pack. The wind outside cannot reach you.","events.15.text":"In February you smell a female wolf. On the far side of the valley, she gives a long howl.","events.16.text":"In spring you give birth to your first litter in the den. The pups are tiny, eyes closed, and they press together.","events.16.fact":"Gray wolves typically have 4–6 pups per litter, and usually only the dominant breeding pair in a pack reproduces (Mech, The Wolf, 1970)","events.17.text":"A gunshot sounds in the distance, and the pack scatters across the snow. It takes a long time to gather again.","events.18.text":"Hunters have set traps by the road. You step around the patch of trampled snow.","events.19.text":"Distemper spreads through a nearby pack. Some wolves cough along the roadside, walking unsteadily.","events.20.text":"Your teeth have worn flat, and your bite is weaker. You can no longer hold down a larger prey.","events.21.text":"Your joints are stiffening. In the den, your place has been moved to the edge, closest to the entrance.","events.22.text":"You are the oldest wolf in the pack. The young wolves no longer make way for you, but they no longer bite you either.","events.23.text":"A crow follows you across the snow, waiting for you to leave scraps.","events.24.text":"A row of cars is parked by the road. Tourists hold long lenses and watch from a distance as you chase a group of elk across the snowfield. You ignore them.","events.25.text":"There are more elk in the valley than last year. You run across the snow for an afternoon, and at last you bring down a young bull.","events.26.text":"A wolf wearing a radio collar walks past you. On the road beyond, a researcher writes its number down in a notebook.","events.26.fact":"Yellowstone wolves have been reintroduced since 1995 and have been tracked with radio collars ever since (U.S. National Park Service, Yellowstone Wolf Project)","events.27.text":"At night you smell the sheepfold on the pasture. A livestock guardian dog barks in the distance, and you circle wide around its voice.","events.28.text":"You kill a lone sheep at the edge of the pasture. The next day, the farmer hangs a sign on the barbed-wire fence.","events.29.text":"The guardian dog chases you a long way through the night. When you return to the forest, you have a bite mark on your hind leg.","events.30.text":"You chase a herd of reindeer across the tundra. Under the snow there is no grass, so the reindeer have to dig through the snow to find lichen.","events.31.text":"Summer lasts only a few weeks on the tundra, and mosquitoes swarm everywhere. You and the pack lie at the edge of the wetland, covered in mosquitoes.","events.32.text":"One winter passes, and for three weeks in a row you do not meet any reindeer. At night you walk along the riverbed, your ears turned toward the wind.","events.33.text":"The wind is strong on the steppe. You chase a herd of gazelles for two kilometers, but they run much faster than you.","events.34.text":"At night, the herd of horses belonging to herders stands in the distance. A young horse is crying. You hear it, but you do not go closer.","events.35.text":"There are almost no trees on the steppe. You lie in a gully and wait all afternoon, until the wind has carried your scent away.","events.36.text":"The adult wolves of the pack take turns leading the way, trampling a broad path through the snow. You follow them, and you never have to slow down.","events.37.text":"More than a dozen wolves surround a moose together. When the meat is shared, you end up in the middle, but you still get a leg bone with marrow in it.","events.38.text":"Two breeding pairs in the pack argue over the position of the den entrance. You back away to one side and watch them bite at each other for a long time.","events.39.text":"There are only six of you, so chasing a moose takes patience. You take turns running, and any wolf that cannot keep up stops and waits.","events.40.text":"The pack is small, so each wolf gets more meat. But when you meet a large pack, you have to keep your distance.","events.41.text":"In winter there are only a few of you in the den. You sleep pressed against the mother wolf, and her heartbeat is slow.","events.42.text":"The two of you search the valley for a place to claim as your territory. There is no scent of other wolves, and no one to share the hunting with you.","events.43.text":"With only two wolves, when one gets hurt, the other has to hunt alone. You hide behind a spruce and wait for its paw to heal.","events.44.text":"In your first winter, you two squeeze into a small rock cave. The wind cannot reach you, but food is never enough.","events.45.text":"You are the first to charge at the herd of reindeer, running ahead of the pack, and your foot plunges through a thin patch of ice. You scramble up and keep running.","events.46.text":"You wait at the mouth of the den for a long time, until you are sure the wind carries no strange scent, and then you slowly walk out.","events.47.text":"You catch a scent you have never smelled before, and follow it to a human garbage heap. Hearing voices far off, you turn and walk away.","events.48.text":"You are the smallest pup in the litter. The mother feeds the other pups first, and you can only wait beside her.","events.49.text":"Your shoulders are broader than your littermates'. One night, while chasing a reindeer, you knock a young deer over with your head.","decisions.0.title":"Leaving the pack","decisions.0.body":"You are a little over a year old. The dominant male has begun to growl at you and drive you to the very back of the feeding line. You can stay in the pack and wait for a chance, or leave now and look for a valley with no wolves yet.","decisions.0.options.0.t":"Leave the pack and go alone","decisions.0.options.0.hint":"May find a mate and territory · High mortality on the way","decisions.0.options.0.result":"You leave the pack's territory before dawn. Their howls soon fade behind you.","decisions.0.options.1.t":"Stay and wait for a place to open up","decisions.0.options.1.hint":"Food secure · Many fights","decisions.0.options.1.result":"You keep following the pack. The dominant male growls at you once more, then walks away.","decisions.1.title":"An injured moose","decisions.1.body":"An adult moose has slipped on the ice and injured a hind leg, and it is slowly walking deeper into the forest. The pack has already circled to its flank.","decisions.1.options.0.t":"Join the attack and bite its hind leg","decisions.1.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · Risk of being kicked","decisions.1.options.0.result":"You bite its leg until it falls in the snow. The pack surges in from every side.","decisions.1.options.1.t":"Wait for it to fall, then pick up what is left","decisions.1.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.1.options.1.result":"You follow the pack and get only a little meat and some bone.","decisions.2.title":"Howls on the border","decisions.2.body":"A chorus of unfamiliar wolves rises from over the ridge. Their scent has reached the edge of your territory.","decisions.2.fact":"Howling helps maintain territory: a pack uses it to let unfamiliar packs know that the land already has owners (Harrington & Mech, 1983)","decisions.2.options.0.t":"Answer the howl and hold the territory","decisions.2.options.0.hint":"Territory secure · May end in a fight","decisions.2.options.0.result":"You all howl together, louder and louder. The other pack is silent for a long time, then slowly withdraws.","decisions.2.options.1.t":"Avoid it and go to another valley","decisions.2.options.1.hint":"Low risk · Less food","decisions.2.options.1.result":"You go around the border. The new valley is unfamiliar, and there is less prey than before.","decisions.3.title":"Where to dig the den","decisions.3.body":"Your belly is growing. You will give birth this spring, and the den must be somewhere that hides you from people and other packs.","decisions.3.options.0.t":"Give birth in the old den","decisions.3.options.0.hint":"Familiar · Close to the road","decisions.3.options.0.result":"You give birth to pups in the old den. Human footprints still mark the snow at the entrance.","decisions.3.options.1.t":"Choose a site far from the road","decisions.3.options.1.hint":"Safe · Takes time to find","decisions.3.options.1.result":"You dig a new den under a rock on the mountainside. When the pups are born, the air around you smells of resin.","decisions.4.title":"A cough in the pack","decisions.4.body":"Several wolves in the pack have begun to cough, their noses running and their eyes red and swollen. A young wolf has collapsed in the snow and does not move.","decisions.4.options.0.t":"Leave the sick pack and forage alone","decisions.4.options.0.hint":"Keeps you safe · Less food","decisions.4.options.0.result":"You leave the pack and find a frozen lemming on a ridge far away.","decisions.4.options.1.t":"Stay in the pack and groom each other","decisions.4.options.1.hint":"Companionship · Virus spreads fast","decisions.4.options.1.result":"You crowd together with the pack and lick each other's faces. A few days later your throat begins to itch.","decisions.5.title":"A dead sheep at the pasture","decisions.5.body":"A dead sheep lies on the grass at the edge of the pasture. Its body is intact, but you smell something sharp and wrong.","decisions.5.options.0.t":"Eat it","decisions.5.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ · May be poisonous","decisions.5.options.0.result":"You swallow half the sheep in one go. The smell churns in your stomach.","decisions.5.options.1.t":"Go around it","decisions.5.options.1.hint":"Safety · Hungry","decisions.5.options.1.result":"You go around the sheep and head for a hillside farther away. There is only a flock of lemmings there.","decisions.6.title":"Too old","decisions.6.body":"Your teeth are worn down, and at night you can no longer catch hares. The pack still keeps a place for you, but you know that before long, the last snow will come.","decisions.6.options.0.t":"Stay on the edge of the pack","decisions.6.options.0.hint":"Secure · Less food","decisions.6.options.0.result":"Every day you get only the bones the others leave behind, but you are still awake, and still in the pack.","decisions.6.options.1.t":"Go alone to the edge of the forest","decisions.6.options.1.hint":"A little more food · Alone","decisions.6.options.1.result":"You walk to the edge of the forest, where there is some small prey, but no companions.","decisions.7.title":"Night on the farm","decisions.7.body":"The farmer has strung a new barbed-wire fence around the edge of your territory, and at night he keeps a dog with a bell. A sheep is bleating on the grass, not far from your den.","decisions.7.options.0.t":"Carry off the sheep at night","decisions.7.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Risk of being shot↑","decisions.7.options.0.result":"You slip through a gap in the barbed wire and carry the sheep back to the woods. A gunshot rings out behind you.","decisions.7.options.1.t":"Circle to the far side of the woods and hunt only hares","decisions.7.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.7.options.1.result":"You go around the pasture and chase hares in the woods. The hares are thin, and you do not eat much.","decisions.8.title":"A tourist car by the road","decisions.8.body":"A road runs through your hunting ground. Tourist cars are parked on the shoulder, with long lenses poking out of the windows. An elk is slowly walking toward you from the other side of the road.","decisions.8.options.0.t":"Pounce on the elk on the road","decisions.8.options.0.hint":"Food↑↑ Vehicle risk↑","decisions.8.options.0.result":"You burst out of the snowbank by the road. As the elk falls, the squeal of car brakes sounds behind you.","decisions.8.options.1.t":"Go around through the woods","decisions.8.options.1.hint":"Safety · Food↓","decisions.8.options.1.result":"You skirt the road through the trees and wait at the edge of the woods until the elk has walked away. It never looks back.","decisions.9.title":"The pack splits","decisions.9.body":"The two breeding pairs in the pack are growing more hostile. Part of the wolves are preparing to leave, taking half of the adults with them to find a new valley. You have to decide which side to follow.","decisions.9.options.0.t":"Follow the half that leaves, to a new valley","decisions.9.options.0.hint":"New territory · Dangerous journey","decisions.9.options.0.result":"You walk for three days and cross a mountain ridge. The old pack is still howling behind you, but you can no longer hear it.","decisions.9.options.1.t":"Stay with the old pack","decisions.9.options.1.hint":"Reliable food · Many fights","decisions.9.options.1.result":"You stay. From then on, at every hunt, you have to fight the two breeding pairs for a share of the meat.","decisions.10.title":"The first winter without a pack","decisions.10.body":"Without a pack to help you, the winter prey is only enough for one wolf. The mother wolf in the den gives a low whimper, as if asking what you should do.","decisions.10.options.0.t":"Go together to hunt a moose on the ridge","decisions.10.options.0.hint":"Food↑ · Both could be injured","decisions.10.options.0.result":"The two of you bite into a moose's hind leg together. It kicks her in the shoulder, but you still bring it down.","decisions.10.options.1.t":"Stay in the den and take turns going out for food","decisions.10.options.1.hint":"Steady · Little food","decisions.10.options.1.result":"You take turns going out. Each time you find only a hare, and sometimes you come back with nothing at all.","opening.title":"Mother chooses the den","opening.body":"You have not been born yet. In spring your mother digs a den in the snow. A south-facing entrance gets sun, but it is close to the road and the pastures; a north-facing one is colder, but far from people.","opening.options.0.t":"Dig on the hillside facing south","opening.options.0.hint":"Warm · Close to people","opening.options.0.result":"You are born in the den on the hillside. Sunlight warms the entrance, and one day you hear a car in the distance.","opening.options.1.t":"Dig in the valley facing north","opening.options.1.hint":"Quiet · Cold · Less prey","opening.options.1.result":"You are born in the den in the valley. The wind is cold, and your mother shields you with her body until your eyes open.","actions.instinct.name":"Follow instinct","actions.instinct.desc":"Keep to the pack's rhythm and do what a gray wolf should do.","actions.forage.name":"Hunt","actions.forage.desc":"Follow the pack after prey, or search alone for carrion and small game.","actions.hide.name":"Lie in ambush","actions.hide.desc":"Crouch under shrubs and cedars, waiting for prey to come close, or keep out of people's way.","actions.rest.name":"Rest","actions.rest.desc":"Sleep in the den or under a rock sheltered from the wind, saving your strength for the next hunt.","actions.explore.name":"Travel far","actions.explore.desc":"Cross the ridges to see whether other valleys have territory standing empty.","actions.mate.name":"Mate and breed","actions.mate.done":"This spring you gave birth to {n} pups in the den.","idle.0":"You leave a deep line of footprints in the snow.","idle.1":"There are no clear borders between wolf packs, only scent and howls.","idle.2":"The wind comes from the north, and you bury your nose in your tail.","idle.3":"Behind a rock you smell moose.","idle.4":"A companion's tail brushes lightly along your back, and you stay close together.","idle.5":"Night is coming soon, and the pack begins to gather.","idle.6":"A howl comes from far away. You do not answer.","idle.7":"A crow follows you, waiting for you to leave scraps.","actions.forage.text.0":"You follow the pack, your legs slipping on the snow.","actions.forage.text.1":"The deer herd scatters, and you lock onto one that has fallen behind.","actions.hide.text.0":"You lie in the snow, and your breath freezes into a thin layer of frost.","actions.hide.text.1":"The gunshots in the distance stop, and you wait another hour.","actions.rest.text.0":"You press among your companions, eyes closed, ears still twitching.","actions.explore.text.0":"You walk a long way, the howls behind you growing fainter.","actions.explore.text.1":"A river blocks the way ahead, and you go around by following its source.","birth.rolls.0.options.4.0":"a sandy den on the Arctic tundra","birth.rolls.0.options.5.0":"a rocky rise on the Mongolian steppe"}};
